// Canonical question-bank data for Forge — shared by forge-quiz.html, anvil.html, and teacher.html.
// Single source of truth: edit banks/subjects here, not in the app files.

const BANKS = {
  "2.1.1": {
    label: "Economic Growth",
    color: "#2563eb",
    questions: [
      {
        id:"GRO-01",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"UK real GDP was £2.1tn in 2022 and £2.15tn in 2023. A student says: 'The UK has high economic growth because GDP is over £2 trillion.' What is wrong with this statement?",
        options:{A:"Nothing — a GDP above £2 trillion does indicate high growth. (all else equal)",B:"The student should have used nominal GDP, not real GDP.",C:"The student is describing the level of GDP, not the rate of growth",D:"Growth can only be measured using GDP per capita."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GRO-01",
        scaffold:"Growth is a rate of change, not an absolute level — this is one of the most common Year 12 errors. The formula is: growth rate = ((GDP₁ − GDP₀) / GDP₀) × 100. Here: ((2.15 − 2.1) / 2.1) × 100 = 0.05 / 2.1 × 100 ≈ 2.4%. The UK has a large GDP because it is a large economy — that tells us nothing about the rate at which it is growing. China grew at ~5% in 2023 with a larger GDP than the UK; that doesn't make China's growth rate bigger than every smaller economy. Examiner tip: whenever a question gives you two GDP figures, calculate the growth rate before drawing any conclusion. 'High GDP' ≠ 'high growth'.",
        reforge:{stem:"Country X has GDP of $500bn growing at 6% p.a. Country Y has GDP of $15tn growing at 1.2% p.a. Which has higher economic growth?",options:{A:"Country Y, because its GDP is larger.",B:"They are equal because both are growing.",C:"Country X, because 6% > 1.2% — growth is about the rate, not the level.",D:"It cannot be determined without knowing population. (in the market described)"},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-02",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"A government cuts income tax to boost consumer spending, raising short-run real GDP. Which best describes what has occurred?",
        options:{A:"Potential growth — productive capacity has increased.",B:"Both actual and potential growth, since GDP increased. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Neither, because tax cuts don't affect growth.",D:"Actual growth — real GDP rose due to higher AD, but productive capacity is unchanged."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GRO-02",
        scaffold:"Edexcel examiners consistently penalise confusion between actual and potential growth — they are fundamentally different concepts requiring different diagrams. Actual growth: AD shifts right → the economy moves along the SRAS curve toward (or beyond) full capacity. Real GDP increases but no new productive capacity is created. Potential growth: LRAS shifts right → the economy can produce more at every price level. This requires investment in factors of production (capital, labour quality, technology). A tax cut works through C → AD → actual growth. It cannot shift LRAS because it does not improve productive capacity. Draw both diagrams and label clearly: actual growth = movement, potential growth = shift of the production frontier.",
        reforge:{stem:"A government invests in broadband and STEM education over 10 years. Which type of growth does this primarily represent?",options:{A:"Actual growth — AD increases immediately.",B:"Neither — it's fiscal policy, not growth.",C:"Actual growth, because G is a component of AD. (in the market described)",D:"Potential growth — productive capacity shifts outward over time."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-03",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"The economy is producing above its trend level of output. What type of output gap does this represent?",
        options:{A:"A negative output gap — spare capacity exists.",B:"A positive output gap — actual GDP exceeds potential GDP.",C:"A positive output gap — spare capacity for further growth. (all else equal)",D:"No output gap exists when the economy is growing."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GRO-03",
        scaffold:"Output gap direction confuses most students because 'positive' sounds good. Positive output gap = actual GDP > potential GDP → the economy is overheating. Resources are overstretched, wages bid up, inflationary pressure builds. SRAS cannot sustain this — costs rise, SRAS shifts left, output falls back to potential. Negative output gap = actual GDP < potential → spare capacity, higher unemployment, deflationary pressure. Examiner language: 'inflationary pressure' (positive gap), 'deflationary pressure / deflationary gap' (negative). On a diagram: positive gap = point to the right of where LRAS meets SRAS; negative = point to the left. Never say a positive output gap is 'good for the economy' — it signals overheating.",
        reforge:{stem:"Unemployment is rising and factories run below capacity. Which is correct?",options:{A:"Positive output gap because production is positive.",B:"Negative output gap — actual output is below potential.",C:"Positive output gap — spare capacity means room to grow. (all else equal)",D:"Output gaps only exist during recessions."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-04",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"On a trade cycle diagram, which phase shows real GDP rising back toward the trend line after a trough?",
        options:{A:"Recovery",B:"Boom",C:"Recession (all else equal)",D:"Slowdown"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GRO-04",
        scaffold:"Trade cycle sequence: boom (above trend) → downturn → recession (two consecutive quarters of negative growth) → trough (lowest point) → recovery (GDP rising back toward trend) → boom again. Recovery is the upswing — GDP is increasing, but it has not yet returned to trend. Students often confuse recovery with boom: in recovery the economy is growing but still below trend potential. On a diagram: the trough is the lowest point of the wave; recovery is the positive slope that follows. Examiner tip: if asked to identify a phase, check two things: (1) direction of GDP change, and (2) position relative to trend line.",
        reforge:{stem:"During recovery, which is most likely?",options:{A:"Real GDP rising; the negative output gap narrowing.",B:"Real GDP falling toward the trough.",C:"Real GDP flat at trend.",D:"Inflation at its highest — economy overheating. (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-05",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"Between 2010 and 2023, UK GDP per capita grew more slowly than total GDP. Which factor best explains this?",
        options:{A:"The UK experienced deflation.",B:"GDP per capita only measures services.",C:"Population growth meant output was shared among more people.",D:"Total GDP was nominal while per capita was real. (all else equal)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GRO-01",
        scaffold:"GDP per capita = total real GDP ÷ population. If population grows at the same rate as GDP, per-capita GDP is unchanged even though total GDP rose. Between 2010–2023, UK population grew due to net migration and natural increase — so GDP had to grow faster than population for per-capita growth to be positive. This distinction matters for welfare analysis: a country could double its GDP purely through population growth with no improvement in average living standards. Examiner application: always distinguish between aggregate GDP growth and per-capita GDP growth when assessing living standards. The question 'has growth improved living standards?' requires per-capita analysis.",
        reforge:{stem:"Country Z's GDP grows at 4% but population also grows at 3.5%. What can we say about living standards?",options:{A:"Rising rapidly at 4%.",B:"Falling because population growth is unsustainable. (all else equal)",C:"Broadly stagnant — per-capita growth is only ~0.5%.",D:"We cannot comment using GDP data."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-06",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"LRAS shifts right through investment in education and technology. What happens to long-run price level and real output?",
        options:{A:"Price level rises, output rises. (in the market described) (all else equal)",B:"Both unchanged.",C:"Price level falls, output falls.",D:"Price level falls (or rises more slowly), output rises."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GRO-02",
        scaffold:"When LRAS shifts right, productive capacity increases. With the same position of AD, the new equilibrium has: (1) higher real output — the economy can produce more goods and services, and (2) lower price level — more supply at the same demand pushes prices down. This is called non-inflationary or supply-side growth — it avoids the inflationary trade-off of demand-side stimulus. Contrast: if AD shifts right with unchanged LRAS, output rises AND the price level rises (inflationary growth). Draw the AD/AS diagram: LRAS shifts right → new intersection of AD with new LRAS at higher Y and lower P. Label both changes clearly — examiners expect both consequences.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which correctly matches growth type to its AD/AS effect?",options:{A:"Actual = LRAS right; Potential = AD right. (all else equal)",B:"Both shift AD right.",C:"Actual growth has no diagram.",D:"Actual = AD right (along SRAS); Potential = LRAS right."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-08",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"The UK's PPF shifts outward. Which could have caused this?",
        options:{A:"A rise in consumer confidence.",B:"Net migration of skilled workers and new capital investment.",C:"A cut in interest rates by the BoE.",D:"A rise in the exchange rate making imports cheaper. (all else equal)"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GRO-02",
        scaffold:"The PPF shows the maximum possible output of two goods given fixed resources. It shifts outward only when productive capacity increases — more or better resources. Causes of outward shift: more capital (investment), more labour (population growth, immigration), better technology (raises productivity of existing resources), improved human capital (education, training). Demand-side factors (rising AD, consumer confidence, lower interest rates) do NOT shift the PPF — they move the economy from inside the PPF toward its boundary but cannot push it outward. Exam trap: 'the economy grew because of rising consumer spending' → this explains actual growth (movement toward PPF), not potential growth (outward PPF shift).",
        reforge:{stem:"Which represents a movement FROM inside the PPF TO the boundary, not a shift?",options:{A:"Discovery of new oil reserves.",B:"Reducing structural unemployment, putting idle workers back into production.",C:"AI breakthrough raising all-sector productivity. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",D:"Large-scale apprenticeship programme."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"GRO-09",spec:"2.1.1",stem:"UK real GDP grew by 0.1% in 2023. A student writes: 'The economy shrank because growth was almost zero.' Is this correct?",
        options:{A:"No — the economy still grew, just slowly. Contraction requires negative growth.",B:"Yes — 0.1% is effectively a contraction.",C:"Yes — growth below 2% counts as recession.",D:"No — but only because 0.1% is above the inflation target. (in the market described)"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GRO-01",
        scaffold:"Recession has a precise definition: two or more consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth. 0.1% growth means real GDP increased — the economy expanded. It is weak growth, but it is growth. Examiners penalise students who use 'recession' loosely. On the mark scheme: 'very slow growth' / 'near-stagnation' is acceptable; 'recession' when growth is positive is not. Related error: confusing disinflation (falling rate of inflation) with deflation (falling price level). Both involve 'things getting smaller' but mean very different things. When reading data questions, identify: (1) is the number positive or negative? (2) is it a level or a rate of change?",
        reforge:{stem:"GDP growth: −0.3% (Q1), −0.1% (Q2), +0.4% (Q3). Which is accurate?",options:{A:"Recession in Q1–Q2 (two consecutive negative quarters), then growth returned in Q3.",B:"Recession for all three quarters.",C:"No recession because the annual total is positive. (assuming other conditions are unchanged)",D:"Recession only applies below −1%."},correct:"A"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "2.1.2": {
    label: "Inflation",
    color: "#dc2626",
    questions: [
      {
        id:"INF-01",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"UK CPI rose from 120 to 126 over a year. A student says: 'Prices are high.' What is the correct interpretation?",
        options:{A:"The student is right — 126 is a high price level. (all else equal)",B:"The CPI doesn't measure prices.",C:"The student is confusing the price level with inflation",D:"126 means prices fell."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-INF-01",
        scaffold:"Inflation = the rate of change of the price level, not the level itself. A CPI of 126 tells you the level; the inflation rate is ((126−120)/120)×100 = 5%. High prices ≠ high inflation — prices could be high but stable (0% inflation).",
        reforge:{stem:"A country's CPI is 200 but hasn't changed in 3 years. What is the inflation rate?",options:{A:"200% — the CPI is very high.",B:"100% — it has doubled from a base of 100. (all else equal) (all else equal)",C:"0% — the price level is unchanged, so inflation is zero.",D:"It cannot be calculated from CPI data."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-02",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A student says the CPI basket 'always contains the same goods.' Why is this wrong?",
        options:{A:"The basket is fixed permanently by law and never updated.",B:"The basket contains only food and fuel — the two largest household expenditures. (all else equal)",C:"The basket changes daily to reflect real-time price movements.",D:"The basket is updated annually to reflect changing consumer spending patterns."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-INF-02",
        scaffold:"The ONS updates the CPI basket every year. Items are added (e.g. oat milk, smartwatches) and removed (e.g. CD players) to reflect how households actually spend. A fixed basket would quickly become unrepresentative.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which is a valid limitation of CPI despite annual basket updates?",options:{A:"It never changes so it's always outdated.",B:"It only measures food prices.",C:"It includes housing costs in the same way as RPI. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"It reflects average spending, so it may not match any individual household’s inflation experience."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-03",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A student writes: 'Demand-pull inflation is too much money chasing too few goods' but cannot explain it using a diagram. Which diagram explanation is correct?",
        options:{A:"SRAS shifts left, pushing the price level up. (assuming other conditions are unchanged) (all else equal)",B:"AD shifts right, increasing real output and the price level — demand-pull inflation.",C:"LRAS shifts right, reducing the price level.",D:"AD shifts left, creating deflation."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-INF-03",
        scaffold:"Demand-pull inflation: AD shifts right → economy moves along the SRAS → higher price level AND higher real output (up to full capacity). Causes include: rising consumer confidence, fiscal stimulus, lower interest rates, weaker exchange rate boosting net exports.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which of these would cause demand-pull inflation?",options:{A:"A rise in oil prices increasing firms' costs.",B:"A large cut in income tax boosting disposable income and shifting AD right.",C:"New regulations increasing the cost of production. (over the period considered)",D:"A fall in consumer confidence."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-04",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A student explains cost-push inflation using only wage increases. Which additional cost factors should they include?",
        options:{A:"Raw material prices (e.g. oil), energy costs",B:"None — wages are the only cost-push factor.",C:"Consumer confidence and government spending. (all else equal)",D:"Interest rates and money supply."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-INF-04",
        scaffold:"Cost-push inflation = SRAS shifts left due to higher production costs. Wages are ONE factor, but also: commodity/energy prices (oil shocks), exchange rate depreciation (imports cost more in domestic currency), higher indirect taxes (VAT), supply chain disruptions.",
        reforge:{stem:"Sterling depreciates by 15%. How might this cause cost-push inflation?",options:{A:"Imported inputs cost more in pounds, raising firms’ costs and shifting SRAS left.",B:"It can't — exchange rates only affect the current account.",C:"It makes exports cheaper, reducing costs.",D:"It increases consumer confidence, causing demand-pull inflation. (all else equal) (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-05",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A student claims: 'All deflation is bad for the economy.' How should this be evaluated?",
        options:{A:"The student is correct — deflation always causes recession. (all else equal) (all else equal)",B:"Deflation is always good because prices are falling.",C:"Deflation from falling demand (malign) can cause debt spirals and recession",D:"Deflation and disinflation are the same thing."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-INF-05",
        scaffold:"Malign deflation: falling AD → lower prices, but also falling output, rising unemployment, rising real debt burden → deflationary spiral (Japan 1990s–2000s). Benign deflation: technological progress / productivity gains → costs fall → prices fall while output rises (e.g. consumer electronics). Context matters.",
        reforge:{stem:"The price of electronics falls by 8% over a year due to improved manufacturing technology, while the overall economy grows at 3%. Is this harmful deflation?",options:{A:"Yes — any falling prices are dangerous.",B:"Yes — it will cause a deflationary spiral.",C:"No — productivity-led deflation can occur while output is rising.",D:"No — because 8% is below the 10% danger threshold. (all else equal)"},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-06",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A student uses RPI and CPI interchangeably. Which key difference should they know?",
        options:{A:"CPI and RPI are identical — they measure the same basket with the same formula. (in the market described) (with other relevant factors held constant)",B:"RPI is updated annually but CPI uses the same formula since it was introduced.",C:"CPI measures wholesale prices; RPI measures retail consumer prices.",D:"CPI uses a geometric mean and excludes some housing costs; RPI uses an arithmetic mean and includes more."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-INF-06",
        scaffold:"CPI: geometric mean, excludes mortgage interest payments and council tax. RPI: arithmetic mean (tends to give higher figure), includes mortgage interest payments. RPI is no longer an official National Statistic due to formula flaws, but is still used for some contracts (e.g. rail fares, student loans).",
        reforge:{stem:"Interest rates rise sharply. What happens to RPI relative to CPI?",options:{A:"RPI falls below CPI.",B:"Both move identically.",C:"Neither is affected by interest rates. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"RPI rises relative to CPI because it includes mortgage interest payments, which have increased."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-07",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"What is the Bank of England's current inflation target?",
        options:{A:"0% — price stability means zero inflation. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"2% CPI — set by the government and targeted by the Bank of England via monetary policy.",C:"5% — a moderate level encourages spending.",D:"2% RPI — the RPI measure is used."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-INF-07",
        scaffold:"The target is 2% CPI (symmetric — undershooting is as much a concern as overshooting). Set by the Chancellor, pursued by the MPC. The letter-writing threshold is ±1pp (i.e. below 1% or above 3%). The target is NOT 0% — moderate inflation greases the wheels of the economy.",
        reforge:{stem:"UK CPI inflation reaches 0.8%. What is the BoE required to do?",options:{A:"Nothing — inflation is low, which is good. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"The Governor must explain in an open letter why inflation is below target and the MPC’s response.",C:"Immediately raise interest rates.",D:"Change the target to 1%."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-08",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"In 2015, the price of a basket of goods was £80. In 2023, it was £104. Using 2015 as the base year (= 100), what is the index number for 2023?",
        options:{A:"130",B:"104 (all else equal)",C:"24",D:"80"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-INF-08",
        scaffold:"Index number = (value in current year / value in base year) × 100 = (104/80) × 100 = 130. This means prices are 30% higher than the base year. Common error: using the raw price (104) as the index.",
        reforge:{stem:"Base year index = 100 (price: £200). Current year price: £230. What is the index number?",options:{A:"115",B:"230 (all else equal)",C:"30",D:"130"},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-09",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"A country's inflation rate reaches 12%. A student labels this 'hyperinflation.' Is this correct?",
        options:{A:"Yes — any double-digit inflation qualifies as hyperinflation.",B:"Yes — hyperinflation is defined as any inflation rate above 10%.",C:"No — the rate is high but does not meet the threshold for hyperinflation.",D:"No — hyperinflation only occurs during or immediately after wartime. (all else equal)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-INF-09",
        scaffold:"Hyperinflation is extreme: prices doubling every few weeks/months. Cagan's threshold: 50% per month. 12% annually is high but manageable with normal monetary policy. Zimbabwe peaked at ~79.6 billion % per month in 2008. Don't conflate uncomfortable inflation with hyperinflation.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which scenario best illustrates hyperinflation?",options:{A:"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.",B:"Japan experiencing 3% inflation after decades near 0%. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Venezuela's currency becomes worthless within weeks as prices rise extremely rapidly, so people switch to foreign currency or barter.",D:"The US Federal Reserve raising rates to 5%."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"INF-10",spec:"2.1.2",stem:"Explain how a wage-price spiral develops. (2 marks)",
        type:"short_answer",
        model_answer:"Workers demand higher wages to keep up with rising prices (knowledge). Firms pass on higher wage costs through higher prices, which triggers further wage demands, creating a self-reinforcing cycle of rising wages and prices (analysis).",
        tag:"MC-INF-10",
        scaffold:"The spiral: prices rise → workers demand higher wages to maintain real income → firms face higher costs → firms raise prices to protect margins → prices rise further → workers demand even higher wages → repeat. It requires a mechanism that sustains the loop (e.g. strong trade unions, accommodative monetary policy).",
        reforge:{stem:"Which factor would most likely BREAK a wage-price spiral?",options:{A:"Trade unions negotiating even larger pay rises. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"Government increasing the minimum wage.",C:"A further depreciation of the exchange rate.",D:"The central bank raises rates, reducing AD and firms’ ability to pass on costs."},correct:"D"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "2.1.3": {
    label: "Employment & Unemployment",
    color: "#7c3aed",
    questions: [
      {
        id:"EMP-01",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"A 45-year-old leaves work to care for an elderly parent. Under the ILO definition, this person is:",
        options:{A:"Unemployed — they don't have a job.",B:"Employed — caring is work.",C:"Underemployed — they want more hours. (for the economy in question)",D:"Economically inactive — not seeking or available for paid work."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-EMP-01",
        scaffold:"The ILO/LFS definition has three strict criteria, all of which must be met simultaneously: (1) without paid work — not even one hour; (2) available to start work within two weeks; (3) actively sought work in the past four weeks. Failing any one criterion means the person is NOT counted as unemployed. Economic inactivity is a distinct category: people who are neither employed nor unemployed — they are not seeking or available for work. Includes: full-time students, carers, long-term sick, discouraged workers (gave up seeking), and early retirees. Exam trap: a redundant worker who stops looking ('discouraged') moves from unemployed to economically inactive — the unemployment rate falls even though nothing improved for that person. Examiners test whether you can distinguish employed / ILO-unemployed / economically inactive.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which person counts as unemployed (ILO)?",options:{A:"A retired teacher, 63, not looking for work.",B:"A graduate applying for jobs for 3 months, available immediately.",C:"A stay-at-home parent not seeking employment. (in the market described)",D:"A full-time university student."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-02",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"A region's coal mines close permanently due to the shift to renewables. Workers can't find jobs because their skills don't match tech-sector vacancies. This is:",
        options:{A:"Cutting interest rates to boost aggregate demand and create jobs.",B:"Funding retraining programmes and improving geographical mobility through housing reform.",C:"Increasing government infrastructure spending to stimulate the economy. (in the market described)",D:"Raising unemployment benefits to increase worker bargaining power."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-EMP-02",
        scaffold:"The three types of unemployment require distinct policy responses — confusing them is a standard examiner-flagged error. Frictional: between jobs, short-term, normal in a dynamic labour market → information provision (job centres, Universal Jobmatch). Cyclical/demand-deficient: caused by recession → demand-side policies (fiscal stimulus, lower interest rates). Structural: long-term mismatch between skills demanded and supplied, often from deindustrialisation → supply-side policies only (retraining, occupational mobility, housing reform for geographical mobility). The coal miners example is structural: their specific skills (mining) have no demand, and they cannot easily retrain or relocate. A government cutting interest rates (cyclical fix) would not help them find work.",
        reforge:{stem:"A newly qualified teacher takes 6 weeks to find a post, turning down two unsuitable offers. This is best classified as:",options:{A:"Frictional — short-term search between entering the market and finding a role.",B:"Structural — skills mismatch.",C:"Cyclical — low aggregate demand.",D:"Voluntary — they chose not to accept. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-03",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"A student draws a Phillips curve showing a permanent trade-off. What critique should they consider?",
        options:{A:"Friedman/Phelps: the trade-off is only short-run",B:"The Phillips curve is always vertical — there's never a trade-off.",C:"It only applies to developing economies.",D:"Inflation and unemployment always move together."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-EMP-03",
        scaffold:"The expectations-augmented Phillips curve (Friedman/Phelps, 1968) is the key critique of the original Phillips relationship. Short run: a trade-off appears — lower unemployment comes with higher inflation (as AD rises, firms hire more but also raise prices). Long run: workers realise real wages have fallen (money wages rose less than prices) → they demand higher nominal wages → costs rise → SRAS shifts left → unemployment returns to the natural rate (NAIRU) at a higher price level. The SRPC shifts UP. Repeat stimulus: each attempt to push unemployment below NAIRU results in a higher inflation rate with no permanent employment gain. LRPC is vertical at the NAIRU. Exam diagram: draw multiple SRPCs shifting up; LRPC vertical; label the short-run move and the long-run adjustment.",
        reforge:{stem:"Government pushes unemployment below the natural rate via expansionary fiscal policy. What does the expectations-augmented Phillips curve predict over time?",options:{A:"Inflation falls as the economy adjusts.",B:"Unemployment stays low permanently with stable higher inflation. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Workers revise expectations up; SRPC shifts up and unemployment returns to the natural rate.",D:"The natural rate itself falls."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-04",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"UK claimant count: 1.6m. ILO unemployment: 1.4m. Which best explains the difference?",
        options:{A:"ILO unemployment is always higher because it uses a stricter definition. (in the market described)",B:"The two measures always produce exactly the same figures by design.",C:"Claimant count includes some benefit recipients not actively seeking work under ILO criteria.",D:"ILO surveys are restricted to London and do not cover the regions."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-EMP-04",
        scaffold:"Two measures, two different methodologies — examiners test whether you understand the precise difference. Claimant count: administrative data — those claiming Universal Credit (or JSA) for unemployment reasons. Quick to produce, but politically influenced (changing eligibility rules changes the count without affecting real unemployment). ILO/LFS: survey-based — must be (1) without paid work, (2) available to start within 2 weeks, (3) actively seeking work in the past 4 weeks. More internationally comparable, wider measure. Common reason claimant > ILO: some claimants don't actively seek work. Common reason ILO > claimant: some actively seeking work don't claim benefits (e.g. recent graduates, those ineligible). Never use the two interchangeably in an essay.",
        reforge:{stem:"Government tightens benefit eligibility, removing 200k from UC. What happens?",options:{A:"Both measures fall by 200k.",B:"ILO falls but claimant count unchanged. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (all else equal)",C:"Both unaffected by benefit policy.",D:"Claimant count falls, but ILO is largely unaffected — those people may still be jobless and seeking."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-05",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"On a labour market diagram, the real wage is set above equilibrium by a minimum wage. The gap between Qs and Qd represents:",
        options:{A:"A labour shortage.",B:"Frictional unemployment. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Full employment.",D:"Real-wage unemployment — Qs exceeds Qd at that wage."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-EMP-05",
        scaffold:"Labour market diagram with a minimum wage above equilibrium: at wage W_min, quantity of labour supplied (Qs) > quantity demanded (Qd). The gap (Qs − Qd) = real-wage (classical) unemployment. More workers want to work at this higher wage but fewer firms are willing to hire — the price floor creates a surplus of labour. Key examiner distinction: this is not cyclical (caused by demand deficiency) or structural (caused by skills mismatch) — it is caused by the wage being artificially held above market-clearing level. Policy: lower the minimum wage (market solution) or increase labour productivity so labour demand shifts right (making the minimum wage no longer binding).",
        reforge:{stem:"What would reduce real-wage unemployment caused by a minimum wage?",options:{A:"Raising the minimum wage further. (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described)",B:"Increasing labour productivity (shifting demand right) so firms hire more at the prevailing wage.",C:"Increasing labour supply.",D:"Reducing the retirement age."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-07",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"Which policy would most directly reduce structural unemployment?",
        options:{A:"Cutting interest rates to boost aggregate demand and create jobs.",B:"Funding retraining programmes and improving geographical mobility through housing reform.",C:"Increasing government infrastructure spending to stimulate the economy. (in the market described)",D:"Raising unemployment benefits to increase worker bargaining power."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-EMP-02",
        scaffold:"Structural unemployment requires supply-side solutions because its cause is on the supply side — skills mismatch or immobility. Occupational immobility: workers' skills don't match available vacancies → retraining programmes, apprenticeships, vocational education. Geographical immobility: workers can't/won't move to where jobs are → affordable housing, transport investment, childcare provision. Demand-side policies (lower interest rates, fiscal stimulus) increase total demand and can cure cyclical unemployment — but if the jobs require skills that unemployed workers don't have, stimulus merely bids up wages for skilled workers without helping the structurally unemployed. Examiner tip: always identify the type of unemployment before evaluating the policy — the match between cause and remedy is what earns evaluation marks.",
        reforge:{stem:"Cyclical unemployment rising in a recession. Which policy directly addresses it?",options:{A:"Expansionary fiscal or monetary policy to increase AD.",B:"Retraining coal miners for tech. (in the market described)",C:"Deregulating the labour market.",D:"Reducing trade union power."},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-08",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"The natural rate of unemployment best refers to:",
        options:{A:"Unemployment at full capacity: frictional + structural, but no cyclical.",B:"Zero unemployment.",C:"The unemployment rate during a recession. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",D:"The rate at which inflation reaches 0%."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-EMP-03",
        scaffold:"The natural rate of unemployment (NAIRU — Non-Accelerating Inflation Rate of Unemployment) is the level of unemployment consistent with stable inflation. At the NAIRU: frictional unemployment exists (people between jobs) and structural unemployment exists (skills mismatches) — but there is no cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment. Full employment is NOT zero unemployment — it means the economy is at potential output with no cyclical component. Reducing unemployment below NAIRU through demand stimulus causes accelerating inflation (as per the Friedman/Phelps expectations-augmented model). The NAIRU itself can be lowered through supply-side reform — better matching, retraining, reducing geographical immobility.",
        reforge:{stem:"If a government reduces occupational immobility through retraining, what happens to the natural rate?",options:{A:"Rises — more enter the labour force.",B:"Unchanged — only demand-side policy affects it.",C:"Falls — structural unemployment decreases.",D:"Falls to zero."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"EMP-10",spec:"2.1.3",stem:"An increase in immigration of skilled workers would shift:",
        options:{A:"Labour demand right — firms demand more.",B:"Labour supply left — immigrants compete for fewer jobs. (all else equal)",C:"Labour supply right — more workers available at each wage.",D:"Neither — immigration has no labour market effect."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-EMP-05",
        scaffold:"Immigration shifts the labour supply curve to the right: at any given wage, more workers are available. On a standard labour market diagram: Ls shifts right → new equilibrium has lower wage (W₁ < W₀) and higher employment (Q₁ > Q₀). However, immigrants also increase aggregate demand (they spend on goods and services), potentially shifting labour demand right too — partially offsetting the wage effect. Exam evaluation: the net wage effect depends on the skill level of migrants (high-skill migrants complement native workers; low-skill migrants may substitute), the elasticity of labour demand, and the time horizon (short run: supply shock; long run: demand adjusts). Never state categorically that immigration 'always lowers wages' — this ignores the demand-side effect.",
        reforge:{stem:"An ageing population with rising retirements and no offsetting immigration would shift:",options:{A:"Labour supply right. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"Labour demand left.",C:"Both curves right.",D:"Labour supply left — fewer workers at each wage."},correct:"D"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "2.1.4": {
    label: "Balance of Payments",
    color: "#059669",
    questions: [
      {
        id:"BOP-01",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"UK current account deficit: 3.1% of GDP (2023). A student writes: 'This is very bad for the UK economy.' Which best evaluates this?",
        options:{A:"Correct — any deficit is harmful.",B:"Wrong — deficits are always beneficial.",C:"Wrong — the current account doesn't matter. (in the market described)",D:"Partly right: a persistent deficit can signal uncompetitiveness"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-BOP-01",
        scaffold:"A current account deficit is not inherently bad — context determines significance. Arguments that it matters: (1) if financed by 'hot money' (short-term capital flows), a sudden reversal (sudden stop) can cause a currency crisis; (2) persistent deficit implies the country is living beyond its means — importing more than it earns; (3) may signal weak export competitiveness. Arguments that it doesn't matter: (1) the US has run persistent deficits for decades while remaining the world's largest economy; (2) a deficit could reflect strong domestic demand and investment (productive imports); (3) it is automatically financed — the financial account must surplus by the same amount. Examiner evaluation: size, persistence, financing method, and cause all determine significance.",
        reforge:{stem:"A developing country's deficit is 2% of GDP, driven by importing capital machinery for new infrastructure. Is this necessarily a problem?",options:{A:"Yes — all deficits reduce GDP.",B:"Not necessarily — the imports are productive investments that may boost future export capacity.",C:"Yes — only domestic machinery should be used. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"No — deficits never matter."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-02",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"A student writes: 'The UK's budget deficit widened to 5% of GDP, worsening the current account deficit.' The error is:",
        options:{A:"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept. (all else equal)",B:"The fiscal deficit (G − T) and current account deficit (M − X) measure different things.",C:"The fiscal deficit cannot be expressed as a percentage of GDP.",D:"The current account only measures trade in goods, not services."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-BOP-02",
        scaffold:"Twin deficits hypothesis suggests fiscal and current account deficits may be linked: G > T → government borrows → higher interest rates → capital inflows → exchange rate appreciates → exports fall, imports rise → current account worsens. But they are conceptually distinct and do not always move together. Fiscal deficit = government's own budget: G − T. Current account = the whole economy's trade position: X − M (plus income and transfers). A government can have a fiscal surplus while running a current account deficit (China historically), or run a fiscal deficit with a current account surplus (Japan). Examiners penalise treating them as the same thing — always define each separately before exploring any link.",
        reforge:{stem:"Which pair correctly identifies two separate deficits?",options:{A:"Fiscal = G − T; Current account = trade balance + primary + secondary income (when negative).",B:"Fiscal = exports − imports; Current account = G − T.",C:"Both the same concept.",D:"Fiscal = national debt; Current account = trade deficit. (assuming other conditions are unchanged)"},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-03",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"After sterling depreciates, a student draws the J-curve starting with an immediate improvement. What's wrong?",
        options:{A:"The current account worsens first, then improves — the J-curve effect.",B:"The current account improves immediately after depreciation. (all else equal)",C:"It gets worse and never recovers.",D:"J-curve only applies to fixed exchange rates."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-BOP-03",
        scaffold:"The J-curve traces the time path of the current account after a depreciation. Immediately after depreciation: import prices rise in domestic currency (same volume × higher price = larger import bill); export contracts are pre-set in foreign currency so revenue doesn't change immediately → current account WORSENS (downward dip of the J). Medium/long run (Marshall-Lerner conditions permitting): export volumes rise (foreigners find UK goods cheaper) and import volumes fall (UK consumers substitute domestic goods) → the volume effects outweigh the price effects → current account IMPROVES (the upward stroke). Exam diagram: draw the J shape clearly — label the initial deterioration, the turning point, and the eventual improvement. State the time lags involved.",
        reforge:{stem:"Six months after a 15% peso depreciation, the current account deficit has widened. Which is most accurate?",options:{A:"The depreciation has failed.",B:"The J-curve predicts immediate improvement, so something else is wrong. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Expected — the economy is in the initial worsening phase; improvement may follow as volumes adjust.",D:"The current account always worsens permanently after depreciation."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-04",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"A student argues: 'If the pound depreciates, the UK current account will definitely improve.' What critical condition are they ignoring?",
        options:{A:"The inflation target must be met first.",B:"The government must run a budget surplus.",C:"The Marshall-Lerner condition — improvement only if PEDx + PEDm > 1.",D:"Interest rates must be cut simultaneously. (for the economy in question)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-BOP-04",
        scaffold:"Marshall-Lerner condition: a depreciation improves the current account ONLY IF the sum of price elasticity of demand for exports (PEDx) plus price elasticity of demand for imports (PEDm) exceeds 1. If PEDx + PEDm > 1: volume effects dominate → current account improves. If PEDx + PEDm < 1: demand is too inelastic (people keep buying the same volumes regardless of price changes) → import bill rises more than export revenue → current account worsens. The condition is less likely to hold in the short run (contracts, habits, search costs delay adjustment) but more likely in the long run (time to find substitutes). UK exports include financial services and pharmaceuticals — relatively price-inelastic. Always question whether ML holds before assuming depreciation helps.",
        reforge:{stem:"A country importing mostly essential energy and food sees its currency depreciate 20%. Why might the current account NOT improve?",options:{A:"Depreciation always improves the current account. (all else equal) (all else equal) (with other relevant factors held constant)",B:"Energy isn't counted in the current account.",C:"Exports are too cheap.",D:"Energy and food are price-inelastic, so demand barely falls and the import bill rises."},correct:"D"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-05",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"The current account of the balance of payments is made up of:",
        options:{A:"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services.",B:"Foreign direct investment and portfolio investment flows only. (all else equal)",C:"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only.",D:"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-BOP-05",
        scaffold:"Current account has four components — examiners regularly test whether students know all four and can classify correctly. (1) Trade in goods (visible balance): exports minus imports of physical goods. (2) Trade in services (invisible balance): exports minus imports of services — financial services, tourism, education, insurance. (3) Primary income: investment income (dividends, interest from overseas assets) and compensation of employees working abroad. (4) Secondary income (current transfers): government transfers (EU contributions when member), remittances, overseas aid. FDI and portfolio investment flows belong in the financial account — a very common misclassification error. In the exam: always check whether a flow is a transaction in current output (current account) or a change in financial assets/liabilities (financial account).",
        reforge:{stem:"A UK pension fund receives £2bn in dividends from US shares. This appears in:",options:{A:"Trade in services.",B:"Primary income on the current account — investment income from abroad.",C:"The financial account. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"Secondary income."},correct:"B"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-06",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"Which policy could reduce a persistent current account deficit?",
        options:{A:"Raise interest rates to strengthen the currency and make exports more competitive.",B:"Supply-side policies to improve productivity and reduce unit labour costs.",C:"Increase government spending to boost AD.",D:"Abolish all tariffs to encourage more imports."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-BOP-01",
        scaffold:"Three broad approaches to reducing a current account deficit, with different mechanisms and trade-offs. (1) Expenditure-reducing: deflationary fiscal/monetary policy → lower national income → fewer imports (but also lower growth, higher unemployment). (2) Expenditure-switching: depreciation, tariffs, subsidies → consumers and foreign buyers switch between domestic and imported goods (but depreciation requires ML to hold; tariffs risk retaliation). (3) Supply-side: productivity improvements reduce unit costs → UK goods more price-competitive → exports rise without needing depreciation (no inflationary side-effect, but slow to take effect). Examiner evaluation: which policy is most appropriate depends on the cause of the deficit (demand-pull vs competitiveness), the time horizon, and any constraint (e.g. exchange rate regime).",
        reforge:{stem:"A government uses contractionary fiscal policy to reduce a current account deficit. This is:",options:{A:"Expenditure-reducing — lower national income reduces import demand.",B:"Expenditure-switching. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"Supply-side policy.",D:"Protectionism."},correct:"A"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-08",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"The balance of payments must always balance overall. This means:",
        options:{A:"Foreign direct investment and portfolio investment flows only.",B:"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services.",C:"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only.",D:"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-BOP-05",
        scaffold:"The balance of payments MUST balance as an accounting identity: Current Account + Capital Account + Financial Account + Net Errors and Omissions = 0. This doesn't mean trade is balanced — it means every deficit on one account is automatically matched by a surplus elsewhere. A current account deficit (more money leaving for imports than entering from exports) must be financed: foreign investors buy UK assets (financial account surplus — capital inflows) or foreign governments accumulate sterling reserves. The UK's large current account deficit is financed by net inflows on the financial account (foreign purchases of UK property, equities, gilts). 'The BoP balances' does NOT mean the current account is zero — it means the overall sum of all accounts is zero.",
        reforge:{stem:"UK current account deficit: £80bn. What must be true?",options:{A:"Financial account deficit of £80bn too. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"Government must borrow £80bn.",C:"Corresponding surplus on financial/capital accounts of ~£80bn — capital flowing in.",D:"BoP doesn't need to balance."},correct:"C"}
      },
      {
        id:"BOP-10",spec:"2.1.4",stem:"Which would be a credit (positive entry) on the UK's current account?",
        options:{A:"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services.",B:"Foreign direct investment and portfolio investment flows only.",C:"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only.",D:"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-BOP-05",
        scaffold:"Credits = money flowing INTO the UK economy; debits = money flowing OUT. Trade in services credit: UK earns money from abroad by exporting services — overseas students paying UK university fees is an export of educational services → credit on the services account. Tourism: overseas visitors spending in the UK = credit; UK residents spending abroad = debit. Financial services: UK banks earning fees from foreign clients = credit. Common misclassification: a UK resident buying foreign shares = debit on financial account, not current account. A UK firm receiving dividends from an overseas subsidiary = credit on primary income (current account). Always ask: which direction is the money flowing, and what type of transaction is it?",
        reforge:{stem:"A French tourist spends £3,000 in London. This is recorded as:",options:{A:"Debit on trade in goods.",B:"Credit on the financial account. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Debit on secondary income.",D:"Credit on trade in services — tourism is a UK service export."},correct:"D"}
      }
    ]
  }
};

BANKS["2.2.1"] = {
  label: "Aggregate Demand",
  color: "#0891b2",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"AD-01",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"Which of the following is the correct formula for aggregate demand?",
      options:{A:"AD = C + S + G + X",B:"AD = C + I + G + (X − M)",C:"AD = C + I + T + (X − M) (all else equal)",D:"AD = GDP − inflation"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-AD-01",
      scaffold:"AD = Consumer spending (C) + Investment (I) + Government spending (G) + Net exports (X−M). Savings (S) and taxation (T) are withdrawals from the circular flow, not components of AD.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government increases G by £10bn while imports rise by £10bn. What is the net effect on AD?",options:{A:"AD rises by £20bn.",B:"AD is unchanged — the rise in G is offset by the rise in M (which reduces net exports).",C:"AD falls by £10bn.",D:"AD rises by £10bn because imports don't count. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"AD-02",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"Interest rates fall from 5% to 3%. Explain the likely effect on TWO components of AD. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Consumption (C) is likely to rise: lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing and the reward for saving, encouraging households to spend more, especially on credit-financed purchases like cars (K+An). Investment (I) is likely to rise: firms face a lower cost of borrowing, making more investment projects profitable (expected rate of return now exceeds the interest rate), so capital spending increases (K+An).",
      tag:"MC-AD-02",
      scaffold:"Interest rate transmission: lower rates → cheaper borrowing + lower saving reward → C rises. Also: lower cost of capital for firms → more projects profitable → I rises. Could also discuss: mortgage costs fall → disposable income rises → C rises; exchange rate may depreciate → X rises / M falls."
    },
    {
      id:"AD-03",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"A fall in consumer confidence would shift:",
      options:{A:"AD left — lower confidence reduces consumption, a major component of AD.",B:"AD right — worried consumers spend more.",C:"SRAS left — confidence affects costs.",D:"LRAS right — confidence drives long-run growth. (over the period considered)"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-AD-03",
      scaffold:"Consumer confidence affects willingness to spend. Lower confidence → households save more (precautionary saving) → C falls → AD shifts left. Confidence is a demand-side factor — it doesn't change firms' costs (SRAS) or the economy's productive capacity (LRAS).",
      reforge:{stem:"House prices rise significantly. Which component of AD is most directly affected and in which direction?",options:{A:"C rises — the wealth effect means homeowners feel wealthier and spend more.",B:"G rises — government collects more stamp duty. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"I falls — houses are too expensive for firms.",D:"X rises — foreigners buy UK houses."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"AD-04",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"A student draws AD shifting right and labels the result 'economic growth and lower inflation.' On a standard AD/AS diagram with an upward-sloping SRAS, why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"AD shifts always lower the price level by boosting productive efficiency.",B:"AD shifts have no effect on the price level — only potential output matters. (all else equal)",C:"An AD shift right along an upward SRAS raises both real output AND the price level.",D:"AD shifts only affect inflation, not real output or employment."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-AD-04",
      scaffold:"On a standard diagram: AD shifts right → moves along upward-sloping SRAS → higher real GDP (growth) BUT also higher price level (inflationary pressure). The only way to get growth WITHOUT inflation is an LRAS/SRAS shift right (supply-side improvement).",
      reforge:{stem:"Which combination correctly shows the effect of an AD shift RIGHT on a standard AD/SRAS diagram?",options:{A:"Output falls, price level falls.",B:"Output rises, price level unchanged.",C:"Output rises, price level rises.",D:"Output unchanged, price level rises."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"AD-05",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"The UK's exchange rate depreciates. Trace the effect on AD through net exports. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"A weaker pound makes UK exports cheaper in foreign currency, increasing demand for exports (X rises) (knowledge). It also makes imports more expensive in pounds, reducing demand for imports (M falls). Net exports (X−M) improve, shifting AD to the right (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-AD-05",
      scaffold:"Depreciation → exports cheaper abroad (more competitive) → X rises. Imports dearer at home → consumers switch to domestic goods → M falls. Both effects improve net exports (X−M), a component of AD. Assumes Marshall-Lerner holds."
    },
    {
      id:"AD-06",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"Which factor would cause a movement ALONG the AD curve rather than a SHIFT of it?",
      options:{A:"A change in consumer confidence.",B:"A change in government spending.",C:"A change in the exchange rate.",D:"A change in the price level."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-AD-06",
      scaffold:"A change in the price level causes a movement along AD (the real balance effect, interest rate effect, and trade effect explain why AD slopes down). Changes in C, I, G, or (X−M) caused by anything OTHER than the price level shift the whole AD curve.",
      reforge:{stem:"Government announces a £20bn infrastructure programme. On an AD/AS diagram, this causes:",options:{A:"A movement along AD to the right.",B:"A shift of SRAS to the right.",C:"A movement along SRAS to the left. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"A shift of AD to the right — G has increased independently of the price level."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"AD-07",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"A student lists 'higher wages' as a factor that shifts AD right. Is this always correct?",
      options:{A:"Yes — higher wages always boost AD by increasing consumer spending. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"Not necessarily — higher wages raise household income and spending but also increase firms' costs.",C:"No — wages have no direct effect on aggregate demand.",D:"Yes, but only if wage rises exceed the rate of inflation."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-AD-07",
      scaffold:"Higher wages → more disposable income → C rises → AD shifts right. BUT higher wages → higher costs for firms → may reduce profits/investment (I falls) and shift SRAS left. The net AD effect is ambiguous. This is why examiners reward nuanced analysis over blanket statements.",
      reforge:{stem:"Oil prices fall globally. What is the most likely combined effect on AD and SRAS?",options:{A:"AD shifts left, SRAS shifts left. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (all else equal)",B:"AD and SRAS both shift right: lower fuel costs support consumption and reduce firms’ costs.",C:"Only AD shifts right.",D:"Only SRAS shifts left."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"AD-08",spec:"2.2.1",stem:"The multiplier effect means that an initial injection of spending leads to:",
      options:{A:"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending.",B:"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending. (in the market described)",C:"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation.",D:"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-TECH-05",
      scaffold:"Multiplier: initial injection (e.g. £1bn G) → becomes income → recipients spend a fraction (MPC) → that spending becomes someone else's income → repeat. Final ΔY = injection × multiplier. Multiplier = 1/(1−MPC) or 1/MPW. The effect is larger than the initial injection because of successive rounds of re-spending.",
      reforge:{stem:"MPC = 0.8. The government injects £5bn. What is the final increase in national income?",options:{A:"£25bn (multiplier = 1/(1−0.8) = 5; 5 × £5bn = £25bn)",B:"£5bn",C:"£4bn",D:"£10bn (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.2.2"] = {
  label: "Aggregate Supply",
  color: "#ca8a04",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"AS-01",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"What is the key difference between SRAS and LRAS?",
      options:{A:"SRAS reflects current costs of production (and slopes upward)",B:"SRAS shows total spending; LRAS shows total output. (all else equal)",C:"SRAS is always vertical; LRAS always slopes upward.",D:"There is no difference — they are the same curve."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-AS-01",
      scaffold:"SRAS slopes upward: as the price level rises, firms supply more because revenues rise relative to (sticky) costs. LRAS is vertical at potential output (classical view): in the long run, output is determined by factors of production and technology, not the price level.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the Keynesian model, what shape is the AS curve?",options:{A:"SRAS is flat with spare capacity, then steepens and becomes vertical at full capacity.",B:"Always vertical.",C:"Always downward-sloping. (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described)",D:"A straight 45° line."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-02",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"Which of the following would shift SRAS to the LEFT?",
      options:{A:"A fall in oil prices.",B:"An increase in labour productivity.",C:"A rise in the national minimum wage increasing firms' costs.",D:"Improved technology reducing production costs. (all else equal) (all else equal)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-AS-02",
      scaffold:"SRAS shifts left when production costs rise: higher wages, higher raw material/energy costs, higher indirect taxes, exchange rate depreciation raising import costs. SRAS shifts right when costs fall. Think: anything that makes it more expensive for firms to produce at every price level.",
      reforge:{stem:"The government cuts employers' national insurance contributions. What happens to SRAS?",options:{A:"Shifts left — firms pay less tax so produce less. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"No effect — NI is paid by workers.",C:"Shifts right — lower labour costs for firms mean they can supply more at every price level.",D:"LRAS shifts right instead."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-03",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"A student labels the vertical axis on an AD/AS diagram as 'price.' Why does this lose marks?",
      options:{A:"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.",B:"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.",C:"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers.",D:"The labels are already correct — no change needed."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-TECH-04",
      scaffold:"AD/AS diagrams show the GENERAL price level (an index of all prices, like CPI) on the y-axis and real GDP/output on the x-axis. 'Price' = one good. 'Price level' = economy-wide average. Examiners consistently flag this as a diagram error.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which pair of axis labels is correct for an AD/AS diagram?",options:{A:"Price (y-axis), Quantity (x-axis).",B:"Inflation rate (y-axis), Nominal GDP (x-axis).",C:"CPI (y-axis), Output (x-axis).",D:"Price level (y-axis), Real GDP (x-axis)."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-04",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"Which of the following would shift LRAS to the RIGHT?",
      options:{A:"A cut in interest rates.",B:"Investment in education and training improving the quality of the labour force.",C:"A rise in consumer confidence.",D:"An increase in government current spending on public sector wages. (all else equal)"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-AS-04",
      scaffold:"LRAS shifts right when productive capacity increases: better education/skills (human capital), new technology, more capital stock (investment), discovery of resources, institutional improvements (property rights, deregulation). Demand-side changes (interest rates, confidence, G) shift AD, not LRAS.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which combination would shift BOTH SRAS right AND LRAS right?",options:{A:"A tax cut for consumers.",B:"A technological breakthrough that reduces production costs AND increases potential output.",C:"Higher government spending on benefits. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"A rise in the exchange rate."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-05",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"Explain the difference between a movement along SRAS and a shift of SRAS. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"A movement along SRAS is caused by a change in the price level (driven by an AD shift), as firms adjust output in response to changing revenues relative to sticky costs (knowledge). A shift of SRAS is caused by a change in production costs (e.g. oil prices, wages, indirect taxes) that alters how much firms are willing to supply at EVERY price level (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-AS-05",
      scaffold:"Movement along = price level changes (AD shifts, economy moves along existing SRAS). Shift of SRAS = costs change independently of the price level (e.g. oil shock, wage rise, tax change)."
    },
    {
      id:"AS-06",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"An economy experiences rising unit labour costs due to wages growing faster than productivity. What is the effect on SRAS?",
      options:{A:"SRAS shifts left — higher unit costs mean firms supply less at every price level.",B:"SRAS shifts right — higher wages mean more spending. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"LRAS shifts right — wages drive long-run growth.",D:"No effect on AS — wages only affect AD."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-AS-06",
      scaffold:"Unit labour costs = wage costs per unit of output. If wages rise faster than productivity, each unit of output costs more to produce → firms' costs increase → SRAS shifts left → inflationary pressure (cost-push). This was flagged in the 2019 examiner report on Estonia's competitiveness.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country's productivity grows at 4% while wages grow at 2%. What happens to unit labour costs and SRAS?",options:{A:"Unit costs fall (productivity outpaces wages), SRAS shifts right — firms can produce more cheaply.",B:"Unit costs rise, SRAS shifts left. (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described)",C:"No change — they cancel out.",D:"LRAS shifts left."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-07",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"A student draws SRAS as a vertical line. When is this appropriate?",
      options:{A:"Always — SRAS is vertical like LRAS.",B:"Never — SRAS must always slope upward. (assuming other conditions are unchanged)",C:"Only in the classical/monetarist model where all markets clear instantly",D:"Only during a recession."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-AS-01",
      scaffold:"Classical model: both SRAS and LRAS are vertical — prices and wages adjust instantly, so output is always at potential. Keynesian/standard model: SRAS slopes upward (sticky wages/prices in the short run). For Edexcel A-level, draw SRAS as upward-sloping unless specifically discussing the classical model.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the Keynesian model, why does SRAS slope upward rather than being vertical?",options:{A:"Because firms always produce more when prices rise. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"Because the government controls output.",C:"Because wages and other input costs are 'sticky' in the short run — they don't adjust immediately to price changes, so rising prices increase profit margins and incentivise higher output.",D:"Because LRAS determines the slope of SRAS."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"AS-08",spec:"2.2.2",stem:"The government introduces a carbon tax on all manufacturing. Which curves are affected and in which direction?",
      options:{A:"AD shifts right — firms pass costs to consumers who spend more. (over the period considered)",B:"LRAS shifts left — productive capacity falls immediately.",C:"AD and SRAS both shift right.",D:"SRAS shifts left — the tax raises production costs; LRAS is unaffected in the short run."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-AS-02",
      scaffold:"A carbon tax raises firms' costs → SRAS shifts left (cost-push). LRAS isn't immediately affected unless the tax causes permanent capacity reduction. AD isn't directly shifted (though the resulting price rise may reduce real incomes and feed back into lower C). The primary, first-round effect is SRAS left.",
      reforge:{stem:"The government removes VAT on energy bills. First-round effect?",options:{A:"AD shifts right.",B:"LRAS shifts right. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (all else equal)",C:"SRAS shifts left.",D:"SRAS shifts right — lower indirect tax reduces firms'/households' energy costs."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.2.3"] = {
  label: "National Income",
  color: "#6366f1",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"NI-01",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"In the circular flow model, which of the following is an injection?",
      options:{A:"Savings",B:"Taxation (all else equal)",C:"Investment",D:"Imports"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-NI-01",
      scaffold:"Injections ADD spending to the circular flow: Investment (I), Government spending (G), Exports (X). Withdrawals REMOVE spending: Savings (S), Taxation (T), Imports (M). The economy is in equilibrium when total injections = total withdrawals (I + G + X = S + T + M).",
      reforge:{stem:"For national income to be in equilibrium:",options:{A:"AD must exceed AS.",B:"Exports must equal imports. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"Total injections (I + G + X) must equal total withdrawals (S + T + M).",D:"Savings must be zero."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-02",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"The multiplier is 4. The government increases spending by £3bn. What is the final increase in national income?",
      options:{A:"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending.",B:"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation.",C:"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending.",D:"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-TECH-05",
      scaffold:"Final ΔY = multiplier × initial injection = 4 × £3bn = £12bn. The multiplier amplifies the initial injection through successive rounds of re-spending.",
      reforge:{stem:"MPT = 0.1, MPS = 0.15, MPM = 0.25. What is the multiplier?",options:{A:"10",B:"4",C:"0.5 (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"2 (multiplier = 1/MPW = 1/(0.1+0.15+0.25) = 1/0.5 = 2)"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-03",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"A student says: 'GDP and GNI are the same thing.' What is the key difference?",
      options:{A:"There is no difference.",B:"GDP measures output produced within a country's borders",C:"GDP includes inflation; GNI does not. (in the market described)",D:"GNI only measures government income."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-NI-03",
      scaffold:"GDP = output produced within borders (regardless of who owns the factors). GNI = GDP + net primary income from abroad (income UK residents earn overseas minus income foreign residents earn in the UK). For countries with large overseas investments (e.g. UK), GNI can differ significantly from GDP.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has many citizens working abroad who send remittances home, but few foreign workers within its borders. Which is likely larger?",options:{A:"GDP — domestic output is always bigger. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"GNI is higher because net income from abroad is positive.",C:"They must be equal.",D:"Neither — remittances aren't counted."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-04",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"What is the difference between nominal GDP and real GDP?",
      options:{A:"Nominal GDP is measured in current prices",B:"Nominal GDP is per person; real GDP is total.",C:"Real GDP includes imports; nominal does not.",D:"They are calculated using different formulas for AD."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-NI-04",
      scaffold:"Nominal GDP can rise simply because prices rose (inflation), even if actual output is unchanged. Real GDP strips out inflation by using a base-year price level, isolating genuine changes in output. Always use real GDP when comparing across time periods.",
      reforge:{stem:"Nominal GDP rises from £2tn to £2.1tn (5%) while inflation is 4%. What is approximate real GDP growth?",options:{A:"Approximately 1% — real growth = nominal growth minus inflation.",B:"5% (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described)",C:"9%",D:"4%"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-05",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"Which of the following would increase the size of the multiplier?",
      options:{A:"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending.",B:"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation.",C:"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity.",D:"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-TECH-05",
      scaffold:"Multiplier = 1/MPW where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM. A higher MPC means a lower MPS (since MPC + MPS = 1 in a simple model), so MPW falls and the multiplier rises. Anything that increases withdrawals (higher tax, saving, or imports) reduces the multiplier.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government raises income tax rates. What happens to the multiplier and why?",options:{A:"Multiplier rises — more tax revenue means more G. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"No change — tax doesn't affect the multiplier.",C:"The multiplier falls because higher MPT means more income is withdrawn rather than respent.",D:"Multiplier rises — higher taxes reduce inflation."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-06",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"Explain one limitation of using GDP to compare living standards between countries. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"GDP does not account for differences in the cost of living between countries (knowledge). A country with a high GDP per capita may have very high prices, meaning residents' purchasing power is lower than the headline figure suggests — this is why economists use PPP (Purchasing Power Parity) adjusted figures for international comparisons (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-NI-06",
      scaffold:"Limitations: doesn't account for PPP/cost of living differences, ignores income distribution, excludes the informal/shadow economy, ignores environmental degradation, doesn't capture non-market activity (household work), and says nothing about quality of life or leisure."
    },
    {
      id:"NI-07",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"In the circular flow, a rise in the savings ratio (MPS) would, other things equal:",
      options:{A:"Increase national income — savings fund investment.",B:"Have no effect — savings always equal investment.",C:"Increase exports.",D:"Decrease national income in the short run"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-NI-01",
      scaffold:"Higher MPS → more income is withdrawn as savings → less re-spent each round → smaller multiplier → national income falls (paradox of thrift in the short run). Note: in the long run, higher savings COULD fund investment, but the immediate circular-flow effect is contractionary.",
      reforge:{stem:"The 'paradox of thrift' suggests that:",options:{A:"Saving is always good for the economy.",B:"Thrift only matters in developing countries.",C:"Higher savings always lead to higher investment immediately. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (for the economy in question)",D:"If everyone tries to save more simultaneously, total spending falls, reducing national income — potentially leaving everyone worse off and unable to save as much as intended."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"NI-08",spec:"2.2.3",stem:"Which of the three methods of measuring GDP counts the value added at each stage of production?",
      options:{A:"The income method.",B:"The output (production) method.",C:"The expenditure method. (all else equal)",D:"The employment method."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-NI-08",
      scaffold:"Three methods, all yielding the same GDP figure: (1) Output/production — sum of value added by each industry (avoids double counting). (2) Income — sum of all factor incomes (wages, profits, rent, interest). (3) Expenditure — sum of all final spending (C + I + G + X − M). Value added = output minus intermediate inputs.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does the output method use 'value added' rather than total sales revenue at each stage?",options:{A:"Sales revenue is confidential.",B:"Total revenue would double-count intermediate goods at different production stages.",C:"Value added is always larger than revenue.",D:"The government requires it for tax purposes. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.3.1"] = {
  label: "Fiscal Policy",
  color: "#b45309",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"FIS-01",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"Which of the following best defines fiscal policy?",
      options:{A:"The Bank of England adjusting the base interest rate to influence borrowing.",B:"Supply-side policies designed to improve the productive capacity of the economy.",C:"The central bank creating money to purchase government bonds (quantitative easing).",D:"Government spending and taxation used to influence aggregate demand."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-FIS-01",
      scaffold:"Fiscal policy = government spending (G) and taxation (T) decisions, set by the Chancellor in the Budget. It directly affects AD (G is a component; T affects C and I). Don't confuse with monetary policy (interest rates, set by the BoE) or supply-side policies (structural reforms).",
      reforge:{stem:"The Chancellor announces a £15bn increase in NHS spending funded by higher income tax. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Monetary policy.",B:"Supply-side policy.",C:"Quantitative easing. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"Fiscal policy — it involves changes to both government spending and taxation."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-02",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"A student cannot explain the difference between automatic stabilisers and discretionary fiscal policy. Which answer correctly distinguishes them?",
      options:{A:"They are the same thing — both require government decisions.",B:"Automatic stabilisers respond without a government decision; discretionary policy requires one.",C:"Automatic stabilisers only work in booms; discretionary only in recessions. (in the market described)",D:"Discretionary policy is automatic; stabilisers require decisions."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-FIS-02",
      scaffold:"Automatic stabilisers kick in without any new policy decision. In a recession: tax receipts fall automatically (less income to tax), welfare spending rises automatically (more claimants) → this cushions the fall in AD. Discretionary = a deliberate new action: 'the Chancellor announced...' The 2022 examiner report flagged this as a question many candidates found challenging.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a recession, unemployment benefit payments rise without any new legislation. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Discretionary fiscal policy. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (all else equal)",B:"An automatic stabiliser: welfare spending rises automatically as unemployment rises.",C:"Monetary policy.",D:"Supply-side policy."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-03",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"A student writes about methods of 'raising tax revenue' but focuses entirely on what the government could SPEND the revenue on. What has gone wrong?",
      options:{A:"Off-task — the question asked about raising revenue, not spending it.",B:"Nothing — spending priorities are useful context for a tax revenue question.",C:"The student needed to name more taxes, without discussing spending at all.",D:"This is a valid interpretation, since raising and spending revenue are the same fiscal policy tool."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-TECH-01",
      scaffold:"The 2022 examiner report explicitly flagged this: many candidates discussed what government could spend revenue on, rather than evaluating the methods of raising it. Answer the question set. Methods = which taxes, at what rates, on whom, with what economic effects (incentives, equity, Laffer curve).",
      reforge:{stem:"'Evaluate methods of increasing tax revenue.' Which response is on-task?",options:{A:"Raising top income-tax rates may increase revenue but could weaken work incentives; broader VAT could be regressive.",B:"The government could spend more on hospitals and schools.",C:"Tax revenue is not important for the economy.",D:"The Bank of England should raise interest rates to increase revenue. (over the period considered) (with other relevant factors held constant)"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-04",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"What is the difference between the fiscal (budget) deficit and the national debt?",
      options:{A:"The fiscal deficit (G − T) and current account deficit (M − X) measure different things.",B:"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept.",C:"The fiscal deficit cannot be expressed as a percentage of GDP.",D:"The current account only measures trade in goods, not services."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-BOP-02",
      scaffold:"Deficit = a flow (this year's gap between spending and revenue). Debt = a stock (total accumulated over decades). Running a deficit of £100bn this year adds £100bn to the national debt. You can run a deficit while the debt-to-GDP ratio falls if GDP grows faster than borrowing. The 2019 examiner report flagged students confusing fiscal deficit with current account deficit.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government runs a surplus of £10bn. What happens to the national debt?",options:{A:"It increases by £10bn.",B:"It is unaffected — surpluses and debt are unrelated. (in the market described)",C:"It decreases by £10bn — the surplus is used to pay down accumulated debt.",D:"It doubles."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-05",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"Evaluate whether expansionary fiscal policy is the most effective way to reduce unemployment. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        KAA_strand_1:"Expansionary fiscal policy (higher G or lower T) → AD shifts right → firms produce more → derived demand for labour rises → unemployment falls. Multiplier amplifies the effect. Apply: UK furlough scheme during COVID or 2008 fiscal stimulus. Draw AD/AS diagram showing rightward AD shift.",
        KAA_strand_2:"However, fiscal policy may cause demand-pull inflation if the economy is near full capacity. Also: time lags (recognition, implementation, impact), crowding out (higher G funded by borrowing → higher interest rates → lower private I), and it only addresses cyclical unemployment — structural unemployment requires supply-side solutions.",
        EV_strand:"Depends on: (1) type of unemployment — fiscal policy tackles cyclical but not structural (need retraining/mobility); (2) position in the cycle — effective when there's spare capacity, inflationary near full employment; (3) alternatives — monetary policy (faster, no political lag), supply-side (addresses root causes but slow). Judgement: fiscal policy is effective for demand-deficient unemployment but must be complemented by supply-side reform for structural unemployment; no single policy is 'most effective' in all contexts."
      },
      tag:["MC-FIS-01","MC-EMP-02","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"Top-level answers need: AD/AS diagram, multiplier reference, real-world application, AND evaluation that distinguishes cyclical from structural unemployment and weighs fiscal against monetary and supply-side alternatives."
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-06",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"The Laffer curve suggests that:",
      options:{A:"Higher tax rates always generate more total revenue.",B:"Tax revenue is independent of the rate at which income is taxed.",C:"The optimal tax rate is always zero to maximise economic activity. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",D:"Beyond a certain tax rate, further increases reduce total revenue as incentives to work diminish."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-FIS-06",
      scaffold:"The Laffer curve is an inverted U: at 0% tax, revenue is zero. At 100%, revenue is also zero (no one works). Somewhere in between is the revenue-maximising rate. Beyond that point, higher rates are counterproductive. The debate is about WHERE the peak is — not whether the relationship exists.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has a top income tax rate of 75% and is experiencing significant tax avoidance and brain drain. Using the Laffer curve, what might happen if it reduces the rate to 50%?",options:{A:"Revenue must fall because the rate is lower. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described) (all else equal)",B:"Revenue is unchanged — rates don't matter.",C:"The Laffer curve only applies to VAT.",D:"Revenue could actually rise if the lower rate incentivises more work and reduces avoidance — the country may have been beyond the peak of the Laffer curve."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-07",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"Which of the following is a problem with using fiscal policy to manage aggregate demand?",
      options:{A:"It works too quickly — the economy overheats before the policy can be reversed.",B:"Time lags: recognition lag (identifying the problem)",C:"Fiscal policy has no effect on AD.",D:"Only the Bank of England can change government spending."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-FIS-07",
      scaffold:"Three lags: (1) Recognition — takes time to confirm a recession is happening. (2) Implementation — budgets are annual; emergency measures need political approval. (3) Impact — spending takes time to feed through (contracts, hiring). By the time the stimulus hits, the economy may have already recovered → pro-cyclical rather than counter-cyclical.",
      reforge:{stem:"Compared to fiscal policy, monetary policy has one key advantage in terms of timing. What is it?",options:{A:"Monetary policy has no time lags at all.",B:"The MPC meets regularly and can change interest rates quickly without needing parliamentary approval, reducing the implementation lag.",C:"Monetary policy works instantly on the economy. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"Fiscal policy is always faster."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FIS-08",spec:"2.3.1",stem:"Crowding out occurs when:",
      options:{A:"Government spending replaces private spending",B:"The government spends less than it taxes.",C:"Exports crowd out imports.",D:"The private sector spends more than the government."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-FIS-08",
      scaffold:"Government borrows to fund higher G → increased demand for loanable funds → interest rates rise → private firms face higher borrowing costs → private I falls. The government spending 'crowds out' private investment. This is a key evaluation point against expansionary fiscal policy. Note: crowding out is weaker when there's spare capacity (interest rates don't rise as much).",
      reforge:{stem:"When is crowding out LEAST likely to occur?",options:{A:"During a deep recession with very low interest rates and spare capacity — government borrowing doesn't push rates up significantly because demand for private borrowing is already weak.",B:"When the economy is at full capacity.",C:"When the government runs a surplus.",D:"Crowding out always occurs regardless of conditions. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.3.2"] = {
  label: "Monetary Policy",
  color: "#0d9488",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MON-01",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"Monetary policy in the UK is set by:",
      options:{A:"The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) of the Bank of England",B:"The Chancellor of the Exchequer. (for the economy in question)",C:"The Prime Minister.",D:"The House of Commons."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-MON-01",
      scaffold:"The BoE has been operationally independent since 1997. The government sets the inflation target (2% CPI); the MPC decides HOW to hit it (interest rates, QE). This independence is meant to remove political short-termism from monetary decisions.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was the Bank of England given operational independence in 1997?",options:{A:"To stop politicians using rate cuts for election gains and strengthen inflation-target credibility.",B:"To allow the government to set interest rates more frequently. (with other relevant factors held constant)",C:"Because the EU required it.",D:"To allow the BoE to set tax rates."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MON-02",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"The MPC cuts the base rate from 5% to 4%. Trace the transmission mechanism to AD. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Lower base rate → commercial banks lower lending rates → cost of borrowing falls for households and firms (K). Households: cheaper mortgages increase disposable income, lower saving reward → consumption (C) rises (An). Firms: more investment projects become profitable (expected return > cost of borrowing) → investment (I) rises (An). Also: lower rates may weaken the exchange rate (less attractive for hot money) → exports cheaper → net exports improve (X−M) → AD shifts right (An).",
      tag:"MC-MON-02",
      scaffold:"Transmission mechanism: base rate → commercial rates → borrowing/saving decisions → C and I → AD. Also: rate cut → exchange rate may depreciate → net exports improve → AD. Also: asset prices may rise (lower discount rate) → wealth effect → C."
    },
    {
      id:"MON-03",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"A student writes: 'Raising interest rates will solve inflation without any trade-offs.' Why is this an oversimplification?",
      options:{A:"It's correct — higher rates always solve inflation painlessly. (all else equal)",B:"Interest rates have no effect on inflation.",C:"Higher rates reduce AD (lower C and I), which reduces demand-pull inflation",D:"Only fiscal policy can control inflation."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-MON-03",
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report flagged this: students discussed 'conflicts that can occur as the central bank attempts to keep inflation on target.' Higher rates → lower inflation BUT also: lower growth, higher unemployment (Phillips curve trade-off), higher mortgage payments reducing living standards, exchange rate appreciation hurting exporters.",
      reforge:{stem:"The MPC raises rates to combat 10% inflation. Which conflict is most likely?",options:{A:"Inflation rises further.",B:"The exchange rate depreciates.",C:"Unemployment rises as firms cut costs in response to lower demand — the classic inflation-unemployment trade-off.",D:"Government spending increases automatically. (with other relevant factors held constant) (over the period considered)"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"MON-04",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"Quantitative easing (QE) involves:",
      options:{A:"The government printing physical banknotes to fund public spending.",B:"The central bank raising its base rate to tighten monetary conditions. (in the market described) (all else equal)",C:"The government reducing public spending to lower the money supply.",D:"The central bank creating new money electronically to buy assets from financial institutions."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-MON-04",
      scaffold:"QE: BoE creates money → buys gilts from banks/pension funds → sellers deposit cash in banks → banks have more reserves → lend more → money supply rises → long-term rates fall → asset prices rise (wealth effect) → C and I stimulated → AD shifts right. Used when base rate is near zero (zero lower bound) and conventional rate cuts are exhausted.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was QE introduced after 2009 rather than further base rate cuts?",options:{A:"The government banned rate cuts.",B:"QE is cheaper than rate cuts.",C:"Rate cuts only work during booms. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"The base rate was near zero, so normal rate cuts were exhausted and QE was needed."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"MON-05",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"A limitation of monetary policy is that it may be ineffective when:",
      options:{A:"Inflation is high.",B:"Interest rates are near zero (liquidity trap)",C:"The exchange rate is floating. (all else equal) (all else equal)",D:"The economy is growing."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-MON-05",
      scaffold:"Liquidity trap: rates at/near zero → can't cut further. QE injects liquidity but banks may hoard it (risk aversion) rather than lend. Firms may not invest even at 0% if expected returns are negative (low confidence). Japan's 'lost decades' illustrate this. Monetary policy is 'pushing on a string.'",
      reforge:{stem:"During the 2008 crisis, despite very low interest rates, bank lending remained subdued. Which concept explains this?",options:{A:"The multiplier was too large. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (with other relevant factors held constant)",B:"A liquidity trap: risk-averse banks and cautious borrowers mean low rates fail to boost spending.",C:"Interest rates were too high.",D:"Fiscal policy was too tight."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MON-06",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"Higher interest rates tend to cause the exchange rate to:",
      options:{A:"Appreciate — higher rates attract hot money inflows, increasing demand for the currency.",B:"Depreciate — investors move money out.",C:"Stay the same — interest rates don't affect exchange rates. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",D:"Depreciate in the short run but appreciate in the long run."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-MON-06",
      scaffold:"Higher UK rates → higher return on UK assets → hot money flows in (foreign investors buy sterling to invest) → demand for £ rises → £ appreciates. This strengthens the currency, making imports cheaper (reducing inflation) but exports more expensive (worsening net exports).",
      reforge:{stem:"The ECB raises eurozone interest rates while UK rates stay unchanged. What is the likely effect on the £/€ exchange rate?",options:{A:"Sterling depreciates because higher eurozone rates attract hot-money flows away from sterling.",B:"Sterling appreciates against the euro. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"No effect on the exchange rate.",D:"Both currencies appreciate."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MON-07",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"A student says: 'Monetary policy can solve cost-push inflation.' Evaluate this claim. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Monetary policy (raising rates) reduces AD, which can lower the price level — but cost-push inflation is caused by SRAS shifting left (higher costs), not excess demand (K). Raising rates would reduce output and increase unemployment without addressing the cost shock (An). This creates a dilemma: the BoE must choose between tolerating above-target inflation or causing a recession — this is why central banks often 'look through' temporary supply shocks rather than responding aggressively (Ev).",
      tag:["MC-MON-03","MC-INF-04"],
      scaffold:"Cost-push = supply-side problem. Monetary policy is a demand-side tool. Using demand restriction to fight a supply shock risks stagflation (rising prices AND falling output). The BoE's 2022 dilemma with energy-driven inflation illustrates this tension."
    },
    {
      id:"MON-08",spec:"2.3.2",stem:"Forward guidance is a monetary policy tool where the central bank:",
      options:{A:"Sets interest rates 10 years in advance. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"Forces banks to lend at specific rates.",C:"Communicates its future policy intentions to influence expectations",D:"Pegs the exchange rate."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-MON-08",
      scaffold:"Forward guidance: the BoE signals where it expects rates to go (e.g. 'rates will remain low until unemployment falls below 7%'). This manages expectations — if firms believe rates will stay low, they're more likely to invest now. It works through the expectations channel rather than direct rate changes.",
      reforge:{stem:"The BoE announces: 'We do not expect to raise rates for at least 12 months.' What is this intended to achieve?",options:{A:"Force banks to lower rates immediately.",B:"Increase inflation expectations to dangerous levels. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"Reduce uncertainty and encourage spending/investment now — businesses and households can plan knowing borrowing costs will remain stable.",D:"Replace QE entirely."},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.3.3"] = {
  label: "Supply-Side Policies",
  color: "#e11d48",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"SSP-01",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Supply-side policies aim to:",
      options:{A:"Shift AD to the right by increasing government spending. (in the market described)",B:"Reduce the money supply to control inflation.",C:"Increase the productive capacity of the economy by shifting LRAS to the right.",D:"Increase imports to improve consumer choice."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-SSP-01",
      scaffold:"Supply-side policies target the economy's potential output — shifting LRAS right. They improve the quantity or quality of factors of production (labour, capital, enterprise, land/resources) or remove barriers to efficient resource allocation. They are about the capacity to produce, not the level of demand.",
      reforge:{stem:"On an AD/AS diagram, successful supply-side policies would show:",options:{A:"AD shifting right along a fixed LRAS. (in the market described) (with other relevant factors held constant)",B:"SRAS shifting left as costs rise.",C:"LRAS shifts right, allowing higher output at a lower or stable price level.",D:"AD shifting left to reduce inflation."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-02",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Which of the following is a market-based (interventionist vs free-market) supply-side policy?",
      options:{A:"Government-funded retraining programmes targeting unemployed workers. (all else equal) (all else equal)",B:"Increased public spending on state education and vocational training.",C:"Building new public infrastructure to improve productive capacity.",D:"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-SSP-02",
      scaffold:"Free-market/market-based: deregulation, privatisation, trade union reform, reducing income tax/corporation tax, flexible labour markets. Interventionist: government spending on education/training, infrastructure investment, R&D subsidies, regional policy. Both shift LRAS right but through different mechanisms.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government invests £5bn in apprenticeship programmes for young people. This is:",options:{A:"A free-market supply-side policy.",B:"Fiscal policy only — it has no supply-side effect. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"Monetary policy.",D:"An interventionist supply-side policy — direct government spending to improve human capital."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-03",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"A student lists 'cutting interest rates' as a supply-side policy. Why is this incorrect?",
      options:{A:"lower rates boost the supply side.",B:"Cutting interest rates is monetary policy aimed at boosting AD (demand-side)",C:"Interest rates have no economic effect.",D:"Only the government can implement supply-side policies. (in the market described)"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-SSP-03",
      scaffold:"Supply-side policies specifically target productive capacity (LRAS). Interest rate cuts target AD through the monetary transmission mechanism. Yes, lower rates may encourage I (which builds capacity over time), but the primary classification is monetary/demand-side. Examiners want clean categorisation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these is correctly classified as a supply-side policy?",options:{A:"Quantitative easing.",B:"Reducing corporation tax to incentivise business investment and attract FDI — targeting the productive capacity of the economy.",C:"Increasing government spending on benefits. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant)",D:"Cutting VAT to boost consumer spending."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-04",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Evaluate the effectiveness of supply-side policies in reducing unemployment. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        KAA_strand_1:"Supply-side policies (e.g. retraining, education reform, deregulation) can reduce structural unemployment by addressing skills mismatches and occupational/geographical immobility. They can also reduce the natural rate of unemployment by making labour markets more flexible. Example: UK apprenticeship levy aimed at closing skills gaps.",
        KAA_strand_2:"However, supply-side policies take a long time to work (education reform may take 5-10 years), are expensive (infrastructure, training programmes), and may increase inequality in the short term (e.g. deregulation/trade union reform can suppress wages). They also do not address cyclical unemployment caused by deficient demand.",
        EV_strand:"Depends on: (1) type of unemployment — supply-side is effective for structural/natural rate but irrelevant for demand-deficient unemployment (need fiscal/monetary); (2) time horizon — long-run benefits vs short-run costs; (3) type of supply-side policy — interventionist (education) may be more equitable but slower; market-based (deregulation) may be faster but increase inequality. Judgement: supply-side policies are necessary for sustainably low unemployment but insufficient alone — demand management is needed in recessions, and the choice between interventionist and market-based approaches involves trade-offs between efficiency and equity."
      },
      tag:["MC-SSP-01","MC-EMP-02","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"Top-level: distinguish structural from cyclical, give real examples, weigh interventionist vs market-based, and reach a judgement about when supply-side works and when it doesn't."
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-05",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Trade union reform (reducing union power) is argued to improve labour market flexibility. Which is a valid counter-argument?",
      options:{A:"Weaker unions may lead to lower wages and worse working conditions",B:"There are no counter-arguments — weaker unions are always better. (all else equal)",C:"Trade unions have no effect on the labour market.",D:"Union reform only affects the public sector."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-SSP-05",
      scaffold:"Weaker unions → wages determined more by market forces → potentially lower wages for low-skilled workers → higher inequality. Also: reduced bargaining power may worsen working conditions, reduce job security. Counter: lower wages may reduce costs and increase employment. This is a classic efficiency vs equity trade-off.",
      reforge:{stem:"Privatisation of a state-owned monopoly is expected to increase efficiency. Which is a valid concern?",options:{A:"The firm may become a private monopoly, raising prices unless regulation is effective.",B:"Private firms are always less efficient.",C:"Privatisation has no effect on efficiency. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"Only public firms can make profits."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-06",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"A key advantage of supply-side policies over demand-side policies is:",
      options:{A:"They work immediately.",B:"They are free to implement.",C:"They can increase output without causing inflation",D:"They always reduce unemployment to zero. (all else equal)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-SSP-01",
      scaffold:"Demand-side (fiscal/monetary) shifts AD right → higher output BUT also higher price level (inflationary). Supply-side shifts LRAS right → higher output at same or lower price level = non-inflationary growth. This is the key comparative advantage. The trade-off: supply-side takes much longer to work.",
      reforge:{stem:"An economy is at full capacity with high inflation. Which policy approach can increase output WITHOUT worsening inflation?",options:{A:"Expansionary fiscal policy.",B:"Cutting interest rates.",C:"Supply-side policies expand LRAS, allowing more output without demand-pull inflation.",D:"Increasing government spending. (in the market described) (with other relevant factors held constant)"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-07",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Which of the following is NOT a supply-side policy?",
      options:{A:"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply.",B:"Government-funded retraining programmes targeting unemployed workers.",C:"Building new public infrastructure to improve productive capacity.",D:"Increased public spending on state education and vocational training."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-SSP-02",
      scaffold:"Increasing JSA is a fiscal transfer (automatic stabiliser) that supports incomes — it doesn't improve productive capacity. In fact, higher benefits could arguably REDUCE labour supply if they weaken the incentive to find work. Infrastructure, deregulation, and tax incentives all aim to expand capacity or improve efficiency.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government reduces the top rate of income tax from 50% to 40%. Supply-side economists argue this will:",options:{A:"Reduce tax revenue with no other effects.",B:"Shift AD left because people pay less tax.",C:"Only benefit the wealthy with no macroeconomic impact. (all else equal) (all else equal)",D:"Stronger incentives can raise labour supply and investment, shifting LRAS right."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"SSP-08",spec:"2.3.3",stem:"Infrastructure investment is sometimes classified as BOTH a demand-side AND supply-side policy. Why?",
      options:{A:"Infrastructure spending can only have one economic effect — fiscal or supply-side, not both. (all else equal)",B:"Infrastructure spending is both fiscal (raises G → AD) and supply-side (raises LRAS) simultaneously.",C:"Infrastructure spending only affects imports, not domestic output or productivity.",D:"Infrastructure spending is always offset by the taxation needed to fund it."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-SSP-08",
      scaffold:"Infrastructure is dual-impact: Short run = demand injection (construction workers hired, materials bought → G increases → AD right). Long run = capacity expansion (better transport reduces costs, improves connectivity, boosts productivity → LRAS right). The 2022 examiner report used the Bow Street station example to illustrate this dual effect.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government builds a new high-speed rail line. In year 1 of construction, the primary macroeconomic effect is:",options:{A:"LRAS shifts right immediately.",B:"AD shifts right because construction spending is government expenditure and creates jobs and income.",C:"SRAS shifts left due to higher costs.",D:"No macroeconomic effect until the line opens. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.4.1"] = {
  label: "Inequality",
  color: "#9333ea",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"INQ-01",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"A student draws a Lorenz curve but labels the axes 'income' (y) and 'people' (x). Why does this lose marks?",
      options:{A:"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.",B:"The labels are already correct — no change needed.",C:"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.",D:"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-TECH-04",
      scaffold:"The 2022 examiner report explicitly flagged Lorenz curve axis labelling as a common error. Both axes must show cumulative percentages. The 45° line of equality shows what perfect equality looks like (bottom 20% earn 20% of income, etc). The further the curve bows from this line, the greater the inequality.",
      reforge:{stem:"On a Lorenz curve, the 45° diagonal line represents:",options:{A:"Average income.",B:"Perfect equality — each percentage of the population earns exactly that percentage of total income.",C:"Perfect inequality — one person earns everything. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",D:"The poverty line."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-02",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"The UK's Gini coefficient is 0.34. What does this mean?",
      options:{A:"The Gini coefficient ranges from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality).",B:"34% of the UK population currently live below the poverty line. (all else equal) (all else equal)",C:"The UK ranks 34th in global league tables of income inequality.",D:"34% of UK national income is captured by the top 1% of earners."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-INQ-02",
      scaffold:"Gini coefficient = Area between Lorenz curve and 45° line ÷ total area under the 45° line. 0 = perfect equality (Lorenz curve IS the 45° line). 1 = perfect inequality (one person has everything). UK at 0.34 means moderate inequality — for comparison, Nordics are ~0.25, South Africa ~0.63.",
      reforge:{stem:"Country A has a Gini of 0.28; Country B has 0.52. Which has greater income inequality?",options:{A:"Country B: a higher Gini coefficient indicates greater inequality.",B:"Country A — higher Gini means more equality.",C:"They are equally unequal.",D:"Gini coefficients can't be compared across countries. (all else equal) (all else equal)"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-03",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"A student explains causes of income inequality but offers no evaluation. In an 8-mark question, how many marks are they likely to lose?",
      options:{A:"None — evaluation is optional on 8-mark questions.",B:"All 8 marks.",C:"Up to 2 marks",D:"1 mark at most."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-TECH-02",
      scaffold:"The 2022 examiner report stressed this repeatedly: on 8-mark questions, marks are split equally (2K + 2Ap + 2An + 2Ev). Many candidates scored 6/8 with strong KAA but zero evaluation. Evaluation means questioning significance, considering counter-arguments, or weighing causes against each other.",
      reforge:{stem:"On a 12-mark levels question (8 KAA + 4 EV), a student writes excellent analysis but no evaluation. What is their likely maximum mark?",options:{A:"12 — analysis is enough.",B:"4 — no evaluation means no marks at all. (assuming other conditions are unchanged)",C:"8 — they can access full KAA marks but score zero on the 4 evaluation marks.",D:"10 — they lose only 2 marks."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-04",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"Explain one cause of income inequality within the UK. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Regional differences in educational attainment mean that workers in some areas have lower skills and qualifications (knowledge). This limits their access to high-paying professional jobs, trapping them in lower-wage employment and widening the income gap between regions like London and the North East (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-INQ-04",
      scaffold:"Causes include: education/skills disparities, regional economic imbalances, discrimination, globalisation (skilled workers gain, unskilled lose), technological change (automation replacing routine jobs), tax and benefit policy, inheritance. K for identifying; An for explaining the mechanism."
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-05",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"What is the difference between income and wealth?",
      options:{A:"They are the same thing.",B:"Income is only wages; wealth is only property.",C:"Wealth is always larger than income.",D:"Income is a flow"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-INQ-05",
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report flagged students confusing these. Income = flow (£ per year — salary, benefits, investment returns). Wealth = stock (£ at a point — house value, pension pot, savings, shares minus debts). You can have high income but low wealth (spend everything), or low income but high wealth (retiree with a paid-off house).",
      reforge:{stem:"A retired homeowner has no salary but owns a £500,000 house and £200,000 in savings. They have:",options:{A:"High income and high wealth.",B:"No wealth because they have no income.",C:"High income because their house is valuable. (in the market described) (all else equal)",D:"Low income (no earnings flow) but high wealth (£700,000 in assets)."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-06",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"A question asks about rising WEALTH inequality, but a student discusses INCOME inequality throughout. What mark impact does this have?",
      options:{A:"None — they are the same concept.",B:"Significant",C:"Minimal — examiners don't distinguish them.",D:"The student gains extra marks for breadth."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-INQ-05",
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report flagged this directly: candidates discussed income inequality when asked about wealth inequality. Wealth inequality causes: inheritance, house price appreciation (owners vs renters), pension differences, share ownership concentrated among the wealthy. Answer the question SET.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which factor specifically drives WEALTH inequality rather than income inequality?",options:{A:"Differences in hourly wages.",B:"Rising house prices can widen the wealth gap between homeowners and non-owners.",C:"Different working hours.",D:"Regional differences in job availability. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-07",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"Progressive taxation means:",
      options:{A:"The marginal rate of tax increases as income rises",B:"Everyone pays the same percentage of their income in tax.",C:"Tax rates decrease as income rises.",D:"Only the wealthy pay tax."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-INQ-07",
      scaffold:"Progressive: marginal rate rises with income (UK: 20% basic → 40% higher → 45% additional). Proportional: same rate for all. Regressive: takes a larger proportion from lower incomes (e.g. VAT — same rate but represents a larger share of a poorer person's income). Progressive taxation is the main fiscal tool for reducing income inequality.",
      reforge:{stem:"VAT is often described as regressive because:",options:{A:"The same tax rate takes a larger share of income from lower earners, who spend more of their income.",B:"It charges a higher rate to the wealthy. (in the market described) (with other relevant factors held constant)",C:"Only poor people pay VAT.",D:"It is progressive in disguise."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"INQ-08",spec:"2.4.1",stem:"Evaluate whether government redistribution (taxes and benefits) is the most effective way to reduce inequality. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        KAA_strand_1:"Progressive taxation takes more from higher earners; transfer payments (benefits, tax credits, state pension) boost incomes at the bottom. Together they compress the income distribution. Apply: UK Gini before taxes/benefits is ~0.52, after redistribution ~0.34 — redistribution reduces inequality significantly.",
        KAA_strand_2:"However, high marginal tax rates may create disincentive effects (Laffer curve), encourage avoidance/evasion, and reduce enterprise. Means-tested benefits can create poverty traps (withdrawal of benefits as income rises → effective marginal tax rates over 60%).",
        EV_strand:"Depends on: (1) pre-distribution vs redistribution — investing in education and skills addresses root causes (human capital) but takes decades; (2) minimum/living wage — raises floor incomes but may cause unemployment if set too high; (3) type of inequality — redistribution addresses income but not wealth (need wealth taxes, inheritance reform). Judgement: redistribution is effective short-term but insufficient alone — sustainable equality requires investment in education and opportunity alongside fiscal transfers."
      },
      tag:["MC-INQ-04","MC-INQ-07","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"Top-level: use before/after Gini data, distinguish income from wealth inequality, weigh redistribution against pre-distribution (education, minimum wage), and consider unintended consequences (disincentives, poverty traps)."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.4.2"] = {
  label: "Poverty",
  color: "#64748b",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"POV-01",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"What is the difference between absolute and relative poverty?",
      options:{A:"They are the same concept measured differently.",B:"Absolute = can't afford basics; relative = below 60% of median income.",C:"Absolute poverty only exists in developing countries. (all else equal) (all else equal)",D:"Relative poverty is always more severe than absolute."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-POV-01",
      scaffold:"Absolute: fixed standard — can you survive? (World Bank: $2.15/day). Relative: moving standard — how far below the typical income are you? (UK: 60% of median). A country can eliminate absolute poverty while relative poverty rises (if median incomes grow but bottom incomes grow slower). They measure different things.",
      reforge:{stem:"GDP per capita doubles over 20 years but the bottom 10% see only a 20% income rise while the median doubles. What happens to each type of poverty?",options:{A:"Both fall equally.",B:"Absolute poverty likely falls, but relative poverty may rise as the bottom 10% lag behind the median.",C:"Both rise because growth causes poverty.",D:"Relative poverty falls but absolute rises. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POV-02",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"A student says: 'Economic growth will automatically eliminate poverty.' Evaluate this. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Growth increases national income and can reduce absolute poverty by raising incomes across the distribution (K). However, if growth is unevenly distributed — e.g. captured by the wealthy or concentrated in certain regions — relative poverty may persist or worsen despite rising GDP (An). Furthermore, 'trickle-down' effects are contested: supply-side growth may take decades to benefit the poorest, and without targeted redistribution (benefits, minimum wage) growth alone does not guarantee poverty reduction (Ev).",
      tag:["MC-POV-01","MC-GRO-01"],
      scaffold:"Growth is necessary but not sufficient. It helps with absolute poverty (more output) but not automatically with relative poverty (distribution matters). Evaluation: type of growth (inclusive vs extractive), redistribution policy, and whether benefits reach the poorest."
    },
    {
      id:"POV-03",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"The poverty trap (or unemployment trap) occurs when:",
      options:{A:"The withdrawal of means-tested benefits as income rises creates very high effective margin",B:"Low-income individuals lack the motivation to seek employment. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",C:"Everyone who enters poverty remains there permanently.",D:"Only universal benefit schemes create poverty traps."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-POV-03",
      scaffold:"Example: a person on benefits earns £100 extra → loses £65 in withdrawn benefits + pays £20 tax/NI → keeps only £15. Effective marginal rate = 85%. Rational response: why bother? This traps people in benefit dependency. Solutions include: Universal Credit taper (gradual withdrawal), raising personal allowance, in-work benefits.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government replaces means-tested benefits with a Universal Basic Income (flat payment to all, not withdrawn as income rises). How does this affect the poverty trap?",options:{A:"It removes benefit withdrawal as income rises, improving work incentives.",B:"It worsens the trap because everyone gets money. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"It has no effect on the poverty trap.",D:"It creates a new trap because no one would work."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"POV-04",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"Which government policy would most directly reduce absolute poverty?",
      options:{A:"Cutting the top rate of income tax to stimulate growth.",B:"Privatising state-owned industries to increase efficiency. (all else equal)",C:"Raising the National Living Wage and strengthening housing benefit",D:"Reducing corporation tax to attract investment."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-POV-04",
      scaffold:"Absolute poverty = can't afford basics. Direct interventions: raise floor incomes (minimum/living wage), provide essentials directly (social housing, free school meals, NHS), strengthen safety net (UC, housing benefit). Supply-side policies may help long-run but don't address immediate deprivation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A charity argues: 'The best anti-poverty policy is a job.' Which economic concept supports this, and what is a limitation?",options:{A:"No concept supports it — jobs don't reduce poverty.",B:"All jobs automatically lift people above the poverty line. (assuming other conditions are unchanged) (all else equal)",C:"Jobs can reduce benefit dependency, but low-paid or insecure work can still mean in-work poverty.",D:"Only public sector jobs reduce poverty."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"POV-05",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"The UK measures relative poverty as income below 60% of median household income. A limitation of this measure is:",
      options:{A:"It is too generous — the threshold is too high.",B:"It only measures wealth, not income.",C:"60% is an arbitrary figure with no policy relevance. (all else equal)",D:"It's relative to the median, which can itself fall."},correct:"D",tag:"MC-POV-01",
      scaffold:"Relative poverty is tied to the median, so it moves with the distribution. In a recession: median income drops → 60% threshold drops → some people 'escape' relative poverty despite being no better off. This is why we need both absolute and relative measures. Relative poverty is really an inequality measure wearing poverty's label.",
      reforge:{stem:"During COVID-19, median incomes fell sharply. What likely happened to the UK's relative poverty rate?",options:{A:"It rose sharply as everyone got poorer.",B:"It was unchanged — poverty doesn't respond to recessions. (in the market described)",C:"It doubled immediately.",D:"It may have fallen because the 60% median-income threshold also fell."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"POV-06",spec:"2.4.2",stem:"Explain one cause of poverty in a developed country like the UK. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Structural unemployment caused by deindustrialisation has left former industrial regions with fewer well-paid jobs (knowledge). Workers in these areas who lack transferable skills face long-term worklessness or are confined to low-wage service-sector employment, pushing households below the poverty line (analysis).",
      tag:["MC-POV-04","MC-EMP-02"],
      scaffold:"Causes: unemployment (structural, cyclical), low wages, insecure work (gig economy), regional deprivation, low educational attainment, family breakdown, disability/ill health, housing costs. K for cause; An for mechanism linking it to poverty."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.5.1"] = {
  label: "Financial Markets",
  color: "#0369a1",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"FMK-01",spec:"2.5.1",stem:"Which of the following is a role of financial markets?",
      options:{A:"Setting the inflation target.",B:"Facilitating saving",C:"Printing physical currency.",D:"Collecting taxes on behalf of the government."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-FMK-01",
      scaffold:"Roles of financial markets: (1) Facilitate saving (deposit accounts, bonds). (2) Lend to individuals and firms (mortgages, business loans). (3) Facilitate exchange of goods/services (payment systems). (4) Provide forward markets for currencies and commodities (manage risk). (5) Provide a market for equities (shares — raising capital for firms).",
      reforge:{stem:"A wheat farmer uses a forward contract to sell next season's harvest at a fixed price today. Which role of financial markets does this illustrate?",options:{A:"Facilitating saving.",B:"Providing forward markets in commodities — allowing the farmer to manage the risk of price fluctuations.",C:"Lending to businesses. (with other relevant factors held constant) (assuming other conditions are unchanged)",D:"Currency exchange."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FMK-02",spec:"2.5.1",stem:"A student explains forward markets in currencies but struggles to explain HOW they work. Which is the correct explanation?",
      options:{A:"A forward contract locks in an exchange rate for a future transaction",B:"Forward markets allow you to buy currency at today's spot rate at any future date.",C:"Forward markets predict future exchange rates with certainty.",D:"Forward markets are the same as the stock market."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-FMK-02",
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report flagged lack of clarity on forward markets. Key: a forward contract is a binding agreement to exchange currency at a pre-agreed rate on a future date. This protects firms from adverse exchange rate movements between agreeing a deal and payment. Example: a UK importer agrees to buy $1m of goods, delivery in 3 months. They buy dollars forward at $1.25/£ — even if sterling falls to $1.10, they're protected.",
      reforge:{stem:"A UK exporter expects to receive €500,000 in 6 months. Why might they use a forward contract?",options:{A:"To lock in today’s exchange rate and protect the sterling value of future euro receipts.",B:"To speculate on the euro rising.",C:"Forward contracts increase their profit. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (in the market described) (all else equal)",D:"They are legally required to."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"FMK-03",spec:"2.5.1",stem:"Market failure in financial markets can occur because of:",
      options:{A:"Perfect information among all participants.",B:"Too much regulation.",C:"Asymmetric information",D:"Low interest rates."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-FMK-03",
      scaffold:"Asymmetric info: borrowers know their risk; lenders don't. Adverse selection: riskier borrowers are more likely to seek loans. Moral hazard: once insured/bailed out, firms take excessive risks (banks before 2008 — 'too big to fail'). Also: speculation creating asset bubbles, externalities from bank failures (systemic risk).",
      reforge:{stem:"After the 2008 bailout, some argued banks would take even more risks in future because they expected to be rescued again. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Adverse selection.",B:"Perfect competition. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"Moral hazard — a bailout expectation weakens banks’ incentive to manage risk.",D:"Efficient markets."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"FMK-04",spec:"2.5.1",stem:"Why might a stock market crash have wider macroeconomic consequences?",
      options:{A:"It doesn't — share prices only affect shareholders. (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",B:"Stock markets are separate from the real economy.",C:"Share prices only matter for inflation.",D:"Falling share prices reduce household wealth (negative wealth effect → lower C)"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-FMK-04",
      scaffold:"Transmission: share prices fall → (1) wealth effect: shareholders feel poorer → C falls. (2) Firms: harder/more expensive to raise capital via equity → I falls. (3) Confidence: negative sentiment spreads → precautionary saving rises. (4) Banks: if holding shares, their balance sheets weaken → lending tightens. All feed into lower AD.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a stock market boom, household spending rises even among people who don't own shares. Which concept explains this?",options:{A:"The multiplier.",B:"Fiscal policy.",C:"Cost-push inflation. (with other relevant factors held constant) (for the economy in question) (all else equal)",D:"The wealth effect and confidence channel can encourage spending as markets rise."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"FMK-05",spec:"2.5.1",stem:"Explain one way financial markets contribute to economic growth. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Financial markets channel funds from savers to borrowers, enabling firms to access capital for investment (knowledge). For example, a company can issue shares on the stock market to raise funds for new factories or R&D, increasing productive capacity and shifting LRAS to the right over time (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-FMK-01",
      scaffold:"Financial markets → growth link: (1) Channel savings into productive investment (allocative efficiency). (2) Reduce risk through diversification and insurance → firms more willing to invest. (3) Provide liquidity → assets can be bought/sold easily → encourages participation. (4) Price discovery → signals where capital should flow."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.5.2"] = {
  label: "Role of Banks",
  color: "#7c3aed",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"BNK-01",spec:"2.5.2",stem:"The primary function of commercial banks is:",
      options:{A:"Setting interest rates for the economy.",B:"Financial intermediation",C:"Printing money.",D:"Regulating other financial institutions."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-BNK-01",
      scaffold:"Commercial banks are intermediaries: they pool deposits (liabilities) and lend them out (assets), earning the spread between deposit and lending rates. They also: provide payment systems, offer financial products (insurance, investments), and — crucially — create money through the credit creation multiplier.",
      reforge:{stem:"A bank pays 2% interest on savings accounts and charges 6% on loans. The 4% difference is called:",options:{A:"Inflation.",B:"The interest rate spread (or margin) — this is how the bank earns profit from its core intermediation role.",C:"The base rate.",D:"Quantitative easing. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BNK-02",spec:"2.5.2",stem:"How do commercial banks 'create' money?",
      options:{A:"Through the credit creation multiplier: when a bank receives a deposit",B:"They print banknotes in their vaults.",C:"They cannot create money — only the central bank can. (all else equal) (all else equal)",D:"By raising interest rates."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-BNK-02",
      scaffold:"Example: £1,000 deposited, 10% reserve ratio. Bank lends £900 → deposited elsewhere → that bank lends £810 → and so on. Total money created = £1,000 × (1/0.1) = £10,000. Banks don't print money — they create it through lending. This is why bank regulation matters: excessive lending inflates the money supply.",
      reforge:{stem:"If the reserve ratio increases from 10% to 20%, what happens to the credit creation multiplier?",options:{A:"It halves because banks hold more reserves, leaving less available to lend.",B:"It doubles.",C:"It is unchanged. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant)",D:"It becomes zero."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"BNK-03",spec:"2.5.2",stem:"Evaluate whether the government was right to bail out the banks in 2008. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Banks are 'systemically important' — their failure would have frozen lending, collapsing the payment system and triggering widespread business failures and unemployment (K+An). However, the bailout cost taxpayers billions, created moral hazard (banks expect future rescues, incentivising risk-taking), and diverted public funds from other priorities like health and education — the opportunity cost was significant (Ev).",
      tag:["MC-BNK-03","MC-FMK-03"],
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report noted top answers discussed: systemic risk (bank failure spreads through the economy), the run on Northern Rock, moral hazard from the expectation of bailouts, and the opportunity cost of public funds. Both sides needed, with context."
    },
    {
      id:"BNK-04",spec:"2.5.2",stem:"Which of the following is a regulatory measure introduced after the 2008 financial crisis?",
      options:{A:"Abolishing all banking regulation.",B:"Allowing banks to lend without any reserves. (all else equal)",C:"Higher capital requirements, so banks can absorb losses.",D:"Merging all banks into one state bank."},correct:"C",tag:"MC-BNK-04",
      scaffold:"Post-2008 reforms: (1) Higher capital/liquidity requirements (Basel III) — more reserves, less leverage. (2) Ring-fencing — separating retail banking from investment banking. (3) Stress testing — regulators check if banks can survive hypothetical shocks. (4) Living wills — plans for orderly failure without taxpayer bailouts. All aim to prevent 'too big to fail.'",
      reforge:{stem:"Ring-fencing requires banks to:",options:{A:"Close all overseas branches. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal) (all else equal)",B:"Merge with competitors.",C:"Separate retail banking from riskier investment banking to protect customers’ deposits.",D:"Stop lending entirely."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BNK-05",spec:"2.5.2",stem:"Why are banks sometimes described as 'too big to fail'?",
      options:{A:"Because large banks are always profitable.",B:"Because the government owns them.",C:"Because they hold all the country's gold reserves. (all else equal)",D:"Because their failure would cause systemic risk"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-BNK-03",
      scaffold:"Systemic risk: banks lend to each other (interbank market). If one large bank fails → other banks lose money → they can't lend → credit freezes → businesses can't borrow → layoffs, closures, recession. This interconnectedness makes government reluctant to let them fail — but bailouts create moral hazard.",
      reforge:{stem:"A policy of 'too big to fail' creates moral hazard because:",options:{A:"Banks become too cautious.",B:"Small banks are unfairly treated. (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",C:"It increases competition.",D:"Banks take excessive risks knowing they'll be rescued if things go wrong — the implicit government guarantee removes the downside, incentivising reckless behaviour."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["2.5.3"] = {
  label: "Central Banks",
  color: "#475569",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CB-01",spec:"2.5.3",stem:"Which of the following is NOT a function of the Bank of England?",
      options:{A:"Setting the base rate via the MPC.",B:"Setting government spending and tax rates.",C:"Acting as lender of last resort to commercial banks.",D:"Issuing banknotes and maintaining financial stability."},correct:"B",tag:"MC-CB-01",
      scaffold:"BoE functions: (1) Monetary policy — setting the base rate (MPC). (2) Lender of last resort — providing emergency liquidity to banks. (3) Issuing banknotes. (4) Financial stability — macro-prudential regulation (FPC). (5) Banker to the government. Fiscal policy (G and T) is set by the Treasury/Chancellor, not the BoE.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a banking crisis, the BoE provides emergency loans to a struggling bank to prevent its collapse. This is the BoE acting as:",options:{A:"Fiscal authority.",B:"Lender of last resort — providing liquidity when no other lender will, to prevent a systemic crisis.",C:"Tax collector.",D:"Regulator of the stock market. (with other relevant factors held constant) (for the economy in question)"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CB-02",spec:"2.5.3",stem:"Central bank independence means:",
      options:{A:"The central bank has operational independence over HOW to meet the target",B:"The central bank sets its own inflation target independently of government. (all else equal)",C:"The central bank operates as a private commercial entity.",D:"The government plays no role whatsoever in monetary policy."},correct:"A",tag:"MC-CB-02",
      scaffold:"Key distinction: the GOVERNMENT sets the target (2% CPI). The BOE decides how to hit it (base rate, QE). This prevents politicians cutting rates before elections. The BoE is accountable (Governor reports to Parliament, letters when target missed) but operationally free. Independence ≠ no oversight.",
      reforge:{stem:"A politician says: 'The Bank of England should cut rates to help my constituency before the election.' Why does central bank independence prevent this?",options:{A:"The MPC sets rates using economic data and the inflation target, not political pressure.",B:"The BoE always ignores politicians.",C:"Politicians are banned from discussing interest rates. (assuming other conditions are unchanged)",D:"The BoE can only raise rates, never cut them."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"CB-03",spec:"2.5.3",stem:"The Financial Policy Committee (FPC) is responsible for:",
      options:{A:"Setting interest rates.",B:"Collecting taxes.",C:"Macro-prudential regulation",D:"Running the stock exchange. (all else equal)"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-CB-03",
      scaffold:"MPC = monetary policy (interest rates, QE). FPC = financial stability (macro-prudential — looking at the system as a whole). PRA = micro-prudential (supervising individual firms). The FPC can impose counter-cyclical capital buffers, restrict loan-to-value ratios on mortgages, etc. Created after 2008 to fill the regulatory gap.",
      reforge:{stem:"The FPC notices that mortgage lending is growing rapidly with very high loan-to-income ratios. What action might it take?",options:{A:"Cut interest rates to encourage more lending. (with other relevant factors held constant) (all else equal)",B:"Print more money.",C:"Impose limits on loan-to-income ratios to prevent a housing bubble and protect financial stability.",D:"Reduce government spending."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CB-04",spec:"2.5.3",stem:"Why might controlling inflation conflict with other macroeconomic objectives? (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"To control inflation, the BoE raises interest rates, which reduces AD and dampens price pressures (K). However, this also slows economic growth and may increase unemployment — the Phillips curve trade-off (An). Furthermore, higher rates strengthen the exchange rate, making exports less competitive and potentially worsening the current account deficit (An). The central bank faces a dilemma: prioritising inflation control comes at the cost of growth, jobs, and trade competitiveness — particularly when inflation is cost-push rather than demand-pull, since rate rises address demand but not supply-side causes (Ev).",
      tag:["MC-MON-03","MC-EMP-03"],
      scaffold:"The 2019 examiner report stressed: top answers started from the inflation control objective and then showed how pursuing it creates conflicts with growth, employment, and the current account. Don't just list conflicts — show the causal chain from monetary tightening to each trade-off."
    },
    {
      id:"CB-05",spec:"2.5.3",stem:"Which of the following best describes the role of the central bank in maintaining financial stability?",
      options:{A:"Ensuring all banks make a profit.",B:"Setting prices for all financial products. (in the market described)",C:"Guaranteeing all investments against loss.",D:"Monitoring systemic risks, regulating bank capital requirements"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-CB-05",
      scaffold:"Financial stability ≠ guaranteeing profits. It means: (1) preventing systemic collapse (lender of last resort). (2) Macro-prudential oversight (FPC monitors risks like asset bubbles, excessive leverage). (3) Stress tests (can banks survive a severe recession scenario?). (4) Resolution planning (living wills — how to wind down a failing bank without a bailout). Goal: a resilient system, not zero risk.",
      reforge:{stem:"A stress test reveals that a bank would become insolvent if house prices fell 30%. What is the likely regulatory response?",options:{A:"Nothing — house prices won't fall that much.",B:"The government guarantees the bank's mortgage portfolio. (over the period considered)",C:"The bank is immediately closed.",D:"The regulator may require more capital, less property exposure or restructuring."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["PSY-SI"] = {
  label: "Social Influence",
  color: "#be185d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"SI-01",stem:"Asch's (1951) line study found that approximately what percentage of participants conformed at least once?",
      options:{A:"5%",B:"50%",C:"75%",D:"100%"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-SI-01",
      scaffold:"About 75% conformed at least once, but the overall conformity rate (across all trials) was ~37%. Students often confuse these two figures. 25% never conformed at all. Key: most people conformed sometimes, not always — suggesting situational pressure rather than permanent trait.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student writes: 'Asch proved that most people always conform.' Why is this an oversimplification?",options:{A:"Only ~37% conformed per trial and 25% never conformed",B:"It is correct, since Asch proved conformity is universal",C:"Asch's study was really about obedience rather than conformity",D:"Only 5% of participants ever conformed on any trial"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-02",stem:"A student defines compliance as 'genuinely changing your mind to agree with the group.' What type of conformity are they actually describing?",
      options:{A:"Compliance — they are correct.",B:"Identification — adopting views to fit into a group role.",C:"Obedience — following an authority figure.",D:"Internalisation"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-SI-02",
      scaffold:"Three types (Kelman): Compliance = public agreement, private disagreement (shallowest — changes behaviour not beliefs). Identification = conforming to a valued group's norms while part of it (moderate). Internalisation = genuine belief change, persists even without the group (deepest). Students constantly swap compliance and internalisation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A person converts to their partner's religion and continues practising it sincerely even after they separate. This is best described as:",options:{A:"Compliance — they only did it to preserve the relationship",B:"Obedience — they simply followed direct instructions",C:"Identification — they took on a group role temporarily",D:"Internalisation — the beliefs persist away from the group"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-03",stem:"In Milgram's (1963) baseline study, what percentage of participants delivered the maximum 450V shock?",
      options:{A:"10%",B:"35%",C:"65%",D:"100%"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-SI-03",
      scaffold:"65% went to 450V — a finding that shocked the psychology community. 100% went to at least 300V. Key: participants showed extreme distress (sweating, trembling, nervous laughter) but still obeyed. This demonstrates the power of situational factors (authority, graduated commitment) over personality.",
      reforge:{stem:"In Milgram's variation where the experimenter gave instructions by telephone rather than in person, obedience dropped to ~21%. What does this suggest?",options:{A:"The study lacked internal validity throughout",B:"Telephone instructions are always ignored by participants",C:"Obedience is entirely determined by personality factors",D:"Proximity of authority is a key situational variable"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-04",stem:"A student explains obedience using the term 'agentic state' but cannot define it. The correct definition is:",
      options:{A:"Seeing oneself as carrying out another's wishes rather than acting autonomously",B:"A state of autonomous conscience in which a person acts on their own judgement",C:"A state of complete free will in which social influence has no effect at all on us",D:"A stable personality type characterised by a natural obedience to all authority"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-SI-04",
      scaffold:"Agentic state (Milgram): the person acts as an 'agent' of the authority, giving up personal responsibility. Opposite = autonomous state (acting on own free will). The 'agentic shift' occurs when someone perceives a legitimate authority and enters the agentic state. This explains WHY people obey even when distressed — they don't feel personally responsible.",
      reforge:{stem:"A nurse administers a drug she thinks is too high a dose because the doctor ordered it. She says: 'I was just following orders.' This illustrates:",options:{A:"The autonomous state — she made her own free decision",B:"Internalisation — she genuinely believes the dose is correct here",C:"The agentic state — she has shifted responsibility to the doctor",D:"Minority influence."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-05",stem:"Moscovici's (1969) blue-green slide study investigated minority influence. What was his key finding about consistency?",
      options:{A:"When the minority gave clearly inconsistent answers across the different trials",B:"When the minority consistently called the blue slides green on every trial",C:"When the majority openly resisted the minority's answers throughout",D:"When only the majority was permitted to influence other individuals"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-SI-05",
      scaffold:"Consistency is key: a consistent minority makes the majority engage in deeper processing ('maybe they have a point'). An inconsistent minority is dismissed. Also important: commitment (the minority is seen as principled) and flexibility (rigid minorities are less persuasive than those who bend slightly).",
      reforge:{stem:"According to the snowball effect, how does minority influence lead to wider social change?",options:{A:"The minority forces the majority to agree immediately",B:"Converts accumulate until the majority position shifts",C:"Social change only ever happens through majority pressure",D:"The minority gives up after being rejected once"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-06",stem:"A student confuses normative social influence (NSI) and informational social influence (ISI). Which correctly distinguishes them?",
      options:{A:"NSI = conforming because you want to be right; ISI = conforming because you want to be liked.",B:"They are the same concept.",C:"NSI = conforming to be accepted/avoid rejection (need for social approval)",D:"NSI only applies to Milgram; ISI only applies to Asch."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-SI-06",
      scaffold:"NSI = 'I'll go along so they like me' (driven by desire to fit in — leads to compliance). ISI = 'They probably know better than me' (driven by uncertainty — more likely to lead to internalisation). NSI operates most strongly when: group is important, fear of rejection. ISI operates most strongly when: task is ambiguous, experts present.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a fire alarm, you're unsure if it's real. You see everyone calmly staying in their seats, so you stay too. This is most likely:",options:{A:"NSI — you want the others to like and accept you",B:"Obedience — someone in authority told you to stay where you are",C:"ISI — the situation is ambiguous so you look to others",D:"Minority influence."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"SI-07",stem:"Evaluate Milgram's research in terms of ethical issues. (4 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Milgram's participants experienced significant psychological distress — trembling, sweating, and nervous breakdowns — violating the ethical principle of protection from harm (AO1). He also deceived participants about the true nature of the study (they believed shocks were real), preventing fully informed consent (AO1). However, Milgram debriefed all participants afterward and followed up: 84% said they were glad to have participated, and a psychiatrist found no lasting harm — suggesting the ethical cost may be outweighed by the study's enormous contribution to understanding destructive obedience (AO3).",
      tag:["MC-SI-03","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"Ethics: deception (fake shocks), lack of informed consent, psychological harm, right to withdraw compromised (prods like 'you must continue'). Counter: debriefing, no lasting harm found, huge social value (explaining Holocaust obedience). A balanced evaluation acknowledges both."
    },
    {
      id:"SI-08",stem:"Which of the following is a limitation of Asch's conformity research?",
      options:{A:"It has no limitations — it is a perfect study.",B:"Too many participants were used.",C:"It was a field experiment with no controls.",D:"It lacks ecological validity"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-SI-08",
      scaffold:"Asch limitations: (1) Artificial task — conforming about line lengths ≠ conforming about opinions/behaviour. (2) Androcentric sample — all male, potentially different results with women. (3) Cultural/temporal bias — 1950s America (McCarthyism) may have increased conformity. (4) Demand characteristics — participants may have guessed the aim. Strengths: high control, replicable, clear operationalised DV.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student says: 'Asch's findings prove that conformity is universal across all cultures.' Why is this claim problematic?",options:{A:"It is correct, since conformity is genuinely universal everywhere",B:"Culture has no effect whatsoever on human behaviour",C:"Asch did not actually study conformity at all",D:"Asch used only American men; conformity varies by culture"},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["PSY-MEM"] = {
  label: "Memory",
  color: "#0e7490",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MEM-01",stem:"According to the multi-store model (Atkinson & Shiffrin, 1968), information flows through memory in which order?",
      options:{A:"Sensory register → short-term memory → long-term memory.",B:"Long-term memory → short-term memory → sensory register.",C:"Short-term memory → sensory register → long-term memory.",D:"All three stores operate simultaneously with no sequence."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MEM-01",
      scaffold:"MSM is a linear model: Sensory register (brief, large capacity, modality-specific) → attention → STM (limited: ~7 items, ~30 seconds) → rehearsal → LTM (unlimited capacity and duration). Without attention, information decays from the sensory register. Without rehearsal, it's lost from STM.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student hears a phone number, repeats it several times, and then remembers it the next day. According to the MSM, what process transferred it to LTM?",options:{A:"Maintenance rehearsal transferred it to long-term memory",B:"Spontaneous decay of the memory trace over a long period",C:"Retrieval failure caused by the absence of cues",D:"Proactive interference from earlier learned material"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-02",stem:"A student says STM has a capacity of 'about 5 items.' What is the more accurate description?",
      options:{A:"STM has an effectively unlimited capacity for holding new information",B:"Miller found STM capacity is around 7±2 items, so five to nine",C:"STM holds exactly three items and no more than that at any time",D:"STM capacity depends entirely on the colour of the items presented"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MEM-02",
      scaffold:"Miller's magic number: 7±2. Not a fixed limit — chunking increases effective capacity (e.g. BBCITVFBI = 9 letters or 3 chunks). Cowan (2001) later suggested 4 chunks is more accurate. Students often forget the ±2 range or the role of chunking.",
      reforge:{stem:"A person remembers the sequence 1-9-1-4-1-9-4-5-2-0-0-1 as '1914, 1945, 2001' (three historical dates). This demonstrates:",options:{A:"Chunking — grouping items into meaningful units",B:"That short-term memory has a genuinely unlimited capacity",C:"That long-term memory has a limited capacity",D:"Retroactive interference from newer learning"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-03",stem:"The working memory model (Baddeley & Hitch, 1974) was proposed because:",
      options:{A:"The multi-store model was far too complex to be tested experimentally",B:"Long-term memory was shown not to exist as a separate store at all",C:"The multi-store model treated STM as a single, passive store",D:"Baddeley fundamentally disagreed with Ebbinghaus about forgetting curves"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MEM-03",
      scaffold:"MSM limitation: STM is unitary (one store). But evidence shows we can do two verbal tasks less well than one verbal + one visual → suggests separate processing systems. The WMM: Central executive (attention controller) + phonological loop (verbal) + visuo-spatial sketchpad (visual) + episodic buffer (integration).",
      reforge:{stem:"You can drive a car (visual-spatial) while having a conversation (verbal) but struggle to listen to a podcast while reading. The WMM explains this because:",options:{A:"Driving does not use memory in any way",B:"The central executive is known to have unlimited storage capacity",C:"The tasks use different slave systems, so they do not compete",D:"The multi-store model explains this situation better"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-04",stem:"A student describes the central executive as 'a big memory store with lots of capacity.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"This is correct, since the central executive does store information",B:"The central executive does not actually exist in the model at all",C:"The central executive processes only visual and spatial information",D:"It is an attentional control system with a very limited capacity"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-MEM-04",
      scaffold:"The CE is the 'boss' — it decides what to attend to and delegates to the slave systems (phonological loop, visuo-spatial sketchpad). It has limited capacity (you can't attend to everything at once) but is NOT a store. Students often describe it as a large memory warehouse — this misses its role as a processor/allocator, not a container.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which component of the WMM is responsible for integrating information from the slave systems and linking it to LTM?",options:{A:"The phonological loop, which handles sound",B:"The central executive, because it is by far the biggest store",C:"The episodic buffer, which combines information sources",D:"The visuo-spatial sketchpad, which handles images"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-05",stem:"Loftus and Palmer (1974) asked participants to estimate the speed of cars in a filmed accident. The verb used ('smashed' vs 'hit') significantly affected estimates. This demonstrates:",
      options:{A:"The effect of leading questions on the accuracy of eyewitness testimony",B:"Whether the participants taking part in the study had unusually poor eyesight",C:"Whether all eyewitnesses are fundamentally unreliable witnesses",D:"Whether speed estimates given by witnesses are always accurate"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MEM-05",
      scaffold:"'Smashed' → higher speed estimates (~41mph) vs 'contacted' (~32mph). Experiment 2: 'smashed' group also more likely to report (non-existent) broken glass. This suggests the leading question didn't just bias the response — it altered the stored memory itself (memory reconstruction). Key concept: post-event discussion/information can contaminate the original memory.",
      reforge:{stem:"After witnessing a robbery, a witness reads a newspaper report describing the thief as 'tall and bearded.' At a later identity parade, the witness selects a tall, bearded suspect. This illustrates:",options:{A:"Completely accurate recall of the original witnessed event",B:"Post-event information contaminating the original memory",C:"The multi-store model of memory operating normally",D:"Proactive interference from previously learned material"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-06",stem:"The cognitive interview (Fisher & Geiselman, 1992) includes four techniques. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",
      options:{A:"Report everything — even seemingly trivial details.",B:"Mental reinstatement of context — imagine the environment and emotions at the time.",C:"Leading questions — use specific prompts to guide the witness.",D:"Change perspective — recall events from a different viewpoint."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MEM-06",
      scaffold:"Four CI techniques: (1) Report everything (even partial/trivial details). (2) Mental reinstatement (recreate the context — place, weather, feelings). (3) Change temporal order (recall events in reverse or from a different starting point). (4) Change perspective (describe from another person's viewpoint). Leading questions are EXCLUDED — they contaminate memory (Loftus). Students sometimes include 'ask specific questions' which is wrong.",
      reforge:{stem:"A police officer asks a witness to describe the robbery scene starting from the moment they left, rather than from the beginning. This uses which CI technique?",options:{A:"Report absolutely everything that the witness is able to recall",B:"Change temporal order, recalling in a different sequence",C:"Asking the witness a series of leading questions",D:"Mental reinstatement of the original context"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-07",stem:"Evaluate the multi-store model of memory. (6 marks — AO1 + AO3)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"The MSM proposes three separate stores: sensory register, STM, and LTM, with information flowing linearly between them via attention and rehearsal (AO1). Support comes from case studies like HM, whose hippocampal damage destroyed the ability to form new LTMs while leaving STM intact — suggesting they are separate stores (AO3). However, the MSM oversimplifies STM as a single unitary store — the working memory model showed STM has multiple components (phonological loop, visuo-spatial sketchpad). It also overstates rehearsal: we form long-term memories without deliberate rehearsal (e.g. flashbulb memories), and not all rehearsal leads to LTM transfer — elaborative rehearsal is more effective than maintenance rehearsal (AO3).",
      tag:["MC-MEM-01","MC-MEM-03"],
      scaffold:"AO1: describe the model clearly (3 stores, flow, encoding/capacity/duration differences). AO3: use evidence (HM, Baddeley, Craik & Lockhart) and explain what it shows. Both strengths AND limitations needed. Don't just list — explain the significance."
    },
    {
      id:"MEM-08",stem:"A student confuses proactive and retroactive interference. Which correctly defines each?",
      options:{A:"Proactive = new learning interferes with old; Retroactive = old learning interferes with new.",B:"Proactive = old learning interferes with recall of new information",C:"They are the same thing.",D:"Proactive = forgetting from STM; Retroactive = forgetting from LTM."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MEM-08",
      scaffold:"Proactive: previously learned material interferes FORWARD in time (old disrupts new). Example: you learned French first, now Spanish vocab keeps coming out as French. Retroactive: newly learned material interferes BACKWARD in time (new disrupts old). Example: you learn a new phone number and can no longer remember the old one. PRO = forward, RETRO = backward.",
      reforge:{stem:"A teacher learns 200 new student names in September. By October, she struggles to recall last year's students' names. This is:",options:{A:"Proactive interference — the old names disrupt the new",B:"Retroactive interference — new names disrupt the old",C:"Decay, because a long time has simply passed",D:"Retrieval failure due to the absence of cues"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["SOC-EDU"] = {
  label: "Education",
  color: "#b91c1c",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"EDU-01",stem:"Functionalists argue that education performs the function of 'role allocation.' This means:",
      options:{A:"Teachers allocate students to friendship groups.",B:"Schools themselves decide exactly which jobs their students will do after leaving",C:"Education sifts and sorts individuals into roles best suited to their talents",D:"Role allocation only benefits working-class students."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-EDU-01",
      scaffold:"Davis and Moore (1945): education is a mechanism for role allocation — it grades people through exams and qualifications, placing the most talented into the most functionally important (and highest-rewarded) roles. This assumes meritocracy: that success reflects ability + effort. Marxists challenge this — they argue role allocation reproduces class inequality, not talent.",
      reforge:{stem:"Marxists criticise the functionalist view of role allocation because:",options:{A:"Roles are allocated by class background rather than by genuine talent",B:"They agree that the education system is genuinely meritocratic",C:"They believe that schools ought to abolish all formal examinations entirely",D:"Role allocation only ever happens within the private school sector"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-02",stem:"Bowles and Gintis (1976) argue that there is a 'correspondence principle' between school and work. This means:",
      options:{A:"Schools teach students all of the specific technical skills their future jobs require",B:"Schools and workplaces have identical timetables.",C:"The correspondence principle only applies to grammar schools.",D:"The social relationships in school mirror those in the capitalist workplace"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-EDU-02",
      scaffold:"Correspondence principle: school mirrors work. Students follow orders (teachers → bosses), are motivated by grades not learning (extrinsic rewards → wages), and experience alienation (no control over curriculum → no control over production). This produces the 'hidden curriculum' — the unwritten lessons of obedience and conformity capitalism needs. Evaluation: deterministic, ignores pupil resistance (Willis).",
      reforge:{stem:"Paul Willis's (1977) study of 'the lads' challenges Bowles and Gintis because:",options:{A:"It proves that the correspondence principle is entirely correct",B:"They resisted school, yet still ended up in manual work",C:"Willis studied middle-class students at a private grammar school",D:"All of the lads went on to become successful professionals"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-03",stem:"A student writes: 'Working-class pupils underachieve because they are less intelligent.' Which sociological response best challenges this?",
      options:{A:"This is a culturally and structurally reductionist view",B:"IQ determines achievement.",C:"Intelligence doesn't exist.",D:"Only factors inside the school itself have any bearing on achievement"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-EDU-03",
      scaffold:"Class and achievement: External factors — material deprivation (Flaherty: low income → no books, tutors, quiet space), cultural deprivation (Bernstein: restricted vs elaborated code; Sugarman: fatalism, present-time orientation). Internal factors — labelling (Becker: ideal pupil), streaming (Lacey: differentiation-polarisation), self-fulfilling prophecy. A full answer addresses BOTH internal and external.",
      reforge:{stem:"Bernstein's (1971) concept of 'elaborated code' refers to:",options:{A:"The informal language people use in texting and on social media platforms",B:"A type of secret code used by teachers to communicate",C:"Context-free, complex speech used in schools and middle-class homes",D:"Working-class dialect that schools ought to adopt in lessons"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-04",stem:"Interactionist sociologists focus on which level of analysis when studying education?",
      options:{A:"Macro — studying large-scale social structures and their functions for society as a whole.",B:"Micro — focusing on small-scale face-to-face classroom interactions.",C:"Global — comparing education systems across different countries.",D:"Sociologists do not study education; it belongs to psychology."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-EDU-04",
      scaffold:"Interactionists (Becker, Hargreaves, Rosenthal & Jacobson) study what happens INSIDE schools: how teachers label pupils, how labels create self-fulfilling prophecies, how streaming polarises subcultures. Strength: reveals processes behind statistics. Weakness: ignores wider structural causes (poverty, policy) — accused of being too micro.",
      reforge:{stem:"Rosenthal and Jacobson's (1968) 'Pygmalion in the Classroom' study demonstrated:",options:{A:"That streaming has no measurable effect on pupil achievement",B:"That IQ tests are perfectly reliable measures of ability",C:"That labelling in schools only ever affects girls",D:"The self-fulfilling prophecy of teacher expectations"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-05",stem:"Evaluate the view that material deprivation is the main cause of working-class underachievement. (10 marks — AO1 + AO2 + AO3)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        AO1:"Material deprivation = poverty and its direct educational consequences. Smith and Noble (1995): 'costs of free schooling' — uniforms, trips, equipment, tutoring. Flaherty (2004): low income → overcrowded housing → no quiet study space, poor diet → illness and absence. Eligibility for free school meals correlates strongly with lower GCSE results.",
        AO2:"Apply to contemporary UK: pupil premium policy attempts to address material deprivation directly. Post-COVID widening of the gap (device poverty, lost learning concentrated among disadvantaged).",
        AO3:"However, material deprivation alone cannot explain the gap. Cultural deprivation (Bernstein, Sugarman) — values and language, not just money. In-school factors (Becker's labelling, Gillborn and Youdell's educational triage) actively disadvantage working-class pupils regardless of material circumstances. Also: some materially deprived pupils DO succeed — individual agency, cultural capital from other sources. Judgement: material deprivation is significant but works alongside cultural and institutional factors — it's a necessary but not sufficient explanation."
      },
      tag:["MC-EDU-03","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"10-mark essays need: knowledge of the named concept (material deprivation), application to education, THEN sustained evaluation using alternative explanations and evidence. The examiner wants you to WEIGH the view, not just describe it."
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-06",stem:"Which education policy introduced a 'marketisation' of schools in England?",
      options:{A:"The Butler Education Act (1944).",B:"The Raising of the School Leaving Age (1972).",C:"The Education Reform Act (1988)",D:"The Comprehensive School system (1960s–70s)."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-EDU-06",
      scaffold:"ERA 1988 (New Right / Thatcher): schools compete for pupils → funding follows the pupil → league tables rank schools → parents 'choose' (market forces in education). Supporters: drives up standards. Critics (Ball): 'myth of parentocracy' — middle-class parents exploit choice better (cultural/economic capital), widening inequality.",
      reforge:{stem:"Ball (1994) argued that marketisation benefits middle-class parents because:",options:{A:"They have the capital to research, move near and appeal to schools",B:"League tables are far too complicated for working-class parents",C:"Working-class parents do not care about their children's education",D:"All schools are identical, so parental choice is meaningless"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-07",stem:"A student applies only ONE perspective (e.g. Marxism) when answering a question about the role of education. What mark-scheme issue does this create?",
      options:{A:"None — one perspective is enough for full marks.",B:"Only Marxism is relevant to education questions.",C:"The student should have relied on common sense rather than sociological theory",D:"The answer lacks evaluation and competing perspectives"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TECH-02",
      scaffold:"AQA Sociology mark schemes for 10- and 20-mark questions explicitly require evaluation. A one-perspective answer typically caps at AO1/AO2 marks and scores zero AO3. Even if the perspective is well-explained, you MUST consider alternatives and reach a judgement. Breadth of perspectives + depth of evaluation = top band.",
      reforge:{stem:"In a 20-mark essay on 'Assess the functionalist view of education,' a top-band answer would:",options:{A:"Only describe functionalist ideas in a great deal of detail",B:"Reject all sociological perspectives as being equally valid",C:"Explain functionalism then evaluate with rival perspectives",D:"Focus entirely on research methods rather than on the education system"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"EDU-08",stem:"Durkheim argued that education performs two main functions for society. Which pair is correct?",
      options:{A:"Creating social solidarity and allocating individuals to roles based on meritocratic achie",B:"Providing childcare and basic economic training.",C:"Exercising social control over pupils and delivering compulsory religious instruction daily",D:"Preparing students for exams and providing supervised care."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-EDU-01",
      scaffold:"Durkheim (functionalist): (1) Social solidarity — school is 'society in miniature'; teaches shared norms, values, history, creating cohesion. (2) Specialist skills — industrial society needs a skilled, specialised workforce; education provides this. Parsons added: universalistic standards (judged on achievement, not ascription) and the bridge between family (particularistic) and wider society.",
      reforge:{stem:"Parsons described education as the 'focal socialising agency' bridging family and society. By this he meant:",options:{A:"Schools replace the family unit entirely during childhood",B:"Families and schools teach children identical sets of values",C:"Education only ever socialises children from middle-class families",D:"It shifts pupils from particularistic to universalistic standards"},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

