// ===== ENGLISH BANK 1 — Literary & Linguistic Terminology =====

BANKS["ENG-TERM-1"] = {
  label: "Literary & Linguistic Terminology",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"ENG-01",
      stem:"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks?",
      options:{
        A:"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.",
        B:"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.",
        C:"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.",
        D:"The student should use the term 'motif' as these words recur throughout the poem."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-01",
      scaffold:"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?",options:{A:"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.",B:"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.",C:"The imagery is a motif that appears throughout the text.",D:"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-02",
      stem:"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise?",
      options:{
        A:"This is stream of consciousness — the narrator records the character's unfiltered thoughts.",
        B:"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.",
        C:"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.",
        D:"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-02",
      scaffold:"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.",
      reforge:{stem:"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?",options:{A:"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.",B:"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.",C:"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator.",D:"Soliloquy — the character thinks aloud for the audience's benefit."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-03",
      stem:"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis?",
      options:{
        A:"Anaphora — the repetition of 'and' at the start of each clause. Naming it as polysyndeton is wrong because polysyndeton refers to something else.",
        B:"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.",
        C:"Asyndeton — the deliberate omission of conjunctions to speed up the rhythm.",
        D:"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-03",
      scaffold:"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'",
      reforge:{stem:"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?",options:{A:"Asyndeton — the omission of conjunctions creates a breathless pace.",B:"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.",C:"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.",D:"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-04",
      stem:"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding?",
      options:{
        A:"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.",
        B:"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.",
        C:"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.",
        D:"Deixis is a feature of spoken language only — it cannot appear in written texts."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-04",
      scaffold:"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).",
      reforge:{stem:"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?",options:{A:"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.",B:"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance.",C:"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.",D:"'We' is an example of the royal plural — Churchill refers only to himself."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-05",
      stem:"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly?",
      options:{
        A:"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.",
        B:"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.",
        C:"No — bathos only applies when the writer makes a deliberate error and falls from high style accidentally.",
        D:"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-05",
      scaffold:"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).",
      reforge:{stem:"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?",options:{A:"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.",B:"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.",C:"Anticlimax — the story ends disappointingly.",D:"Juxtaposition — the serious and trivial are placed together."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-06",
      stem:"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct?",
      options:{
        A:"Yes — dramatic irony is when a character makes an error the audience can see.",
        B:"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.",
        C:"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.",
        D:"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-06",
      scaffold:"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.",
      reforge:{stem:"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?",options:{A:"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting.",B:"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.",C:"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.",D:"This is situational irony — the situation turns out opposite to what was expected."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-07",
      stem:"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition?",
      options:{
        A:"The student is correct — register simply means the level of formality in a text.",
        B:"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.",
        C:"Register means accent and dialect — the geographical variety of language used.",
        D:"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-07",
      scaffold:"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.",
      reforge:{stem:"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?",options:{A:"The shift from formal to informal register shows the politician is poorly educated.",B:"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.",C:"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.",D:"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-08",
      stem:"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write?",
      options:{
        A:"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.",
        B:"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'",
        C:"The student should say 'the motif of darkness symbolises evil — a common Gothic convention.'",
        D:"The student is correct — repetition for emphasis is an acceptable AO2 observation."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-08",
      scaffold:"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.",
      reforge:{stem:"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?",options:{A:"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.",B:"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.",C:"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.",D:"The student is correct that the repetition shows the long duration of waiting."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== ENGLISH BANK 2 — Exam Technique & AO Application =====

BANKS["ENG-TECH-1"] = {
  label: "Exam Technique & AO Application",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"ENG-09",
      stem:"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost?",
      options:{
        A:"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603.",
        B:"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.",
        C:"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.",
        D:"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-09",
      scaffold:"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?",options:{A:"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.",B:"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'",C:"They have already earned AO3 marks by providing the historical context.",D:"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-10",
      stem:"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically?",
      options:{
        A:"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.",
        B:"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.",
        C:"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.",
        D:"AO4 failure — the student has not connected the text to any other literary works."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-10",
      scaffold:"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.",options:{A:"Dickens shows that Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London, which demonstrates class anxiety in Victorian England.",B:"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.",C:"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel.",D:"The visit shows that Joe and Pip have grown apart because Pip has become richer."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-11",
      stem:"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4?",
      options:{
        A:"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.",
        B:"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.",
        C:"A secondary critic's view — the student needs to reference a named scholar.",
        D:"More context about Plath's biography and her time in a psychiatric hospital."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-11",
      scaffold:"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?",options:{A:"The student should quote more of the poem to support their point.",B:"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.",C:"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.",D:"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-12",
      stem:"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks?",
      options:{
        A:"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.",
        B:"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.",
        C:"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.",
        D:"'Explore' means the student should consider the wider context of the novel, not just the opening."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-12",
      scaffold:"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?",options:{A:"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.",B:"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics.",C:"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.",D:"The student should focus on Othello's language rather than Iago's since he is the title character."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-13",
      stem:"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here?",
      options:{
        A:"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.",
        B:"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.",
        C:"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.",
        D:"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-13",
      scaffold:"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?",options:{A:"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.",B:"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'",C:"The student should use 'likewise' instead of 'similarly'.",D:"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-14",
      stem:"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing?",
      options:{
        A:"They need to include more quotations from the text.",
        B:"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.",
        C:"They need to write longer responses — Band 3 responses are usually too short.",
        D:"They should include more biographical context about the author."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-14",
      scaffold:"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?",options:{A:"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'",B:"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'",C:"'I think the lighthouse represents hope because it guides ships to safety.'",D:"'The lighthouse is significant throughout the novel and appears at key moments.'"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-15",
      stem:"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening?",
      options:{
        A:"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.",
        B:"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim.",
        C:"The student should not identify techniques at the beginning — save them for the body paragraphs.",
        D:"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-15",
      scaffold:"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.",
      reforge:{stem:"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'",options:{A:"'This extract is about the sea and uses many techniques to describe it effectively.'",B:"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'",C:"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'",D:"'The techniques used in this extract include personification, short sentences, and direct address, which are all effective.'"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ENG-16",
      stem:"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice?",
      options:{
        A:"The student needs to write about the rhyme scheme and count the syllables in each line.",
        B:"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?",
        C:"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.",
        D:"The student needs to compare the poem's form to other poems in the collection."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENG-16",
      scaffold:"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?",options:{A:"It doesn't — the quality of the language analysis is what earns marks.",B:"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.",C:"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.",D:"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== RS BANK 2 — Religion & Ethics Applied =====
BANKS["RS-2"] = {
  label: "Religion & Ethics — Applied",
  color: "#065f46",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"RS-09",stem:"Fletcher's Situation Ethics claims there is only one intrinsically good thing. What is it?",
      options:{A:"Agape — selfless, unconditional love; every rule can be set aside if love is better served.",B:"Happiness — the greatest pleasure for the greatest number of people.",C:"Obedience to God's revealed commands in scripture.",D:"Virtue — a settled disposition of good character built through habit."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-RS-09",
      scaffold:"Fletcher: agape is the sole intrinsic good; everything else is instrumentally good. Four working principles: pragmatism, relativism, positivism, personalism. Six propositions include: love is the only norm, love and justice are the same, love wills the neighbour's good. Key criticism: how do we predict which act is most loving? It collapses into a form of act utilitarianism with love replacing pleasure.",
      reforge:{stem:"A critic says Situation Ethics is 'really just utilitarianism with love substituted for happiness.' What feature of Fletcher's theory does this criticism target?",options:{A:"Its reliance on scripture as the final authority.",B:"Its consequentialist structure — judging each act by predicted outcomes.",C:"Its claim that rules can never be broken.",D:"Its focus on building character over time."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-10",stem:"The Euthyphro dilemma poses a problem for Divine Command Theory. Which pair of options captures the dilemma?",
      options:{A:"Either God exists or morality exists — Plato argues the two are mutually exclusive.",B:"Either scripture is literal or metaphorical — the dilemma concerns biblical interpretation.",C:"Either things are good independently of God (limiting his authority)",D:"Either humans are free or God is omniscient — the classic free will versus foreknowledge problem."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-RS-10",
      scaffold:"From Plato's Euthyphro. Horn 1: if God commands what is already good, goodness exists independently and God is not the source of morality. Horn 2: if things are good merely because God commands them, morality is arbitrary — cruelty could have been good had God commanded it. Responses: the 'third way' (God's nature IS goodness — Aquinas), or biting the bullet on divine sovereignty (Ockham).",
      reforge:{stem:"Aquinas's response to the Euthyphro dilemma argues that:",options:{A:"Morality is entirely independent of God.",B:"God's commands are arbitrary but must still be obeyed.",C:"The dilemma is unanswerable and Divine Command Theory fails.",D:"The dilemma is false — goodness is neither prior to God nor arbitrary, because God's own unchanging nature is the standard of goodness."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-11",stem:"A student writes: 'Aristotle's golden mean tells us always to act moderately.' Why is this inaccurate?",
      options:{A:"Aristotle rejected the idea of a mean entirely — that virtue concept belongs to Plato.",B:"The golden mean applies only to intellectual virtues such as wisdom, not moral ones.",C:"The mean is arithmetic — the exact midpoint between the two extreme vices in every case.",D:"The mean is relative to the person and situation"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RS-11",
      scaffold:"The doctrine of the mean: virtue lies between excess and deficiency (courage between rashness and cowardice), but it is the mean 'relative to us', judged by practical wisdom (phronesis), not a mathematical midpoint. Some actions are intrinsically wrong with no mean. Virtue is acquired by habituation and aims at eudaimonia (flourishing). Common exam error: treating the mean as 'always be moderate'.",
      reforge:{stem:"For Aristotle, a person becomes courageous primarily by:",options:{A:"Repeatedly performing courageous acts until courage becomes a settled disposition of character.",B:"Learning the correct definition of courage.",C:"Following the commands of a moral authority.",D:"Calculating which acts produce the best outcomes."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-12",stem:"In the euthanasia debate, the 'sanctity of life' and 'quality of life' positions differ fundamentally on:",
      options:{A:"Whether pain relief should ever be given to patients who are near death.",B:"Whether life has intrinsic value regardless of condition, or depends on quality of experience.",C:"Whether euthanasia should be administered by doctors or authorised family members.",D:"Whether the law should formally distinguish active from passive euthanasia in sentencing."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RS-12",
      scaffold:"Sanctity of life (religious roots): life is a sacred gift from God, intrinsically valuable, not ours to end — supported by imago dei and 'do not kill'. Quality of life: value depends on the person's experience — autonomy, suffering, capacity for meaningful life (Singer, Glover). Middle positions: doctrine of double effect (pain relief that foreseeably shortens life), ordinary vs extraordinary means. Strong answers apply BOTH lenses to the same case.",
      reforge:{stem:"The doctrine of double effect permits a doctor to give life-shortening pain relief when:",options:{A:"The patient has signed a consent form requesting death.",B:"The family agrees that death is in the patient's interests.",C:"The intention is to relieve pain, death is a foreseen but unintended side effect, and the relief is proportionate to the suffering.",D:"The patient has fewer than six months to live."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-13",stem:"Ayer's emotivism claims that moral statements such as 'stealing is wrong' are:",
      options:{A:"True or false depending on the consequences of stealing.",B:"Commands issued by society to control behaviour.",C:"Expressions of emotion — closer to 'boo to stealing!' than to factual claims",D:"Statements about God's will expressed in secular language."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-RS-13",
      scaffold:"Ayer (Language, Truth and Logic): the verification principle — meaningful statements are either analytic or empirically verifiable. Moral claims are neither, so they lack factual meaning; they express approval/disapproval ('boo/hurrah theory'). Criticisms: the verification principle fails its own test; moral disagreement seems to be about more than feelings (we argue with reasons); Hare's prescriptivism refines this — moral language also prescribes universalisable action.",
      reforge:{stem:"A standard objection to the verification principle underlying emotivism is that:",options:{A:"The principle itself is neither analytic nor empirically verifiable, so by its own standard it is meaningless.",B:"It makes all scientific claims meaningless.",C:"It proves that moral statements are objectively true.",D:"It was rejected by Ayer before he published it."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-14",stem:"Moore's 'naturalistic fallacy' in meta-ethics is the error of:",
      options:{A:"The naturalistic fallacy is the belief that whatever occurs in nature is morally good.",B:"Defining 'good' in natural terms (e.g. pleasure or survival) fails",C:"The naturalistic fallacy is deriving natural laws directly from moral principles.",D:"The naturalistic fallacy claims moral facts do not exist in any objective sense."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RS-14",
      scaffold:"Moore (Principia Ethica): 'good' cannot be defined in natural terms. Open question argument: for any proposed definition ('good = pleasant'), it remains an open, sensible question whether that thing is good — so the definition fails. Related but distinct: Hume's is/ought gap (cannot derive prescriptive conclusions from descriptive premises alone). Students often conflate the two — Moore targets definitions; Hume targets inferences.",
      reforge:{stem:"Hume's is/ought gap differs from Moore's naturalistic fallacy because Hume's point concerns:",options:{A:"The definition of the word 'good'.",B:"Whether goodness is a natural property.",C:"The existence of God as a source of morality.",D:"The logical move from descriptive premises (what is) to prescriptive conclusions (what ought to be) — no valid inference connects them without a moral premise."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-15",stem:"Hard determinism holds that free will is an illusion because every choice is fully caused by prior events. What follows for moral responsibility on this view?",
      options:{A:"Moral responsibility collapses under hard determinism — praise and blame lose their desert-based justification.",B:"People remain fully responsible because they subjectively experience their choices as free.",C:"Only criminals lose moral responsibility under determinism; law-abiding people are exempt.",D:"Responsibility transfers entirely from individuals to God as the first cause of all events."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-RS-15",
      scaffold:"Hard determinism (e.g. d'Holbach; Clarence Darrow's legal defences): universal causation + incompatibilism → no free will → no desert-based responsibility. Libertarianism: genuine agent causation exists (Kant's noumenal self; conscience as evidence). Compatibilism (Hume, Ayer): freedom means acting according to your own desires without external constraint — free will and determinism are compatible. Exam trap: treating compatibilism as a weak halfway house rather than a distinct redefinition of freedom.",
      reforge:{stem:"A compatibilist would say a person acts freely when:",options:{A:"Their action has no prior cause whatsoever.",B:"They act on their own desires without external coercion — even if those desires were themselves causally determined.",C:"They could have done otherwise in an absolute, uncaused sense.",D:"A random quantum event initiates their decision."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"RS-16",stem:"An Eduqas evaluation question asks: 'Ethical theories are of no practical use in moral decision-making.' Discuss. What does a top-band response require?",
      options:{A:"A description of every ethical theory on the specification, listed in order.",B:"A firm personal opinion stated clearly in the introduction and repeated in the conclusion.",C:"Sustained evaluation of the claim against specific theories and cases",D:"Equal paragraph lengths for each side, presenting both views without taking any position."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-RS-16",
      scaffold:"AO2 band descriptors reward: sustained argument, evaluation of the actual claim (not just describing theories), use of scholarly views as tools of argument, counter-argument handled rather than listed, and a conclusion that follows from the reasoning. Common failure: 'juxtaposition' — describing one view, then another, without ever weighing them. Phrases that signal evaluation: 'this succeeds only if...', 'the force of this depends on...', 'a stronger objection is...'.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which sentence shows genuine AO2 evaluation rather than AO1 description?",options:{A:"Kant believed we should act only on universalisable maxims.",B:"Utilitarianism was developed by Bentham and refined by Mill.",C:"There are many different ethical theories that people use.",D:"Kant's refusal to permit lying even to save a life suggests the theory prizes consistency over the very persons it claims to respect — a tension Kantians must resolve."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== HSC BANK 2 — Care Values, Communication & Safeguarding =====
BANKS["HSC-2"] = {
  label: "Care Values, Communication & Safeguarding",
  color: "#be185d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"HSC-09",stem:"A care worker says to a distressed resident: 'I understand this feels overwhelming for you right now.' Their colleague says: 'You poor thing, how awful.' What distinction do these responses illustrate?",
      options:{A:"Empathy means reflecting the person's feelings from their perspective; sympathy means feeling sorry for them from the outside.",B:"Active listening versus passive listening — one involves full attention, the other does not.",C:"Formal versus informal communication registers used in professional and personal settings.",D:"Verbal versus non-verbal communication — words compared to body language and gesture."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HSC-09",
      scaffold:"Empathy: entering the person's frame of reference, communicating understanding of THEIR experience — central to person-centred practice (Rogers). Sympathy: observer's own emotional reaction, which can feel patronising and positions the person as pitiable. Exam application: link empathy to outcomes — trust, disclosure, emotional wellbeing (PIES). Higher bands name the value AND show its impact on the individual.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is empathy generally preferred over sympathy in professional care relationships?",options:{A:"Empathy takes less time to express during busy shifts.",B:"Sympathy is illegal in regulated care settings.",C:"Empathy maintains the person's dignity and supports trust, while sympathy can feel patronising and creates distance.",D:"Empathy avoids any emotional involvement with the individual."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-10",stem:"A young person discloses to a support worker that they are being harmed at home, then says 'promise you won't tell anyone.' What is the correct professional response to this request?",
      options:{A:"Promise confidentiality — trust is the foundation of any caring relationship.",B:"Explain honestly that this cannot be kept secret: safety comes first",C:"Refuse to listen further until the designated safeguarding lead is physically present.",D:"Tell the young person they must report it directly to the police themselves."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HSC-10",
      scaffold:"Confidentiality is a core care value but is NOT absolute: it can and must be breached where there is risk of significant harm, especially safeguarding concerns. Best practice: never promise secrecy; be honest about what must happen next; share on a need-to-know basis; record accurately; follow the setting's safeguarding procedure and report to the designated safeguarding lead. Exam trap: treating confidentiality as an unbreakable rule.",
      reforge:{stem:"The principle governing who information is shared with after a safeguarding disclosure is:",options:{A:"Whole-team transparency — everyone in the setting should be informed.",B:"Family first — parents must always be told immediately.",C:"Manager's discretion — the manager decides based on convenience.",D:"Need-to-know — information is shared only with those whose role requires it to keep the person safe."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-11",stem:"Which behaviour best demonstrates active listening in a one-to-one with a service user?",
      options:{A:"Maintaining steady eye contact while internally planning what you will say next.",B:"Taking comprehensive written notes throughout so every word is captured accurately.",C:"Paraphrasing, reflecting feelings, open body language, and allowing silences",D:"Nodding continuously and verbally agreeing with every statement the person makes."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HSC-11",
      scaffold:"Active listening (SOLER: Squarely face, Open posture, Lean in, Eye contact, Relax): paraphrasing, reflecting, summarising, minimal encouragers, tolerating silence, clarifying questions. Constant note-taking breaks connection; constant agreement is not listening. Link to communication cycle (Argyle): message sent → received → decoded → feedback. Application marks come from linking the technique to the individual's needs — e.g. slower pace for someone with dementia.",
      reforge:{stem:"Argyle's communication cycle suggests communication fails most often when:",options:{A:"A message is decoded differently from how it was intended and no feedback loop exists to catch the misunderstanding.",B:"Two people speak different first languages.",C:"The message is delivered too slowly.",D:"Written communication is used instead of spoken."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-12",stem:"A consultant tells a patient: 'Your MI presented atypically, so we're starting dual antiplatelet therapy post-PCI.' Which barrier to communication does this illustrate, and what is the appropriate adjustment?",
      options:{A:"A physical barrier — the solution is a quieter room.",B:"Jargon — technical language excludes the patient",C:"An emotional barrier — the solution is postponing the conversation.",D:"A cultural barrier — the solution is an interpreter."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HSC-12",
      scaffold:"Barriers: language/jargon, sensory impairment, environmental (noise, privacy), emotional (fear, distress), cultural, cognitive (dementia, learning disability). Each barrier has matched adjustments: plain language + teach-back for jargon; hearing loops/BSL for sensory; advocacy and easy-read for cognitive. Top-band answers name barrier + specific adjustment + impact on the individual (empowerment, informed consent, reduced anxiety).",
      reforge:{stem:"The 'teach-back' method checks understanding by:",options:{A:"Testing the patient with a written quiz after the appointment.",B:"Having a relative confirm they understood on the patient's behalf.",C:"Asking the person to explain in their own words what they have understood, so gaps can be corrected immediately.",D:"Repeating the same explanation twice at a slower speed."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-13",stem:"A care assistant notices a colleague roughly handling residents and mocking them. Reporting this concern through the organisation's procedures is known as:",
      options:{A:"Grievance — a complaint about your own employment terms.",B:"Mediation — resolving the dispute informally between colleagues.",C:"Supervision — discussing practice with your line manager at a scheduled meeting.",D:"Whistleblowing — raising concerns about wrongdoing or poor practice that puts others at risk"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HSC-13",
      scaffold:"Whistleblowing: reporting concerns about danger, illegality, or poor practice affecting others — protected disclosure. Distinct from a grievance (about your own situation). Route: line manager or designated safeguarding lead first, escalating to regulator if unaddressed. Duty of care requires acting — witnessing and not reporting is itself a failure. Exam link: connect to the individual impact (abuse prevented, dignity protected) and to organisational culture.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does a duty of care mean the care assistant MUST act rather than stay silent?",options:{A:"Staying silent while aware of harm makes the observer complicit in the risk to residents — duty of care extends to acting on concerns, not just personal conduct.",B:"Because colleagues who report receive a financial reward.",C:"Because all conversations in care homes are recorded.",D:"Because the colleague would eventually report themselves."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-14",stem:"A day centre gives every attendee identical printed activity schedules. A visually impaired attendee cannot read theirs. The manager says 'we treat everyone the same here.' What principle is being misunderstood?",
      options:{A:"Confidentiality — printed activity schedules should not be shared with all attendees.",B:"Equality means equal access through different provision, not identical treatment",C:"Safeguarding — printed schedules create a data protection risk for vulnerable adults.",D:"Independence — attendees should create their own personalised activity schedules."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HSC-14",
      scaffold:"Equality vs uniformity: treating people 'the same' can produce unequal outcomes when needs differ. Reasonable adjustments give equal ACCESS. Equity: adjusting provision to need. Link to values: dignity, inclusion, anti-discriminatory practice. Strong answers use the concrete adjustment (large print, braille, audio, digital screen reader) and its impact: participation, independence, self-esteem.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which scenario shows equitable rather than merely identical provision?",options:{A:"All residents receive the same standard meal regardless of dietary needs.",B:"Every patient is allocated exactly ten minutes with the GP.",C:"All notices are displayed at the same height in the same font.",D:"A hospital provides interpreters for patients who need them while others attend appointments unsupported — different provision, equal access to care."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-15",stem:"An elderly man with capacity refuses medication that his family insists he should take. Care staff continue offering information but respect his refusal. Which two principles are being balanced?",
      options:{A:"Empowerment and autonomy (his right to decide) balanced against duty of care (staff's obligation to inform and review).",B:"Confidentiality against data protection — both legal frameworks govern care decisions.",C:"Safeguarding against whistleblowing — the two overlap in this scenario.",D:"Equality against diversity — the care home must treat all residents consistently."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HSC-15",
      scaffold:"A person with capacity has the right to refuse treatment even where refusal seems unwise — autonomy is not conditional on making the choice professionals prefer. Duty of care is met by informing, documenting, reviewing, and keeping the offer open — not by overriding. Family wishes do not override the individual's decision. Capacity is decision-specific and assumed unless assessed otherwise. Classic exam scenario: distinguish respecting refusal from neglect.",
      reforge:{stem:"If the same man were assessed as LACKING capacity for this decision, what changes?",options:{A:"Nothing — refusal is always final regardless of capacity.",B:"Decisions would be made in his best interests, involving him as far as possible, choosing the least restrictive option, and consulting those who know him.",C:"His family would gain the automatic legal right to decide everything.",D:"Staff could administer any treatment without consultation."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"HSC-16",stem:"An exam scenario describes 'Priya, a support worker, helping Tomas, who has early-stage dementia, choose his own clothes each morning even though dressing him would be quicker.' The question asks you to explain the care value shown. Which answer earns application marks?",
      options:{A:"Priya is showing a care value, which is important in health and social care settings.",B:"The care value shown is independence, which all workers must demonstrate in practice.",C:"Priya promotes Tomas's independence by supporting choice rather than doing it for him",D:"Priya is being kind, which makes Tomas happy and reflects good professional conduct."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HSC-16",
      scaffold:"Application formula: name the value → show it in THIS scenario (use the names, quote the action) → explain the impact on THIS individual (link to PIES or condition). Generic answers ('this is important in care') score AO1 only. The dementia link matters: retaining skills and autonomy slows learned helplessness and supports identity. This structure applies to every scenario question in the paper.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does the phrase 'even though dressing him would be quicker' matter for the answer?",options:{A:"It shows Priya is inefficient and needs more training.",B:"It suggests the care home is understaffed.",C:"It indicates Tomas is being deliberately slow.",D:"It signals the deliberate trade-off: Priya prioritises Tomas's independence over task-efficiency, which is exactly what person-centred practice requires — naming this trade-off demonstrates real understanding."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== FRENCH BANK 2 — Pronouns, Negatives & Complex Structures =====
BANKS["FR-2"] = {
  label: "Pronouns, Negatives & Complex Structures",
  color: "#1d4ed8",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"FR-09",stem:"Complete: 'Tu vas souvent en France?' — 'Oui, j'___ vais chaque été.'",
      options:{A:"y — replacing 'en France'; y stands for a place or à + thing.",B:"en — replacing 'en France' because it begins with en.",C:"le — replacing the whole idea of going.",D:"lui — referring back to France."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FR-09",
      scaffold:"y replaces à/en/dans + place or à + thing ('j'y pense'). en replaces de + noun, or a quantity ('j'en ai trois'). The preposition 'en' in 'en France' does NOT mean the pronoun must be 'en' — the pronoun follows the FUNCTION (place → y). Common error: matching pronouns to surface words rather than grammatical role. Position: before the conjugated verb.",
      reforge:{stem:"Complete: 'Tu as acheté des pommes?' — 'Oui, j'___ ai acheté trois.'",options:{A:"y",B:"les",C:"en — replacing 'des pommes'; en is required with quantities and de + noun.",D:"leur"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-10",stem:"A student writes: 'Je le téléphone tous les soirs' (I phone him every evening). What is the error?",
      options:{A:"'Téléphone' requires the subjunctive after a pronoun.",B:"'Le' should be placed after the verb: 'je téléphone le'.",C:"'Tous les soirs' cannot follow a pronoun construction.",D:"Téléphoner takes an indirect object (téléphoner À quelqu'un), so the pronoun must be 'lui': 'Je lui téléphone.'"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FR-10",
      scaffold:"Direct object pronouns (le, la, les) replace nouns with no preposition. Indirect object pronouns (lui, leur) replace à + person. Verbs taking à + person: téléphoner à, parler à, répondre à, demander à, dire à, donner à, offrir à. Trap: English 'phone him' feels direct, but French requires the indirect construction. Same for 'répondre' — je lui réponds, never je le réponds.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which sentence is correct?",options:{A:"Je la parle tous les jours.",B:"Je lui parle tous les jours — parler à quelqu'un takes an indirect object pronoun.",C:"Je les parle souvent.",D:"Je le parle quand il arrive."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-11",stem:"Put the pronouns in the correct order: 'He gives it (the book) to me.'",
      options:{A:"Il le me donne — incorrect: third-person pronoun placed before first-person.",B:"Il me donne le — incorrect: 'le' placed after the verb instead of before it.",C:"Il me le donne — first/second-person pronouns (me, te, nous, vous) always precede third-person ones (le, la, les).",D:"Il donne me le — both pronouns incorrectly placed after the verb."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FR-11",
      scaffold:"Double pronoun order before the verb: (1) me/te/se/nous/vous, (2) le/la/les, (3) lui/leur, (4) y, (5) en. So: 'il me le donne', but 'il le lui donne' (le before lui — third person direct precedes third person indirect). Exception order in positive commands: 'donne-le-moi'. This ordering is mechanical — learn the column sequence and it never fails.",
      reforge:{stem:"'She gives it (the letter) to them.' Which is correct?",options:{A:"Elle leur la donne.",B:"Elle la donne leur.",C:"Elle leur donne la.",D:"Elle la leur donne — with two third-person pronouns, direct (la) precedes indirect (leur)."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-12",stem:"Translate precisely: 'Elle ne boit que du café.'",
      options:{A:"She only drinks coffee — ne...que expresses restriction ('only'), not negation.",B:"She never drinks coffee.",C:"She doesn't drink coffee.",D:"She no longer drinks coffee."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FR-12",
      scaffold:"Negative and restrictive forms: ne...pas (not), ne...jamais (never), ne...plus (no longer), ne...rien (nothing), ne...personne (nobody), ne...que (only — restrictive, not truly negative: the article stays 'du café', not 'de café'). Trap: reading every ne... as negation. Also note position with compound tenses: 'elle n'a rien bu' but 'elle n'a vu personne' (personne follows the participle).",
      reforge:{stem:"'Il ne travaille plus ici.' means:",options:{A:"He doesn't work here much.",B:"He no longer works here — ne...plus marks a change from a previous state.",C:"He works here more than before.",D:"He only works here."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-13",stem:"'I have been living in Lyon for three years (and still do).' A student writes: 'J'ai habité à Lyon depuis trois ans.' What is the error?",
      options:{A:"'À Lyon' should be 'en Lyon'.",B:"'Depuis' should be 'pendant' with any past action.",C:"'Trois ans' requires the plural article 'les'.",D:"An action continuing into the present uses the PRESENT tense with depuis: 'J'habite à Lyon depuis trois ans.'"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FR-13",
      scaffold:"depuis + present = action started in the past, still continuing ('j'habite ici depuis 2020'). English uses the perfect ('have lived'), which lures students into passé composé — wrong when the action continues. pendant = completed duration ('j'ai habité à Lyon pendant trois ans' — I lived there for three years, then left). il y a = ago. Examiner reports flag depuis + wrong tense every single year.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Elle attend le bus depuis vingt minutes.' The correct translation is:",options:{A:"She waited for the bus for twenty minutes.",B:"She waits for the bus every twenty minutes.",C:"She has been waiting for the bus for twenty minutes — present tense + depuis for a continuing action.",D:"She will wait twenty minutes for the bus."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-14",stem:"Which structure correctly expresses 'We have just eaten'?",
      options:{A:"Nous avons juste mangé.",B:"Nous venons de manger — venir de + infinitive expresses the recent past.",C:"Nous sommes justes de manger.",D:"Nous mangeons juste."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FR-14",
      scaffold:"venir de + infinitive = recent past ('to have just done'). In the present: 'je viens de finir' (I have just finished). In the imperfect: 'je venais de finir' (I had just finished) — never the passé composé of venir for this meaning. 'Juste' as a translation of English 'just' in this sense is an anglicism examiners penalise. Related structures: être en train de + infinitive (to be in the middle of), aller + infinitive (near future).",
      reforge:{stem:"'They had just left when I arrived.' Which is correct?",options:{A:"Ils venaient de partir quand je suis arrivé — imperfect of venir de for 'had just'.",B:"Ils sont venus de partir quand je suis arrivé.",C:"Ils viennent de partir quand j'arrivais.",D:"Ils avaient juste parti quand je suis arrivé."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-15",stem:"'After finishing his homework, he went out.' Which is the correct French structure?",
      options:{A:"Après finir ses devoirs, il est sorti.",B:"Après finissant ses devoirs, il est sorti.",C:"Après avoir fini ses devoirs, il est sorti.",D:"Après qu'il finisse ses devoirs, il est sorti."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FR-15",
      scaffold:"après + perfect infinitive: après avoir + past participle (avoir verbs), après être + past participle with agreement (être verbs): 'après être arrivée, elle...'. Contrast: avant de + infinitive ('avant de partir'). Using 'après + bare infinitive' or a gerund is a classic error. This structure is an easy way to show complex grammar in essays and translations — examiners explicitly credit it.",
      reforge:{stem:"'After arriving (f.), she called her mother.' Which is correct?",options:{A:"Après arriver, elle a appelé sa mère.",B:"Après être arrivée, elle a appelé sa mère — être verb, past participle agrees with the feminine subject.",C:"Après avoir arrivé, elle a appelé sa mère.",D:"Après arrivée, elle a appelé sa mère."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"FR-16",stem:"'She had her car repaired.' A student writes: 'Elle a réparé sa voiture.' Why is this wrong, and what is correct?",
      options:{A:"It means she repaired it herself — 'done by someone else' needs faire + infinitive.",B:"'Sa voiture' should be 'la voiture' after avoir.",C:"'Réparé' must agree: 'réparée'. The rest is correct.",D:"The sentence needs the passive: 'Sa voiture était réparée par elle.'"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FR-16",
      scaffold:"Causative faire + infinitive = to have something done / make someone do something: 'faire construire une maison', 'elle fait travailler les élèves'. The past participle 'fait' before an infinitive never agrees: 'elle les a fait réparer'. Distinguish from the passive (la voiture a été réparée) which does not say who arranged it. This structure appears in translation tasks yearly and reliably separates top-band candidates.",
      reforge:{stem:"'The teacher makes the students work.' Which is correct?",options:{A:"Le professeur travaille les élèves.",B:"Le professeur fait les élèves travailler.",C:"Le professeur fait travailler les élèves — causative faire + infinitive, with the agent following the infinitive.",D:"Le professeur est fait travailler les élèves."},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== GERMAN BANK 2 — Cases, Word Order & Complex Structures =====
BANKS["DE-2"] = {
  label: "Cases, Word Order & Complex Structures",
  color: "#b45309",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"DE-09",stem:"'Ich fahre mit meinem Bruder in die Stadt.' Why is it 'meinem Bruder' and not 'meinen Bruder'?",
      options:{A:"'Bruder' is the direct object of fahren, which takes the dative.",B:"Masculine nouns always take -em endings after possessives.",C:"'Mit' always takes the dative case, so the masculine possessive becomes 'meinem'.",D:"The sentence is about movement, and movement triggers the dative."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DE-09",
      scaffold:"Dative-only prepositions: aus, bei, mit, nach, seit, von, zu (plus gegenüber). These take the dative regardless of motion or meaning. Accusative-only: durch, für, gegen, ohne, um. Learn both lists cold — they are pure memory, no logic needed. Note 'in die Stadt' in the same sentence takes accusative because 'in' is a two-way preposition with motion (see next question). Mixing the fixed-case and two-way groups is the most common case error at A Level.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which sentence is correct?",options:{A:"Er kommt aus der Schweiz — 'aus' always takes the dative; die Schweiz becomes der Schweiz.",B:"Er kommt aus die Schweiz.",C:"Er kommt aus den Schweiz.",D:"Er kommt ausser der Schweiz."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-10",stem:"'Die Katze springt auf ___ Tisch' (the cat jumps onto the table) vs 'Die Katze schläft auf ___ Tisch' (the cat sleeps on the table). Which pair is correct?",
      options:{A:"dem / den — dative for the jump, accusative for sleeping.",B:"den / dem — accusative for motion towards (springt auf), dative for static location (schläft auf).",C:"den / den — 'auf' always takes the accusative.",D:"dem / dem — 'auf' always takes the dative."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DE-10",
      scaffold:"Two-way prepositions (Wechselpräpositionen): an, auf, hinter, in, neben, über, unter, vor, zwischen. Motion towards a destination (wohin?) → accusative. Static location (wo?) → dative. Test: can you ask 'wohin?' (where to?) — accusative. 'Wo?' (where?) — dative. Trap: motion WITHIN a place is still dative ('er läuft im Park' — he runs around in the park), because the question is 'wo läuft er?', not 'wohin?'.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Sie hängt das Bild an ___ Wand.' (She hangs the picture on the wall.) Which is correct and why?",options:{A:"der Wand — the wall is a location.",B:"die Wand — hanging involves motion towards the wall, so accusative.",C:"der Wand — 'an' always takes the dative.",D:"das Wand — neuter agreement with 'Bild'."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-11",stem:"'Der Mann, ___ ich gestern geholfen habe, ist mein Nachbar.' (The man I helped yesterday is my neighbour.) Which relative pronoun is correct?",
      options:{A:"den — the man is the object of helping.",B:"der — the man is the subject of the main clause.",C:"dem — helfen takes the dative, so the relative pronoun must be dative: dem.",D:"dessen — showing possession of the help."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DE-11",
      scaffold:"The relative pronoun takes its GENDER and NUMBER from the noun it refers to, but its CASE from its role inside the relative clause. Helfen, danken, folgen, gefallen, gehören all take the dative — so their objects in relative clauses are dem/der/dem/denen. Common error: matching the case of the main clause instead. Checklist: (1) find the antecedent's gender, (2) work out the pronoun's job in its own clause, (3) apply the case table.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Das ist die Frau, ___ Auto gestohlen wurde.' (That is the woman whose car was stolen.) Which is correct?",options:{A:"die",B:"der",C:"das",D:"deren — genitive feminine, showing possession: the woman's car."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-12",stem:"Which sentence correctly expresses the hypothetical 'If I had more time, I would learn Italian'?",
      options:{A:"Wenn ich mehr Zeit hätte, würde ich Italienisch lernen — Konjunktiv II + würde.",B:"Wenn ich mehr Zeit habe, werde ich Italienisch lernen.",C:"Wenn ich mehr Zeit hatte, lernte ich Italienisch.",D:"Wenn ich mehr Zeit haben würde, ich würde Italienisch lernen."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DE-12",
      scaffold:"Konjunktiv II for hypotheticals: haben → hätte, sein → wäre, werden → würde, können → könnte, müssen → müsste. Standard pattern: wenn + Konjunktiv II ..., würde + infinitive. Avoid doubling würde in the wenn-clause with haben/sein/modals — use hätte/wäre/könnte directly. Word order: the main clause after a wenn-clause starts with the verb ('..., würde ich...'). Past hypothetical: hätte/wäre + past participle ('wenn ich das gewusst hätte, wäre ich gekommen').",
      reforge:{stem:"'If I had known that, I would have come.' Which is correct?",options:{A:"Wenn ich das wusste, kam ich.",B:"Wenn ich das gewusst hätte, wäre ich gekommen — past hypothetical: hätte + participle, then wäre + participle for a sein-verb.",C:"Wenn ich das gewusst habe, bin ich gekommen.",D:"Wenn ich das wissen würde, würde ich kommen."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-13",stem:"'Die Brücke wird gebaut' vs 'Die Brücke ist gebaut'. What is the difference in meaning?",
      options:{A:"No difference — werden and sein are interchangeable in the passive.",B:"The first is future tense; the second is present tense.",C:"The first is formal register; the second is colloquial.",D:"Werden-passive shows the ongoing process; sein-passive shows the resulting state."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DE-13",
      scaffold:"Vorgangspassiv (werden + participle): the action/process — 'das Haus wird renoviert' (is being renovated). Zustandspassiv (sein + participle): the completed state — 'das Haus ist renoviert' (is renovated, done). Past process: wurde gebaut. Perfect process: ist gebaut WORDEN (note: worden, not geworden, in the passive perfect). Agent introduced with von (person) or durch (means). Examiners consistently flag 'ist ... geworden' errors in passive constructions.",
      reforge:{stem:"'The house was built in 1900.' (completed past action) Which is correct?",options:{A:"Das Haus wurde 1900 gebaut — simple past passive with wurde for a completed historical action.",B:"Das Haus ist 1900 gebaut geworden.",C:"Das Haus war 1900 bauen.",D:"Das Haus hat 1900 gebaut."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-14",stem:"'I have been living in Berlin for five years (and still do).' A student writes: 'Ich habe seit fünf Jahren in Berlin gewohnt.' What is the error?",
      options:{A:"'In Berlin' should come before 'seit fünf Jahren'.",B:"An action continuing into the present takes the PRESENT tense with seit: 'Ich wohne seit fünf Jahren in Berlin.'",C:"'Gewohnt' should be 'gelebt' with seit.",D:"'Seit' should be 'für' when translating English for."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DE-14",
      scaffold:"seit + present tense = action began in the past and continues now (exactly like French depuis). English 'have been living' lures students into the perfect tense — wrong when the action continues. seit takes the dative (seit einem Jahr, seit fünf Jahren — note dative plural -n). 'Für' for durations is an anglicism here. Completed past duration uses lang: 'Ich habe fünf Jahre lang in Berlin gewohnt' (I lived there for five years, then left).",
      reforge:{stem:"'Er lernt seit sechs Monaten Deutsch.' The correct translation is:",options:{A:"He learned German for six months.",B:"He learns German every six months.",C:"He has been learning German for six months — present + seit for a continuing action.",D:"He will learn German in six months."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-15",stem:"Which sentence correctly expresses 'She works hard IN ORDER TO pass the exam'?",
      options:{A:"Sie arbeitet hart, damit sie die Prüfung besteht.",B:"Sie arbeitet hart, um die Prüfung bestehen.",C:"Sie arbeitet hart, um die Prüfung zu bestehen — um...zu + infinitive when both clauses share the same subject.",D:"Sie arbeitet hart, zu bestehen die Prüfung."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DE-15",
      scaffold:"Same subject in both halves → um ... zu + infinitive (zu before the infinitive at the end). Different subjects → damit + full clause with verb at the end ('Ich erkläre es langsam, damit alle es verstehen'). 'Damit sie...' with the same subject is grammatically possible but um...zu is preferred and rewarded. With separable verbs, zu goes inside: 'um früh aufzustehen'. Related infinitive structures: ohne ... zu (without doing), anstatt ... zu (instead of doing).",
      reforge:{stem:"'I explain it slowly SO THAT the students understand it.' (different subjects) Which is correct?",options:{A:"Ich erkläre es langsam, damit die Schüler es verstehen — damit + clause because the subjects differ.",B:"Ich erkläre es langsam, um die Schüler es zu verstehen.",C:"Ich erkläre es langsam, um zu verstehen die Schüler.",D:"Ich erkläre es langsam, dass die Schüler verstehen es."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"DE-16",stem:"'Ich fahre am Montag mit dem Zug nach München.' What ordering rule do the adverbials follow?",
      options:{A:"Alphabetical order of the first noun in each phrase.",B:"Time — Manner — Place: am Montag (when), mit dem Zug (how), nach München (where to).",C:"Place — Manner — Time, mirroring English word order.",D:"Shortest phrase first, longest phrase last."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DE-16",
      scaffold:"German adverbial order: Time, Manner, Place (TMP) — the reverse of typical English (place before time: 'to Munich by train on Monday'). Direct translation from English word order is the giveaway error in essays and translations. Emphasis can move ONE element to position 1 ('Am Montag fahre ich...'), which then inverts subject and verb — a cheap way to demonstrate word-order control that examiners credit. Everything else stays TMP after the verb.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which sentence has correct word order?",options:{A:"Ich gehe ins Kino heute Abend mit meinen Freunden.",B:"Ich gehe mit meinen Freunden ins Kino heute Abend.",C:"Ich gehe heute Abend ins Kino mit meinen Freunden.",D:"Ich gehe heute Abend mit meinen Freunden ins Kino — Time (heute Abend), Manner (mit meinen Freunden), Place (ins Kino)."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== MATHS BANK 2 — Pure: Calculus, Graphs & Algebra Traps =====
BANKS["MATH-2"] = {
  label: "Pure — Calculus, Graphs & Algebra Traps",
  color: "#7c3aed",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MATH-09",stem:"A student finds dy/dx = 0 at x = 2 and concludes x = 2 is a minimum because the function 'was decreasing before it'. What is the rigorous way to classify a stationary point?",
      options:{A:"Use the second derivative test: positive means minimum, negative means maximum.",B:"A stationary point is always a minimum if x is positive.",C:"Substitute x = 2 into the original function — a positive y-value means minimum.",D:"All stationary points on a cubic are minima."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MATH-09",
      scaffold:"Second derivative test: f''(x) > 0 → local minimum (curve concave up); f''(x) < 0 → local maximum. f''(x) = 0 → INCONCLUSIVE (could be max, min, or point of inflection — e.g. y = x⁴ has f''(0) = 0 but a minimum at 0). Then use the gradient table: sign of f'(x) just before and after. Common mark loss: asserting 'f''=0 means point of inflection' — false without checking.",
      reforge:{stem:"For y = x⁴, we have dy/dx = 4x³ = 0 at x = 0, and d²y/dx² = 12x² = 0 at x = 0. What is the nature of the stationary point?",options:{A:"A point of inflection, because the second derivative is zero.",B:"A minimum — the second derivative test is inconclusive, but the gradient is negative before 0 and positive after, so it is a minimum.",C:"A maximum, because x⁴ grows very fast.",D:"Cannot be determined."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-10",stem:"A student writes: ∫(3x² + 2) dx = x³ + 2x. What mark would this typically lose, and why?",
      options:{A:"None — the answer is fully correct.",B:"All marks — the integration itself is wrong.",C:"The final accuracy mark — the '+ C' constant of integration is missing.",D:"The method mark — integration should start by differentiating."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MATH-10",
      scaffold:"Indefinite integrals always need + C: differentiating any constant gives zero, so infinitely many functions share the same derivative. + C matters materially in differential equations, where a boundary condition determines C. Definite integrals need NO constant (it cancels in F(b) − F(a)). Examiner reports list omitted + C among the most frequent avoidable losses at A Level.",
      reforge:{stem:"A curve has dy/dx = 6x and passes through (1, 5). Find y.",options:{A:"y = 3x² — integrate and stop.",B:"y = 6x² + C for any C.",C:"y = 3x² + 5.",D:"y = 3x² + 2 — integrate to get 3x² + C, then substitute (1, 5): 5 = 3 + C, so C = 2."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-11",stem:"For the quadratic 3x² − 5x + k = 0 to have two distinct real roots, what condition must k satisfy?",
      options:{A:"k > 25/12 — the discriminant must be negative.",B:"k < 25/12 — the discriminant b² − 4ac = 25 − 12k must be strictly positive.",C:"k = 25/12 exactly.",D:"k can be any real number; all quadratics have two roots."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MATH-11",
      scaffold:"Discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac. Two distinct real roots: Δ > 0. Equal (repeated) roots: Δ = 0. No real roots: Δ < 0. Here: 25 − 12k > 0 → k < 25/12. Traps: (1) sign errors when b is negative — b² is always positive; (2) mixing up strict vs non-strict inequalities — 'real roots' (allowing equal) means Δ ≥ 0, 'distinct' means Δ > 0; (3) forgetting that 'no real roots' still means two complex roots.",
      reforge:{stem:"The line y = 2x + c is tangent to the curve y = x². What does tangency tell us about the discriminant of x² − 2x − c = 0?",options:{A:"It equals zero — tangency means exactly one repeated intersection point, so 4 + 4c = 0 and c = −1.",B:"It is positive — the line crosses twice.",C:"It is negative — the line never meets the curve.",D:"The discriminant is irrelevant to tangency."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-12",stem:"A student sketches y = f(x + 3) by shifting y = f(x) three units to the RIGHT. What has gone wrong?",
      options:{A:"Nothing — f(x + 3) shifts right by 3.",B:"The shift should be vertical, not horizontal.",C:"f(x + 3) shifts LEFT by 3: the input reaches any given value 3 units earlier. Right shifts come from f(x − 3).",D:"f(x + 3) stretches the graph horizontally by factor 3."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MATH-12",
      scaffold:"Transformations inside the bracket act on x and go the 'wrong way': f(x + a) → left a; f(x − a) → right a; f(ax) → horizontal stretch factor 1/a. Outside the bracket acts on y, intuitively: f(x) + a → up a; af(x) → vertical stretch factor a. Combined transformations: apply inside-the-bracket changes to x-coordinates and outside changes to y-coordinates independently. This 'inside is counterintuitive' rule is one of the most-tested sketch skills.",
      reforge:{stem:"The graph of y = f(2x) compared with y = f(x) is:",options:{A:"Stretched horizontally by factor 2 — everything is twice as wide.",B:"Compressed horizontally by factor 1/2 — the input doubles, so features occur at half the x-value.",C:"Stretched vertically by factor 2.",D:"Reflected in the y-axis."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-13",stem:"Complete the square for y = x² − 6x + 11 and state the turning point.",
      options:{A:"y = (x − 3)² + 2, turning point (3, 2).",B:"y = (x − 6)² + 11, turning point (6, 11).",C:"y = (x + 3)² + 2, turning point (−3, 2).",D:"y = (x − 3)² + 11, turning point (3, 11)."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MATH-13",
      scaffold:"y = x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c. Here: (x − 3)² − 9 + 11 = (x − 3)² + 2. Turning point: (−b/2, c − b²/4) — for the form (x − p)² + q it is (p, q), minimum since the coefficient of x² is positive. Sign trap: (x − 3)² gives turning point at x = +3, not −3. When a ≠ 1, factor out a from the x-terms first. Completing the square also proves the quadratic formula and identifies max/min without calculus.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is the minimum VALUE of y = x² − 6x + 11 (not the x where it occurs)?",options:{A:"3 — the x-coordinate of the vertex.",B:"11 — the constant term.",C:"2 — the completed-square form (x − 3)² + 2 shows the squared term is at least 0, so y is at least 2.",D:"−9 — the subtracted square."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-14",stem:"A student expands (2 + x)⁵ and writes the x³ term as ⁵C₃ x³ = 10x³. What has been forgotten?",
      options:{A:"Nothing — 10x³ is the correct term.",B:"The remaining power of the FIRST term: the x³ term is ⁵C₃ · 2² · x³ = 10 · 4 · x³ = 40x³.",C:"The binomial coefficient should be ⁵C₂.",D:"Powers of x cannot exceed 2 in this expansion."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MATH-14",
      scaffold:"(a + b)ⁿ general term: ⁿCᵣ aⁿ⁻ʳ bʳ — BOTH factors carry powers. Dropping aⁿ⁻ʳ when a ≠ 1 is the classic slip: here a = 2 contributes 2² = 4. Second trap: (2 − x)⁵ — the sign belongs to b = −x, so odd powers of x are negative. Third: 'coefficient of x³' means the number only (40), while 'term in x³' means 40x³ — read the question. Check: coefficients of (2+x)⁵ should sum to 3⁵ = 243 when x = 1.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the expansion of (3 − 2x)⁴, the coefficient of x² is:",options:{A:"⁴C₂ · 3² · (−2)² = 6 · 9 · 4 = 216 — both remaining powers included, and (−2)² is positive.",B:"⁴C₂ = 6 — just the binomial coefficient.",C:"−216 — the negative sign carries through.",D:"54 — using 3² but forgetting (−2)²."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-15",stem:"A student simplifies log(x + y) to log x + log y. Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"It is correct — logs turn addition into addition.",B:"The correct rule is log(x + y) = log x · log y.",C:"There is no rule for log(x + y) — only for products.",D:"The rule only fails when x and y are negative."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MATH-15",
      scaffold:"Log laws: log a + log b = log(ab); log a − log b = log(a/b); log aⁿ = n log a. There is no simplification for log(a + b) or log(a − b) — full stop. Related invented rules to unlearn: log(a)/log(b) ≠ log(a/b) (the left side is change-of-base material), and (log a)ⁿ ≠ log(aⁿ). Domain trap: solutions of log equations must keep every argument positive — always check candidates against the original equation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Solve log₂(x) + log₂(x − 2) = 3.",options:{A:"x = 4 only — combine to log₂(x(x−2)) = 3, so x² − 2x = 8, giving x = 4 or x = −2, but x = −2 makes both arguments negative and must be rejected.",B:"x = 4 or x = −2 — both roots of the quadratic.",C:"x = −2 only.",D:"x = 8 — since 2³ = 8."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"MATH-16",stem:"Differentiate y = (3x² + 1)/x with respect to x. A student answers dy/dx = 6x/1 = 6x, 'differentiating top and bottom separately'. What is the correct approach at AS level?",
      options:{A:"Differentiate numerator and denominator separately — the quotient rule is applied term by term.",B:"Split the fraction first: y = 3x + x⁻¹, so dy/dx = 3 − x⁻² — the fraction must be resolved algebraically before differentiating.",C:"Multiply numerator and denominator by x, then differentiate the resulting expression.",D:"dy/dx = 6x · x = 6x² — the product rule has been applied incorrectly."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MATH-16",
      scaffold:"Differentiation is term-by-term ONLY for sums and differences — never across a quotient or product. At AS: rewrite first — split fractions (each numerator term over the denominator), convert roots and reciprocals to index form (√x = x^½, 1/x² = x⁻²), THEN apply the power rule. 'Differentiate top and bottom' is a persistent invented rule. The quotient rule arrives in Year 2, but splitting remains faster whenever it is possible.",
      reforge:{stem:"Differentiate y = (x³ + 4√x)/x².",options:{A:"dy/dx = 3x²/2x — top over bottom derivatives.",B:"dy/dx = x + 4x^(−3/2).",C:"dy/dx = 1 − 6x^(−5/2) — split into x + 4x^(−3/2) first, then differentiate term by term.",D:"dy/dx = 3x² + 2x^(−1/2)."},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== BUSINESS BANK 2 — Finance & Marketing Decisions =====
BANKS["BUS-2"] = {
  label: "Finance & Marketing Decisions",
  color: "#0e7490",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"BUS-09",stem:"A firm raises its price by 10% and revenue FALLS. What does this indicate about price elasticity of demand for its product?",
      options:{A:"Demand is price inelastic — the quantity fall was smaller than the price rise.",B:"Demand is price elastic (|PED| > 1) — the quantity fall exceeded the price rise, so total revenue fell.",C:"Demand is perfectly inelastic — quantity did not respond at all to the price change.",D:"PED equals exactly 1 — revenue is always unaffected by price changes at unit elasticity."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BUS-09",
      scaffold:"Revenue test: price ↑ + revenue ↑ → inelastic. Price ↑ + revenue ↓ → elastic. Price ↑ + revenue unchanged → unit elastic. Determinants of PED: availability of substitutes (biggest), necessity vs luxury, brand loyalty, proportion of income, time horizon. Strategy link: inelastic demand supports premium pricing; elastic demand rewards competitive pricing and differentiation to REDUCE elasticity. Numerical answers should quote %ΔQd / %ΔP.",
      reforge:{stem:"A business wants to make demand for its product LESS price elastic over time. Which action most directly achieves this?",options:{A:"Cutting price below all competitors.",B:"Increasing output capacity.",C:"Building brand loyalty and differentiation so customers see fewer acceptable substitutes.",D:"Switching to cheaper raw materials."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-10",stem:"A profitable business fails because it cannot pay suppliers on time. What concept explains how a business can be profitable yet fail?",
      options:{A:"Profit measures success over time — large firms report it every quarter.",B:"The business must have been miscounting revenue as profit.",C:"Diseconomies of scale eventually erode profitable businesses.",D:"Cash flow and profit differ: cash pays bills, profit does not — a firm can be profitable yet insolvent."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-BUS-10",
      scaffold:"Profit = revenue − costs over a period (accruals basis). Cash flow = timing of money actually moving. Causes of profitable-but-cash-poor: long debtor collection periods, overtrading (growing too fast), stockpiling, seasonal gaps, capital expenditure. Remedies: negotiate longer payables/shorter receivables, debt factoring, overdraft, JIT stock. Exam mantra: 'cash pays the bills, profit does not' — more businesses fail from cash-flow problems than from lack of profitability.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which action improves a firm's short-term cash position WITHOUT necessarily changing its profit?",options:{A:"Raising selling prices across the range.",B:"Negotiating 60-day payment terms with suppliers instead of 30 — cash leaves later, though total costs are unchanged.",C:"Cutting the wage bill by making staff redundant.",D:"Increasing advertising to boost sales."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-11",stem:"A firm's break-even output is 8,000 units and it currently sells 10,000. Its margin of safety is:",
      options:{A:"2,000 units — current output minus break-even output",B:"8,000 units — the break-even level itself.",C:"18,000 units — the two figures added together.",D:"25% of fixed costs."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BUS-11",
      scaffold:"Margin of safety = actual (or budgeted) output − break-even output. Here 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000 units, or 20% of current sales. Interpretation: the cushion against demand shocks — a small margin signals vulnerability. Linked calculations: break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit; contribution = price − variable cost per unit. Evaluation angle: break-even analysis assumes constant price and costs and that everything produced is sold — limitations worth one evaluative paragraph.",
      reforge:{stem:"If fixed costs RISE and everything else stays the same, what happens to break-even output and the margin of safety?",options:{A:"Break-even falls; margin of safety rises.",B:"Both stay the same — fixed costs do not affect break-even.",C:"Break-even output rises (more units needed to cover higher fixed costs), so the margin of safety shrinks.",D:"Break-even rises; margin of safety also rises."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-12",stem:"Why do businesses segment their markets rather than treating all customers identically?",
      options:{A:"Segmentation is a legal requirement for all consumer-facing firms under UK marketing law.",B:"Segmentation lets firms target distinct groups with a tailored mix",C:"Segmentation is designed to guarantee that every possible customer purchases the product.",D:"Segmentation reduces the total size of the addressable market to a manageable level."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BUS-12",
      scaffold:"Segmentation bases: demographic (age, income), geographic, psychographic (lifestyle, values), behavioural (usage, loyalty). Benefits: targeted marketing mix, higher conversion per pound spent, niche opportunities, price discrimination between segments. Risks: segments too small to be profitable, higher costs from multiple mixes, cannibalisation. Link chain for analysis marks: segment identified → mix tailored → customer need met better → sales/margin impact.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rail company charges more at 8am than at 11am for the same journey. Which segmentation base and pricing logic is this?",options:{A:"Geographic segmentation with cost-plus pricing.",B:"Demographic segmentation with penetration pricing.",C:"Psychographic segmentation with skimming.",D:"Behavioural segmentation (commuters vs leisure travellers) with price discrimination — commuters have inelastic demand and pay more."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-13",stem:"In the Boston Matrix, a product with HIGH market share in a LOW-growth market is classified as a:",
      options:{A:"Cash cow — it generates strong, steady cash with little investment need",B:"Star — high share always means star status.",C:"Question mark (problem child) — its future is uncertain.",D:"Dog — low growth makes any product a dog."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BUS-13",
      scaffold:"Boston Matrix axes: market GROWTH (not product growth) and RELATIVE market share. Star: high share/high growth — invest to hold. Cash cow: high share/low growth — milk it. Question mark: low share/high growth — invest selectively or divest. Dog: low share/low growth — divest or harvest. Classic error: confusing share with growth, and assuming dogs must always be dropped (they may support the range or retain loyal customers). Portfolio logic: cows fund question marks to become stars.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a business rationally KEEP a product classified as a dog?",options:{A:"Dogs always turn into stars eventually.",B:"It may complete a product range, share fixed costs, or serve loyal customers whose other purchases are profitable — classification alone does not decide strategy.",C:"The Boston Matrix legally prevents divestment.",D:"Dogs generate more cash than cash cows."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-14",stem:"A firm has revenue of £500,000, cost of sales £300,000, and other operating expenses £120,000. Its gross profit margin and operating profit margin are:",
      options:{A:"Gross 60%, operating 24% — calculated by dividing each by total costs.",B:"Gross 16%, operating 40% — operating profit is always higher than gross profit.",C:"Gross 40% (200k ÷ 500k), operating 16% (80k ÷ 500k)",D:"Both 40% — gross and operating margins are always identical by accounting definition."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BUS-14",
      scaffold:"Gross profit = revenue − cost of sales = 200,000; GPM = 40%. Operating profit = gross profit − operating expenses = 80,000; OPM = 16%. The GAP between the two margins reveals overhead weight. Improving GPM: raise prices, cut direct costs, change supplier. Improving OPM: control overheads. Interpretation needs comparison — against last year, competitors, or industry norms; a 'good' margin is context-dependent (supermarkets thin, software fat).",
      reforge:{stem:"A firm's gross margin is stable but its operating margin has fallen sharply over two years. The most likely cause is:",options:{A:"Rising raw material costs.",B:"Falling selling prices.",C:"Cheaper suppliers being used.",D:"Rising overheads such as rent, salaries or marketing — costs below gross profit but above operating profit."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-15",stem:"An established profitable business needs finance for a major expansion. Compared with a bank loan, funding it from retained profit has which key advantage and drawback?",
      options:{A:"Retained profit: no interest or repayment and no dilution, but it depletes reserves and is limited to what has been accumulated.",B:"Retained profit: unlimited funds available with no ownership risk; drawback is the high interest rate charged.",C:"Retained profit: improves gearing to 100% and shareholders must approve the use.",D:"Retained profit: tax deductible and requires collateral from the business assets."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BUS-15",
      scaffold:"Retained profit: internal, free of interest, no dilution — but finite, may disappoint shareholders expecting dividends, and depletes reserves. Loan: fast, preserves ownership, interest is tax-deductible — but repayments strain cash flow, may need security, raises gearing. Share issue: large sums, no repayment — but dilutes control, dividend expectations. Match source to context: amount needed, urgency, risk appetite, current gearing. Evaluation: 'it depends on' the firm's existing gearing and cash position.",
      reforge:{stem:"A highly geared firm (existing debt is large relative to equity) needs expansion finance. Why might further loans be unwise?",options:{A:"Loans are illegal above 50% gearing.",B:"Interest payments would fall as gearing rises.",C:"Additional debt raises fixed interest commitments and insolvency risk if revenues dip, and lenders will charge more or refuse — equity or retained profit spreads the risk better.",D:"Banks only lend to new businesses."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-16",stem:"Sales of a mature product have begun to decline. The marketing team proposes 'extension strategies'. Which of the following is an extension strategy, and what is its purpose?",
      options:{A:"Deleting the product immediately to free up resources for new development.",B:"Repositioning for a new segment, updating packaging, or finding new uses",C:"Doubling the price to maximise revenue from the remaining loyal customers.",D:"Halting all promotion since the product is already established in the market."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BUS-16",
      scaffold:"Product life cycle: development → introduction → growth → maturity → decline. Extension strategies at maturity/early decline: new segments or markets, product modification, new uses, repackaging, price adjustment, promotion refresh. Evaluation: extension spends money on a possibly doomed product — the alternative is investing in new products (link to Boston Matrix portfolio decisions). Cash flow varies by stage: negative in development/introduction, strongest in maturity.",
      reforge:{stem:"At which life-cycle stage is a product's CASH FLOW typically most negative, and why?",options:{A:"Maturity — competition is most intense.",B:"Growth — sales are rising too fast.",C:"Decline — sales are falling.",D:"Development — costs are incurred (research, design, testing) while the product earns no revenue at all."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== CHEMISTRY BANK 2 — Bonding, Energetics & Equilibria =====
BANKS["CHEM-2"] = {
  label: "Bonding, Energetics & Equilibria",
  color: "#0891b2",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CHEM-09",stem:"A student says water has a high boiling point 'because the O–H covalent bonds are very strong'. What is the correct explanation?",
      options:{A:"The high boiling point is due to hydrogen bonding between molecules",B:"The O–H bonds break during boiling, requiring large energy.",C:"Water molecules are unusually heavy for their size.",D:"Oxygen's high electronegativity makes the whole molecule ionic."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CHEM-09",
      scaffold:"Boiling breaks INTERmolecular forces, never the covalent bonds within molecules. Water's high bp comes from hydrogen bonding (H bonded to O, plus a lone pair on O). Order of strength: covalent/ionic bonds >> hydrogen bonds > permanent dipole-dipole > London (induced dipole) forces. Classic error: attributing physical properties (bp, mp) to bond breaking rather than to overcoming intermolecular forces. Only in giant covalent structures (diamond, SiO2) does melting break covalent bonds.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does HF have a higher boiling point than HCl, despite HCl having more electrons?",options:{A:"HCl molecules are smaller and pack more loosely.",B:"HF forms hydrogen bonds (F is highly electronegative with lone pairs), which are stronger than the permanent dipole-dipole forces in HCl.",C:"HCl is a giant covalent structure.",D:"Chlorine is less reactive than fluorine."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-10",stem:"An exothermic reaction has which sign for ΔH, and what happens to the energy of the surroundings?",
      options:{A:"ΔH is positive; the surroundings cool down.",B:"ΔH is zero; energy is conserved so nothing changes.",C:"ΔH is positive; the surroundings heat up.",D:"ΔH is negative; energy is released to the surroundings, which heat up."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CHEM-10",
      scaffold:"Exothermic: energy released to surroundings, ΔH negative, surroundings warm, products lower in energy than reactants. Endothermic: energy absorbed, ΔH positive, surroundings cool. The sign refers to the SYSTEM. Common error: confusing the system and surroundings, or thinking 'releases energy' means positive ΔH. Bond breaking is endothermic (needs energy); bond making is exothermic (releases energy). ΔH = energy to break bonds − energy released making bonds.",
      reforge:{stem:"Using bond enthalpies, a reaction is found to be exothermic. This means:",options:{A:"More energy was released making new bonds than was needed to break the original bonds.",B:"More energy was needed to break bonds than was released making them.",C:"No bonds were broken during the reaction.",D:"The activation energy was zero."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-11",stem:"For the equilibrium N2(g) + 3H2(g) ⇌ 2NH3(g) (forward reaction exothermic), what does INCREASING the temperature do to the yield of ammonia?",
      options:{A:"Increases yield — higher temperature always speeds up product formation.",B:"Decreases yield — Le Chatelier's principle: the system shifts in the endothermic (reverse)",C:"No effect — temperature does not shift equilibria.",D:"Doubles the yield for every 10°C rise."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CHEM-11",
      scaffold:"Le Chatelier: a system at equilibrium shifts to oppose an imposed change. For an exothermic forward reaction, raising T favours the endothermic reverse → less product, lower Kc. Raising pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles (here, products, 2 vs 4). The Haber compromise: moderate T (~450°C) balances yield (favoured by low T) against rate (favoured by high T). Key distinction: a catalyst speeds up equilibrium but does NOT change the position or yield.",
      reforge:{stem:"Adding a catalyst to the Haber process equilibrium does what to the yield of ammonia?",options:{A:"Increases the yield by shifting equilibrium right.",B:"Decreases the yield by favouring the reverse reaction.",C:"Leaves the yield unchanged — a catalyst speeds both directions equally, reaching the same equilibrium faster.",D:"Removes the need for high pressure."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-12",stem:"A student writes the electron configuration of a Ca2+ ion as 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2. What is the error?",
      options:{A:"Calcium cannot form a 2+ ion.",B:"The 3p subshell should hold 10 electrons.",C:"The 4s2 should be removed: Ca2+ has lost its two outer 4s electrons",D:"The configuration should start at 2s, not 1s."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CHEM-12",
      scaffold:"Ca (Z=20): 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2. Forming Ca2+ removes the two highest-energy electrons — the 4s electrons — giving the argon configuration. Key rule: 4s FILLS before 3d but EMPTIES first when ionising. For transition metals: Fe is [Ar]3d6 4s2, but Fe2+ is [Ar]3d6 (4s lost first), Fe3+ is [Ar]3d5. Common error: removing 3d or 3p electrons instead of the outer 4s. The stable half-filled 3d5 explains Fe3+'s stability.",
      reforge:{stem:"Iron is [Ar]3d6 4s2. What is the electron configuration of Fe3+?",options:{A:"[Ar]3d3 4s2 — three 3d electrons removed.",B:"[Ar]3d5 — the two 4s electrons are removed first, then one 3d, leaving a stable half-filled 3d5.",C:"[Ar]3d6 4s2 — ions keep the same configuration.",D:"[Ar]3d4 4s1."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-13",stem:"In terms of collision theory, why does increasing concentration increase the rate of reaction?",
      options:{A:"It increases the average kinetic energy of the particles, raising the activation energy.",B:"It lowers the activation energy so fewer collisions are needed to react.",C:"More particles per unit volume means more frequent collisions",D:"It changes the reaction mechanism from endothermic to exothermic."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CHEM-13",
      scaffold:"Rate depends on the frequency of SUCCESSFUL collisions (correct orientation AND energy ≥ activation energy). Concentration/pressure: more particles per volume → more frequent collisions. Temperature: increases both frequency AND (crucially) the proportion of particles with E ≥ Ea — the dominant effect. Catalyst: provides an alternative pathway with LOWER Ea, so a greater proportion of collisions succeed. Only temperature and catalysts change the proportion of successful collisions; concentration changes only frequency.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does a small temperature rise increase rate far more than the same proportional rise in concentration?",options:{A:"Temperature increases the frequency of collisions AND greatly increases the proportion of particles exceeding the activation energy — the latter effect dominates.",B:"Temperature lowers the activation energy directly.",C:"Concentration has no effect on rate at all.",D:"Higher temperature makes all collisions successful."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-14",stem:"A student describes a dative (coordinate) covalent bond as 'weaker than a normal covalent bond because only one atom provides the electrons'. Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Dative bonds are ionic in nature — the electron donor becomes positively charged.",B:"Once formed, a dative bond is identical to any other covalent bond",C:"Dative bonds share three electrons rather than two, making them stronger.",D:"Only metal atoms can act as electron donors in dative bonding."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CHEM-14",
      scaffold:"A dative/coordinate bond differs only in ORIGIN: both shared electrons come from one atom (the donor, with a lone pair) rather than one from each. Once formed it is indistinguishable from and equal in strength to a normal covalent bond. Examples: NH4+ (N donates to H+), H3O+, and ligand-metal bonds in complex ions. Shown by an arrow → from donor to acceptor. Common error: treating the bond as weaker or as a different bond type after formation.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the ammonium ion NH4+, all four N–H bonds are found to be identical in length and strength. Why?",options:{A:"The dative bond is longer than the other three.",B:"The extra proton makes one bond ionic.",C:"Nitrogen only bonds to three hydrogens; the fourth is separate.",D:"Once formed, the dative bond is indistinguishable from the other three covalent N–H bonds — all four are equivalent."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-15",stem:"25.0 cm3 of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH is neutralised by HCl. How many moles of NaOH are present?",
      options:{A:"0.0025 mol — moles = concentration × volume in dm3 = 0.100 × 0.0250.",B:"2.5 mol — 0.100 × 25.",C:"0.025 mol — 0.100 × 25 ÷ 100.",D:"0.100 mol — the concentration is the number of moles."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CHEM-15",
      scaffold:"n = c × V where V MUST be in dm3 (litres): 25.0 cm3 = 0.0250 dm3. So n = 0.100 × 0.0250 = 0.00250 mol. The single most common titration error is forgetting to convert cm3 to dm3 (divide by 1000), giving an answer 1000× too large. In titration calculations: (1) moles of known, (2) use the balanced equation ratio, (3) moles of unknown, (4) divide by its volume for concentration. Check units at every step.",
      reforge:{stem:"The 0.00250 mol of NaOH reacts with HCl in a 1:1 ratio and required 20.0 cm3 of HCl. What is the concentration of the HCl?",options:{A:"0.125 mol dm⁻³ — n(HCl) = 0.00250, then c = n ÷ V = 0.00250 ÷ 0.0200.",B:"0.00250 mol dm⁻³.",C:"0.05 mol dm⁻³.",D:"2.50 mol dm⁻³."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-16",stem:"Why does electronegativity INCREASE across a period (left to right)?",
      options:{A:"Atoms get larger across a period, attracting bonding electrons more strongly.",B:"The number of electron shells increases across a period, reducing shielding.",C:"Nuclear charge rises across a period while shielding stays similar",D:"Metallic character increases across a period, making atoms more electronegative."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CHEM-16",
      scaffold:"Electronegativity trends depend on: nuclear charge, atomic radius, and shielding. Across a period: proton number rises, shielding roughly constant (same shell), radius decreases → stronger attraction for bonding pair → electronegativity increases. Down a group: more shells, greater shielding, larger radius → weaker attraction → electronegativity decreases. Same three factors explain ionisation energy and atomic radius trends. Common error: claiming atoms get bigger across a period (they get smaller).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does electronegativity DECREASE down a group?",options:{A:"Nuclear charge decreases down a group.",B:"Atoms have more shells and greater shielding, increasing the atomic radius so the nucleus attracts the bonding pair less strongly, despite the higher nuclear charge.",C:"The number of outer electrons decreases.",D:"Elements become more non-metallic down a group."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== BIOLOGY BANK 2 — Cells, Transport & Enzymes =====
BANKS["BIO-2"] = {
  label: "Cells, Transport & Enzymes",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"BIO-09",stem:"A student states that osmosis is 'the movement of water from low to high concentration'. Why is this imprecise?",
      options:{A:"Osmosis moves solutes from low to high concentration across a membrane.",B:"Osmosis is the net movement of water from higher to lower water potential across a partially permeable membrane",C:"Osmosis is active transport of water, requiring ATP to move against the gradient.",D:"Water always moves from high solute concentration to low solute concentration in osmosis."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-09",
      scaffold:"Precise definition: net movement of water molecules from higher to lower WATER POTENTIAL across a partially permeable membrane. Water potential (ψ) is measured in kPa; pure water = 0, solutions are negative. 'Concentration' is ambiguous — water moves from high water concentration (dilute) to low water concentration (concentrated solute), i.e. down its water potential gradient. Osmosis is passive (no ATP). Marks are lost for omitting 'partially permeable membrane' or 'water potential'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A plant cell is placed in pure water. What happens and why?",options:{A:"Water leaves the cell because the cell has lower water potential; it becomes flaccid.",B:"Nothing happens — pure water has no effect on cells.",C:"Water enters the cell by osmosis (cell has lower/more negative water potential than pure water); the cell becomes turgid as the cell wall resists further expansion.",D:"The cell bursts because plant cells have no wall."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-10",stem:"What distinguishes active transport from facilitated diffusion?",
      options:{A:"Active transport uses ATP and moves substances against the concentration gradient",B:"Active transport moves only water molecules; facilitated diffusion handles all other ions.",C:"Facilitated diffusion requires ATP to open channel proteins; active transport is entirely passive.",D:"Active transport and facilitated diffusion are two names for the same membrane process."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-10",
      scaffold:"Facilitated diffusion: passive, down the gradient, via channel or carrier proteins, no ATP. Active transport: against the gradient, requires ATP and carrier proteins that change shape. Both use membrane proteins — the difference is energy and direction. Co-transport (e.g. glucose-sodium in the ileum) links the two. Common error: thinking any protein-mediated transport is 'active'. Simple diffusion needs no protein at all (small non-polar molecules like O2, CO2).",
      reforge:{stem:"Glucose is absorbed from the gut lumen into epithelial cells even when its concentration is already higher inside the cell. This is possible because of:",options:{A:"Simple diffusion through the phospholipid bilayer.",B:"Co-transport: sodium ions moving down their gradient (established by active transport) drag glucose against its gradient via a co-transporter protein.",C:"Osmosis pulling glucose along with water.",D:"Facilitated diffusion down the glucose gradient."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-11",stem:"A student explains enzyme action using the 'lock and key' model exclusively. What does the induced fit model add?",
      options:{A:"Induced fit means enzymes are used up and must be replaced during each reaction.",B:"Induced fit means the active site is perfectly rigid and will only accept one substrate shape.",C:"Induced fit: the active site flexes around the substrate on binding, straining bonds and lowering activation energy.",D:"Induced fit means one enzyme can catalyse any substrate because the site is adaptable."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-11",
      scaffold:"Lock and key: rigid complementary active site. Induced fit (the accepted model): the active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate on binding, straining substrate bonds and stabilising the transition state → lowers activation energy. Enzymes are catalysts (not used up), highly specific (complementary tertiary structure), and lower Ea without changing ΔG. Common error: describing the active site as 'exactly the same shape as the substrate' — it is complementary and dynamic, not identical.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does an enzyme increase the rate of a reaction?",options:{A:"By increasing the activation energy required.",B:"By raising the temperature of the surroundings.",C:"By being consumed to provide energy for the reaction.",D:"By lowering the activation energy — providing an alternative route so a greater proportion of collisions are successful at a given temperature."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-12",stem:"Above its optimum temperature, an enzyme's rate falls sharply. What is the correct explanation?",
      options:{A:"The substrate molecules evaporate at high temperature, leaving nothing to react.",B:"The enzyme is consumed faster at high temperature, depleting the catalyst.",C:"Vibration above the optimum breaks bonds in the tertiary structure",D:"The enzyme moves too fast to form stable collisions with the substrate."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-12",
      scaffold:"Below optimum: rate rises with temperature (more kinetic energy, more frequent successful collisions). At optimum: maximum rate. Above optimum: thermal vibration breaks the hydrogen/ionic bonds holding the tertiary structure → active site distorts → substrate no longer fits → denaturation (usually irreversible). Key phrasing: 'active site changes shape and is no longer complementary'. Common error: saying the enzyme 'dies' or 'melts' — enzymes are not alive; use 'denatures'. Extreme pH denatures similarly.",
      reforge:{stem:"A denatured enzyme cannot catalyse its reaction because:",options:{A:"It has been chemically converted into its substrate.",B:"Its tertiary structure and therefore its active site shape have changed, so the substrate is no longer complementary and cannot form an enzyme-substrate complex.",C:"It has run out of energy.",D:"Its primary structure (amino acid sequence) has been permanently rearranged."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-13",stem:"Which feature is found in a prokaryotic cell but NOT in a eukaryotic cell?",
      options:{A:"A cell surface membrane, present in all living cells including prokaryotes.",B:"Ribosomes, which are present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.",C:"A plasmid — a small circular DNA loop separate from the main chromosome.",D:"A cell wall, found in bacteria, plants and fungi but not animal cells."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-13",
      scaffold:"Prokaryotic features: no membrane-bound organelles, no true nucleus (circular DNA free in cytoplasm), plasmids, smaller (70S) ribosomes, murein/peptidoglycan cell wall, sometimes capsule/flagella. Eukaryotic: nucleus, membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi), 80S ribosomes. Shared: cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes, DNA. Traps: cell walls exist in BOTH (different materials); ribosomes exist in both (different sizes). Only plasmids, circular free DNA, and 70S ribosomes are prokaryote-distinctive here.",
      reforge:{stem:"Both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells contain ribosomes. What is the difference?",options:{A:"Prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S (smaller); eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S (larger).",B:"Only prokaryotes have ribosomes.",C:"Prokaryotic ribosomes are membrane-bound; eukaryotic are free.",D:"Eukaryotic ribosomes are made of DNA; prokaryotic of RNA."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-14",stem:"A student claims a large organism 'has a large surface area to volume ratio, so it needs a transport system'. Correct the reasoning.",
      options:{A:"Large organisms have a SMALL SA:V ratio — diffusion alone cannot meet their needs",B:"Large organisms have a large ratio and can diffuse everything directly through the body surface.",C:"Surface area to volume ratio stays constant regardless of organism size.",D:"Transport systems actively reduce the surface area to volume ratio in large organisms."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-14",
      scaffold:"As size increases, VOLUME grows faster than SURFACE AREA, so the SA:V ratio DECREASES. Small organisms (large SA:V) exchange by diffusion across the body surface. Large organisms (small SA:V) have longer diffusion distances and higher demands → need specialised exchange surfaces (alveoli, gills, villi — all increasing SA) and mass transport (circulatory) systems. Common error: reversing the direction of the ratio. Exchange surface adaptations: large SA, thin, moist, good blood supply, ventilation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Alveoli, gills and root hair cells share which key adaptation for efficient exchange?",options:{A:"They actively transport all substances using ATP.",B:"They are impermeable to prevent water loss.",C:"A large surface area combined with a thin exchange surface, maintaining a steep concentration gradient over a short diffusion distance.",D:"They are located deep inside the organism away from the environment."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-15",stem:"During the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle), what is the role of RuBisCO?",
      options:{A:"RuBisCO splits water molecules in the thylakoid to release oxygen during photolysis.",B:"RuBisCO fixes CO2 onto RuBP to form glycerate-3-phosphate — the entry point of the Calvin cycle.",C:"RuBisCO absorbs light energy to excite electrons in the chlorophyll molecule.",D:"RuBisCO produces ATP directly by phosphorylating ADP during the dark reaction."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-15",
      scaffold:"Calvin cycle (stroma, light-independent): CO2 + RuBP (5C) → 2× GP (3C), catalysed by RuBisCO. GP is reduced to triose phosphate (TP) using reduced NADP and ATP from the light-dependent reactions. TP forms glucose and regenerates RuBP. Water splitting (photolysis) and ATP/NADPH production happen in the LIGHT-DEPENDENT stage (thylakoids). Common error: placing photolysis or light absorption in the Calvin cycle, or forgetting the cycle depends on products of the light stage.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does the Calvin cycle stop shortly after light is removed, even though it is called 'light-independent'?",options:{A:"RuBisCO requires light to function.",B:"CO2 is only available in the light.",C:"It relies on ATP and reduced NADP produced by the light-dependent reactions; without light these run out, so GP cannot be reduced to TP.",D:"The stroma freezes in the dark."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"BIO-16",stem:"Two alleles, one for brown eyes (B) and one for blue (b), where B is dominant. A student says a brown-eyed parent 'must be BB'. Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Brown eyes are actually recessive — only one allele is needed to suppress blue.",B:"A brown-eyed person could be BB or Bb — dominant phenotype cannot confirm genotype without a test cross.",C:"Eye colour is determined by environment, not genetics.",D:"Brown-eyed parents cannot carry the blue-eye allele and will only have brown-eyed children."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-16",
      scaffold:"Dominant phenotype = at least one dominant allele → genotype is BB or Bb (unknown without further info). Recessive phenotype = must be homozygous recessive (bb). This is why two brown-eyed (Bb × Bb) parents can have a blue-eyed child (1/4 bb). Terms: allele (version of a gene), homozygous (same alleles), heterozygous (different), genotype (alleles present), phenotype (observable trait). A test cross with a homozygous recessive reveals an unknown dominant genotype.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two brown-eyed parents have a blue-eyed child. What must the parents' genotypes be?",options:{A:"Both BB — brown-eyed parents are always homozygous.",B:"One BB and one bb.",C:"Both bb — blue-eyed children need blue-eyed parents.",D:"Both Bb (heterozygous) — each carries a recessive b allele, so a bb child is possible with probability 1/4."},correct:"D"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== PHYSICS BANK 2 — Mechanics, Electricity & Waves =====
BANKS["PHYS-2"] = {
  label: "Mechanics, Electricity & Waves",
  color: "#4f46e5",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"PHYS-09",stem:"A ball is thrown vertically upward. At the highest point of its flight, what are its velocity and acceleration?",
      options:{A:"Velocity zero, acceleration zero — the ball is momentarily at rest.",B:"Velocity zero, acceleration 9.81 m s⁻² downward",C:"Velocity 9.81 m s⁻¹ upward, acceleration zero.",D:"Both velocity and acceleration point upward."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PHYS-09",
      scaffold:"At the apex, velocity = 0 (the ball changes direction) but acceleration = g = 9.81 m s⁻² downward throughout the flight (only gravity acts, ignoring air resistance). Common error: assuming a = 0 because v = 0. Velocity and acceleration are independent — an object can have zero velocity while accelerating. The ball decelerates going up, stops, then accelerates down, all under constant g. This is the classic misconception that 'no motion means no force'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A car momentarily at rest at the top of a hill, about to roll back down, has:",options:{A:"Zero velocity and zero acceleration.",B:"Zero velocity but a non-zero acceleration down the slope, caused by the component of gravity along the incline.",C:"Maximum velocity and zero acceleration.",D:"Zero acceleration because it is not yet moving."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-10",stem:"Two resistors, 4 Ω and 12 Ω, are connected in PARALLEL. What is their combined resistance?",
      options:{A:"16 Ω — resistances in parallel add directly.",B:"8 Ω — the average of the two.",C:"3 Ω — using 1/R = 1/4 + 1/12 = 4/12, so R = 3 Ω",D:"48 Ω — multiply the two values."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-PHYS-10",
      scaffold:"Series: R = R1 + R2 (resistances add). Parallel: 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2, and the total is ALWAYS smaller than the smallest individual resistor (more paths for current). Here 1/R = 1/4 + 1/12 = 3/12 + 1/12 = 4/12, R = 3 Ω. Sense check: 3 Ω < 4 Ω ✓. Common errors: adding parallel resistances directly, or forgetting to invert 1/R at the end. For two resistors only, the product-over-sum shortcut works: (4×12)/(4+12) = 48/16 = 3 Ω.",
      reforge:{stem:"The same two resistors (4 Ω and 12 Ω) are now connected in SERIES. The combined resistance is:",options:{A:"16 Ω — series resistances add directly: 4 + 12.",B:"3 Ω, as in the parallel case.",C:"8 Ω, the average.",D:"48/16 = 3 Ω using product over sum."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-11",stem:"A student states that in a transverse wave 'the particles move in the same direction as the wave travels'. Correct this.",
      options:{A:"In a transverse wave, particles oscillate perpendicular to the wave's direction of travel",B:"Particles remain completely stationary in all wave types — only energy moves.",C:"Transverse waves travel through space but cannot transfer energy to matter.",D:"All waves are transverse — longitudinal waves are a theoretical category only."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-PHYS-11",
      scaffold:"Transverse: particle oscillation perpendicular to energy transfer (e.g. light, EM waves, water surface, waves on a string). Longitudinal: particle oscillation parallel to energy transfer, with compressions and rarefactions (e.g. sound). In BOTH, particles oscillate about a fixed point — they do NOT travel with the wave; only energy propagates. Only transverse waves can be polarised. Common error: thinking matter moves along with the wave.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which property is unique to transverse waves and provides evidence for their nature?",options:{A:"They can reflect off surfaces.",B:"They can be polarised — restricted to oscillate in a single plane — which is impossible for longitudinal waves.",C:"They can be refracted.",D:"They transfer energy without transferring matter."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-12",stem:"A 2 kg object experiences a resultant force of 10 N. A student calculates its acceleration as 20 m s⁻² (F × m). What is the correct value?",
      options:{A:"20 m s⁻² — the student multiplied force by mass correctly.",B:"5 m s⁻² — F = ma rearranges to a = F/m = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 m s⁻².",C:"12 m s⁻² — calculated as F + m rather than F ÷ m.",D:"0.2 m s⁻² — calculated as m ÷ F rather than F ÷ m."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PHYS-12",
      scaffold:"Newton's second law: F = ma, so a = F/m (force divided by mass, not multiplied). Here a = 10/2 = 5 m s⁻². Resultant (net) force matters — sum all forces first, accounting for direction. Units: N = kg m s⁻². Common errors: multiplying instead of dividing, using weight instead of mass, or using a single force instead of the resultant. Always identify the resultant force before applying F = ma.",
      reforge:{stem:"The same 2 kg object is now pushed with 10 N forward while a 4 N friction force opposes it. Its acceleration is:",options:{A:"7 m s⁻² — using the 14 N total.",B:"5 m s⁻² — ignoring friction.",C:"3 m s⁻² — resultant force = 10 − 4 = 6 N, so a = 6/2 = 3 m s⁻².",D:"2 m s⁻² — a = friction/mass."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-13",stem:"Distinguish clearly between scalar and vector quantities. Which set contains ONLY vectors?",
      options:{A:"Speed, distance, energy, temperature — all magnitude only.",B:"Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force — all have magnitude AND direction.",C:"Mass, time, speed, power — all scalar quantities with magnitude only.",D:"Distance, speed, mass, work — scalars that are sometimes confused with vectors."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PHYS-13",
      scaffold:"Vectors (magnitude AND direction): displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum, weight. Scalars (magnitude only): distance, speed, mass, time, energy, temperature, work, power. Watch the pairs: distance (scalar) vs displacement (vector); speed (scalar) vs velocity (vector). Weight is a vector (force); mass is a scalar. Vectors add using tip-to-tail/pythagoras; scalars add arithmetically. Common trap: classifying work or energy as vectors — they are scalars despite involving force.",
      reforge:{stem:"A runner completes one lap of a 400 m track, finishing where they started. Their distance and displacement are:",options:{A:"Distance 400 m, displacement 400 m.",B:"Distance 0 m, displacement 400 m.",C:"Distance 400 m, displacement 0 m — displacement is the straight-line vector from start to finish, which is zero for a complete lap.",D:"Both are zero."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-14",stem:"In a simple circuit, what does the electromotive force (e.m.f.) of a battery actually represent?",
      options:{A:"The force that pushes electrons, measured in newtons.",B:"The current the battery supplies, measured in amps.",C:"The resistance inside the battery.",D:"The energy transferred to each coulomb of charge by the battery"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-PHYS-14",
      scaffold:"e.m.f. = energy transferred TO charge per coulomb by the source (J/C = V). Potential difference = energy transferred FROM charge per coulomb to a component. Despite its name, e.m.f. is NOT a force — it is an energy-per-charge quantity in volts. Terminal p.d. < e.m.f. because of 'lost volts' across internal resistance r: e.m.f. = I(R + r). Common errors: calling e.m.f. a force, or confusing it with current. V = W/Q underlies both e.m.f. and p.d.",
      reforge:{stem:"A battery has e.m.f. 12 V but its terminal potential difference is only 11.5 V when supplying current. Why?",options:{A:"The battery is faulty and should read 12 V.",B:"Some energy per coulomb (the 'lost volts') is dissipated across the battery's internal resistance, so terminal p.d. = e.m.f. − Ir.",C:"The voltmeter is measuring current instead.",D:"e.m.f. always exceeds terminal p.d. by exactly 0.5 V."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-15",stem:"A student says heavier objects fall faster in a vacuum. Referring to free fall, why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Heavier objects do fall faster because they contain more gravitational pull.",B:"In a vacuum all objects fall with the same acceleration g — mass cancels in a = F/m = mg/m.",C:"Lighter objects fall faster in a vacuum because they have less resistance.",D:"Objects do not fall in a vacuum as there is no air to push them down."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PHYS-15",
      scaffold:"In free fall (vacuum): a = g for all masses. Reasoning via F = ma: gravitational force W = mg, so a = W/m = mg/m = g — mass cancels. A heavier object feels a bigger force but needs a bigger force to accelerate equally (more inertia). In air, resistance depends on shape/speed, so a feather falls slower — but that is air resistance, not gravity. The famous Apollo 15 hammer-feather drop demonstrated equal fall in a vacuum.",
      reforge:{stem:"In air, a feather falls more slowly than a coin. In the mechanics of this, the key factor is:",options:{A:"The coin experiences a stronger gravitational field.",B:"Air resistance has a much greater decelerating effect on the low-mass, high-surface-area feather relative to its weight, so it reaches terminal velocity almost immediately.",C:"The feather has more inertia than the coin.",D:"Gravity does not act on feathers."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PHYS-16",stem:"A 500 g mass hangs from a spring, extending it by 0.10 m. Taking g = 9.81 m s⁻², what is the spring constant k?",
      options:{A:"49.05 N m⁻¹ — using F = kx with F = mg = 0.500 × 9.81 = 4.905 N, so k = 4.905/0.10",B:"5000 N m⁻¹ — using the mass in grams.",C:"0.98 N m⁻¹ — dividing extension by force.",D:"4.905 N m⁻¹ — forgetting to divide by extension."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-PHYS-16",
      scaffold:"Hooke's law: F = kx, so k = F/x. The force is the WEIGHT: F = mg = 0.500 kg × 9.81 = 4.905 N (convert 500 g to 0.500 kg). Then k = 4.905/0.10 = 49.05 N m⁻¹. Common errors: using mass in grams, using mass instead of weight for F, or dividing x by F. Elastic potential energy stored = ½kx² = ½Fx (area under a force-extension graph). Units of k: N m⁻¹.",
      reforge:{stem:"How much elastic potential energy is stored in the spring at this 0.10 m extension?",options:{A:"0.49 J — using E = ½kx² = ½ × 49.05 × 0.10².",B:"4.905 J — using E = Fx.",C:"0.245 J — using ½mx.",D:"49.05 J — using kx."},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== NEW BANKS: LAW-2, CRIM-2, PSY-PATH =====

BANKS["LAW-2"] = {
  label: "Tort Law",
  color: "#1e40af",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"TORT-01",
      stem:"In Donoghue v Stevenson [1932], Lord Atkin established the 'neighbour principle'. Which statement best captures its legal significance?",
      options:{
        A:"It created a duty of care between manufacturers and the ultimate consumer, establishing the modern law of negligence.",
        B:"It held that manufacturers owe a duty only to the retailers who purchase their products.",
        C:"It introduced the concept of contributory negligence as a partial defence.",
        D:"It limited liability in tort to cases where a contract existed between the parties."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-LAW-08",
      scaffold:"Donoghue v Stevenson: Mrs Donoghue found a decomposed snail in ginger beer. No contract with the manufacturer (she didn't buy it). Lord Atkin's 'neighbour principle' — you must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that you can reasonably foresee would injure your neighbour (anyone closely and directly affected). This established negligence as an independent tort, no contract required.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was it significant that Mrs Donoghue had no contract with the manufacturer?",options:{A:"It meant she could not bring any legal action at all.",B:"Contract law did not apply, so a new tortious duty was needed — showing tort operates independently of contract.",C:"She had to rely on criminal law instead.",D:"It proved the manufacturer was not liable."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-02",
      stem:"The three-stage Caparo test for establishing a duty of care requires which elements?",
      options:{
        A:"Proximity, causation, and remoteness of damage.",
        B:"Intention, foreseeability, and breach.",
        C:"Foreseeability of harm, proximity of relationship, and that it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty.",
        D:"Foreseeability of harm, voluntary assumption of risk, and consent."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-LAW-09",
      scaffold:"Caparo Industries v Dickman [1990] — three-stage test: (1) Was the damage reasonably foreseeable? (2) Was there sufficient proximity between claimant and defendant? (3) Is it fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty? All three must be satisfied. Students confuse this with the elements of breach (the reasonable person standard) — that's a separate question.",
      reforge:{stem:"A financial adviser gives negligent advice to a stranger at a party. Why might stage 3 of Caparo prevent a duty arising?",options:{A:"Financial loss can never be claimed in tort.",B:"There was no proximity between the parties.",C:"It may not be fair, just and reasonable to impose liability for informal social advice — courts are reluctant to extend duty of care too widely.",D:"The advice was not foreseeable."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-03",
      stem:"The standard of care in negligence is judged by reference to:",
      options:{
        A:"What the defendant believed was reasonable given their personal experience.",
        B:"The standard of a reasonable person in the defendant's position.",
        C:"The highest possible standard of professional conduct available.",
        D:"What the claimant expected the defendant to do."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-LAW-10",
      scaffold:"The objective 'reasonable person' standard — Blyth v Birmingham Waterworks (1856). Not what this defendant thought was reasonable (subjective), but what a reasonable person in the same circumstances would have done. For professionals, the Bolam test applies: the standard of a reasonable professional in that field — but still objective, not the highest possible standard.",
      reforge:{stem:"A trainee doctor makes an error that an experienced consultant would not have made. Are they judged by the trainee's standard?",options:{A:"Yes — the law makes allowances for inexperience.",B:"No — they are judged by the standard of a reasonably competent doctor in that post, not a junior. The role, not the individual's experience, sets the standard.",C:"Only if the patient knew they were a trainee.",D:"Yes, but damages are reduced proportionally."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-04",
      stem:"Which case established the 'eggshell skull' (thin skull) rule in tort law?",
      options:{
        A:"Paris v Stepney Borough Council [1951]",
        B:"Haley v London Electricity Board [1965]",
        C:"Smith v Leech Brain & Co [1962]",
        D:"Wagon Mound No 1 [1961]"
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-LAW-11",
      scaffold:"Smith v Leech Brain: a splash of molten metal burned a worker's lip; he had a pre-existing condition that caused cancer and he died. The burn itself was foreseeable — the cancer and death were not. Rule: you take your victim as you find them. If the type of harm was foreseeable, the defendant is liable for its full extent even if the severity was not foreseeable due to the claimant's pre-existing vulnerability.",
      reforge:{stem:"A defendant negligently scratches a claimant who, unknown to the defendant, has a rare bleeding disorder and nearly dies. Applying the thin skull rule:",options:{A:"The defendant is only liable for a minor scratch — the rest was not foreseeable.",B:"The defendant is liable for all consequences if the type of harm (physical injury) was foreseeable, even though the severity was not.",C:"Liability is shared equally between the defendant and the claimant's doctor.",D:"There is no liability because the outcome was unforeseeable."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-05",
      stem:"Remoteness of damage in negligence is governed primarily by which case?",
      options:{
        A:"Donoghue v Stevenson [1932]",
        B:"Wagon Mound No 1 [1961]",
        C:"Caparo v Dickman [1990]",
        D:"Bolton v Stone [1951]"
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-LAW-12",
      scaffold:"The Wagon Mound (No 1): oil spilled into Sydney Harbour caught fire from welding sparks, destroying ships. The Privy Council held you are only liable for damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable — not all direct consequences (overruling the old Re Polemis direct consequence test). Key distinction: the type of damage must be foreseeable, even if its exact extent is not (thin skull rule still applies).",
      reforge:{stem:"A defendant negligently spills chemicals. The chemicals react unexpectedly with soil to cause a rare explosion. Applying Wagon Mound remoteness:",options:{A:"The defendant is liable because they caused the spill.",B:"The defendant is only liable if an explosion of this type was a reasonably foreseeable consequence of the spill.",C:"The defendant is liable for all consequences of their negligence.",D:"The defendant is not liable because no harm was intended."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-06",
      stem:"Volenti non fit injuria as a defence in negligence means:",
      options:{
        A:"The claimant contributed to their own harm.",
        B:"The defendant acted on the instructions of a third party.",
        C:"The claimant voluntarily accepted the risk of harm, providing a complete defence.",
        D:"The damage was too remote to be recoverable."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-LAW-13",
      scaffold:"Volenti: a complete defence — if the claimant fully and freely consented to the legal risk of harm, the defendant owes no duty. Hard to establish: consent must be free and informed (not under economic pressure). Distinguished from contributory negligence (partial — reduces damages proportionally). Classic case: Dann v Hamilton [1939] — claimant knew driver was drunk but got in anyway; court held volenti did not apply because she didn't fully accept the legal risk.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does volenti differ from contributory negligence?",options:{A:"They are the same defence with different names.",B:"Volenti is a complete defence extinguishing liability entirely; contributory negligence is partial and reduces damages by the claimant's percentage of fault.",C:"Contributory negligence is a complete defence; volenti only reduces damages.",D:"Volenti applies only in criminal law."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-07",
      stem:"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 imposes a 'common duty of care' on occupiers towards:",
      options:{
        A:"All persons who enter the premises, regardless of how they enter.",
        B:"Lawful visitors only.",
        C:"Trespassers and lawful visitors equally.",
        D:"Only paying customers who have a contract with the occupier."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-LAW-14",
      scaffold:"OLA 1957: duty owed to lawful visitors — those with express or implied permission to enter (invitees, licensees). The Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 separately governs the lower duty owed to trespassers. Students confuse the two Acts. 1957 = visitors. 1984 = trespassers. Under 1957, the standard is to take such care as is reasonable to see the visitor will be reasonably safe for the purpose of their visit.",
      reforge:{stem:"A child trespasses onto a farm and is injured by unfenced machinery. Which Act is most relevant?",options:{A:"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 — all entrants are covered.",B:"Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 — this Act governs the duty owed to trespassers.",C:"The law of contract, since the farmer impliedly invited the child.",D:"Neither Act — trespassers have no legal protection."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TORT-08",
      stem:"In psychiatric injury cases, a 'secondary victim' must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms. Which is NOT one of them?",
      options:{
        A:"Proximity of relationship to the primary victim.",
        B:"Proximity to the accident in time and space.",
        C:"Perception of the accident through unaided senses.",
        D:"Proof that the defendant intended to cause psychiatric harm."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-LAW-15",
      scaffold:"Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire [1991] — Hillsborough disaster. Control mechanisms for secondary victims: (1) close tie of love and affection with primary victim; (2) present at the scene or its immediate aftermath; (3) perceived events with own unaided senses (not TV). These are policy controls to limit floodgates. Intention is irrelevant — negligence is a fault-based tort, not intention-based.",
      reforge:{stem:"A claimant watches a disaster unfold live on TV and suffers PTSD. Can they recover as a secondary victim?",options:{A:"Yes — foreseeability is all that matters.",B:"Yes, if they have a close relationship with a primary victim.",C:"No — secondary victims must perceive events through unaided senses; TV broadcasts do not satisfy the Alcock control mechanism.",D:"Yes, if the broadcaster negligently showed graphic footage."},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["CRIM-2"] = {
  label: "Criminological Theories",
  color: "#374151",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CRIM-09",
      stem:"Robert Merton's strain theory argues that crime results from:",
      options:{
        A:"A disconnect between culturally approved goals and the legitimate means available to achieve them.",
        B:"Biological predispositions that make some individuals naturally deviant.",
        C:"The absence of formal social control mechanisms in urban environments.",
        D:"Learned behaviour acquired through association with criminal peers."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CRIM-06",
      scaffold:"Merton (1938): American Dream ideology promotes wealth as a goal for everyone, but legitimate means (education, jobs) are unequally distributed. The resulting strain produces 5 adaptations: Conformity (accept both), Innovation (accept goal, reject means — most criminal), Ritualism, Retreatism, Rebellion. Innovators use illegal means (theft, drug dealing) to achieve cultural goals. Explains working-class crime but not white-collar crime.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of Merton's adaptations best describes a person who rejects both cultural goals and legitimate means, withdrawing entirely from society?",options:{A:"Ritualism — going through the motions without believing in success.",B:"Conformity — accepting both goals and means.",C:"Retreatism — rejecting both goals and means, withdrawing (e.g. chronic drug users, homeless).",D:"Rebellion — replacing goals and means with alternatives."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-10",
      stem:"Social learning theory (Sutherland's Differential Association) explains crime as:",
      options:{
        A:"An innate trait that is genetically inherited.",
        B:"A result of rational cost-benefit calculation.",
        C:"Learned through interaction with others, particularly those who hold favourable attitudes toward law violation.",
        D:"Caused by anomie when social norms break down during rapid social change."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CRIM-07",
      scaffold:"Sutherland (1939): crime is learned through communication within intimate groups. If a person is exposed to more definitions favourable to crime than unfavourable, they are likely to offend. Frequency, duration, priority, and intensity of association all matter. This challenges biological and psychological theories — no one is born criminal; crime is a learned behaviour like any other. Supported by evidence on gang membership and peer influence.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which finding would most challenge Differential Association theory?",options:{A:"Children raised in high-crime neighbourhoods are more likely to offend.",B:"Peer groups influence attitudes to law-breaking.",C:"Identical twins raised apart show similar rates of offending, suggesting a genetic component that learning theory cannot explain.",D:"First-time offenders often know others who have committed crimes."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-11",
      stem:"Left Realism (Lea and Young) identifies the 'square of crime'. Which four elements does it include?",
      options:{
        A:"Offender, victim, police, and media.",
        B:"Poverty, unemployment, inequality, and social exclusion.",
        C:"Police, courts, prisons, and probation services.",
        D:"Victim, offender, police, and public."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CRIM-08",
      scaffold:"Lea and Young's square of crime: the four elements that must interact to produce crime — (1) offender, (2) victim, (3) police/criminal justice system, (4) public. Left Realists focus on working-class crime and its real impact on working-class communities (unlike Marxists who focus on corporate crime). They advocate for better policing that is accountable to local communities and social reforms to reduce relative deprivation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Left Realists criticise both traditional Marxism and Right Realism for taking crime seriously. Why?",options:{A:"Because crime does not really exist — it is a social construction.",B:"Marxists focus too much on corporate crime and ignore street crime affecting working-class victims; Right Realists blame the individual and ignore structural causes. Left Realists argue both matter.",C:"Because only white-collar crime causes real harm.",D:"Because they both ignore the role of biological factors."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-12",
      stem:"Labelling theory (Becker) argues that deviance is:",
      options:{
        A:"An objective quality that inheres in the act itself.",
        B:"Genetically determined and therefore fixed.",
        C:"Not a quality of the act but of the application of rules and sanctions by others — created by social reaction.",
        D:"Primarily caused by economic inequality."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CRIM-09",
      scaffold:"Becker (1963) 'Outsiders': 'deviance is not a quality of the act a person commits, but rather a consequence of the application by others of rules and sanctions.' The same act may be labelled deviant or not depending on who does it, to whom, and who observes it. Labels trigger a 'master status' (deviant above all else), which can lead to a self-fulfilling prophecy and secondary deviance (Lemert). Explains why official statistics over-represent the poor and ethnic minorities.",
      reforge:{stem:"Lemert distinguished 'primary deviance' from 'secondary deviance'. Secondary deviance refers to:",options:{A:"Minor rule-breaking that goes unnoticed.",B:"Deviance committed by a second person copying the first offender.",C:"Deviance that results from internalising the deviant label — the person reorganises their identity around being deviant after societal reaction.",D:"Crime committed in a secondary location away from home."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-13",
      stem:"Feminists critique traditional criminology for:",
      options:{
        A:"Focusing too much on female offenders at the expense of male crime.",
        B:"Ignoring biological explanations of crime.",
        C:"Constructing theories based almost entirely on male subjects and then applying them universally, rendering women invisible.",
        D:"Over-emphasising the role of poverty in causing crime."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CRIM-10",
      scaffold:"Feminist criminology (Smart, Heidensohn): traditional criminology was 'malestream' — built on male samples, male researchers, male subjects. Women were either ignored or, when included (e.g. Lombroso), explained through sexist stereotypes. Heidensohn argues women's low crime rates reflect social control (patriarchal expectations, domestic roles, chivalry factor in CJS). Smart argues women who do offend are doubly condemned — for the crime and for violating gender norms.",
      reforge:{stem:"The 'chivalry thesis' (Pollak) argues the CJS treats women more leniently. Which evidence challenges this?",options:{A:"Women are less likely to be arrested than men for equivalent offences.",B:"Women who commit violent crimes are sometimes treated more harshly than men because they violate gender expectations — doubly deviant — suggesting chivalry is not universal.",C:"Female prison populations are smaller than male populations.",D:"Women receive shorter sentences on average."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-14",
      stem:"Right Realism (Wilson and Herrnstein) explains crime primarily through:",
      options:{
        A:"Structural inequality and relative deprivation.",
        B:"Rational choice combined with individual traits such as low self-control, impulsiveness, and low intelligence.",
        C:"Labelling by the criminal justice system creating criminal careers.",
        D:"The collapse of community bonds caused by rapid industrialisation."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CRIM-11",
      scaffold:"Wilson and Herrnstein (1985): crime is the result of individual rational choice — people weigh costs and benefits. But choice is shaped by individual traits: low IQ, impulsiveness, low self-control (Gottfredson and Hirschi), and biology. Policy implication: situational crime prevention (make crime harder/riskier) and 'broken windows' policing (Wilson and Kelling). Critics: ignores structural causes, over-represents working-class crime, ignores white-collar crime.",
      reforge:{stem:"Zero-tolerance policing, associated with Right Realism, was implemented in New York in the 1990s. Which is a valid critique?",options:{A:"It reduced crime too effectively, leaving police with nothing to do.",B:"It increased police funding unnecessarily.",C:"It disproportionately targeted young Black and Latino men, raising questions about structural discrimination that Right Realism ignores.",D:"It focused too much on corporate crime."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-15",
      stem:"Hirschi's social bond theory argues that people conform (rather than asking why they offend) because of four bonds. Which is NOT one of them?",
      options:{
        A:"Attachment to others.",
        B:"Commitment to conventional activities.",
        C:"Involvement in community organisations.",
        D:"Genetic predisposition to conformity."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CRIM-12",
      scaffold:"Hirschi (1969) — Control Theory: asks why most people don't offend, not why some do. Four bonds to conventional society: (1) Attachment — caring what others think; (2) Commitment — investment in conventional goals (job, qualifications); (3) Involvement — time spent in legitimate activities (less time for crime); (4) Belief — internalised moral norms. Genetic predisposition is a biological explanation, not part of Hirschi's social bond theory.",
      reforge:{stem:"A teenager drops out of school, loses their job, and spends time with peers who offend. How does social bond theory explain increased offending risk?",options:{A:"They have developed a biological predisposition to crime.",B:"They have been labelled deviant by teachers.",C:"Three bonds have weakened — commitment (no qualifications to protect), involvement (more free time), and attachment (peer group normalises crime).",D:"Merton's strain theory better explains this than social bond theory."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-16",
      stem:"Which theoretical perspective would most likely argue that corporate crime is a more serious social problem than street crime?",
      options:{
        A:"Right Realism",
        B:"Biological positivism",
        C:"Traditional Marxism",
        D:"Rational choice theory"
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CRIM-13",
      scaffold:"Traditional Marxism (Chambliss, Box): the law serves ruling-class interests. Corporate and white-collar crime (tax evasion, health and safety violations, environmental damage) causes more harm — financial, physical, and social — than street crime, but is under-policed, under-prosecuted, and under-punished. The CJS focuses on working-class crime to control the proletariat and protect capitalist interests. Right Realists focus on individual street crime; rational choice theorists see all crime as a cost-benefit calculation regardless of class.",
      reforge:{stem:"Marxists argue official crime statistics are ideologically constructed. What do they mean?",options:{A:"Police make mathematical errors when counting crime.",B:"Statistics reflect media sensationalism rather than real patterns.",C:"The selective enforcement and recording of crime reflects ruling-class power — working-class crime is over-represented because the law and CJS serve to protect capitalist interests and criminalise the powerless.",D:"Statistics are accurate but only reported annually."},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["PSY-PATH"] = {
  label: "Psychopathology",
  color: "#9d174d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"PATH-01",
      stem:"Statistical infrequency as a definition of abnormality classifies behaviour as abnormal when:",
      options:{
        A:"It occurs rarely — over two standard deviations from the population mean",
        B:"It causes significant personal distress to the individual who experiences it",
        C:"It deviates from the social norms and expectations of the culture",
        D:"It prevents the individual from functioning effectively in daily life"
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-PATH-01",
      scaffold:"Statistical infrequency: uses normal distribution. Most behaviour clusters around the mean; behaviour 2+ SDs away is statistically abnormal. Example: IQ below 70 (2 SDs below mean of 100) = intellectually disabled. Strength: objective, quantifiable. Weaknesses: (1) some statistically rare traits are desirable (genius IQ); (2) doesn't account for cultural context; (3) where to draw the line is arbitrary.",
      reforge:{stem:"Using statistical infrequency alone, which individual would be classified as abnormal?",options:{A:"A person with depression who feels unable to get out of bed.",B:"A person with an IQ of 145 — more than 2 standard deviations above the mean.",C:"A person who violates cultural norms by eating extremely loudly in public places",D:"A person who reports feeling anxious in social situations."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-02",
      stem:"The behavioural approach to explaining phobias proposes that phobias are acquired through:",
      options:{
        A:"Unconscious conflicts originating in the early childhood psychosexual stages",
        B:"Irrational, catastrophising thought patterns about the phobic stimulus",
        C:"The genetic inheritance of a biological predisposition towards fear",
        D:"Classical conditioning, as a neutral stimulus is paired with fear"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-PATH-02",
      scaffold:"Behaviourist explanation: Watson and Rayner's 'Little Albert' study — a neutral stimulus (white rat) was paired with a loud noise (UCS → UCR: fear) until the rat alone (CS) elicited fear (CR). The two-process model (Mowrer): (1) classical conditioning acquires the phobia; (2) operant conditioning maintains it (avoidance is negatively reinforced by anxiety reduction). Supports systematic desensitisation as a treatment.",
      reforge:{stem:"Mowrer's two-process model explains why phobias are maintained as well as acquired. What is the second process?",options:{A:"The phobia is maintained by repeatedly pairing the CS with the UCS.",B:"The phobia is maintained by social learning from observing others who are fearful",C:"Operant conditioning — avoidance reduces anxiety, so it continues",D:"The phobia is maintained by the unconscious repression of traumatic memories."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-03",
      stem:"Systematic desensitisation treats phobias by:",
      options:{
        A:"Exposing the patient immediately to the most feared stimulus until anxiety fades",
        B:"Replacing maladaptive thoughts about the phobic stimulus with rational ones",
        C:"Pairing the stimulus with relaxation through gradual, hierarchical exposure",
        D:"Prescribing medication that suppresses the physiological fear response"
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-PATH-03",
      scaffold:"Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe): (1) patient learns relaxation techniques (deep breathing, progressive muscle relaxation); (2) therapist and patient construct a desensitisation hierarchy (least → most feared); (3) patient works through hierarchy while maintaining relaxation. Counter-conditioning replaces the fear CR with relaxation. Works through reciprocal inhibition (anxiety and relaxation cannot coexist). Supported by evidence; good for specific phobias.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does systematic desensitisation differ from flooding?",options:{A:"Systematic desensitisation uses medication; flooding uses talking therapy.",B:"Flooding involves gradual exposure with relaxation; systematic desensitisation is immediate and intense.",C:"They are the same technique described with different names for different phobia types.",D:"Systematic desensitisation is gradual; flooding is immediate and intense"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-04",
      stem:"Beck's cognitive triad in depression involves negative thoughts about:",
      options:{
        A:"Past, present, and future relationships.",
        B:"Oneself, the world, and the future.",
        C:"Childhood, adolescence, and adulthood.",
        D:"Feelings, thoughts, and behaviours."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PATH-04",
      scaffold:"Beck's cognitive triad: (1) Negative view of self ('I am worthless, unlovable'); (2) Negative view of the world ('Nothing ever works out, everyone is against me'); (3) Negative view of the future ('Things will never improve'). Maintained by cognitive errors: overgeneralisation, catastrophising, selective abstraction. Basis for CBT — challenge and restructure these negative automatic thoughts (NATs).",
      reforge:{stem:"A depressed patient says: 'I failed one exam, so I am a complete failure and will fail everything for the rest of my life.' Which cognitive errors are present?",options:{A:"Positive attribution and selective abstraction.",B:"Overgeneralisation and catastrophising about future events",C:"Reciprocal determinism combined with negative reinforcement",D:"Primary and secondary appraisal errors."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-05",
      stem:"The cognitive approach explains OCD as caused by:",
      options:{
        A:"An overactive basal ganglia that produces persistent intrusive urges",
        B:"A learned association between an anxiety-provoking stimulus and a compulsive act",
        C:"A genetic predisposition combined with unusually low serotonin levels",
        D:"Catastrophic misinterpretation of intrusive thoughts as truly dangerous"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-PATH-05",
      scaffold:"Cognitive explanation (Rachman): Everyone has occasional intrusive thoughts, but most people dismiss them. OCD patients interpret these thoughts as threatening and personally significant ('I thought about harming my child — does that make me dangerous?'). This catastrophic misinterpretation causes anxiety; compulsions are performed to neutralise it. This maintains the cycle. Supports CBT as treatment — challenge the misinterpretation rather than the compulsion.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does performing a compulsion maintain OCD according to the cognitive model?",options:{A:"It strengthens the neural pathways associated with the compulsion, making it harder to resist.",B:"It prevents serotonin reuptake, worsening the neurochemical imbalance.",C:"It conditions the patient to associate the trigger stimulus with anxiety.",D:"It reduces anxiety temporarily, negatively reinforcing the cycle"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-06",
      stem:"Drug therapy for depression typically works by targeting which neurotransmitter system?",
      options:{
        A:"Serotonin — SSRIs block its reuptake, raising synaptic availability",
        B:"Dopamine — the drugs increase dopamine reuptake within the limbic system",
        C:"Acetylcholine — blocking its receptors throughout the prefrontal cortex",
        D:"GABA — the drugs enhance GABA activity in order to reduce inhibition"
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-PATH-06",
      scaffold:"SSRIs (Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors) — e.g. fluoxetine (Prozac). The low serotonin hypothesis: depression associated with low serotonin levels. SSRIs block the reuptake transporter so serotonin remains in the synapse longer, increasing postsynaptic receptor activation. Evidence: SSRIs are effective (Kirsch meta-analysis shows effect; BUT effect size is often modest compared to placebo for mild-moderate depression). Side effects limit compliance.",
      reforge:{stem:"A patient stops taking SSRIs after two weeks because they 'don't feel any better'. What should they be told?",options:{A:"SSRIs take two to four weeks to reach therapeutic levels",B:"SSRIs work immediately, so they should simply try a completely different drug",C:"The diagnosis of depression must be wrong.",D:"They should double the dose to speed up the effect."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-07",
      stem:"Which statement correctly identifies a limitation of the deviation from social norms definition of abnormality?",
      options:{
        A:"It is too objective, relying only on statistics and ignoring all context",
        B:"Social norms vary across cultures and change over time, so labels shift",
        C:"It applies only to mental illness and cannot define physical illness",
        D:"It is effectively identical to the statistical infrequency definition"
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PATH-07",
      scaffold:"Deviation from social norms: abnormal = behaviour that violates the written or unwritten rules of society. Strength: explains why some statistically rare behaviour (e.g. genius) is not considered abnormal. Key limitation: cultural relativism — homosexuality was classified as a mental disorder in the DSM until 1973; misattributing political dissent as mental illness in the USSR shows how norms can be used as a tool of social control. Also: norms change over time.",
      reforge:{stem:"In some cultures, hearing the voices of deceased relatives is a normal spiritual experience. How does this challenge the deviation from social norms definition?",options:{A:"It shows that statistical infrequency is a better definition.",B:"It means that hearing voices can never be a symptom of any mental illness",C:"It proves that abnormality is always biologically determined.",D:"Social norms are culturally specific, so the definition is not universal"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"PATH-08",
      stem:"CBT (Cognitive Behavioural Therapy) treats mental disorders by targeting:",
      options:{
        A:"Unconscious conflicts, using free association and dream analysis",
        B:"Maladaptive thoughts and behaviours, challenging cognitive distortions",
        C:"Neurochemical imbalances, through direct electrical stimulation of the brain",
        D:"Learned fear associations, using systematic counter-conditioning"
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PATH-08",
      scaffold:"CBT integrates cognitive and behavioural techniques. Cognitive component: identify negative automatic thoughts (NATs), challenge cognitive distortions (Beck), replace with rational alternatives. Behavioural component: behavioural activation (for depression — scheduling positive activities), exposure tasks (for phobias/OCD). Evidence: NICE recommends CBT as first-line treatment for depression, anxiety, OCD, PTSD. Limitation: requires motivation and cognitive ability; not suitable for severe psychosis.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might CBT be less effective for a patient with severe depression?",options:{A:"Severe depression impairs the concentration that CBT requires",B:"CBT only works for anxiety disorders, not depression.",C:"CBT has been proven entirely ineffective by all of the major meta-analyses",D:"CBT is too expensive for most patients to access."},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

