BANKS["HIST-2"] = {
  label: "Cold War & Superpower Relations",
  color: "#78350f",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CW-01",
      stem:"The Truman Doctrine (1947) was significant primarily because it:",
      options:{
        A:"Committed the USA to providing military and economic aid to countries threatened by communist takeover, marking a shift from isolationism to global containment.",
        B:"Created NATO as a collective security alliance against Soviet aggression.",
        C:"Divided Germany into four occupation zones between the Allied powers.",
        D:"Established the Marshall Plan to rebuild Western European economies."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HIST-06",
      scaffold:"Truman Doctrine (March 1947): Truman asked Congress for $400m to support Greece and Turkey against communist insurgencies. Crucially, it stated the USA would support 'free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation.' This was a general commitment to containment worldwide — not just Europe. Distinct from the Marshall Plan (economic aid, June 1947) and NATO (military alliance, 1949).",
      reforge:{stem:"How did the Truman Doctrine differ from the Marshall Plan?",options:{A:"They were identical — both provided military aid to Europe.",B:"The Truman Doctrine was a political commitment to contain communism globally; the Marshall Plan was specifically economic aid to rebuild Western European economies devastated by WWII.",C:"The Marshall Plan was military; the Truman Doctrine was economic.",D:"The Truman Doctrine applied only to Asia; the Marshall Plan to Europe."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-02",
      stem:"The Berlin Blockade (1948–49) ended because:",
      options:{
        A:"NATO threatened military action against the Soviet Union.",
        B:"The Western Allies successfully supplied West Berlin by airlift, making the blockade ineffective, and the Soviets lifted it after negotiations.",
        C:"West Berlin surrendered to Soviet demands.",
        D:"The USA deployed nuclear weapons to West Germany as a deterrent."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HIST-07",
      scaffold:"Stalin blockaded all land routes to West Berlin in June 1948 to force the Allies out. The Allied airlift (Operation Vittles) flew over 200,000 flights, delivering 2.3 million tonnes of supplies in 11 months. The blockade became a propaganda victory for the West — Soviet aggression looked worse than Western resolve looked strong. Stalin lifted it in May 1949 having gained nothing. NATO was formed the same month.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was the Berlin Airlift significant beyond its immediate practical purpose?",options:{A:"It demonstrated that conventional military force was the best response to Soviet pressure.",B:"It was a propaganda victory showing Western resolve and Soviet aggressiveness without risking direct military confrontation — strengthening support for NATO and containment.",C:"It proved the Soviet economy was stronger than the West.",D:"It led directly to German reunification."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-03",
      stem:"The Cuban Missile Crisis (1962) was resolved primarily through:",
      options:{
        A:"A US military invasion of Cuba that destroyed the missile sites.",
        B:"UN peacekeeping forces being deployed to Cuba.",
        C:"Direct negotiation between Kennedy and Khrushchev — the Soviets withdrew missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to remove US missiles from Turkey.",
        D:"Khrushchev backing down unconditionally after Kennedy's naval blockade."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HIST-08",
      scaffold:"The resolution was a diplomatic compromise, not a US victory: (1) Soviet missiles removed from Cuba; (2) USA publicly pledged not to invade Cuba; (3) USA secretly agreed to remove Jupiter missiles from Turkey within 6 months. Khrushchev could present this as a success domestically (Cuba protected). Kennedy presented it as a US victory publicly. The secret Turkey deal was not revealed for decades. Led directly to the Moscow-Washington hotline.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do some historians argue Khrushchev's position in the Cuban Missile Crisis was stronger than it appeared?",options:{A:"The Soviet missiles were more powerful than US intelligence estimated.",B:"He secured the removal of US Jupiter missiles from Turkey, protecting Soviet security interests, while appearing to back down publicly — achieving strategic goals without war.",C:"Cuba became a permanent Soviet military base.",D:"The USA was forced to recognise Soviet nuclear superiority."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-04",
      stem:"Détente in the 1970s is best understood as:",
      options:{
        A:"A period of American military dominance that forced Soviet concessions.",
        B:"A temporary relaxation of Cold War tensions through diplomacy, arms control agreements (SALT I & II), and trade — while both superpowers continued competing in the developing world.",
        C:"A permanent end to Cold War rivalry following Soviet economic collapse.",
        D:"A Chinese-led initiative to mediate between the superpowers."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HIST-09",
      scaffold:"Détente (French: relaxation): Nixon and Kissinger pursued a more pragmatic foreign policy — playing the 'China card' (Nixon visits China 1972), SALT I (1972) limiting strategic nuclear weapons, Helsinki Accords (1975) recognising post-WWII European borders. BUT competition continued in Africa (Angola), Middle East, and indirectly. Détente collapsed with the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979) and Reagan's hawkish response.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which event most clearly signalled the end of détente?",options:{A:"The Iranian Revolution (1979) — Carter's inability to free the hostages damaged his presidency.",B:"Nixon's resignation following Watergate (1974) — ending détente abruptly.",C:"The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979) — Carter boycotted the Moscow Olympics and withdrew SALT II from Senate ratification, ending détente.",D:"The fall of Saigon (1975) — demonstrating US military weakness to the Soviet Union."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-05",
      stem:"NSC-68 (1950) recommended that the USA should:",
      options:{
        A:"Dramatically increase defence spending to counter Soviet military expansion and assert American global leadership.",
        B:"Negotiate directly with the Soviet Union to reduce nuclear stockpiles.",
        C:"Pursue a policy of rollback rather than mere containment.",
        D:"Withdraw from European commitments and focus on defending the Western Hemisphere."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HIST-10",
      scaffold:"NSC-68 (April 1950): top-secret National Security Council report arguing the Soviet threat was existential. Recommended quadrupling US defence spending from $13bn to $50bn annually. Truman had been reluctant to increase military spending, but the Korean War (June 1950) provided the justification. NSC-68 underpinned the militarisation of containment — shifting from Kennan's political/economic containment to military confrontation.",
      reforge:{stem:"How did NSC-68 represent a departure from Kennan's original containment strategy?",options:{A:"It agreed with Kennan that containment should be primarily political and economic.",B:"NSC-68 militarised containment — recommending massive defence spending and treating every Soviet move as a military threat, whereas Kennan envisioned containment as primarily political, economic, and diplomatic.",C:"It proposed negotiating spheres of influence with the Soviets.",D:"It advocated nuclear first-strike capability."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-06",
      stem:"The policy of 'Mutually Assured Destruction' (MAD) was considered stabilising because:",
      options:{
        A:"It gave the USA clear nuclear superiority over the USSR.",
        B:"It encouraged both superpowers to develop conventional military forces instead.",
        C:"It required both sides to seek UN approval before using nuclear weapons.",
        D:"Both sides understood that any nuclear first strike would trigger a devastating retaliatory response, making nuclear war irrational — deterring both sides from initiating conflict."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HIST-11",
      scaffold:"MAD logic: if both sides have second-strike capability (can survive a first strike and retaliate), nuclear war becomes suicidal for the aggressor. This 'balance of terror' was paradoxically stabilising — neither side would initiate nuclear war. BUT it was also incredibly fragile: accidental launches, miscalculation (Able Archer 83, Stanislav Petrov incident 1983), or technical failure could trigger catastrophe. Critics argued it was morally bankrupt.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why did both superpowers invest heavily in second-strike capability (submarines, dispersed silos) rather than simply building more warheads?",options:{A:"Submarines were cheaper to build than land-based missiles.",B:"To ensure that even if a first strike destroyed ground-based missiles, enough nuclear weapons would survive to inflict unacceptable retaliatory damage — making a first strike irrational and maintaining deterrence.",C:"International agreements banned land-based missiles.",D:"Submarines could be used for conventional warfare as well."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-07",
      stem:"The collapse of the Soviet Union (1991) is best explained by which combination of factors?",
      options:{
        A:"Structural economic weaknesses, the cost of the arms race and Afghanistan, Gorbachev's reforms (glasnost and perestroika) which released nationalist and reform pressures the system could not contain, and the demonstration effect of Western prosperity.",
        B:"Reagan's military build-up alone forced Soviet bankruptcy.",
        C:"Gorbachev's reforms were the only cause — the USSR was otherwise stable.",
        D:"The Soviet military staged a coup that ended communist rule."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HIST-12",
      scaffold:"Multi-causal: (1) Structural — Soviet command economy stagnating, technology gap widening; (2) Military overstretch — arms race cost 15-20% of GDP, Afghanistan (1979-89) was 'Soviet Vietnam'; (3) Gorbachev's reforms — glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) were intended to save the system but instead unleashed forces that destroyed it; (4) 1989 revolutions in Eastern Europe removed the buffer; (5) August coup (1991) fatally weakened communist authority. No single cause suffices.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why did Gorbachev's reforms backfire so fatally?",options:{A:"Gorbachev was secretly working for the CIA and deliberately undermined the USSR.",B:"Glasnost permitted public criticism of problems that perestroika couldn't fix fast enough — creating a crisis of legitimacy as people saw failures openly but reforms stalled.",C:"Western economic sanctions prevented Gorbachev's reforms from producing results.",D:"The Soviet military directly opposed and sabotaged every reform Gorbachev attempted."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CW-08",
      stem:"The Korean War (1950–53) was significant for the Cold War primarily because:",
      options:{
        A:"It resulted in a decisive US victory that pushed communism out of Asia.",
        B:"It ended with North Korea joining the Western alliance.",
        C:"It led directly to the formation of NATO.",
        D:"It was the first major military test of containment and demonstrated that the USA would use force to resist communist expansion, ending in stalemate at the 38th parallel — establishing the pattern of limited war."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HIST-13",
      scaffold:"Korea: first hot war of the Cold War. North Korea (Soviet and Chinese backed) invaded South Korea (June 1950). UN forces under MacArthur pushed North Korea back; China intervened when forces approached the Yalu River. Ended in armistice (1953) roughly at the original border. Significance: containment proven viable by force; MacArthur fired for wanting to extend the war to China; established 'limited war' doctrine — fighting to prevent communist gains without triggering WWIII.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was Truman's firing of General MacArthur significant?",options:{A:"MacArthur had lost the Battle for Seoul and Truman dismissed him for incompetence.",B:"It established the principle of civilian control over the military — MacArthur publicly challenged Truman's limited-war strategy, so Truman dismissed him to assert constitutional authority.",C:"MacArthur was found to be passing intelligence to Soviet agents.",D:"His dismissal ended the Korean War immediately by removing the aggressive US commander."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["POL-2"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: UK Government & Branches",
  color: "#1e3a5f",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"POL-09",
      stem:"The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law. Which statement correctly describes its effect on parliamentary sovereignty?",
      options:{
        A:"It allows UK courts to strike down Acts of Parliament that violate Convention rights.",
        B:"It removed Parliament's right to legislate on human rights matters.",
        C:"It transferred human rights jurisdiction entirely to the European Court of Human Rights.",
        D:"Courts can issue a 'declaration of incompatibility' if legislation breaches the Convention, but Parliament retains the sovereign right to change or not change the law."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-POL-05",
      scaffold:"HRA 1998: Section 4 allows courts to declare legislation incompatible with the ECHR — but crucially, this does NOT strike down the law. Parliament remains sovereign and can choose to amend, repeal, or ignore the declaration. This is a key difference from the USA, where the Supreme Court can strike down unconstitutional legislation. The HRA is a constitutional statute (AXA General Insurance v Lord Advocate) but not entrenched.",
      reforge:{stem:"A UK court issues a declaration of incompatibility under the HRA. What must the government do?",options:{A:"Immediately repeal the offending legislation.",B:"Take the case to the European Court of Human Rights.",C:"Nothing — a declaration of incompatibility has no legal binding effect on Parliament; the government may choose to amend the law, but is not legally required to.",D:"Suspend the legislation until Parliament votes."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-10",
      stem:"Insider pressure groups differ from outsider groups primarily in that:",
      options:{
        A:"Insiders have regular, formal access to government ministers and civil servants and are consulted during policy-making, while outsiders are excluded from this process.",
        B:"Insiders have more members and larger budgets.",
        C:"Outsiders use only illegal protest methods.",
        D:"Insiders only operate at local government level."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-POL-06",
      scaffold:"Grant's insider/outsider distinction: Insiders (e.g. BMA, CBI, NFU) are consulted by government, may sit on advisory committees, have direct access to ministers. Outsiders (e.g. Greenpeace, Fathers4Justice) lack this access — either by choice (ideological outsiders like Amnesty UK) or by exclusion (outsiders by necessity). Insider status gives greater influence on policy detail; outsiders rely on public opinion and media pressure. The distinction is not about legality.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a pressure group choose to remain an outsider even if offered insider status?",options:{A:"Insider groups receive less media coverage.",B:"Some groups prioritise ideological independence over influence — accepting insider status might require moderating demands or endorsing government policy they oppose, compromising their campaigning identity.",C:"Outsider groups have legally protected status that insiders lose.",D:"Government only consults insider groups on trivial matters."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-11",
      stem:"Judicial independence in the UK is protected by which mechanism?",
      options:{
        A:"Senior judges hold office 'during good behaviour' and can only be removed by a vote of both Houses of Parliament, insulating them from executive pressure.",
        B:"Judges are elected by the public every five years.",
        C:"The Lord Chancellor personally approves all judicial decisions.",
        D:"Judges are appointed by a cross-party parliamentary committee with a veto."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-POL-07",
      scaffold:"Constitutional Reform Act 2005 strengthened judicial independence: (1) Created the Supreme Court (replacing Law Lords in Parliament); (2) Created Judicial Appointments Commission (independent of Lord Chancellor); (3) Lord Chancellor's role reformed. Senior judges cannot be dismissed by the executive — they hold office 'during good behaviour' and removal requires an address of both Houses. Security of tenure means judges can make unpopular rulings without fear of dismissal.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was the creation of the Supreme Court (2005) significant for judicial independence?",options:{A:"It gave the Supreme Court the power to strike down Acts of Parliament.",B:"It removed the Law Lords from the House of Lords, ending the fusion of judicial and legislative functions — judges no longer sat in Parliament, creating clearer separation of powers and reinforcing the appearance and reality of judicial independence.",C:"It allowed judges to review government spending decisions.",D:"It transferred all appeals from UK courts to the Supreme Court."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-12",
      stem:"Which of the following is the strongest argument that pressure groups enhance democracy?",
      options:{
        A:"They replace political parties as the main vehicle for political participation.",
        B:"Large pressure groups always reflect majority public opinion.",
        C:"They provide a continuous channel for citizen participation between elections, represent specific interests that parties may ignore, and hold government accountable through scrutiny and publicity.",
        D:"They are subject to democratic elections by their members."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-POL-08",
      scaffold:"Pro-democracy arguments: (1) Participation: elections every 5 years are insufficient; pressure groups allow ongoing engagement; (2) Representation: parties must appeal to majorities — pressure groups give voice to minorities and specific interests; (3) Accountability: scrutinise government, expose failures (e.g. Liberty on civil liberties); (4) Information: provide expertise to government. Counter: some groups represent narrow interests, lack internal democracy, and wealthy groups have disproportionate access.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which argument MOST effectively challenges the view that pressure groups enhance democracy?",options:{A:"Pressure groups often have more members than political parties.",B:"Insider groups with financial resources have disproportionate access to government compared to poorly-funded groups representing less wealthy interests — this may reinforce rather than correct existing inequalities of power.",C:"Pressure groups sometimes use illegal protest tactics.",D:"The government is not legally required to listen to pressure groups."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-13",
      stem:"The doctrine of judicial neutrality requires that judges:",
      options:{
        A:"Always rule in favour of the government in constitutional cases.",
        B:"Apply the law as written without allowing personal, political, or moral views to influence their decisions.",
        C:"Consult the public before issuing rulings on controversial matters.",
        D:"Refer all politically sensitive cases to Parliament for decision."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-POL-09",
      scaffold:"Judicial neutrality: judges are supposed to apply the law impartially, without political bias. BUT: critics (Griffith, 'The Politics of the Judiciary') argue senior judges are drawn from a narrow social elite (white, male, public school, Oxbridge) which shapes their outlook. Cases involving trade unions, immigration, or civil liberties show a pattern of conservative rulings. Judicial review is inherently political — deciding whether the executive has acted lawfully involves value judgements.",
      reforge:{stem:"Griffith argued that judicial neutrality is a myth. What was his main evidence?",options:{A:"Judges openly campaign for political parties and publicly declare their views.",B:"Senior judges were disproportionately from elite, white, male, Oxbridge backgrounds — critics argue this produces unconscious bias in interpretation of the law.",C:"Government ministers have powers to direct individual judges on how they should rule.",D:"Judges consistently refused to apply the Human Rights Act after its introduction in 1998."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-14",
      stem:"A student argues that the UK now has a codified constitution in all but name. Which evidence BEST supports this?",
      options:{
        A:"The UK has joined the European Convention on Human Rights.",
        B:"Parliament passes laws using a simple majority like any other legislation.",
        C:"The Prime Minister has a fixed term of four years.",
        D:"A growing body of constitutional statutes (HRA, Constitutional Reform Act, Fixed-term Parliaments Act) are treated by courts as higher law, and constitutional conventions increasingly constrain government behaviour — creating de facto constitutional limits."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-POL-10",
      scaffold:"The 'quasi-codified' argument: (1) Constitutional statutes (Thoburn v Sunderland): courts treat certain Acts (ECA 1972, HRA 1998, etc.) as superior to ordinary legislation — they cannot be impliedly repealed; (2) Supreme Court's Miller judgments (2017, 2019) asserted courts can review prerogative powers; (3) Conventions increasingly formalised. Counter-argument: Parliament can still repeal any statute; the FTPA was repealed in 2022; no formal entrenchment exists.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 was repealed in 2022. What does this reveal about the UK constitution?",options:{A:"Constitutional statutes are fully entrenched and cannot be repealed.",B:"That despite the 'constitutional statute' doctrine, Parliament retains ultimate sovereignty — any Act, however significant, can be repealed by a simple majority, confirming the constitution remains fundamentally uncodified and flexible.",C:"The government acted unconstitutionally in repealing it.",D:"The Supreme Court should have prevented the repeal."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-15",
      stem:"Which case demonstrated most dramatically that UK courts could scrutinise the exercise of royal prerogative?",
      options:{
        A:"Factortame (1990)",
        B:"R (Miller) v Prime Minister [2019] — the Supreme Court ruled the prorogation of Parliament for five weeks was unlawful.",
        C:"AXA General Insurance v Lord Advocate (2011)",
        D:"Entick v Carrington (1765)"
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-POL-11",
      scaffold:"Miller 2 (2019): Boris Johnson prorogued Parliament for 5 weeks during the Brexit crisis. The Supreme Court ruled unanimously that prorogation was unlawful — it had the effect of frustrating Parliament's ability to carry out its constitutional functions without reasonable justification. Parliament was recalled immediately. Significant: the court asserted it could review prerogative powers that had previously been considered non-justiciable — a major expansion of judicial review.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why was Miller 2 (2019) controversial among those who support parliamentary sovereignty?",options:{A:"It allowed the EU to override the UK Parliament's decision on Brexit.",B:"Critics argued the Court overstepped its role by reviewing a political decision — defenders said it correctly upheld the constitutional principle that Parliament must authorise triggering Article 50.",C:"The ruling prevented Brexit from occurring by blocking the referendum result.",D:"It permanently removed the royal prerogative to ratify international treaties."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"POL-16",
      stem:"The Salisbury Convention dictates that the House of Lords should not:",
      options:{
        A:"Delay money bills for more than one month.",
        B:"Reject at second or third reading any government bill that was included in the governing party's manifesto.",
        C:"Appoint more than 800 life peers in total.",
        D:"Amend legislation already passed by the House of Commons."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-POL-12",
      scaffold:"Salisbury Convention (1945): after Labour's landslide, the Conservative-dominated Lords agreed not to wreck manifesto commitments the electorate had endorsed — the Lords would give government bills a second reading even if they opposed them. Distinct from the Parliament Acts (which give the Commons power to pass bills without the Lords after delay). The convention is unwritten and was tested by the Lords' resistance to the Hunting Act 2004 and welfare reform bills.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does the Salisbury Convention limit the democratic legitimacy argument for Lords reform?",options:{A:"The Salisbury Convention proves the Lords already behave democratically in all votes.",B:"Because the Lords follow the convention and don't block manifesto bills, reformers argue democratic reform isn't urgent — the informal check is sufficient.",C:"The Salisbury Convention was formally written into statute law in 2005.",D:"The Lords have never broken the Salisbury Convention in its entire history."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["CS-2"] = {
  label: "Data Structures & Networks",
  color: "#1e3a5f",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CS-09",
      stem:"A stack data structure operates on which principle?",
      options:{
        A:"Last In, First Out — the most recently added item is removed first.",
        B:"First In, First Out — the first item added is the first removed.",
        C:"Random access — any element can be accessed directly by index.",
        D:"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-05",
      scaffold:"Stack: LIFO (Last In, First Out). Think of a stack of plates — you add (push) and remove (pop) from the top only. Operations: push (add to top), pop (remove from top), peek (view top without removing), isEmpty. Used in: call stack (function calls), undo functionality, expression evaluation, depth-first search. Contrasted with a queue (FIFO — first in, first out), which adds at the back and removes from the front.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which real-world scenario is best modelled by a stack?",options:{A:"A printer queue — documents print in the order they were submitted.",B:"A browser's back button — the most recently visited page is the first returned to when you press back.",C:"A hospital waiting list — patients are seen in order of arrival.",D:"A playlist — songs play in the order they were added."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-10",
      stem:"In a binary tree, what is the maximum number of nodes at depth d (where the root is at depth 0)?",
      options:{
        A:"d",
        B:"2d",
        C:"d²",
        D:"2^d"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-06",
      scaffold:"Binary tree: each node has at most 2 children (left and right). At depth 0: 1 node (root = 2⁰). At depth 1: up to 2 nodes (2¹). At depth 2: up to 4 nodes (2²). At depth d: up to 2^d nodes. A perfect binary tree of height h has 2^(h+1) − 1 total nodes. This exponential growth is what makes binary search efficient — each level doubles the number of nodes.",
      reforge:{stem:"A binary search tree has height 4 (root at height 0). What is the maximum total number of nodes it can contain?",options:{A:"16 nodes — 2⁴ = 16 (height 4, leaf nodes only).",B:"31 nodes — using 2^(h+1) − 1 where h = 4 gives 2⁵ − 1 = 31 total nodes.",C:"20 nodes — intermediate guess between 16 and 31.",D:"8 nodes — 2³, which would be height 3."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-11",
      stem:"The OSI model layer responsible for logical addressing (IP addresses) and routing packets between networks is:",
      options:{
        A:"Layer 1 — Physical",
        B:"Layer 2 — Data Link",
        C:"Layer 4 — Transport",
        D:"Layer 3 — Network"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-07",
      scaffold:"OSI model (7 layers, bottom to top): Physical (bits/cables), Data Link (MAC addresses, frames, switches), Network (IP addresses, routing, routers), Transport (TCP/UDP, port numbers, end-to-end delivery), Session, Presentation, Application. The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing — IP addresses — and routing between different networks. Data Link (Layer 2) handles physical addressing within the same network using MAC addresses.",
      reforge:{stem:"A packet travels from a computer in London to a server in Tokyo. Which OSI layer handles the routing decisions along the way?",options:{A:"Layer 2 — Data Link, using MAC addresses.",B:"Layer 4 — Transport, using port numbers.",C:"Layer 7 — Application, using domain names.",D:"Layer 3 — Network, using IP addresses to make routing decisions at each router hop."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-12",
      stem:"TCP differs from UDP primarily in that TCP:",
      options:{
        A:"Provides reliable, ordered delivery through acknowledgements, retransmission of lost packets, and flow control — at the cost of greater overhead and latency.",
        B:"Is faster because it sends data without waiting for acknowledgement.",
        C:"Can only be used for video streaming applications.",
        D:"Operates at the Network layer rather than the Transport layer."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-08",
      scaffold:"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): connection-oriented (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), reliable (acknowledgements + retransmission), ordered (sequence numbers), flow controlled. Used for: web (HTTP/HTTPS), email, file transfer. UDP (User Datagram Protocol): connectionless, no acknowledgements, no ordering, no retransmission — faster but unreliable. Used for: video streaming, online gaming, DNS, VoIP — where speed matters more than perfect delivery.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is UDP preferred over TCP for live video streaming?",options:{A:"UDP provides better data security than TCP for video streams.",B:"UDP is faster — it sends packets without acknowledgements or retransmission, accepting some data loss. Streaming tolerates this; file transfers cannot.",C:"TCP cannot handle the video file formats used in live streaming.",D:"UDP is more reliable than TCP for large file transfers where accuracy matters most."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-13",
      stem:"A queue implemented using an array has a 'rear' pointer at index 7 in an array of size 8. What problem does a circular queue solve?",
      options:{
        A:"It allows the queue to hold more than 8 elements.",
        B:"It sorts elements as they are added.",
        C:"It enables priority-based removal of elements.",
        D:"It reuses vacated front positions by wrapping the rear pointer back to index 0 — preventing false 'full' conditions when space exists at the front of the array."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-09",
      scaffold:"Linear queue problem: after dequeue operations, front pointer advances but those array positions cannot be reused without shifting all elements (O(n)). When rear reaches the end, the array appears full even if spaces exist at the front. Circular queue: uses modular arithmetic ((rear + 1) % size) to wrap around — rear pointer goes 7 → 0 → 1, reusing freed positions. Full condition: (rear + 1) % size == front. More efficient for fixed-size queues.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does a circular queue detect whether it is full?",options:{A:"When the rear pointer equals the array size.",B:"When all elements have the same value.",C:"When (rear + 1) % size == front — the next position rear would advance to is where front currently is, meaning no more space without overwriting existing elements.",D:"When the front pointer reaches index 0."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-14",
      stem:"Public key cryptography (asymmetric encryption) works because:",
      options:{
        A:"The receiver's public key encrypts the message, but only the receiver's mathematically linked private key can decrypt it — the public key cannot reverse its own encryption.",
        B:"Both sender and receiver use the same secret key, shared securely in advance.",
        C:"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm.",
        D:"A trusted third party holds the key and decrypts messages on both sides."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-10",
      scaffold:"Asymmetric encryption: key pair — public key (shared openly) and private key (kept secret). Anyone can encrypt with the public key; only the private key holder can decrypt. Based on mathematical problems that are easy to compute in one direction but infeasible to reverse (e.g. multiplying two large primes is easy; factoring their product is computationally infeasible). Used in HTTPS (TLS), SSH, digital signatures. Solves the key distribution problem of symmetric encryption.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does public key cryptography solve a problem that symmetric encryption cannot?",options:{A:"Asymmetric encryption is mathematically unbreakable; symmetric encryption is not.",B:"Symmetric encryption requires sharing a secret key in advance — a problem over public networks. Asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, avoiding this key-exchange vulnerability.",C:"Public keys are longer and therefore provide stronger encryption by default.",D:"Symmetric encryption cannot physically be used for internet-based communication."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-15",
      stem:"In a graph, a depth-first search (DFS) differs from breadth-first search (BFS) in that DFS:",
      options:{
        A:"Always finds the shortest path between two nodes.",
        B:"Uses a queue to track which nodes to visit next.",
        C:"Visits nodes in order of their distance from the starting node.",
        D:"Explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking, using a stack (or recursion)."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-11",
      scaffold:"DFS: goes deep first — follows a path until it reaches a dead end, then backtracks. Uses a stack (LIFO) or recursion. Suitable for: maze solving, topological sort, cycle detection. BFS: explores all neighbours at the current depth before going deeper. Uses a queue (FIFO). Finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. Memory: DFS uses O(depth) space; BFS uses O(width) space — BFS can use much more memory on wide graphs.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is BFS preferred over DFS for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph?",options:{A:"BFS is always faster than DFS for finding any path in a graph.",B:"DFS finds a path but not necessarily the shortest — it goes deep first. BFS explores level by level and guarantees the shortest path in an unweighted graph.",C:"DFS cannot traverse graphs that contain cycles.",D:"BFS uses less memory than DFS in all cases because it doesn't use a stack."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-16",
      stem:"Normalisation in relational databases aims to:",
      options:{
        A:"Speed up database queries by duplicating frequently accessed data.",
        B:"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.",
        C:"Reduce data redundancy and update anomalies by organising data into well-structured tables with clear dependencies.",
        D:"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CS-12",
      scaffold:"Normalisation: process of structuring a relational database to reduce redundancy and dependency. Key normal forms: 1NF (atomic values, no repeating groups); 2NF (no partial dependencies — non-key attributes depend on the whole primary key); 3NF (no transitive dependencies — non-key attributes depend only on the primary key). Benefits: eliminates update anomalies (changing one row inconsistently), insertion anomalies, deletion anomalies. Trade-off: more joins required for queries.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is an update anomaly, and how does normalisation prevent it?",options:{A:"An error caused by using incorrect SQL syntax in a query.",B:"An update anomaly — duplicated data in a non-normalised table means one fact must be changed in multiple rows; missing one creates inconsistency.",C:"A security vulnerability created by storing unencrypted sensitive data.",D:"Slow query performance caused by joining too many tables simultaneously."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// Additional Paper 1 questions grounded in the current Edexcel B specification
BANKS["GCSE-GEO-HAZ"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-27",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why do many subtropical deserts occur around 30° north and south of the Equator?",options:{A:"Descending air creates high pressure",B:"Rising air creates permanent low pressure and heavy rainfall",C:"Warm ocean currents remove all water vapour from the atmosphere",D:"Polar air sinks directly over every subtropical landmass"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-27",scaffold:"In the Hadley cells, air rises near the Equator, moves poleward at altitude and descends around 30° north and south. Descending air creates high pressure, limiting cloud formation and rainfall, which helps explain the global distribution of arid areas.",reforge:{stem:"A region near 30°S has frequent clear skies and very little rainfall. Which circulation process best explains this?",options:{A:"Air descends, creating high pressure",B:"Warm air rises continuously over the region",C:"The ITCZ remains fixed above the region all year",D:"A polar cell brings saturated air from the South Pole"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-28",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"What can gases trapped in an ice core reveal about past climate?",options:{A:"The exact number of earthquakes in every year",B:"Past air and greenhouse gases",C:"Only the direction of ocean currents",D:"The precise population of each ancient settlement"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-28",scaffold:"Air bubbles trapped in ice preserve samples of past atmospheres, while the ice layers provide a time sequence. Scientists can use them alongside other evidence, such as tree rings and historical records, to reconstruct climate change.",reforge:{stem:"Why are ice cores useful evidence for natural climate change?",options:{A:"They show the exact future temperature for every year",B:"They provide preserved samples from past atmospheres",C:"They record only modern industrial emissions",D:"They measure the strength of every past ocean current"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-29",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which human activity is a significant source of methane?",options:{A:"Building stone walls on upland slopes",B:"Using satellites to track storms",C:"Livestock and landfill",D:"Planting trees in a protected forest"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-29",scaffold:"Methane is released by sources including livestock digestion, rice cultivation, landfill decomposition and fossil-fuel extraction. It is a greenhouse gas, so human activity can increase the enhanced greenhouse effect through more than carbon dioxide alone.",reforge:{stem:"Why can expanded cattle farming contribute to global warming?",options:{A:"Cattle remove all carbon dioxide from the atmosphere",B:"Cattle make ocean currents stop moving",C:"Methane from livestock",D:"Cattle reduce the energy reaching Earth from the Sun"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-30",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why do climate projections for 2100 show a range rather than one certain value?",options:{A:"Climate models cannot use any observed evidence",B:"Future climate is controlled only by daily weather",C:"Sea level cannot respond to temperature change",D:"Future pathways and model limits"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-30",scaffold:"Projections depend on future emissions pathways, the response of ice sheets and oceans, and how models represent feedbacks such as albedo and water vapour. A range communicates uncertainty; it does not mean that climate change evidence is absent.",reforge:{stem:"What is one reason sea-level projections contain uncertainty?",options:{A:"Sea level is unrelated to ocean warming",B:"Tide gauges cannot record any present-day change",C:"All projections assume the same future emissions pathway",D:"Future emissions are not known exactly"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-31",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why do tropical cyclones usually form more than about 5° from the Equator?",options:{A:"Coriolis effect is weak at the Equator",B:"Sea temperatures are always below 27°C at the Equator",C:"High pressure prevents evaporation in all equatorial oceans",D:"The ITCZ never produces rising air near the Equator"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-31",scaffold:"Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water, rising moist air and rotation supplied by the Coriolis effect. The Coriolis effect is too weak at the Equator, so most cyclones form several degrees north or south before tracking poleward.",reforge:{stem:"A tropical cyclone begins at 12°N rather than directly on the Equator. Which condition is especially important?",options:{A:"Sufficient Coriolis rotation",B:"The sea surface must be frozen",C:"Air must sink in the storm centre",D:"The storm must form over a mountain range"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-32",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why does a tropical cyclone often intensify over warm ocean water?",options:{A:"Warm water increases surface friction and removes energy",B:"Condensation releases heat",C:"Cold air sinks into the eye and raises pressure",D:"The Coriolis effect disappears over warm water"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-32",scaffold:"Warm ocean water increases evaporation. Moist air rises, cools and condenses, releasing latent heat that powers further uplift and lowers pressure. This positive feedback can intensify the cyclone while the storm remains over a warm sea.",reforge:{stem:"Why can a tropical cyclone weaken after moving over colder water?",options:{A:"Cold water always increases the storm's rainfall",B:"Colder water supplies less energy",C:"The eye becomes a high-pressure anticyclone immediately",D:"The storm gains more energy from surface friction"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-33",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why are composite volcanoes often more explosive than shield volcanoes?",options:{A:"Their lava is always hotter and more fluid",B:"They only form at conservative boundaries",C:"Viscous magma traps gas",D:"They have no ash or pyroclastic material"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-33",scaffold:"Composite volcanoes commonly form above subduction zones where viscous, silica-rich magma traps gases. Pressure can build until an explosive eruption occurs. Shield volcanoes usually have more fluid basaltic lava and gentler slopes.",reforge:{stem:"A volcano has steep sides and erupts ash and pyroclastic flows. Which explanation is most likely?",options:{A:"It has only low-viscosity basaltic lava",B:"It is a shield volcano at a constructive boundary",C:"Viscous magma traps gases",D:"It has no ash or pyroclastic material"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-HAZ-34",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which is a secondary impact of an earthquake?",options:{A:"Buildings collapsing during the initial shaking",B:"People being injured by falling masonry immediately",C:"Roads being damaged by the first seismic waves",D:"A tsunami after seafloor movement"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-HAZ-34",scaffold:"Primary impacts happen during the earthquake itself, such as collapsed buildings and damaged roads. A tsunami caused by seafloor displacement is a secondary hazard that follows the initial ground movement and can extend impacts along coasts.",reforge:{stem:"Why can a tsunami be classed as a secondary hazard following an undersea earthquake?",options:{A:"It is the same event as the first ground shaking",B:"It only affects inland mountain areas",C:"It happens before the earthquake begins",D:"Seabed displacement follows the shaking"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-DEV"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-35",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What does the Gini coefficient help geographers measure?",options:{A:"Inequality in income or wealth",B:"The annual rainfall of a country",C:"The number of births per woman",D:"The percentage of workers in farming"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-35",scaffold:"The Gini coefficient is an inequality measure. A higher value indicates a less equal distribution, while a lower value indicates greater equality. It complements measures such as GDP per capita and HDI rather than replacing them.",reforge:{stem:"A country has a much higher Gini coefficient than a neighbouring country. What does this suggest?",options:{A:"Less equal income distribution",B:"The country must have lower rainfall",C:"Every household has the same income",D:"The country has no poverty"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-36",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which demographic pattern is most associated with a highly developed country?",options:{A:"High infant mortality and a very youthful population",B:"Low mortality and an ageing population",C:"High fertility and low access to healthcare",D:"A wide population pyramid base caused by high birth rates"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-36",scaffold:"Developed countries generally have lower infant and maternal mortality, longer life expectancy and lower fertility. Their population structures often show a narrower base and a larger older population, although individual countries vary.",reforge:{stem:"Why can a low infant mortality rate be used as evidence of development?",options:{A:"It proves all households have identical incomes",B:"Better healthcare and sanitation reduce deaths",C:"It measures only the size of the manufacturing sector",D:"It means the country has no elderly people"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-37",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"How can colonialism contribute to present-day global inequality?",options:{A:"It guarantees equal trade between all countries",B:"It prevents every country from using its natural resources",C:"Colonial extraction left unequal patterns",D:"It only changes a country's climate"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-37",scaffold:"Colonial rule often organised territories around exporting raw materials and serving external interests. Long-term effects can include unequal infrastructure, borders and trade relationships, although contemporary inequality also has political, environmental and economic causes.",reforge:{stem:"Which example best illustrates a possible long-term economic legacy of colonialism?",options:{A:"All former colonies have identical development levels",B:"Colonial history has no link to present trade patterns",C:"Raw-material exports can persist",D:"A country develops only because it has a large desert"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-38",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which feature is most typical of a bottom-up development strategy?",options:{A:"A national government builds one major dam without local consultation",B:"A TNC chooses the project location using only its own priorities",C:"An international bank funds a capital-city transport scheme",D:"A locally led small project"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-38",scaffold:"Bottom-up strategies are usually small-scale, locally controlled and designed around community needs. Top-down strategies are more likely to be large-scale, externally funded and planned by governments or organisations above the local level.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a bottom-up water project be more successful in a remote village?",options:{A:"It always uses the most expensive technology available",B:"It avoids any local participation",C:"It is designed only for a national capital",D:"Local needs guide maintenance"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-39",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Why have some countries benefited more than others from globalisation?",options:{A:"Infrastructure and skills",B:"Globalisation affects all countries in exactly the same way",C:"Only countries with no exports can attract investment",D:"Countries benefit only when they stop trading"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-39",scaffold:"Globalisation can bring trade, FDI, technology and employment, but benefits vary. Countries with reliable infrastructure, educated workers, stable institutions and market access may attract higher-value investment, while others remain dependent on low-value commodities.",reforge:{stem:"Which factor could help an emerging country gain more from globalisation?",options:{A:"Infrastructure and skills help",B:"Removing all environmental regulation without replacement",C:"Depending on one unprocessed export only",D:"Blocking all foreign trade"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-40",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which is an environmental consequence of rapid economic development?",options:{A:"Economic growth automatically removes all pollution",B:"Industry can raise pollution",C:"Development always reduces energy demand",D:"Manufacturing has no effect on water quality"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-40",scaffold:"Rapid development can raise incomes and services but also increase resource use, industrial emissions, traffic and waste. The balance depends on technology, regulation, energy sources and how benefits and costs are distributed.",reforge:{stem:"Why can rapid industrial growth create a development dilemma?",options:{A:"It prevents any change to living standards",B:"Growth can raise jobs and pollution",C:"It guarantees that every region benefits equally",D:"It removes the need for environmental management"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-41",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What does a proportional flow-line map show particularly well?",options:{A:"The exact age of every person in a country",B:"The height of land along a cross-section",C:"Trade or migration flows",D:"The average rainfall inside one small field"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-41",scaffold:"Proportional flow lines use arrows whose width represents the size of a movement between places. They can show trade, migration or investment flows, although a key and careful interpretation are needed.",reforge:{stem:"On a flow-line map, a much thicker arrow from Country X to Country Y most likely represents:",options:{A:"A lower-value flow in the opposite direction",B:"No movement between the countries",C:"Larger flows",D:"A higher altitude in Country Y"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEV-42",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"How can foreign investment create both benefits and costs for an emerging country?",options:{A:"It always removes local employment",B:"It has no effect on government revenue or development",C:"It only occurs in countries with no natural resources",D:"Jobs and technology, but leakage and pollution"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-DEV-42",scaffold:"FDI by TNCs can provide employment, infrastructure, skills and export earnings. Possible costs include profit leakage, low wages, environmental damage, dependence on external decisions and tax concessions that reduce public revenue.",reforge:{stem:"Which judgement is most balanced when evaluating a TNC factory in an emerging country?",options:{A:"The factory is automatically beneficial because it creates jobs",B:"The factory is automatically harmful because it is foreign-owned",C:"The factory can affect only the company's shareholders",D:"Wages, tax, local links and environmental controls matter"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-INDIA"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-IND-21",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why is GDP per capita more useful than total GDP when comparing average income between countries?",options:{A:"It divides output by population",B:"It measures only the informal economy",C:"It shows how income is distributed exactly",D:"It removes the need for any other development indicator"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-21",scaffold:"GDP per capita is total GDP divided by population, so it gives an average output or income figure that allows countries of different population sizes to be compared. It does not show inequality or every aspect of quality of life.",reforge:{stem:"India has a very large total GDP but a much lower GDP per capita than many developed countries. Why?",options:{A:"A large population shares total output",B:"GDP per capita always ignores population",C:"Total GDP is calculated from rainfall",D:"India has no economic activity"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-22",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which evidence would best show regional inequality within India?",options:{A:"A single national GDP figure",B:"Rural incomes and services lag",C:"India's total land area",D:"The date of India's independence"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-22",scaffold:"National averages can hide differences within a country. Comparing income, infrastructure, healthcare, education or sanitation between richer urban cores and poorer rural regions gives evidence of regional inequality.",reforge:{stem:"Why can a national average hide development problems in India?",options:{A:"National averages include no numerical data",B:"Urban and rural gaps are large",C:"All Indian regions have identical access to services",D:"Averages measure only the climate"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-23",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How has rapid economic development contributed to urbanisation in India?",options:{A:"It has removed all rural employment",B:"It has stopped people moving between regions",C:"City jobs attract migrants",D:"It has reduced the need for housing in cities"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-23",scaffold:"Economic change creates urban employment in manufacturing, services and construction. Rural push factors such as low farm incomes or flooding combine with urban pull factors such as jobs, wages and services to increase rural-to-urban migration.",reforge:{stem:"Which combination best explains rural-to-urban migration in India?",options:{A:"Rural high wages and urban drought",B:"Urban lack of services and rural factory jobs",C:"Rural pushes meet urban jobs",D:"No economic difference between rural and urban areas"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-24",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which is an environmental consequence of India's rapid economic development?",options:{A:"Industrial growth has no effect on water or air quality",B:"Economic growth automatically protects all farmland",C:"Urban expansion removes all waste production",D:"Growth raises pollution"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-24",scaffold:"India's growth has brought jobs, incomes and infrastructure but also serious environmental pressures. Industrial emissions, vehicle pollution, untreated sewage, landfill and greenhouse gases affect people at local, regional and global scales.",reforge:{stem:"Why can development improve living standards while harming the environment?",options:{A:"Environmental impacts occur only in developed countries",B:"Higher incomes always mean less waste",C:"Pollution cannot affect human health",D:"Jobs can bring pollution"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-25",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How has India's changing international role affected its geopolitical influence?",options:{A:"More global influence",B:"India has withdrawn from all international organisations",C:"India no longer trades with the EU or USA",D:"Geopolitical influence is measured only by population"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-25",scaffold:"As India's economy has grown, its influence through organisations such as the G20, BRICS and the WTO, as well as regional and global trade relationships, has increased. This has not removed every conflict or inequality.",reforge:{stem:"Which development is evidence of India's increased international influence?",options:{A:"G20 and BRICS participation",B:"A complete end to trade with major economies",C:"The closure of every Indian company abroad",D:"A fall in all international relationships"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-26",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which is a possible cost of TNC investment in India?",options:{A:"TNCs can never create employment",B:"Profit leakage and tax concessions",C:"TNCs only build schools in rural villages",D:"Foreign investment prevents all economic growth"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-26",scaffold:"TNCs can bring capital, jobs, skills and export income, but profits may be repatriated, local firms may struggle, and low taxes or weak regulation may limit the benefits for public services and the environment.",reforge:{stem:"Why might India offer tax incentives to TNCs despite a possible loss of tax revenue?",options:{A:"To ensure no foreign company invests",B:"Investment can create jobs",C:"To prevent technology transfer",D:"To reduce all employment in cities"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-27",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"What could a proportional flow-line map show about India's international relationships?",options:{A:"The age structure of one city",B:"The depth of the Ganges at each point",C:"Trade and investment flows",D:"The exact height of India's mountains"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-27",scaffold:"A proportional flow-line map uses arrows with widths that represent the size of flows. In India's case, it could show trade, FDI or migration links with regions such as the EU, USA or neighbouring countries.",reforge:{stem:"A wide arrow from India to the USA on a trade map would indicate:",options:{A:"A small flow of goods or services",B:"No trade connection",C:"A larger flow",D:"A high level of rainfall in the USA"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-IND-28",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why might geographers compare a region's value with the national mean?",options:{A:"To calculate the country's total population",B:"To decide whether every household is identical",C:"To measure the length of a coastline",D:"Above or below the national mean"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-IND-28",scaffold:"Comparing a core or periphery region with the national mean helps reveal spatial inequality. A difference from the mean can be calculated for indicators such as income, literacy, life expectancy or access to services.",reforge:{stem:"A rural state has an income 30% below India's national mean. What does this comparison help demonstrate?",options:{A:"The state has no economic activity",B:"Every state has the same income",C:"The national mean is always the lowest value",D:"Below-average regional income"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-URB"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-URB-11",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"What has been a major global trend in urbanisation since 1980?",options:{A:"Urban share is rising",B:"Every world region has become urbanised at the same rate",C:"Cities have disappeared from lower-income countries",D:"Urbanisation has stopped because rural populations are growing"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-11",scaffold:"The global urban share has risen since 1980, although rates differ between regions. Many developing and emerging countries are urbanising rapidly, while some developed countries have a slower or more mature urban transition.",reforge:{stem:"Why can urbanisation rates differ between global regions?",options:{A:"Countries urbanise at different stages",B:"Every country has identical migration patterns",C:"Urbanisation is controlled only by latitude",D:"Rural-to-urban migration never occurs"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-12",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"What is a megacity?",options:{A:"A city with the highest buildings in a country",B:"Over 10 million people",C:"A city containing only formal employment",D:"A capital city with no informal settlements"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-12",scaffold:"Edexcel defines a megacity as an urban area with a population of over 10 million. Megacities can have very different levels of wealth, functions, infrastructure and quality of life.",reforge:{stem:"Which statement correctly uses the term megacity?",options:{A:"Every capital city is automatically a megacity",B:"At least 10 million residents",C:"A megacity has no rural population nearby",D:"A megacity can only be located in a developed country"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-13",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"What does urban primacy describe?",options:{A:"The number of parks in a city",B:"The movement of people from a city to a village",C:"One city dominates",D:"The order in which buildings were constructed"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-13",scaffold:"Urban primacy occurs when one city dominates a country's population, economy or political influence compared with other cities. It can attract investment and services but may increase regional inequality.",reforge:{stem:"A country has one city that is far larger and more economically powerful than all other cities. This is evidence of:",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation",B:"De-industrialisation",C:"Urban primacy",D:"A rural settlement hierarchy"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-14",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Which is an example of informal employment in a rapidly growing city?",options:{A:"A worker with a permanent contract in a registered bank",B:"A civil servant working in a government office",C:"An employee in a formal factory with legal protections",D:"An unregistered street trader"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-14",scaffold:"Informal employment is often unregistered and lacks secure contracts, legal protection or regular benefits. It can provide vital livelihoods when formal jobs are insufficient, but conditions may be insecure and poorly paid.",reforge:{stem:"Why can informal employment be important in a developing or emerging city?",options:{A:"It guarantees a pension and paid leave for everyone",B:"It only exists inside large multinational companies",C:"It removes the need for urban services",D:"Informal work supplies income"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-15",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"What is counter-urbanisation?",options:{A:"Movement from urban areas to rural areas",B:"Movement from rural areas to the city centre",C:"Growth of a city's CBD through new offices",D:"The replacement of formal jobs by informal jobs"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-15",scaffold:"Counter-urbanisation is the movement of people from urban areas to rural areas, often because of high house prices, congestion or a preference for a quieter environment. It differs from urbanisation, which is movement towards urban areas.",reforge:{stem:"A family leaves a crowded city for a cheaper village within commuting distance. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation",B:"Urbanisation",C:"Rural depopulation caused by drought",D:"Urban primacy"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-16",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Why is accessibility an important influence on urban land use?",options:{A:"Accessibility determines the colour of every building",B:"Accessible land attracts businesses",C:"Accessible land is always the cheapest land",D:"Accessibility prevents all residential development"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-16",scaffold:"Accessible sites near roads, railways or central locations are attractive to businesses and services because workers, customers and deliveries can reach them more easily. High accessibility can also make land expensive.",reforge:{stem:"Why are offices often found in accessible central locations?",options:{A:"Central sites are always free from congestion",B:"Businesses need workers and transport",C:"Planning rules require every office to be rural",D:"Office workers do not need transport"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-17",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Which comparison would provide the strongest evidence that quality of life varies within a megacity?",options:{A:"The city has one average population figure",B:"Every district has the same road network",C:"Wealthy services versus poor housing",D:"The city has more than one type of building"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-17",scaffold:"Quality of life can vary within one megacity because income, housing, services, employment and environmental conditions differ between areas. A comparison should use evidence from contrasting neighbourhoods rather than only a city-wide average.",reforge:{stem:"Which pair of indicators would best compare quality of life between two city neighbourhoods?",options:{A:"Building colour and road names",B:"Distance from the Equator and annual rainfall",C:"Water access and income",D:"The number of countries in the city"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URB-18",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Which approach is most likely to improve a megacity settlement sustainably?",options:{A:"Remove all residents without offering alternative housing",B:"Build a distant high-rise estate without transport links",C:"Ignore informal businesses when planning redevelopment",D:"Upgrade services with residents"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-URB-18",scaffold:"Sustainable strategies should improve quality of life while recognising residents' livelihoods and reducing environmental pressure. In-situ upgrading can provide services and safer housing without automatically removing communities or destroying local economic networks.",reforge:{stem:"Why can resident participation improve an urban-upgrading project?",options:{A:"It guarantees that no government funding is needed",B:"It means infrastructure standards can be ignored",C:"It prevents any change to housing conditions",D:"Residents shape sustainable upgrades"},correct:"D"}}
);

// Additional Paper 2 questions grounded in the current Edexcel B specification
BANKS["GCSE-GEO-UKLAND"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-31",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Which landform is most strongly associated with glacial erosion in the UK?",options:{A:"U-shaped valley",B:"A spit formed by longshore drift",C:"A beach built by constructive waves",D:"A levee deposited beside a lower-course river"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-31",scaffold:"Glacial erosion widens and deepens a V-shaped river valley, producing a broad U-shaped valley with steep sides. The other options are mainly coastal or fluvial depositional features.",reforge:{stem:"A broad valley has steep sides and a flat floor. Which process most likely shaped it?",options:{A:"Glacial erosion",B:"Longshore drift moving beach sediment",C:"Deposition beside a river mouth",D:"Constructive waves building a beach"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-32",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Why can granite form upland landscapes in the UK?",options:{A:"It is always deposited by rivers in floodplains",B:"It is relatively resistant to erosion",C:"It dissolves faster than most surrounding rocks",D:"It is a soft sediment deposited by longshore drift"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-32",scaffold:"Granite is an intrusive igneous rock that is generally resistant to erosion. Where surrounding rocks are worn away more quickly, granite can remain as higher relief or upland terrain.",reforge:{stem:"A granite area stands above nearby weaker rocks. What best explains this relief?",options:{A:"Granite is made from beach sediment",B:"Granite resists erosion",C:"Granite is always found in lowland basins",D:"Granite is transported downstream by traction"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-33",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A cliff retreats from 120 m to 90 m in 10 years. What is the mean erosion rate?",options:{A:"12 m per year",B:"30 m per year",C:"3 m per year",D:"0.3 m per year"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-33",scaffold:"Mean erosion rate is total retreat divided by time: (120 − 90) ÷ 10 = 30 ÷ 10 = 3 metres per year. The calculation uses change in distance, not the final distance alone.",reforge:{stem:"A cliff retreats from 100 m to 80 m in 5 years. What is the mean rate of retreat?",options:{A:"20 m per year",B:"5 m per year",C:"4 m per year",D:"0.4 m per year"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-34",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What does lag time on a storm hydrograph measure?",options:{A:"The total rainfall in a storm",B:"The highest discharge recorded",C:"The length of the river channel",D:"Rain peak to discharge peak"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-34",scaffold:"Lag time is the interval between peak rainfall and peak discharge. A short lag time means the river responds quickly; a longer lag gives more time for warning and can reduce the immediate flood peak.",reforge:{stem:"On a storm hydrograph, what does a long lag time show?",options:{A:"Rainfall has stopped before the storm began",B:"The river has no drainage basin",C:"Discharge is always at its maximum",D:"Peak follows rainfall later"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-35",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"How can a groyne reduce erosion on part of a beach?",options:{A:"It traps sediment moving alongshore",B:"It increases the force of every destructive wave",C:"It makes the entire coast resistant to weathering",D:"It stops all water entering the drainage basin"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-35",scaffold:"Groynes interrupt longshore drift and build a wider beach on their up-drift side. The extra sediment absorbs wave energy, although the down-drift coastline may receive less sediment.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a wider beach form beside a groyne?",options:{A:"It traps sediment",B:"The groyne removes every wave from the coast",C:"The beach becomes a river floodplain",D:"The rock underneath stops all sub-aerial processes"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-36",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Why is floodplain zoning used?",options:{A:"To encourage new housing on the highest-risk land",B:"To keep high-risk land from new housing",C:"To make every river channel deeper",D:"To prevent all farming beside rivers"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-36",scaffold:"Floodplain zoning limits development in areas likely to flood. It reduces exposure of people and property while allowing some compatible uses, such as recreation or carefully managed agriculture.",reforge:{stem:"Which planning decision best reduces flood exposure?",options:{A:"Building more homes on the floodplain",B:"Restricting development in high-risk zones",C:"Removing all wetlands from the river valley",D:"Paving the whole floodplain for car parks"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-37",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What is a likely feature of strategic realignment on a coast?",options:{A:"A new sea wall protects every metre of shoreline",B:"Groynes are placed at regular intervals along the whole coast",C:"Selected land is allowed to flood and form new habitats",D:"Beach sediment is removed to expose the cliff base"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-37",scaffold:"Strategic realignment allows the sea to move inland in selected locations, often creating salt marsh or mudflat that absorbs wave energy. It can be more sustainable but may require land and property to be surrendered.",reforge:{stem:"Why can strategic realignment reduce long-term flood risk?",options:{A:"It forces all waves to break offshore",B:"It removes the need to understand coastal processes",C:"New wetlands can absorb wave energy",D:"It makes every coastal community equally protected"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKLAND-38",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Which river transport process moves large pebbles by rolling them along the bed?",options:{A:"Suspension",B:"Saltation",C:"Solution",D:"Traction"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-UKLAND-38",scaffold:"Traction is the rolling of large particles along the river bed. Saltation involves smaller particles bouncing, suspension carries fine sediment within the water and solution carries dissolved material.",reforge:{stem:"A heavy cobble rolls along the bed of a river. Which process is this?",options:{A:"Suspension of fine silt",B:"Saltation of sand",C:"Solution of minerals",D:"Traction"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-UKHUMAN"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-21",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What does a population pyramid with a very broad base usually indicate?",options:{A:"High birth rate",B:"A large elderly population and low fertility",C:"No migration into the area",D:"A population made up only of working-age adults"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-21",scaffold:"A broad base represents many children and usually suggests a high birth rate or youthful population. A narrow base is more typical of an ageing population with lower fertility.",reforge:{stem:"A district's population pyramid has many children but few older people. What is most likely?",options:{A:"A youthful population",B:"A very low birth rate",C:"No schools are needed",D:"The district has no working-age residents"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-22",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Why is population density often higher in an urban core than in a rural area?",options:{A:"Rural areas always have taller buildings",B:"Jobs and services attract people",C:"Urban cores have no transport connections",D:"Agriculture requires every household to live centrally"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-22",scaffold:"Urban cores concentrate employment, education, healthcare, shops and transport. These opportunities attract residents and support denser housing than is usually found in rural areas.",reforge:{stem:"Which factor commonly attracts people towards an urban core?",options:{A:"Fewer services and longer journeys",B:"Employment and transport links",C:"A complete absence of housing pressure",D:"Less access to education and healthcare"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-23",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Which activity belongs to the quaternary sector?",options:{A:"Coal mining",B:"Car manufacturing",C:"Scientific research",D:"Running a restaurant"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-23",scaffold:"The quaternary sector involves information, knowledge and research, such as scientific development or data services. Primary extracts resources, secondary manufactures and tertiary provides services.",reforge:{stem:"A university research laboratory is part of which economic sector?",options:{A:"Primary",B:"Secondary",C:"Quaternary",D:"Agricultural"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-24",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is foreign direct investment (FDI)?",options:{A:"Money sent by tourists to a holiday destination",B:"A government census of overseas residents",C:"Trade in second-hand goods between households",D:"Foreign firm investment"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-24",scaffold:"FDI occurs when a company or investor from one country invests in productive activity in another, such as opening a factory, office or distribution centre. It can create jobs but may also increase external influence.",reforge:{stem:"A Japanese company builds and operates a factory in the UK. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Domestic migration",B:"Deindustrialisation",C:"A government subsidy",D:"Foreign investment"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-25",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is the main purpose of an enterprise zone?",options:{A:"Incentives attract businesses",B:"It prevents all firms from locating there",C:"It replaces every local service with farming",D:"It guarantees that no land-use conflict occurs"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-25",scaffold:"Enterprise zones use incentives such as reduced taxes, simpler planning or improved infrastructure to attract firms and jobs. Their benefits may be uneven and they can move activity from nearby areas.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a government create an enterprise zone in a declining area?",options:{A:"Attract investment",B:"To stop new businesses opening",C:"To reduce transport connections",D:"To remove all public services"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-26",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Which is an example of rural diversification?",options:{A:"Closing every farm shop in a village",B:"A farm adding visitor accommodation",C:"Replacing all rural employment with mining",D:"Moving a city CBD into a national park"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-26",scaffold:"Rural diversification adds new income streams alongside traditional farming, such as farm shops, accommodation, leisure or tourism. It can support employment but may increase traffic or environmental pressure.",reforge:{stem:"How might a farm diversify its income?",options:{A:"By ending all visitor activity",B:"By adding a farm café or holiday lets",C:"By reducing the range of local services",D:"By moving its fields into the city centre"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-27",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is one likely effect of counter-urbanisation on a popular rural area?",options:{A:"Housing becomes cheaper for every local resident",B:"Urban employment disappears immediately",C:"Housing demand can raise local prices",D:"The rural area loses all transport links"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-27",scaffold:"People moving from cities to accessible rural areas can increase demand for homes and services. This may raise prices and create affordability problems for younger residents, even while bringing spending and new skills.",reforge:{stem:"Why might counter-urbanisation disadvantage some young people in a rural village?",options:{A:"It removes every source of employment",B:"It makes all homes empty throughout the year",C:"Higher housing demand",D:"It prevents any new services opening"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-28",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Which judgement best summarises a possible TNC impact on the UK?",options:{A:"TNCs only affect local weather",B:"TNCs always remove all UK jobs",C:"TNCs cannot change regional economies",D:"Jobs and capital, but profit leakage"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-UKHUMAN-28",scaffold:"TNCs may bring investment, employment, technology and links to global markets. However, profits may leave the UK, decisions may be made elsewhere and local firms may face stronger competition.",reforge:{stem:"Which statement gives a balanced view of foreign investment in a UK region?",options:{A:"It is automatically beneficial in every community",B:"It is automatically harmful because the firm is foreign",C:"It affects only the company's overseas headquarters",D:"It can create jobs while profits leave the region"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-ENQUIRY"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-22",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"Which sampling method gives every member of a population an equal chance of selection?",options:{A:"Random sampling",B:"Systematic sampling at fixed intervals",C:"Convenience sampling from nearby people",D:"Stratified sampling using known groups"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-22",scaffold:"Random sampling uses a method such as random numbers so each member has an equal chance of selection. Systematic and stratified methods can also be representative but use additional structure.",reforge:{stem:"A researcher uses random numbers to choose households from a complete list. Which method is this?",options:{A:"Random sampling",B:"Opportunistic sampling",C:"Quota sampling",D:"Systematic sampling"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-23",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"What is a bar chart particularly useful for showing?",options:{A:"A continuous relationship between two measurements",B:"Comparisons between categories",C:"The exact location of points using coordinates",D:"The cumulative total of a data set"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-23",scaffold:"Bar charts compare separate categories, with gaps between bars. Line graphs are more suitable for continuous change, while scatter graphs show relationships between two variables.",reforge:{stem:"A class wants to compare mean EQS scores at four separate sites. Which display is suitable?",options:{A:"A scatter graph with no categories",B:"A bar chart",C:"A flow-line map",D:"A triangular graph"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-24",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"Why can percentage change be useful when comparing fieldwork results?",options:{A:"It removes all sampling error",B:"It proves that one variable caused another",C:"Compare relative change",D:"It replaces the need to record original values"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-24",scaffold:"Percentage change puts a change into context by comparing it with the starting value. This allows results with different scales to be compared, although the original values should still be reported.",reforge:{stem:"Why might percentage change be preferred to raw change when comparing two sites?",options:{A:"It makes all results identical",B:"It removes the need for a hypothesis",C:"Compare different starts",D:"It guarantees a reliable conclusion"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-25",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"What does standard deviation describe?",options:{A:"The most common result",B:"The middle result in an ordered set",C:"The difference between the highest and lowest result",D:"The spread of results around the mean"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-25",scaffold:"Standard deviation measures how spread out results are around the mean. A smaller value means readings are more tightly clustered; a larger value means greater variation.",reforge:{stem:"A data set has a large standard deviation. What does this suggest?",options:{A:"Every reading is identical",B:"The median must equal the mean",C:"The sample contains no variation",D:"Spread around mean"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-26",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"Why should fieldwork measurements be repeated?",options:{A:"To improve reliability",B:"To guarantee that the hypothesis is correct",C:"To remove the need for a sampling method",D:"To make every result equal to the mean"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-26",scaffold:"Repeating measurements helps identify anomalies and gives a more reliable average. It does not guarantee accuracy or prove a hypothesis, especially if the method itself is biased.",reforge:{stem:"What is a main benefit of taking several velocity readings at one site?",options:{A:"The mean is more reliable",B:"The equipment cannot produce errors",C:"The hypothesis becomes automatically true",D:"The site no longer needs to be described"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-27",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"What does validity mean in fieldwork?",options:{A:"The data was collected quickly",B:"Measures the intended variable",C:"The results contain a large sample",D:"The graph has a title and key"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-27",scaffold:"A valid method measures the intended variable. For example, a flow meter is more valid than a float for measuring mean river velocity because it is designed for that purpose.",reforge:{stem:"A questionnaire asks about transport but only offers answers about housing. Which quality is weakest?",options:{A:"Reliability",B:"Validity",C:"Sample size",D:"Precision of the graph"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-28",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"Why is a scatter graph useful in geographical enquiry?",options:{A:"It shows the order of survey questions",B:"It locates land uses on an OS map",C:"Shows relationships",D:"It displays one total for each category"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-28",scaffold:"A scatter graph plots paired measurements for two variables and can show positive, negative or no correlation. Correlation alone does not prove causation.",reforge:{stem:"A group compares river width with discharge at several sites. Which graph best shows the relationship?",options:{A:"A pie chart",B:"A bar chart",C:"Scatter graph",D:"A choropleth map"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENQ-29",spec:"GCSE-ENQUIRY",stem:"What is the purpose of a line of best fit on a scatter graph?",options:{A:"To show every reading exactly",B:"To calculate the sample size",C:"To replace the original data",D:"To show the overall trend"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-ENQUIRY-29",scaffold:"A line of best fit summarises the general pattern in paired data. It should not be forced through every point and does not prove that one variable causes the other.",reforge:{stem:"A line of best fit slopes upwards. What does it indicate?",options:{A:"Every point has the same value",B:"The data has no relationship",C:"The sample must be biased",D:"Positive trend"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-RIVERFIELD"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-11",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"How is river discharge calculated?",options:{A:"Area × velocity",B:"Channel width divided by gradient",C:"Rainfall divided by drainage-basin area",D:"Velocity multiplied by lag time"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-11",scaffold:"Discharge is the volume of water passing a point each second. It is calculated as cross-sectional area multiplied by mean velocity, usually expressed in cubic metres per second.",reforge:{stem:"A river has a cross-sectional area of 2 m² and mean velocity of 0.5 m/s. What is its discharge?",options:{A:"1 m³/s",B:"2.5 m³/s",C:"4 m³/s",D:"0.25 m³/s"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-12",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"Why does channel width usually increase downstream?",options:{A:"The river always becomes steeper",B:"Discharge usually increases",C:"Sediment disappears from the channel",D:"The drainage basin becomes smaller"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-12",scaffold:"Tributaries add water and discharge generally increases downstream. Greater discharge and valley development allow the channel to become wider and deeper, although local geology and human activity can modify the pattern.",reforge:{stem:"Which process helps explain a wider channel downstream?",options:{A:"Less water enters the river",B:"Tributaries add to discharge",C:"The river loses all sediment",D:"The valley becomes narrower everywhere"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-13",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"What does a clinometer measure when used to investigate river gradient?",options:{A:"River discharge",B:"The size of bedload",C:"The angle of slope",D:"The volume of rainfall"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-13",scaffold:"A clinometer measures the angle between a sight line and the horizontal. Repeated readings can be used to estimate the gradient between two points along the River Darent.",reforge:{stem:"A student uses a clinometer between two ranging poles. What are they measuring?",options:{A:"Channel depth",B:"Sediment roundness",C:"Slope angle",D:"Flood frequency"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-14",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"Which hazard should be included in a risk assessment beside the River Darent?",options:{A:"A lack of global trade",B:"A change in population density",C:"A difference in rock type",D:"Slippery banks and deep water"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-14",scaffold:"River fieldwork risks include slippery banks, deep or fast-flowing water, unstable ground and weather changes. A risk assessment should identify hazards and explain control measures such as staying away from the channel edge.",reforge:{stem:"Which control would reduce risk during river fieldwork?",options:{A:"Ignore the weather forecast",B:"Stand in the deepest flow",C:"Work alone at an unfamiliar site",D:"Keep away from unstable banks"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-15",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"What does Bradshaw's model predict about sediment downstream?",options:{A:"It becomes smaller and rounder",B:"It becomes larger and more angular",C:"It disappears before reaching the middle course",D:"It changes into impermeable rock"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-15",scaffold:"Bradshaw's model predicts that sediment becomes smaller and rounder downstream because repeated collisions cause attrition and smaller particles are transported more easily.",reforge:{stem:"A pebble at a lower-course site is smoother than one near the source. Which process helps explain this?",options:{A:"Attrition during transport",B:"Less erosion at the source",C:"Glacial deposition in the channel",D:"A shorter river long profile"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-16",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"Which graph is most suitable for showing channel width at several sites along the River Darent?",options:{A:"A pie chart",B:"A line graph",C:"A triangular graph",D:"A choropleth map"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-16",scaffold:"A line graph can show how a continuous measurement changes between ordered sites along the river. Sites should be placed in downstream order and the axes should include units.",reforge:{stem:"Which display best shows how Darent channel width changes from source to mouth?",options:{A:"A pie chart",B:"A line graph",C:"A barometer",D:"A proportional symbol map"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-17",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"Why take depth readings at regular intervals across a river channel?",options:{A:"To make the channel steeper",B:"To remove all measurement error",C:"Regular cross-section",D:"To measure air temperature"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-17",scaffold:"Regular intervals provide a systematic cross-section of depth. The readings can be plotted to estimate cross-sectional area and help compare channel shape between sites.",reforge:{stem:"A student measures depth every 0.5 m across a channel. What is the main advantage?",options:{A:"It guarantees no anomalous reading",B:"It changes the river's flow",C:"Regular depth data",D:"It measures only the deepest point"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-RVF-18",spec:"GCSE-RIVERFIELD",stem:"What is a limitation of using a float to measure river velocity?",options:{A:"It cannot move with the current",B:"It only works in dry channels",C:"It measures the river's discharge directly",D:"It mainly measures surface speed"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-RIVERFIELD-18",scaffold:"A float travels at or near the surface, where velocity may be faster than the average through the whole channel. A flow meter can measure velocity at selected depths more directly.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a flow meter improve on a float method?",options:{A:"It removes the need to measure distance",B:"It measures rainfall instead of water",C:"It only records the fastest surface current",D:"It can measure velocity below the surface"},correct:"D"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-URBFIELD"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-URF-11",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"What is the purpose of an Environmental Quality Survey (EQS)?",options:{A:"It scores environmental features",B:"It measures national migration totals",C:"It calculates river discharge",D:"It records only the age of buildings"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-11",scaffold:"An EQS converts observations such as litter, noise, building condition and green space into scores. Using the same criteria at each Stratford site supports comparison, although scoring can remain subjective.",reforge:{stem:"Why use the same EQS criteria at Old Stratford and the Olympic Park?",options:{A:"To compare environmental quality",B:"To make the sites identical",C:"To measure population change directly",D:"To replace all observations with census data"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-12",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"A questionnaire asks respondents to choose strongly agree, agree, disagree or strongly disagree. What type of scale is this?",options:{A:"A ratio scale",B:"Ordinal scale",C:"A grid reference",D:"A random sample"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-12",scaffold:"These ordered response categories form a Likert or ordinal scale. The responses have a clear order, but the intervals between categories cannot be assumed to be equal.",reforge:{stem:"Which scale is commonly used to record attitudes towards regeneration?",options:{A:"A nominal scale with no order",B:"Likert scale",C:"A contour scale",D:"A time-series axis"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-13",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"Why might stratified sampling be used in Stratford fieldwork?",options:{A:"It selects only the easiest people to reach",B:"It guarantees that every answer is positive",C:"It represents important groups or zones",D:"It removes the need for a clear hypothesis"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-13",scaffold:"Stratified sampling divides the population into relevant groups or zones and samples from each. This can ensure that contrasting areas such as Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate and East Village are represented.",reforge:{stem:"A class samples equal numbers of residents from four Stratford zones. Which aim does this support?",options:{A:"Avoiding all comparison between zones",B:"Sampling only visitors",C:"Representing contrasting zones",D:"Making the questionnaire anonymous"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-14",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"Which is a useful source of secondary data for comparing urban quality of life?",options:{A:"A student's new EQS score",B:"A fresh pedestrian count",C:"A field sketch made today",D:"Census or IMD data"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-14",scaffold:"Census and Index of Multiple Deprivation data are secondary sources because they were collected by another organisation. They can provide wider context for primary observations in Stratford.",reforge:{stem:"Which source was collected before the Stratford fieldwork by another organisation?",options:{A:"Today's questionnaire answers",B:"This morning's traffic count",C:"A student's site photograph",D:"Census data"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-15",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"Why repeat pedestrian counts at different times of day?",options:{A:"To show time variation",B:"To make the street permanently busier",C:"To remove all sampling limitations",D:"To prove that regeneration caused every movement"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-15",scaffold:"Pedestrian flows vary with school, work, shopping and leisure patterns. Counts at different times reveal temporal variation and are more representative than one isolated observation.",reforge:{stem:"What is the main benefit of counting pedestrians at 9am, 1pm and 3pm?",options:{A:"It captures changes through the day",B:"It guarantees the same number each time",C:"It measures building age directly",D:"It removes the need to record weather"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-16",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"What makes a fieldwork hypothesis testable?",options:{A:"It uses only opinions",B:"Measurable variables",C:"It cannot be disproved",D:"It avoids naming a location"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-16",scaffold:"A testable hypothesis links variables that can be observed or measured, such as environmental quality and distance from a regeneration site. It should be precise enough to guide data collection.",reforge:{stem:"Which hypothesis is most testable for Stratford fieldwork?",options:{A:"Stratford feels interesting",B:"Measurable EQS trend",C:"Regeneration is always good",D:"The area has many changes"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-17",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"What is gentrification?",options:{A:"The complete removal of public transport",B:"A fall in all urban land values",C:"Investment changes an area",D:"The movement of every resident to a rural area"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-17",scaffold:"Gentrification involves investment and wealthier residents changing a neighbourhood. It may improve buildings and services but can raise rents and displace lower-income residents.",reforge:{stem:"Which change could indicate gentrification near a regeneration project?",options:{A:"Falling investment and empty shops",B:"Lower rents for every resident",C:"Rising investment and rents",D:"The end of all population change"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-URF-18",spec:"GCSE-URBANFIELD",stem:"What should a strong evaluation of a fieldwork conclusion do?",options:{A:"Repeat the hypothesis without evidence",B:"List equipment without explaining its effects",C:"Ignore anomalies and sampling choices",D:"Compare evidence with the hypothesis"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-URBANFIELD-18",scaffold:"A strong evaluation returns to the hypothesis, uses the results and considers limitations such as sample size, timing, subjectivity and anomalies. It should explain how those issues affect confidence in the conclusion.",reforge:{stem:"Which statement best evaluates a Stratford conclusion?",options:{A:"The hypothesis was correct because it sounded sensible",B:"Only the weather mattered",C:"The sample was perfect because it had a graph",D:"Limited sites reduce confidence"},correct:"D"}}
);

// Additional Paper 3 questions grounded in the current Edexcel B specification
BANKS["GCSE-GEO-BIOSPHERE"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-20",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"A climate graph shows high temperatures and heavy rainfall in every month. Which biome is the best match?",options:{A:"Tropical rainforest",B:"Temperate grassland with a cool winter",C:"Boreal forest with a short growing season",D:"Tundra with permanently frozen subsoil"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-20",scaffold:"A tropical rainforest climate is hot and wet throughout the year, allowing continuous plant growth and supporting high biodiversity. Climate graphs should be read using both temperature and precipitation, not just latitude.",reforge:{stem:"Which climate pattern would best support a tropical rainforest biome?",options:{A:"Low rainfall with a large daily temperature range",B:"Year-round heat and rainfall",C:"Cold winters with a brief summer thaw",D:"Warm summers followed by freezing winters"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-21",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why can fewer sunshine hours reduce the productivity of a biome?",options:{A:"They permanently increase soil depth and drainage",B:"Less light limits photosynthesis",C:"They make every plant species migrate immediately",D:"Less light always causes precipitation to stop"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-21",scaffold:"Plants need light for photosynthesis. Fewer sunshine hours can shorten or weaken the growing season, reducing biomass production and the food available to consumers.",reforge:{stem:"A high-latitude biome receives very little winter sunlight. What is the most direct effect on plants?",options:{A:"Soil nutrients increase without decomposition",B:"Precipitation becomes impossible",C:"Photosynthesis and growth slow",D:"All vegetation becomes broad-leaved"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-22",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How can poor drainage alter a biome locally?",options:{A:"It changes the latitude of the location",B:"It guarantees a longer period of sunshine",C:"Waterlogged soil favours tolerant plants",D:"It makes the underlying rock more resistant"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-22",scaffold:"Drainage is a local abiotic control. Waterlogged soils contain less oxygen, so species that tolerate saturated conditions can replace plants that need freely drained ground, even within the same regional climate.",reforge:{stem:"Two nearby slopes share the same climate, but one has thin alkaline soil over limestone. Why might their vegetation differ?",options:{A:"The slopes must be located in entirely different climate zones",B:"Altitude has no effect on plant growth",C:"Rainfall cannot reach limestone areas",D:"Local soil conditions select different plants"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-23",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How does forest vegetation help regulate water in the hydrological cycle?",options:{A:"It prevents all evaporation from the land surface",B:"It converts groundwater directly into ocean currents",C:"It stops precipitation reaching a drainage basin",D:"Interception and roots slow runoff"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-23",scaffold:"Leaves intercept rainfall while roots encourage infiltration and store water. Forest vegetation also returns moisture to the atmosphere through transpiration, helping regulate flows through the hydrological cycle.",reforge:{stem:"Why can removing forest increase the risk of rapid river flooding?",options:{A:"Less interception increases surface runoff",B:"Deforestation makes all rainfall evaporate",C:"Tree removal lowers every river channel",D:"Bare soil stores more water than an established network of roots"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-24",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Which example best shows Indigenous use of a biosphere resource?",options:{A:"A community gathers plants for medicine",B:"A global company strip-mines an entire region",C:"A national grid floods valleys for electricity",D:"An export firm clears forest for hardwood"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-24",scaffold:"Indigenous and local communities may obtain food, medicine, building materials and fuel directly from their environment. This differs in scale and purpose from commercial exploitation for global mineral, timber, water or energy markets.",reforge:{stem:"What most clearly distinguishes local subsistence use from commercial exploitation of a biome?",options:{A:"Local use can never alter an ecosystem",B:"Resources mainly meet community needs",C:"Commercial activity uses no machinery",D:"Only commercial users collect fuel wood"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-25",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why can industrialisation increase pressure on the biosphere?",options:{A:"Factories always use fewer materials than farms",B:"Industry raises energy and material demand",C:"Industrialisation prevents urban growth and migration",D:"Manufacturing removes the need for water supplies"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-25",scaffold:"Industrial growth requires energy, water, minerals and land, while also generating waste. Alongside population growth, urbanisation and rising affluence, this increases demand for the biosphere's goods and regulating services.",reforge:{stem:"Which combination is most likely to raise national resource consumption?",options:{A:"Falling population and less industrial output",B:"Lower incomes and reduced urbanisation",C:"Rising affluence and industrial growth",D:"Less manufacturing and lower energy demand"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-26",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"On a population projection graph, what does a widening gap between high and low projections show?",options:{A:"Both projections predict identical population totals",B:"Population must stop changing after the base year",C:"Uncertainty grows further into the future",D:"The lower projection has the steeper growth rate"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-26",scaffold:"Population projections use assumptions about future fertility, mortality and migration. As the time horizon increases, different assumptions compound, so the range between scenarios often becomes wider.",reforge:{stem:"Why should a future population projection be treated as a scenario rather than a certain forecast?",options:{A:"Population graphs cannot include any measured data",B:"Past population has no link to future change",C:"Every country follows the same demographic path",D:"Future fertility and mortality may change"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-BIO-27",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"A graph shows food supply rising faster after farmers adopt higher-yield crops. Which theory does this evidence support most directly?",options:{A:"Malthus, because innovation lowers carrying capacity",B:"Malthus, because technology cannot affect supply",C:"Neither, because resources never change over time",D:"Boserup, because pressure stimulates innovation"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-BIO-27",scaffold:"Boserup argued that population pressure can encourage technological change and agricultural intensification, increasing resource supply. Malthus expected population growth to outpace food supply unless checked by higher deaths or lower births.",reforge:{stem:"A population repeatedly exceeds available food and is reduced by famine. Which interpretation fits Malthus most closely?",options:{A:"A positive check restores balance",B:"Innovation always expands food supply first",C:"Resources are unrelated to population size",D:"Affluence removes every environmental limit"},correct:"A"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-FORESTS"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-23",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why are emergent trees found above the main rainforest canopy?",options:{A:"They grow towards stronger sunlight",B:"They avoid all exposure to wind and rain",C:"They obtain nutrients from permanently frozen soil",D:"They survive because the forest has no competition"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-23",scaffold:"Rainforest vegetation is stratified because plants compete intensely for light. Emergent trees rise above the canopy, while shade-tolerant vegetation occupies lower layers where less sunlight penetrates.",reforge:{stem:"Why do many rainforest lianas climb tree trunks?",options:{A:"They need frozen ground for their roots",B:"They reach light without thick trunks",C:"They reduce rainfall reaching every canopy leaf",D:"They obtain energy by feeding on tree bark"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-24",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What does a complex tropical rainforest food web indicate?",options:{A:"Each species feeds on only one other organism",B:"Species are strongly interdependent",C:"Energy returns from consumers to the Sun",D:"Decomposers have no role in nutrient cycling"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-24",scaffold:"A complex food web contains many feeding relationships. This can create resilience through alternative links, but the loss of an important or keystone species may still cause knock-on effects across several trophic levels.",reforge:{stem:"A seed-dispersing animal disappears from a rainforest food web. What is a likely long-term consequence?",options:{A:"All plants immediately become decomposers",B:"The soil gains nutrients without any litter",C:"Dependent tree recruitment may decline",D:"Every predator gains an alternative food source"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-25",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"A nutrient-cycle diagram has a large biomass store and rapid transfers from litter to soil. Which biome does it most likely represent?",options:{A:"Tundra with widespread permafrost",B:"Taiga with a long frozen winter",C:"Tropical rainforest",D:"Cold desert with sparse vegetation"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-25",scaffold:"Warm, wet rainforest conditions support rapid decomposition and plant uptake. Most nutrients are held in biomass, while the litter and soil stores remain relatively small because transfers between them are fast.",reforge:{stem:"Which nutrient-cycle pattern is most typical of the taiga?",options:{A:"Rapid decomposition throughout every season",B:"Most nutrients stored in lush broad-leaved biomass",C:"No litter store because needles decay instantly",D:"Slow transfers and a large litter store"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-26",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"How can hydroelectric development cause tropical deforestation?",options:{A:"Reservoirs increase the area of intact forest",B:"Dams prevent any road construction or further settlement in the region",C:"Electricity generation removes demand for land",D:"Reservoirs flood forest and access roads fragment it"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-26",scaffold:"HEP schemes can clear forest directly for dams, reservoirs and transmission lines. New access roads may also open previously remote areas to logging, settlement, farming and mineral extraction.",reforge:{stem:"Why can mineral extraction damage forest beyond the mine itself?",options:{A:"Roads open forest to further clearance",B:"Mining always increases canopy cover nearby",C:"Ore extraction requires no water or transport",D:"Mine waste cannot enter rivers or soils"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-27",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is one advantage of GIS when investigating forest loss?",options:{A:"It reveals spatial patterns over time",B:"It proves the cause of every cleared patch",C:"It removes cloud cover from all satellite images",D:"It guarantees all mapped data are accurate"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-27",scaffold:"GIS can layer and compare satellite images, roads, protected areas and land uses to reveal where forest loss is concentrated and how patterns change. Further evidence is still needed to establish causes.",reforge:{stem:"A GIS map shows clearance clustered beside new roads. What is the safest conclusion?",options:{A:"Road building is the only possible cause",B:"There is a spatial association",C:"The map proves all logging is illegal",D:"Forest loss is evenly distributed"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-28",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why can oil extraction threaten taiga biodiversity indirectly?",options:{A:"Oil wells make winters warmer at every location",B:"Pipelines and roads fragment habitats",C:"Extraction converts conifers into tropical species",D:"Animals cannot migrate through any intact forest"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-28",scaffold:"Energy development brings pipelines, roads and settlements as well as the extraction site. These divide habitats, disturb migration, increase spill risk and make remote forest more accessible to further exploitation.",reforge:{stem:"Which impact is most likely where a pipeline crosses a taiga migration route?",options:{A:"Every species gains a larger continuous habitat",B:"Nutrient cycling immediately becomes tropical",C:"Movement and breeding may be disrupted",D:"The route prevents all human access"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-29",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why do views conflict over creating a protected taiga wilderness?",options:{A:"Protection has no effects on jobs, incomes, habitats or ecosystems",B:"All stakeholders value timber in the same way",C:"Conservation can restrict resource-based livelihoods",D:"National parks permit unlimited commercial logging"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-29",scaffold:"Conservation groups and Indigenous communities may prioritise habitats and cultural value, while firms and some workers may emphasise timber, minerals, energy and employment. The conflict is about how benefits and costs are distributed.",reforge:{stem:"Which stakeholder is most likely to oppose a ban on logging in a taiga town?",options:{A:"A group restoring undisturbed wilderness",B:"Scientists monitoring rare migratory birds",C:"Tourists seeking intact forest landscapes",D:"Workers dependent on the local sawmill"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-FOR-30",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is a possible limitation of REDD forest protection payments?",options:{A:"REDD makes standing forest economically worthless",B:"It bans every local use of forest resources worldwide",C:"It applies only to renewable energy production",D:"Weak monitoring can allow continued clearance"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-FOR-30",scaffold:"REDD can make forest conservation financially attractive by rewarding reduced emissions from deforestation. Its success depends on reliable baselines, monitoring, governance and fair distribution of payments; clearance may also shift elsewhere.",reforge:{stem:"What would most improve confidence that a REDD scheme has reduced deforestation?",options:{A:"Independent monitoring against a baseline",B:"Removing all satellite and field evidence",C:"Paying only after timber exports increase",D:"Ignoring forest loss outside the project boundary"},correct:"A"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-ENERGY"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-22",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Which physical condition gives a country strong hydroelectric potential?",options:{A:"Steep relief and reliable rainfall",B:"Flat land with little surface water",C:"Thick coal seams beneath every river",D:"Low winds and prolonged drought"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-22",scaffold:"HEP works best where reliable precipitation supplies river flow and steep relief creates a large head of water. Technology, investment and environmental acceptability also affect whether this physical potential is developed.",reforge:{stem:"Which climate is most favourable for large-scale solar generation?",options:{A:"Persistent cloud with very short daylight hours",B:"High sunshine and limited cloud",C:"Frequent snowfall throughout the year",D:"Reliable rainfall in steep valleys"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-23",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why can energy use per person remain low in a developing country?",options:{A:"Primary industries always require more energy than manufacturing",B:"Limited electricity access constrains consumption",C:"Traditional fuels are included as high grid demand",D:"Low incomes guarantee abundant modern technology"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-23",scaffold:"Low incomes, limited grid infrastructure and reliance on traditional fuels can restrict access to modern energy. Economic structure matters too: industrial manufacturing generally uses more energy than subsistence primary activity.",reforge:{stem:"Why might rapid industrialisation increase energy use per person?",options:{A:"Factories eliminate household electricity demand",B:"Industrial growth reduces transport activity",C:"Manufacturing requires substantial power",D:"Economic development prevents grid expansion"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-24",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why can a global recession cause oil prices to fall?",options:{A:"Recession turns oil into a renewable resource",B:"Governments must close every oil field",C:"Lower economic activity reduces demand",D:"Oil reserves become evenly distributed"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-24",scaffold:"During recession, industrial output, freight and travel often decline, reducing demand for oil. If supply does not fall as quickly, oversupply puts downward pressure on price.",reforge:{stem:"An oil-producing group cuts output while demand remains steady. What price effect is most likely?",options:{A:"Prices must remain fixed by geology",B:"Prices fall because supply has increased",C:"Oil consumption immediately becomes zero",D:"Prices may rise as supply tightens"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-25",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What is a likely economic trade-off when developing oil in an isolated region?",options:{A:"There are no costs once oil is discovered",B:"Only environmental organisations and no other stakeholders receive revenue",C:"Isolation makes transport unnecessary",D:"Jobs and tax revenue, but high infrastructure costs"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-25",scaffold:"A remote conventional oil project may create employment, export income and tax revenue, but roads, pipelines, ports and worker settlements are expensive. A balanced judgement also considers spill risk and impacts on local ecosystems and livelihoods.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a government still approve expensive offshore drilling?",options:{A:"Expected revenue may exceed development costs",B:"Offshore drilling cannot cause an oil spill",C:"Imported energy is always less secure than domestic oil",D:"Remote reserves require no specialist technology"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-26",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Which action is an example of energy conservation rather than energy efficiency?",options:{A:"Turning unused lights off",B:"Replacing bulbs with lower-energy LEDs",C:"Improving loft insulation in a house",D:"Using a more efficient electric motor"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-26",scaffold:"Conservation reduces energy use through behaviour, such as switching off lights or travelling less. Efficiency delivers the same service using less energy, such as insulation, LEDs or more efficient engines.",reforge:{stem:"Which household change is best described as energy efficiency?",options:{A:"Heating fewer rooms during winter",B:"Installing effective loft insulation",C:"Switching the television off overnight",D:"Choosing to walk instead of driving"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-27",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What is a major challenge when using hydrogen as a low-carbon transport fuel?",options:{A:"Hydrogen cannot store or transfer any energy",B:"Production and storage need energy and infrastructure",C:"Fuel cells always release more carbon than coal",D:"Hydrogen is found in unlimited pure deposits ready for direct collection"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-27",scaffold:"Hydrogen can produce water at the point of use, but it must first be manufactured, compressed or liquefied, transported and stored. Its overall carbon footprint depends strongly on how the hydrogen is produced.",reforge:{stem:"When would hydrogen offer the greatest carbon reduction?",options:{A:"When produced entirely from unabated coal",B:"When storage leaks are ignored",C:"When low-carbon electricity produces it",D:"When vehicles also burn diesel continuously"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-28",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"A household's footprint falls from 10 to 7 global hectares. What is the percentage decrease?",options:{A:"7%",B:"10%",C:"30%",D:"70% of the original footprint"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-28",scaffold:"Percentage decrease = change divided by original value, multiplied by 100. The change is 3 global hectares, so 3 / 10 x 100 = 30%.",reforge:{stem:"A carbon footprint drops from 8 tonnes to 6 tonnes. What is the percentage decrease?",options:{A:"2%",B:"20%",C:"75% of the original footprint",D:"25%"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-ENE-29",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Which stakeholder is most likely to favour a rapid move away from fossil fuels?",options:{A:"A TNC protecting newly developed oil reserves",B:"A government dependent on fossil-fuel taxation",C:"A consumer prioritising the lowest immediate price",D:"A climate-science organisation"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-ENE-29",scaffold:"Stakeholder views reflect different priorities. Climate scientists and environmental groups are likely to emphasise emissions and long-term risk, while consumers, governments and energy TNCs may weigh affordability, employment, tax revenue and existing assets differently.",reforge:{stem:"Why might an oil TNC support a slower energy transition?",options:{A:"It has invested capital in fossil-fuel assets",B:"Oil companies receive no revenue from extraction",C:"A rapid transition always raises oil demand",D:"TNCs cannot invest in renewable technology"},correct:"A"}}
);

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-DECISIONS"].questions.push(
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-15",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What makes an explanation based on a resource figure 'blended'?",options:{A:"It links figure evidence with geographical knowledge",B:"It copies every label and every statistic from the figure in sequence",C:"It replaces evidence with an unsupported opinion",D:"It uses two quotations without explaining either"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-15",scaffold:"Pearson describes strong short explanations as blended when they select relevant evidence from the figure and develop it using the student's own geographical knowledge or terminology.",reforge:{stem:"A figure shows strong winds and severe soil erosion. Which response best develops the evidence?",options:{A:"The figure contains arrows and labels",B:"Strong winds remove exposed topsoil, limiting nutrients for tree growth",C:"The figure proves wind is the only control on forest distribution and tree growth",D:"Soil erosion is important because the source says it is"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-16",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why is detailed description alone insufficient in an 'assess' answer?",options:{A:"Assess questions require no resource evidence",B:"The impacts must be weighed and judged",C:"Only a list of technical terms receives credit",D:"A conclusion must repeat the question exactly"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-16",scaffold:"Assessment goes beyond identifying or describing impacts. A strong answer weighs their significance using criteria such as severity, scale, duration, likelihood or reversibility and reaches a supported judgement.",reforge:{stem:"Which sentence most clearly assesses an environmental impact?",options:{A:"The reservoir covers part of the valley and the source marks the exact area flooded",B:"The source shows several affected species",C:"Habitat loss is most serious because it is long-term and difficult to reverse",D:"There are environmental, social and economic impacts"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-17",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What does it mean to synthesise evidence from two figures?",options:{A:"Describe the first figure and ignore the second",B:"Select whichever figure supports an initial opinion",C:"Connect evidence to form one explanation",D:"Copy one statistic from each without comparison"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-17",scaffold:"Synthesis means bringing information together so that the sources jointly support a line of reasoning. Merely mentioning both figures separately does not explain the relationship between them.",reforge:{stem:"Figure 2 shows plentiful geothermal resources and Figure 3 shows oil-dependent transport. Which conclusion best synthesises them?",options:{A:"Only geothermal energy should be described",B:"The figures must contradict each other",C:"Transport demand is unrelated to the energy mix",D:"Different needs require a diverse energy mix"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-18",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why should a 12-mark decision answer explain why alternatives were rejected?",options:{A:"Every option must receive exactly the same space",B:"Rejected options can never contain valid benefits",C:"The final choice is awarded without any reasoning",D:"Comparison strengthens the chosen priority"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-18",scaffold:"A convincing justification shows why the chosen option performs better against important criteria, while acknowledging genuine strengths and weaknesses in alternatives. Dismissing them without evidence weakens the comparison.",reforge:{stem:"Which structure best supports a justified final decision?",options:{A:"Choose, compare, reject, then conclude",B:"Describe one option and omit all sources",C:"List every figure without making a choice",D:"State a preference only in the final sentence"},correct:"A"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-19",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"A question asks whether another country could copy an energy plan. What must the answer consider?",options:{A:"Whether its conditions make the plan transferable",B:"Only whether the original plan was successful",C:"The colour scheme used in the resource booklet",D:"Whether every country has identical physical resources"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-19",scaffold:"Transferability depends on physical conditions, finance, technology, governance, land availability and social priorities. A strategy can be adapted rather than copied exactly, for example using locally suitable renewable sources or vegetation.",reforge:{stem:"A sunny low-income country cannot afford Iceland's geothermal technology. What is the strongest judgement?",options:{A:"It must abandon every form of renewable energy",B:"Solar may be more transferable if finance is available",C:"Physical geography never affects energy choices",D:"The Icelandic plan can be copied without any financial or physical adaptation"},correct:"B"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-20",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"How can renewable energy create impacts at contrasting geographical scales?",options:{A:"Its effects are always confined to one household",B:"It can cut global emissions but harm local habitats",C:"Local environmental costs automatically cancel global benefits",D:"Scale refers only to the size of a map"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-20",scaffold:"A renewable project can contribute to lower emissions at national or global scale while creating local costs such as flooded habitat, landscape change or disrupted livelihoods. Assessment should weigh both scales.",reforge:{stem:"A dam supplies low-carbon national electricity but floods one local valley. What concept helps evaluate this tension?",options:{A:"Map projection",B:"Weather variability",C:"Geographical scale",D:"Population density alone"},correct:"C"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-21",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why should the provenance of a resource-booklet source be considered?",options:{A:"Every published source is equally reliable regardless of its author or purpose",B:"The newest source must always be accepted",C:"Its purpose and author may shape the evidence",D:"Provenance matters only for photographs"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-21",scaffold:"The author, purpose, date and method can affect a source's reliability or perspective. A company prospectus and an environmental impact assessment may select different evidence even when discussing the same project.",reforge:{stem:"A mining company predicts large job gains from its own proposal. What is the most careful response?",options:{A:"Reject the claim because companies are always wrong",B:"Accept the figure because it is precise",C:"Ignore the source and use no employment evidence",D:"Use it cautiously and compare independent evidence"},correct:"D"}},
  {id:"GCSE-DEC-22",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What best improves the quality of a Paper 3 extended conclusion?",options:{A:"Repeating every advantage in the same order",B:"Adding unrelated facts remembered from another topic",C:"Choosing the option with the longest description",D:"Judging by explicit criteria and evidence"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GEO-DEC-22",scaffold:"A strong conclusion identifies the criteria that matter most - such as sustainability, cost, scale, time or impact - and uses evidence to explain why the chosen option outweighs alternatives. Precision is more valuable than length alone.",reforge:{stem:"Which final sentence gives the strongest geographical judgement?",options:{A:"Option B's long-term benefits outweigh its evidenced local costs",B:"Option B is best because I like it most",C:"All three options have environmental, social and economic advantages and disadvantages in different places",D:"The booklet contains many useful figures"},correct:"A"}}
);

// Geography expansion to 640 questions, mapped to Edexcel B Issue 4
const addGeo640 = (bankId, spec, rows) => BANKS[bankId].questions.push(...rows.map(row => {
  const [id, stem, A, B, C, D, correct, scaffold, reforgeStem, rA, rB, rC, rD, reforgeCorrect] = row;
  return {id, spec, stem, options:{A,B,C,D}, correct, tag:`MC-GEO-${id.slice(5)}`, scaffold, reforge:{stem:reforgeStem,options:{A:rA,B:rB,C:rC,D:rD},correct:reforgeCorrect}};
}));

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-HAZ", "GCSE-HAZ", [
  ["GCSE-HAZ-35","What is the main role of the Ferrel cell in global atmospheric circulation?","It creates permanent equatorial low pressure","It transfers air between tropical and polar cells","It produces every ocean current directly","It prevents weather systems in mid-latitudes","B","The Ferrel cell lies between the Hadley and Polar cells and helps transfer heat through mid-latitude westerlies.","Why are prevailing winds commonly westerly across the UK?","Polar air moves east at the Equator","The Hadley cell reaches the North Pole","Airflow within the Ferrel cell is deflected","Ocean currents reverse Earth's rotation","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-36","How can a warm ocean current affect a nearby west-facing coast?","It guarantees desert conditions inland","It stops atmospheric circulation above the sea","It freezes coastal water throughout winter","It raises coastal temperatures and moisture","D","Warm currents transfer heat to the atmosphere, moderating nearby coastal temperatures and often increasing available moisture.","Why is western Europe milder than some places at the same latitude?","Warm Atlantic water transfers heat toward it","Europe receives no cold air in winter","The Polar cell only operates over Asia","Mountain ranges create tropical conditions","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-37","Why should tree-ring evidence be combined with other climate proxies?","Every tree records global sea level exactly","Tree growth can reflect several local influences","Wide rings always prove higher temperatures","Tree rings extend equally far back everywhere","B","Ring width can be affected by temperature, rainfall, disease and competition, so comparison with ice cores and historical evidence improves confidence.","A tree has unusually narrow rings for five years. What is the safest interpretation?","Global climate certainly cooled everywhere","The tree's age can no longer be estimated","Local growing conditions were less favourable","Atmospheric carbon dioxide fell to zero","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-38","How can a major volcanic eruption temporarily cool global climate?","Ash permanently blocks all incoming sunlight","Lava absorbs heat from every ocean basin","Volcanoes stop greenhouse gases forming","Sulphate aerosols reflect incoming radiation","D","Sulphur-rich gases can form aerosols that reflect solar radiation, producing short-lived cooling despite volcanic carbon emissions.","Which observation would support volcanic cooling after an eruption?","A brief temperature dip follows aerosol spread","Sea level rises before any eruption occurs","Solar output permanently doubles worldwide","All rainfall stops near every volcano","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-39","Why does ocean warming contribute to sea-level rise?","Warm seawater expands in volume","Warm water becomes much denser and sinks","Ocean heating creates new continental crust","Evaporation permanently removes ocean water","A","Thermal expansion occurs as seawater warms; melting land ice adds a separate contribution to sea-level rise.","Which process raises sea level without adding new water to the oceans?","Melting of a valley glacier","Thermal expansion of seawater","Increased snowfall on Antarctica","Formation of sea ice in winter","B"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-40","Why do future climate projections use several emissions pathways?","Past temperature records cannot be measured","Every model assumes greenhouse gases vanish","Future human choices remain uncertain","Sea level responds only to volcanic activity","C","Future population, energy use, technology and policy are uncertain, so models test contrasting emissions scenarios rather than one fixed future.","What does a wider projection range by 2100 mainly indicate?","Observed warming has not occurred","All models use identical assumptions","Daily weather cannot be forecast","Uncertainty compounds over longer periods","D"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-41","Where are the strongest winds usually found in a mature tropical cyclone?","Inside the calm centre of the eye","Across every outer rain band equally","At the cold front behind the cyclone","In the eyewall surrounding the eye","D","The eyewall contains intense uplift, lowest pressure gradients and the strongest winds; the eye itself is relatively calm.","A satellite image shows a clear eye surrounded by dense cloud. What does the cloud ring mark?","The eyewall with intense convection","A zone where air only sinks","The point where Coriolis becomes zero","A cold ocean current beneath the storm","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-42","What most directly generates a tropical-cyclone storm surge?","Earthquake movement beneath the seabed","Strong winds push seawater toward the coast","River deposition raises offshore islands","Cold air freezes water across the shoreline","B","Onshore winds pile water against the coast, while low atmospheric pressure also allows sea level to rise locally.","Why is a shallow, funnel-shaped bay especially vulnerable to storm surge?","It removes all wave energy offshore","It causes sea level to fall at high tide","It concentrates water driven toward the coast","It prevents wind acting on the sea surface","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-43","How can GIS help monitor a tropical cyclone?","It prevents the cyclone changing direction","It replaces all satellite observations","It measures only damage after landfall","It maps changing position and exposure","D","GIS can combine forecast tracks with population, elevation and infrastructure layers to identify places at risk as a storm moves.","A forecast track shifts east overnight. What should emergency planners update first?","The mapped evacuation zones at risk","The historical name of the cyclone","The latitude of the Equator","The plate-boundary map of the region","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-44","Which physical factor can make a coastal community more vulnerable to cyclones?","A high proportion of trained doctors","Low-lying land exposed to storm surge","Strong building codes across the settlement","Several well-maintained evacuation routes","B","Low elevation and direct coastal exposure increase the likelihood and depth of surge flooding, regardless of national wealth.","How can mangrove removal increase cyclone vulnerability?","It strengthens every coastal building","It lowers the local population density","It removes a natural barrier to waves","It makes weather forecasts less accurate","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-45","Why might an evacuation warning fail to prevent cyclone deaths?","Forecasts always show the exact landfall point","Warnings automatically strengthen weak houses","Every household owns private transport","People may lack transport or trust","D","Evacuation effectiveness depends on communication, trust, mobility, shelter capacity and whether people can afford to leave.","Which action would most improve an evacuation plan in an informal settlement?","Provide transport and accessible shelters","Issue warnings only through paid television","Close shelters before the storm arrives","Assume all residents own a car","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-46","Why can tectonic plates move over the asthenosphere?","The inner core is completely liquid","The asthenosphere can deform slowly","Continental crust is denser than the core","The mantle contains no thermal energy","B","The asthenosphere is hot and mechanically weak enough to flow slowly, allowing rigid lithospheric plates to move above it.","Which description best distinguishes lithosphere from asthenosphere?","Both layers are equally rigid and cold","The asthenosphere forms the solid inner core","Rigid plates overlie a weaker layer","The lithosphere consists only of ocean water","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-47","Why do volcanic islands in a hotspot chain often become older with distance from the active volcano?","The hotspot moves rapidly across a fixed plate","Older islands pull the mantle plume sideways","Ocean currents transport volcanoes after eruption","The plate moves over a near-fixed plume","D","A relatively stationary mantle plume forms new volcanoes as the plate moves, leaving extinct and older islands progressively farther away.","What evidence most strongly supports a moving plate above a hotspot?","Island ages increase along the chain","Every island erupts at the same time","The chain crosses no oceanic crust","Earthquakes stop beneath the active island","A"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-48","Where are deep-focus earthquakes most likely to occur?","At a divergent ridge with new crust forming","Within a descending subduction slab","Along every conservative boundary equally","At a hotspot far from any plate margin","B","At convergent boundaries, a subducting plate remains brittle at depth and generates earthquakes along the Benioff zone.","A cross-section shows earthquake foci becoming deeper inland from a trench. What does this indicate?","A mantle plume rising vertically","Two plates moving apart at a ridge","An oceanic plate is subducting","No plate movement is occurring","C"],
  ["GCSE-HAZ-49","A magnitude 7 earthquake releases roughly how much more energy than a magnitude 6 event?","About twice as much energy","About seven times as much energy","Exactly one hundred times as much energy","About thirty-two times as much energy","D","Earthquake magnitude is logarithmic: a one-unit increase represents about 32 times more energy release.","Why is a magnitude 8 event far more powerful than a magnitude 7 event?","Each whole magnitude is about 32 times the energy","Magnitude increases by one joule at each level","The scale measures only the length of shaking","All magnitude 8 events have identical impacts","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-DEV", "GCSE-DEV", [
  ["GCSE-DEV-43","Why can HDI give a different development ranking from GDP per capita?","It includes health and education as well as income","It measures only the value of exported goods","It ignores life expectancy in every country","It ranks countries solely by population size","A","HDI combines income, education and life expectancy, so countries with similar incomes can rank differently on broader human outcomes.","Two countries have equal income per person but different HDI scores. What best explains the gap?","They must have identical public services","Education and life expectancy differ","One country has a larger land area","HDI measures only annual rainfall","B"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-44","What does it suggest if the richest income quintile receives most national income?","Income is distributed almost equally","There is substantial income inequality","Every person in the country is wealthy","The country must have a low total GDP","B","Income quintiles divide the population into five equal groups; a very large top-quintile share indicates unequal distribution.","Which pattern indicates the most equal income distribution?","The top fifth receives nearly all income","The bottom fifth receives no recorded income","Each quintile receives a similar share","One quintile contains half the population","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-45","What is one limitation of an index of perceived political corruption?","It directly measures every hidden transaction","It gives identical scores in every survey","Perceptions may differ from actual corruption","It can only be used for weather forecasting","C","Corruption is difficult to observe directly, so perception indices depend on survey evidence and should be interpreted alongside other political measures.","Why should a corruption ranking be used cautiously?","Its methods and perceptions may vary","It records every offence with certainty","A low score proves corruption is absent","Political governance cannot be compared","D"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-46","Why is maternal mortality commonly used as a development indicator?","It measures the size of the service sector","It records average household electricity use","It shows the total value of foreign investment","It reflects healthcare access and maternal wellbeing","D","Maternal mortality is shaped by healthcare, nutrition, transport, education and inequality, making it a useful social indicator.","A country reduces maternal deaths sharply. What development change is most likely?","Improved access to skilled healthcare","A decline in every form of employment","A fall in all international trade","A shift from urban to subsistence living","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-47","How can mountainous terrain contribute to regional inequality?","It makes all regions equally accessible","It can isolate communities from markets and services","It guarantees high agricultural yields everywhere","It prevents any minerals forming underground","B","Steep relief can raise transport and infrastructure costs, limiting access to employment, healthcare and education.","Why might a landlocked country face higher trade costs?","It has direct access to several ocean ports","Its population must be evenly distributed","Goods depend on routes through neighbours","Landlocked states cannot export any products","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-48","What is neo-colonialism?","The complete end of external influence after independence","A policy that removes all international investment","Equal control of trade by every participating country","Continued external influence through economic power","D","Neo-colonialism describes indirect control through trade, debt, investment or political influence after formal colonial rule has ended.","Which situation best illustrates neo-colonial influence?","A foreign lender shapes national policy through debt","A village chooses its own crop rotation","Two regions share identical rainfall totals","A country closes every international connection","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-49","In Rostow's model, what characterises the 'take-off' stage?","Traditional subsistence activity remains unchanged","Industrial investment and growth accelerate","All economic growth permanently stops","The service sector employs every worker","B","Take-off involves rising investment, industrialisation and self-sustaining economic growth, although the model is criticised as overly linear.","What is a common criticism of Rostow's development model?","It contains no stages of economic change","It focuses entirely on environmental limits","It assumes countries follow a Western path","It argues trade always causes dependency","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-50","How does dependency theory explain persistent underdevelopment?","Poor countries lack any natural resources","Climate is the only influence on development","All trade benefits countries in equal measure","Core economies extract value from the periphery","D","Frank argued that unequal trade and investment transfer wealth from peripheral countries to developed core economies.","Which trade pattern most supports dependency theory?","Raw materials leave cheaply and manufactured goods return at higher prices","All countries export goods of equal value","Peripheral countries control every global TNC","Trade removes the need for foreign currency","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-51","Why can intermediate technology suit a bottom-up development project?","It requires costly imported experts indefinitely","It is affordable and maintainable locally","It replaces community knowledge with automation","It operates only at a national megaproject scale","B","Intermediate technology is designed around local skills, resources and maintenance capacity rather than maximum technical complexity.","Which water project best represents intermediate technology?","A nationwide desalination grid run abroad","A dam requiring permanent foreign engineers","A repairable community hand pump","A satellite network with imported components","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-52","What is one risk of an IGO-funded infrastructure megaproject?","Large projects can never increase connectivity","Construction creates no employment at all","Infrastructure has no effect on national debt","Loan repayments may burden public finances","D","Large infrastructure can improve power or transport but may create debt, displacement and unequal benefits if costs are poorly managed.","What should be included in a balanced assessment of an IGO-funded dam?","Power benefits weighed against debt and displacement","Only the dam's maximum electricity output","A claim that all affected people will benefit","The assumption that environmental costs are zero","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-53","How can a new TNC factory create a local multiplier effect?","It removes income from every local household","Workers and suppliers spend income locally","All profits remain outside the host country","The factory prevents supporting firms opening","B","Wages and purchases from local suppliers can generate further demand, jobs and tax revenue beyond the original investment.","Which evidence would show a strong local multiplier effect?","Most inputs are imported and profits leave","The factory employs only temporary foreign staff","Local suppliers and shops gain sales","Nearby businesses close as spending falls","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-54","Why have some countries gained less from digital globalisation?","Internet access is equal in every country","Digital services require no skilled workers","Geography no longer affects connectivity","Weak networks and skills restrict participation","D","Reliable communications, electricity and digital skills shape whether firms and workers can join higher-value global networks.","Which policy would most directly reduce a digital divide?","Expand broadband access and technical education","Ban all electronic communication with firms","Concentrate networks in one wealthy district","Reduce electricity reliability in rural areas","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-55","A population pyramid has a narrow base and a wide upper section. What does it suggest?","Rapid population growth and high fertility","Low fertility and an ageing population","Very high infant mortality with few elderly","Recent immigration of children only","B","A narrow base indicates fewer recent births, while larger older cohorts suggest low mortality and population ageing.","Which challenge is most associated with an ageing population?","Demand for primary schools rises fastest","The dependent child population always doubles","Greater pressure on pensions and care","Life expectancy must fall below infancy","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEV-56","Why can tied aid provide less benefit than its headline value suggests?","Recipients control every purchasing decision","Aid never funds infrastructure or services","The donor receives no economic advantage","Purchases may be restricted to donor firms","D","Tied aid can require recipients to buy goods or expertise from the donor, reducing local choice and sometimes raising costs.","Which feature would make aid more responsive to local priorities?","Communities help decide how funds are used","All equipment must come from one donor","Repayment terms are kept confidential","Projects ignore local maintenance skills","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-INDIA", "GCSE-IND", [
  ["GCSE-IND-29","Which statement describes India's situation rather than its site?","It occupies a large South Asian peninsula","It lies beside major Indian Ocean trade routes","Much of the north contains fertile plains","The Himalayas form a high mountain barrier","B","Situation describes location relative to other places and connections; site describes physical characteristics of the place itself.","Why is India's Indian Ocean position economically significant?","It isolates India from maritime trade","It determines every regional rainfall total","It connects ports to global shipping routes","It prevents trade with East Asia","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-30","How could India's youthful population create a demographic dividend?","Every young person automatically gains a job","A larger workforce always lowers productivity","The elderly population immediately disappears","Employment and skills can raise output","D","A large working-age population can support growth if education, healthcare and enough productive jobs are available.","When might a youthful population become an economic challenge instead?","When job creation fails to match workforce growth","When all workers gain advanced training","When dependency ratios fall sustainably","When firms invest in productive employment","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-31","What does a declining primary-sector share and rising tertiary share indicate?","The economy is becoming less diversified","Economic structure is shifting toward services","Agriculture now employs every urban worker","Manufacturing and services have both disappeared","B","Development commonly changes the relative importance of sectors as productivity rises and services expand.","Why can sector shares change even when farm output rises?","Agriculture must be producing less food","Sector data measure only export destinations","Other sectors may grow more quickly","Economic sectors cannot change together","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-32","Why has India attracted outsourced information-technology services?","Time zones prevent contact with overseas clients","English-language skills are unavailable","Telecommunications are unnecessary for outsourcing","Skilled labour can serve global firms remotely","D","A large skilled workforce, English use, telecommunications and time-zone advantages have supported India's service exports.","What technological change most enabled international service outsourcing?","Fast digital communications across borders","A return to subsistence agriculture","The closure of international airports","Reduced access to higher education","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-33","How can a special economic zone encourage foreign direct investment?","It bans firms from exporting their output","It offers infrastructure and investment incentives","It requires every firm to remain state-owned","It prevents transport links reaching factories","B","SEZs may offer reliable infrastructure, streamlined regulation and tax incentives to attract domestic and foreign firms.","What is one possible cost of generous SEZ incentives?","Every investor leaves before production begins","Industrial areas cannot create any employment","Tax revenue may be reduced initially","Exports become illegal within the zone","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-34","How can investment in education support India's development?","Education lowers worker productivity in every sector","It prevents movement into higher-value employment","It guarantees equal incomes across all states","Skills can attract higher-value industries","D","Education develops human capital, enabling workers and firms to move into more productive manufacturing and service activities.","Which outcome would best show successful human-capital investment?","More skilled employment and higher productivity","A permanent fall in school attendance","Greater dependence on unprocessed exports","Fewer workers able to use technology","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-35","Why has rural-urban migration contributed to India's regional differences?","All migrants move to the least connected districts","Investment and workers concentrate in growth centres","Migration makes every state's income identical","Cities lose services whenever migrants arrive","B","Major cities and industrial corridors attract labour and capital, while some rural or peripheral regions receive less investment.","What is one possible benefit of migration for a rural origin area?","Every working-age adult permanently leaves","Urban growth stops in destination cities","Remittances support rural households","Local incomes must fall to zero","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-36","Why may rapid economic growth affect women and men differently?","Growth automatically removes gender inequality","Only men can work in service industries","Education has no link to employment access","Opportunities depend on norms and skills","D","Benefits depend on education, location, social expectations, safety and access to formal work, so growth is not shared evenly.","Which change would most widen women's access to formal employment?","Safer transport and improved education","Removing childcare from every workplace","Restricting access to digital skills","Reducing legal protection at work","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-37","How can severe urban air pollution reduce the benefits of economic growth?","Pollution raises life expectancy automatically","Ill health lowers wellbeing and productivity","Air quality affects only uninhabited areas","Industrial emissions cannot enter cities","B","Respiratory illness creates healthcare costs, missed work and reduced quality of life, offsetting some economic gains.","Which policy tackles both transport emissions and congestion?","Subsidise the most polluting private vehicles","Remove all bus routes from city centres","Expand reliable low-emission public transport","Move every workplace beyond the urban fringe","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-38","Why can untreated industrial wastewater create impacts beyond a factory site?","Rivers prevent pollutants moving downstream","Wastewater improves drinking-water quality","Industrial chemicals disappear on contact with water","Pollutants spread through river systems","D","Contaminants can move downstream, harming ecosystems, agriculture, fishing and communities using the water.","Which evidence best shows a downstream industrial impact?","Water quality declines below an industrial discharge","Rainfall rises across the whole river basin","Factories use less electricity during weekends","The river becomes steeper near its source","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-39","How has economic growth increased India's geopolitical influence?","India has withdrawn from every international forum","Its market and economy strengthen its global voice","Economic growth prevents regional diplomacy","Global influence depends only on land area","B","A larger economy, strategic location and expanding trade give India greater weight in regional and international organisations.","Which development would most increase a country's geopolitical leverage?","Complete isolation from international trade","A permanent ban on diplomatic relations","Greater economic and strategic importance","A decline in all regional connections","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-40","Why can closer trade relations with the USA or EU create debate within India?","Trade agreements have no domestic effects","Every industry gains by exactly the same amount","Foreign competition cannot affect local firms","Benefits and costs differ among groups","D","Exporters and consumers may gain, while some domestic producers or workers face stronger competition and adjustment costs.","What makes an evaluation of a trade agreement balanced?","Compare gains for exporters with costs for exposed sectors","Assume every household receives equal benefits","Discuss only the total value of national exports","Ignore employment and environmental effects","A"],
  ["GCSE-IND-41","Three state incomes are 80, 100 and 120. How far is 80 below the mean?","10 income units","20 income units","30 income units","40 income units","B","The mean is 100, so the first state's value is 20 units below it.","A district value is 45 and the regional mean is 60. What is the difference from the mean?","5 below the mean","10 below the mean","15 below the mean","25 below the mean","C"],
  ["GCSE-IND-42","On a proportional flow-line map, what does a wide arrow entering India show?","The height of land along the border","The direction of prevailing winds","The age structure of the destination","A relatively large inward flow","D","Flow-line width represents magnitude, while the arrow direction shows movement such as imports, investment or migration.","Two export arrows point to different markets. How should their values be compared?","Use their widths with the map key","Count the countries crossed by each arrow","Measure only the arrow's compass direction","Assume the longer arrow is always larger","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-URB", "GCSE-URB", [
  ["GCSE-URB-19","Why is the urban population growing fastest in many emerging regions?","All developed cities are becoming rural","Migration and natural increase combine","Urban jobs have disappeared from emerging economies","Population growth occurs only in villages","B","Rural-urban migration and natural increase within youthful city populations can operate together.","Why can a city's population keep growing after migration slows?","Every resident moves back to the countryside","Urban housing becomes permanently empty","Births may continue to exceed deaths","Natural increase applies only to rural areas","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-20","Why can capital-city functions reinforce the growth of a dominant city?","They distribute institutions evenly among regions","They reduce demand for high-level services","They prevent national and international migration","They concentrate decisions and investment","D","Government, finance and corporate headquarters attract skilled workers, infrastructure and further investment to an already dominant city.","What is one national cost when investment is concentrated in a dominant city?","Other regions may receive less investment","Every smaller city gains equal employment","Political influence becomes evenly distributed","Regional inequality automatically disappears","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-21","How does natural increase contribute to megacity growth?","International migrants replace every existing resident","Births exceed deaths within the city","The urban boundary contracts each year","Death rates rise above birth rates","B","Natural increase is population growth from births exceeding deaths among the existing urban population.","Which data are needed to calculate a city's natural increase?","Land values and building heights","Migration and commuter flows","Birth and death totals","Road length and traffic speed","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-22","Why can international migration increase a city's global connectivity?","Migrants remove every overseas link","Migration prevents cultural exchange","Cities become independent of remittances","Networks link people, trade and investment","D","Migrant networks can strengthen flows of finance, knowledge, culture and business between origin and destination places.","Which is an example of a transnational urban link?","Remittances sent to relatives overseas","A household moving within one suburb","A shop changing its opening hours","A local bus using a different route","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-23","What distinguishes informal employment from formal employment?","Informal work always pays higher wages","It often lacks contracts and legal protection","Formal work never contributes to taxation","Informal workers cannot provide useful services","B","Informal employment operates outside full regulation and may lack contracts, security, benefits and safe conditions.","Why can informal work still be an urban opportunity?","It guarantees pensions for every worker","It removes all risk of exploitation","It provides income where formal jobs are scarce","It prevents workers developing any skills","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-24","What is suburbanisation?","Movement from villages into the CBD","Loss of all housing from the city edge","Growth caused only by international migration","Population and development shift outward","D","Suburbanisation moves residents and development from central areas toward lower-density outer districts.","Which change most clearly indicates suburbanisation?","New housing expands around the urban edge","CBD land values rise after office construction","Factories close beside an inner-city canal","Rural residents move into central apartments","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-25","How can deindustrialisation change an inner-city area?","It increases heavy manufacturing employment","Factory closures create dereliction and job loss","It immediately removes all social inequality","Industrial land automatically becomes farmland","B","The decline of manufacturing can leave vacant land, unemployment and population loss before regeneration occurs.","Which strategy most directly reuses derelict industrial land?","Build further factories on untouched farmland","Leave contaminated land without treatment","Remediate and redevelop the brownfield site","Move the urban boundary around the site","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-26","Why are central urban land values often high?","Central sites have unlimited vacant land","Planning rules never apply in city centres","Accessibility has no effect on demand","Many users compete for accessible sites","D","CBD accessibility attracts offices, retail and services, while limited space increases competition and bid rent.","Which land use can usually afford the highest central rent?","A high-turnover commercial business","Extensive low-value grazing land","A large reservoir and floodplain","A remote subsistence farm","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-27","How can satellite imagery help identify an industrial land-use zone?","It records every worker's occupation","Large roofs and transport yards are visible","It proves all factories are currently operating","It measures household income directly","B","Industrial zones may show large buildings, yards, warehouses and links to major roads or railways.","What is a limitation of identifying land use from one satellite image?","Images cannot show any building shape","Roads are never visible from above","Function may be unclear without ground evidence","Satellite data always show future land use","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-28","Why did Mumbai's coastal situation support its growth?","The coast prevented all international contact","Mountains supplied unlimited building land","Its islands made overland travel unnecessary","A port connected trade and investment","D","Mumbai's west-coast port and national connections supported trade, industry, migration and global-city functions.","Which feature describes Mumbai's site?","Low-lying coastal land and former islands","Its trade links with overseas markets","Its role in India's financial system","Its rail connections to inland states","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-29","Which function is most characteristic of a megacity CBD?","Low-density subsistence farming","Concentrated offices, retail and finance","Large-scale extraction of mineral ores","Permanent storage of untreated waste","B","The CBD concentrates high-value commercial functions where accessibility and customer flows are greatest.","Why are buildings often taller in a CBD?","Planning always requires identical heights","Central land has no commercial demand","High land values encourage intensive use","Tall buildings reduce every transport cost","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-30","Why do insecure property rights reduce quality of life?","They guarantee investment in every dwelling","Residents can always obtain low-cost mortgages","Governments must provide permanent services","Households risk eviction and underinvestment","D","Without secure tenure, households may be displaced and are less able to invest in homes or gain formal service connections.","Which policy would most directly improve tenure security?","Recognise occupancy and provide legal titles","Demolish homes without consulting residents","Ban residents from improving structures","Remove addresses from every settlement","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-31","Why can inadequate waste collection create a flood hazard?","Waste increases the capacity of every drain","Blocked drains increase surface flooding","Uncollected waste prevents intense rainfall","Floodwater cannot carry contaminants","B","Waste can block drainage channels, while contaminated floodwater spreads disease through dense settlements.","Which linked strategy addresses both waste and health?","Close all community collection points","Store refuse permanently in drainage channels","Regular collection with safe disposal","Remove access to clean water supplies","C"],
  ["GCSE-URB-32","What is one advantage of a city-wide mass-transit strategy?","It benefits only households owning two cars","It requires no public investment or planning","It eliminates every journey immediately","It can reduce congestion for many users","D","High-capacity public transport can improve access and reduce road congestion, though affordability and coverage affect who benefits.","What is one limitation of a new metro line?","High construction costs and possible displacement","It cannot carry more than one passenger","Rail transport always raises road traffic","Stations make employment less accessible","A"],
  ["GCSE-URB-33","Why can a community-managed toilet scheme improve sustainability?","It relies entirely on distant decision-makers","Local participation supports maintenance","It removes the need for clean water","Community schemes cannot improve health","B","Local involvement can align facilities with needs, build ownership and create systems for maintenance and user fees.","Which evidence best shows a sanitation project is sustainable?","Facilities close when external staff leave","Residents were not consulted before construction","Safe facilities remain maintained and used","Waste is discharged into nearby watercourses","C"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-UKLAND", "GCSE-UKLAND", [
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-39","Which feature is most likely to be formed by glacial deposition?","A corrie with a steep back wall","A drumlin made of deposited till","A narrow gorge below a waterfall","A wave-cut notch at a cliff base","B","Drumlins are streamlined hills of till deposited and shaped beneath moving ice.","What evidence would suggest a valley was once glaciated?","A wide U-shaped profile with moraines","A narrow V-shaped valley with spurs","A floodplain crossed by meanders","A beach backed by sand dunes","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-40","How is slate classified?","A sedimentary rock formed from shells","An igneous rock cooled below ground","A metamorphic rock changed by heat and pressure","An unconsolidated deposit left by rivers","C","Slate forms when shale is altered by heat and pressure and is common in resistant upland landscapes.","Why are slate landscapes often associated with upland relief?","Slate dissolves instantly in rainwater","Slate is relatively resistant to erosion","Slate forms only on modern beaches","Slate prevents any slope movement","B"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-41","Why can limestone drainage basins have low surface runoff?","Limestone is impermeable and saturated","Limestone prevents water entering joints","Rainfall evaporates before reaching the rock","Water infiltrates through permeable fractures","D","Carboniferous limestone is permeable through joints and bedding planes, encouraging infiltration and underground drainage.","Which feature is most likely in a limestone area?","A disappearing stream entering a swallow hole","A granite tor formed by block weathering","A clay floodplain with no infiltration","A volcanic cone above a hotspot","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-42","How does freeze-thaw weathering break rock?","Salt dissolves every mineral in the rock","Water freezes in cracks and expands","Plant roots melt the rock surface","Rainwater deposits new layers of clay","B","Repeated freezing and thawing widens cracks until angular fragments detach.","Where is freeze-thaw weathering most effective?","Where temperatures remain above 25°C","Where rock has no cracks or joints","Where temperatures cross freezing repeatedly","Where rainfall never reaches the ground","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-43","Why can prolonged rainfall trigger mass movement on a slope?","Saturation always increases rock strength","Water removes the pull of gravity","Wet soil becomes lighter and more stable","Saturation adds weight and reduces friction","D","Water increases pore pressure and mass while lowering resistance along a slip plane.","Which slope is most vulnerable to rotational slumping?","A saturated cliff made of weak clay","A dry granite plateau with no joints","A flat chalk surface far inland","A beach during a calm low tide","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-44","How can a cove form on a concordant coastline?","Soft rock is exposed after hard rock is breached","Alternating rocks meet the coast at right angles","Longshore drift seals every opening with sand","A river cuts through only resistant granite","A","A narrow breach through resistant outer rock allows waves to erode softer rock behind, widening a cove.","Which structure defines a concordant coastline?","Rock bands run parallel to the shore","Rock bands meet the shore at right angles","Only one rock type occurs inland","Faults are absent from the coastline","B"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-45","What is the usual sequence as waves exploit a joint in a headland?","Stack, cave, arch, stump","Arch, cave, stump, stack","Cave, arch, stack, stump","Stump, stack, cave, arch","C","Hydraulic action and abrasion enlarge a crack into a cave, then an arch; roof collapse leaves a stack and later a stump.","What most directly causes an arch to become a stack?","Deposition fills the opening with beach material","The headland becomes harder over time","Longshore drift moves the arch inland","The weakened roof collapses","D"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-46","Why is erosion often concentrated on coastal headlands?","Wave refraction focuses energy there","Bays receive all incoming wave energy","Headlands are always made of soft clay","Constructive waves remove cliff material fastest","A","Wave fronts bend in shallow water, concentrating energy on projecting headlands and dispersing it within bays.","How does wave refraction encourage deposition in a bay?","It makes the bay rock less resistant","Wave energy spreads and becomes lower","It prevents sediment entering the bay","All waves become destructive at once","B"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-47","How does an offshore bar form?","A stack collapses across a river mouth","Cliff retreat leaves a rocky platform","A spit extends across a bay","A glacier deposits till parallel to shore","C","A spit may continue across a bay where no strong river current breaks it, forming a bar and often trapping a lagoon.","What feature commonly forms behind a coastal bar?","A lagoon in sheltered water","A waterfall and plunge pool","A glacial ribbon lake","A tectonic rift valley","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-48","Which is a sub-aerial process affecting a sea cliff?","Hydraulic action by breaking waves","Weathering weakening rock above the shore","Traction moving shingle along the seabed","Wave refraction around a headland","B","Sub-aerial processes act from the atmosphere and slopes, including weathering, runoff and mass movement.","Why can sub-aerial weathering accelerate marine erosion?","It makes incoming waves constructive","It transports sediment only offshore","It weakens rock before waves attack","It raises the cliff above wave reach","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-49","How does saturated ground before a storm affect a river hydrograph?","It increases infiltration capacity greatly","It lowers discharge throughout the event","It prevents rainfall reaching the channel","It increases runoff and shortens lag time","D","Antecedent saturation leaves little storage capacity, so rainfall reaches the channel rapidly.","Which hydrograph is most likely after several wet weeks?","A steep rising limb and high peak","A long lag with a very low peak","No change in discharge after rainfall","A falling limb before rain begins","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-50","Why does an elongated drainage basin often have a longer lag time than a circular one?","Water reaches the channel from all points together","Runoff arrives over a wider period","Elongated basins always have impermeable rock","Basin shape prevents tributaries forming","B","Distances to the outlet vary more in an elongated basin, spreading tributary inputs over time.","Which basin shape is most likely to produce a sharp peak discharge?","A long narrow basin with sparse tributaries","An elongated basin with permeable soils","A compact circular drainage basin","A basin without any channel network","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-51","Why are natural levees highest beside a river channel?","The finest sediment settles first near the bank","Floodwater speeds up across the floodplain","Coarser load is deposited as water spills out","River erosion cuts ridges into the floodplain","C","When floodwater leaves the channel it loses velocity; heavier sediment is deposited first beside the banks.","What happens to sediment size farther across a floodplain during a flood?","Deposits generally become finer","Deposits become progressively coarser","No sediment leaves the channel","Only dissolved load is deposited beside banks","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKLAND-52","How can river restoration reduce downstream flood risk?","It straightens and deepens the entire river","It reconnects the channel with floodplain storage","It builds housing across the washland","It removes vegetation from every bank","B","Restoring meanders, wetlands and floodplain storage slows flow and temporarily stores water.","What is one trade-off of floodplain restoration?","It guarantees no future flood can occur","It increases channel velocity everywhere","Some land is allowed to flood more often","It requires concrete walls along all banks","C"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-UKHUMAN", "GCSE-UKHUMAN", [
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-29","How can international migration alter the UK's age structure?","Migrants are always older than retirees","Working-age arrivals can widen middle cohorts","Migration changes ethnicity but never age","Every migrant settles in the same region","B","Many migrants are working age, affecting labour supply, dependency ratios and demand for housing and services.","Which service may face greater demand after an influx of young families?","Residential care homes only","Motorway freight terminals","Schools and maternity services","Remote forestry plantations","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-30","Why has migration increased cultural diversity in UK cities?","Migrant communities remove existing cultures","Immigration prevents international connections","Policies make every city identical","People bring languages, faiths and traditions","D","Migration adds cultural practices, businesses and social networks, with patterns varying among cities and neighbourhoods.","Which urban change best indicates greater cultural diversity?","A wider range of community services and businesses","The closure of every international food shop","Identical age structures in all districts","A complete end to overseas migration","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-31","Why has employment in UK primary industries declined?","Extraction and farming now require more workers","Mechanisation and global competition reduce labour demand","The UK no longer consumes raw materials","Primary activities occur only in city centres","B","Technology raises output per worker, while imports and resource depletion can reduce domestic employment.","Why can farm output rise while agricultural employment falls?","Workers become less productive each year","All farms switch to hand tools","Machinery increases labour productivity","Food production moves into the CBD","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-32","What best characterises quaternary-sector employment?","Manual extraction of coal and stone","Assembly-line production of consumer goods","Retail services requiring no information","Knowledge, research and data-intensive work","D","The quaternary sector includes research, information technology, consultancy and other knowledge-based activities.","Why do major cities attract quaternary firms?","Skilled labour and specialist networks cluster there","Knowledge firms need to be far from universities","Digital businesses avoid high-connectivity locations","Research work depends on nearby farmland","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-33","How can privatisation encourage foreign investment in the UK?","It prevents private firms buying assets","Overseas firms can acquire or operate services","It guarantees all profits stay in the UK","Privatisation removes competition from markets","B","Selling state assets or opening services to competition can create opportunities for overseas investors and TNCs.","What is one concern about foreign ownership of infrastructure?","It always lowers every household bill","It ends all access to international capital","Decisions and profits may leave the UK","It makes regulation legally impossible","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-34","Which Census comparison could reveal inner-city inequality?","Rock type and annual precipitation","River discharge and channel width","Wind direction and coastal erosion","Employment, health and housing data","D","Small-area Census data allow social and economic indicators to be compared across urban neighbourhoods.","Why should several indicators be used to assess inequality?","Deprivation has multiple dimensions","Income alone measures every aspect of life","All neighbourhood data show the same pattern","Health and housing cannot be compared","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-35","Why do retail parks often locate near the urban fringe?","Land is costlier and roads are inaccessible","Larger sites and major-road access are available","Customers cannot reach fringe locations by car","Planning encourages all shops into the CBD","B","Fringe locations offer space for large stores and parking near ring roads, often at lower land cost than city centres.","How can out-of-town retail affect a traditional centre?","It guarantees higher footfall on every high street","It prevents any change in land values","Trade may be drawn away from central shops","It moves the CBD to a rural national park","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-36","How has e-commerce contributed to high-street change?","Online retail requires more central shop floors","Delivery systems eliminate distribution centres","Every consumer stops visiting physical shops","Some spending shifts away from stores","D","Online purchasing can reduce demand for some retail premises while increasing warehousing and delivery activity.","Which land use may expand because of online shopping?","Warehousing near strategic transport routes","Heavy industry in every pedestrian precinct","Subsistence farming inside shopping centres","Reservoir storage beneath high streets","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-37","What is studentification?","Older households move into isolated farms","Student concentration changes a neighbourhood","Industry replaces every residential property","A city population becomes uniformly elderly","B","Large student populations can change housing tenure, rents, services, noise and seasonal occupancy.","Which change is commonly linked to studentification?","A fall in all demand for rental housing","Permanent closure of local leisure services","More houses converted to shared rentals","The disappearance of short-term residents","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-38","How can accessible green space improve urban quality of life?","It increases pollution and heat exposure","It removes opportunities for exercise","It guarantees equal income distribution","It supports recreation and cooling","D","Parks provide recreation, habitats, shade and cooling, though benefits depend on access, safety and maintenance.","Which evidence best tests whether green-space access is unequal?","Map walking distance alongside deprivation data","Count trees in one affluent park only","Use national GDP without neighbourhood data","Record weather at a distant rural station","A"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-39","What is one benefit of energy-efficient affordable housing?","It raises heat loss through walls and roofs","Lower energy use can reduce household bills","It guarantees property prices never change","Efficient homes require no initial investment","B","Insulation and efficient systems can improve comfort and reduce energy costs, especially for lower-income households.","Why might an affordable-housing scheme still face opposition?","It removes all demand for local services","It can only be built on industrial land","Residents may object to density or location","Affordable homes always increase commuting distance","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-40","How does daily commuting show city-rural interdependence?","Workers and income flow between connected places","Rural residents never use urban employment","Cities produce every food and raw material locally","Commuting prevents transport congestion","A","Accessible rural areas supply labour and homes, while cities provide jobs and specialist services.","What is one rural cost of heavy out-commuting?","Every local wage is spent within the village","Road congestion and dormitory settlements","Urban employers lose access to labour","Public transport automatically becomes cheaper","B"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-41","Why can rural service provision decline?","Sparse populations raise provision costs","Rural areas always have the youngest population","Distance has no effect on service access","Every village supports a major hospital","A","Small, dispersed populations and ageing structures can make schools, buses, shops and healthcare expensive to maintain.","Which group is especially affected by losing a rural bus route?","Households with several private vehicles","Businesses using only international air freight","People unable to drive to services","Commuters living beside a city metro","C"],
  ["GCSE-UKHUMAN-42","Why can the Index of Multiple Deprivation identify hidden rural need?","It measures only national average income","It excludes access to services","It ranks places solely by population density","It combines several dimensions of deprivation","D","IMD includes income, employment, health, education, crime, housing access and environment; poor service access may matter in otherwise attractive rural areas.","Why might a picturesque village still contain deprivation?","Some residents face low incomes and poor service access","Tourism guarantees high wages for all households","High environmental quality removes housing costs","Rural deprivation can only occur in cities","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-ENQUIRY", "GCSE-ENQ", [
  ["GCSE-ENQ-30","Which is primary data in a geographical enquiry?","A national Census table downloaded online","Measurements collected by the students","A historical map published by an archive","Rainfall normals from the Met Office","B","Primary data are collected first-hand for the investigation; secondary data were collected previously by another source.","Which source is secondary data?","A student's beach-width measurement","Today's responses to a class questionnaire","An Environment Agency flood-risk map","A field sketch drawn at the site","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-31","When is systematic sampling most appropriate?","When every site must be chosen by chance","When only convenient sites are available","When researchers select preferred results","When measurements are taken at regular intervals","D","Systematic sampling follows a consistent interval, such as every 10 metres or every fifth person.","What is one risk of systematic sampling?","A repeating pattern may bias the sample","It can never be repeated by another group","It produces only qualitative information","Researchers cannot record locations","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-32","Why use stratified sampling in a questionnaire?","It removes every source of response bias","It represents important population groups","It selects only the easiest respondents","It guarantees a very large sample","B","Stratification ensures relevant groups are included in proportions or numbers suited to the enquiry.","Which sample is stratified by age?","Interview the first ten people seen","Choose names using a random generator","Select respondents from each age band","Question only people near a college","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-33","What is a bipolar environmental-quality scale?","A measurement with no ordered values","A survey using only yes or no answers","A map showing two different settlements","A rating between opposite descriptions","D","A bipolar scale places observations between contrasting endpoints, such as very clean and very littered.","Why should every group use the same bipolar criteria?","It improves consistency between observers","It ensures every site receives the same score","It removes the need for judgement","It converts opinions into national Census data","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-34","What is the purpose of a pilot study?","To replace the full investigation permanently","To test and improve the method","To guarantee the hypothesis is correct","To remove all need for risk assessment","B","A small trial can reveal unclear questions, unsuitable intervals, equipment problems or missing recording categories.","A pilot questionnaire confuses several respondents. What should happen next?","Keep the wording so results remain unclear","Discard the whole enquiry immediately","Revise the questions before full sampling","Tell respondents which answer to select","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-35","What does the interquartile range measure?","The difference between maximum and minimum only","The most frequent value in a dataset","The total of all values divided by frequency","The spread of the middle half of data","D","IQR equals upper quartile minus lower quartile and is less affected by extreme values than the full range.","Why might IQR be preferred when data contain outliers?","Extreme values have less influence on it","It always equals the arithmetic mean","It identifies the cause of every anomaly","It converts qualitative data into maps","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-36","When is the median especially useful?","When categories have no meaningful order","When extreme values distort the mean","When every value must be added together","When the highest value is required","B","The median is the middle ordered value and is robust when a distribution is skewed or contains outliers.","House prices include one exceptionally expensive property. Which average is least distorted?","The range of all property prices","The arithmetic mean","The median house price","The maximum observed price","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-37","When is a choropleth map appropriate?","Showing individual point locations without areas","Plotting change continuously through time","Displaying exact routes between settlements","Shading areas by a standardised rate","D","Choropleths compare areas using rates or densities; raw totals can mislead where area or population differs.","Which dataset best suits a choropleth map?","Population density by local authority","Locations of individual bus stops","Daily temperature through one month","Migration flows between two countries","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-38","What is a key requirement for proportional-symbol maps?","Every symbol must be identical in size","Symbol size must follow a clear scale","Symbols should hide the mapped locations","The largest value uses the smallest symbol","B","Symbol area or size represents magnitude, so a legend is essential for accurate comparison.","What is one limitation of large proportional symbols?","They cannot represent quantitative values","They always indicate population density","They may overlap and obscure places","They remove the need for a base map","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-39","Why should totals be standardised before comparing areas with different populations?","Standardising proves one factor caused the pattern","Raw totals already account for population size","Every area must be given the same total","Rates allow a fairer comparison","D","Converting totals to rates or percentages controls for different population sizes and makes area comparisons more meaningful.","Two wards have different populations. How should crime totals be compared most fairly?","Calculate a crime rate for each ward","Use the larger raw total as the higher risk","Remove the population figures from the data","Assume both wards have equal populations","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-40","What should a student do with an anomalous fieldwork result?","Delete it without recording any reason","Check it and consider possible causes","Change it to match the expected trend","Use it as proof the method was perfect","B","An anomaly may reveal error or genuine local variation; it should be checked, retained transparently and discussed.","A repeated measurement remains unusually high. What is the best interpretation?","It must be a recording mistake","It should be replaced by the mean","It may represent genuine local variation","The entire enquiry becomes invalid","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-41","Which statement correctly distinguishes reliability from validity?","Reliable data always prove causation","Validity means results can be repeated exactly","The terms have identical meanings","Reliability is consistency; validity is fitness for purpose","D","Repeated similar results show reliability, while validity asks whether the method actually measures the intended concept.","A noise survey gives similar readings but is used to measure housing quality. What is the main concern?","The measure may lack validity","The readings cannot be reliable","The sample is necessarily random","The data must be qualitative","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-42","Why use triangulation in an enquiry?","It reduces evidence to one preferred source","Different methods can corroborate findings","It ensures all data are quantitative","It prevents conclusions changing","B","Combining observations, measurements, questionnaires and secondary sources can strengthen confidence when patterns agree.","Which combination best triangulates urban quality of life?","Three identical photographs from one point","Only one resident interview","EQS, questionnaires and Census data","A national map with no local evidence","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-43","Which practice makes a questionnaire more ethical?","Collect names when they are unnecessary","Pressure people to answer every item","Hide the purpose of the investigation","Gain consent and protect anonymity","D","Participants should understand the task, choose whether to take part and have unnecessary personal information protected.","What should a student do if a respondent declines?","Thank them and do not pressure them","Record invented answers on their behalf","Photograph them without permission","Ask for private details before leaving","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENQ-44","What makes a fieldwork conclusion well evidenced?","It repeats the hypothesis without results","It links the claim to analysed data","It ignores findings that disagree","It describes equipment instead of patterns","B","A strong conclusion answers the enquiry using specific processed results and acknowledges anomalies or limitations.","Which conclusion is strongest?","The hypothesis is true because expected","The sites looked different to us","EQS rose at four of five sites, supporting the hypothesis with one anomaly","No result needs to be quoted in a conclusion","C"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-RIVERFIELD", "GCSE-RIVERFIELD", [
  ["GCSE-RVF-19","How can river cross-sectional area be estimated?","Add velocity to channel gradient","Multiply width by mean depth","Divide sediment size by discharge","Subtract wetted perimeter from width","B","A simple estimate multiplies channel width by mean depth, using several depth readings across the channel.","A channel is 4 m wide with mean depth 0.5 m. What is its cross-sectional area?","0.125 square metres","1.5 square metres","2 square metres","8 square metres","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-20","What is the wetted perimeter of a river channel?","The straight-line width across the surface","The total drainage-basin boundary","The distance from source to mouth","The bed and banks touching water","D","Wetted perimeter is the length of channel boundary in contact with flowing water across a cross-section.","Why can a large wetted perimeter reduce velocity?","More boundary contact increases friction","It removes all turbulence from the flow","It makes the channel gradient steeper","More water avoids the bed and banks","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-21","What does a larger hydraulic radius usually indicate?","More channel contact for each unit of water","A more efficient channel shape","A river without any measurable depth","A lower discharge in every situation","B","Hydraulic radius compares cross-sectional area with wetted perimeter; a larger value generally means proportionally less friction.","Which channel is likely to be most efficient?","A shallow channel with rough exposed bed","A channel split around many boulders","A deep channel with less relative friction","A dry channel with a wide floodplain","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-22","Why can impermeable geology increase flood risk?","It stores all rainfall deep underground","It guarantees low drainage density","It causes rainfall intensity to fall","Less infiltration produces more runoff","D","Impermeable rock limits infiltration, increasing overland flow and producing a faster river response.","Which geology is likely to produce the slowest storm response?","Permeable chalk with underground storage","Saturated clay with compacted soil","Impermeable rock beneath urban paving","Bare rock on a steep valley side","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-23","How do saturated antecedent conditions affect fieldwork interpretation?","They guarantee typical discharge for the season","Recent rain may produce unusually high flow","They prevent a float moving downstream","Saturation makes channel width impossible to measure","B","Measurements after prolonged rain may not represent normal conditions and should be compared with weather or gauge records.","What secondary data would best contextualise unusually high discharge?","A national population pyramid","A coastal geology photograph","Recent rainfall and river-level records","A retail land-use survey","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-24","Why can a circular drainage basin respond quickly to heavy rain?","It always contains permeable limestone","It has no tributaries reaching the river","All slopes are necessarily gentle","Runoff arrives near the outlet together","D","Similar travel distances from many parts of a compact basin can synchronise tributary flows and raise peak discharge.","What field evidence helps assess basin shape and tributary pattern?","A topographic map of the drainage network","A single pebble measured at one site","A questionnaire about shopping habits","A climate graph from another country","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-25","Which is a useful qualitative method for assessing flood risk?","Calculate discharge from width and velocity","Annotate land use and bank condition","Measure depth at fixed intervals","Record pebble length with callipers","B","Annotated photographs or field sketches can record vegetation, defences, buildings and land use that influence exposure and runoff.","What makes an annotated river photograph analytical?","It includes only the date and weather","It is drawn without looking at the site","Labels explain how features affect risk","It replaces every quantitative measurement","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-26","Why use an Environment Agency flood-risk map?","It is primary data collected by the class","It shows exact future flood depths with certainty","It removes the need for site observations","It provides wider secondary-risk context","D","Flood maps show modelled zones beyond the limited sites students can visit, but assumptions and dates should be considered.","What is one limitation of a flood-risk map?","Modelled zones contain uncertainty","Maps cannot display areas or boundaries","Flood risk never changes over time","Secondary data have no source date","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-27","How can ranging poles help measure river gradient?","They calculate water chemistry automatically","They provide reference heights for angle measurement","They record velocity below the water surface","They sort sediment into size groups","B","Poles placed a measured distance apart provide consistent sighting points for a clinometer reading.","What reduces error in a clinometer gradient survey?","Hold poles at different unknown heights","Estimate distance without a tape","Use equal marked sighting heights","Take only one hurried reading","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-28","Why use a roundness chart when studying river sediment?","It measures the mineral composition exactly","It determines the age of each pebble","It weighs suspended sediment in the flow","It standardises visual shape estimates","D","A reference scale makes judgements of angularity or roundness more consistent between observers.","What is one limitation of a roundness chart?","Matching shapes remains partly subjective","It cannot be used at more than one site","It measures only river velocity","It guarantees identical observer scores","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-29","How can selecting only easy-to-reach pebbles bias a sediment sample?","It guarantees a random sample","Accessible material may be unrepresentative","It increases the river's sediment load","It makes every pebble equally rounded","B","Convenience sampling may overrepresent deposits near one bank or exposed bar rather than the whole channel.","Which method gives each visible pebble a more equal selection chance?","Choose only the largest stones","Collect stones beside the access path","Use random coordinates or numbers","Select the smoothest pebble first","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-30","Why repeat river measurements in different seasons?","Season never affects river discharge","Repeated visits remove every safety risk","One season always represents annual conditions","Flow and channel conditions vary over time","D","Seasonal rainfall, vegetation and antecedent conditions can change discharge, velocity and sediment exposure.","What does a single summer visit limit most?","Confidence that results represent the year","Accuracy of the map's north arrow","Ability to identify the river name","Whether measurements have units","A"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-31","Which control is most important before entering a river channel?","Increase sample size before checking depth","Assess depth, flow and safe access","Wear ordinary shoes to feel the bed","Work alone to reduce disturbance","B","A dynamic risk assessment should consider velocity, depth, slippery surfaces, weather and safe entry points.","What should students do if flow becomes faster after heavy rain?","Continue because the plan cannot change","Stand farther apart across the channel","Stop in-channel work and reassess","Remove buoyancy aids to move easily","C"],
  ["GCSE-RVF-32","What would most improve the validity of a downstream-change investigation?","Measure unrelated variables at random sites","Use different equipment at every location","Choose sites only where parking is easy","Sample comparable sites along the course","D","Sites should represent meaningful downstream positions while controlling avoidable differences in method and obstruction.","Why might a site immediately below a weir reduce validity?","The structure locally alters flow and sediment","The site is necessarily too far downstream","Weirs have no influence on channel processes","All river sites contain identical conditions","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-URBFIELD", "GCSE-URBANFIELD", [
  ["GCSE-URF-19","Which method best measures residents' perceptions of quality of life?","A river velocity float","A structured questionnaire","A geological cross-section","A beach sediment ruler","B","Questionnaires can collect attitudes to safety, services, housing and environmental quality.","Why combine perception data with environmental measurements?","Perceptions are always objectively correct","Measurements reveal every personal experience","The sources capture different dimensions","Combining data guarantees agreement","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-20","What is a semantic differential scale?","A tally of pedestrians passing a point","A measure of sound in decibels","A random list of street addresses","A rating between opposite adjectives","D","Respondents rate a place along a scale such as unsafe-safe or unattractive-attractive.","Why should opposite adjectives be clearly defined?","It reduces inconsistent interpretation","It ensures all respondents choose the middle","It turns opinions into ratio data","It removes the need for sampling","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-21","What might a high pedestrian count indicate in a shopping area?","River flood risk is increasing","The area has activity and footfall","Every pedestrian lives locally","Environmental quality must be high","B","Pedestrian flow can indicate vitality or accessibility but does not alone reveal residents' quality of life.","Why should pedestrian counts be interpreted cautiously?","They cannot be recorded quantitatively","A high count proves regeneration caused growth","Purpose and timing affect the total","Counts are identical throughout the day","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-22","What does a sound-level meter add to an urban enquiry?","A subjective opinion of building design","A count of different land uses","A direct measure of air pollution","Quantitative evidence of noise","D","Decibel readings provide comparable quantitative data, though traffic and temporary events affect them.","How should noise measurements be made comparable?","Use the same duration and position","Measure for random unrecorded periods","Hold the meter at different heights","Ignore unusual construction noise","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-23","Why create a land-use map around each urban fieldwork site?","It measures household income directly","It helps explain observed spatial patterns","It replaces every environmental survey","It shows future planning decisions with certainty","B","Land use provides context for differences in noise, footfall, traffic, building condition and service access.","Which mapping category would include shops and offices?","Agricultural land use","River-channel morphology","Commercial land use","Natural woodland succession","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-24","Why might deprivation data and an EQS show different patterns?","Both measure exactly the same concept","EQS includes every household income","IMD records only visible litter","Social and environmental measures differ","D","A place can look physically improved while residents still experience low income, poor health or insecure housing.","What is the strongest response to conflicting EQS and IMD results?","Explain that they measure different dimensions","Delete the dataset that challenges the hypothesis","Average unrelated scores into one number","Assume the newest-looking area has no deprivation","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-25","Which Census variable can help assess housing conditions?","Daily river discharge","Housing tenure and overcrowding","Beach sediment roundness","Prevailing wind direction","B","Census data on tenure, occupancy and household composition provide neighbourhood housing context.","Which Census variable helps investigate population ageing?","Annual retail turnover","Average building height","Age-group structure","Distance from the CBD","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-26","What does a high IMD rank for deprivation indicate?","Every street has poor environmental quality","The area contains no wealthy households","Crime is the only measured problem","Several deprivation domains contribute","D","IMD combines weighted domains and describes area-level deprivation, not every individual resident.","Why should IMD not be used to label every resident as deprived?","It summarises areas, not individuals","It contains no quantitative information","It measures only rural settlements","It is collected by student fieldworkers","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-27","Which questionnaire wording is least leading?","Do you agree this excellent scheme improved everything?","How satisfied are you with the scheme?","Why has regeneration failed local people?","Isn't the new park much safer now?","B","Neutral wording avoids signalling a preferred response and improves validity.","What is wrong with asking 'How bad is traffic here?'","It contains no topic for respondents","It can only produce numerical data","It assumes traffic is bad","It protects respondent anonymity","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-28","Why record whether respondents are residents, workers or visitors?","Only visitors can judge environmental quality","The groups must receive identical opinions","Residence status determines every answer","Perspectives may differ among users","D","People who live, work or visit may experience services, housing and change differently.","Which sample best compares stakeholder perceptions?","Include residents, workers and visitors","Question only classmates at one site","Select respondents who support regeneration","Exclude people living in the area","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-29","Why repeat an urban survey on weekdays and weekends?","Land use becomes identical every day","Activity patterns vary by day","Weather cannot change between visits","Repeated visits eliminate observer bias","B","Commuting, shopping and leisure flows vary through the week, affecting noise, traffic and footfall.","What should be recorded when comparing surveys on different days?","Only the final average score","The preferred conclusion beforehand","Time, weather and unusual events","Respondents' names and addresses","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-30","How can GIS improve presentation of EQS results?","It proves environmental quality caused migration","It changes negative scores into positive ones","It removes spatial variation from the data","It maps scores to reveal clusters","D","Mapped scores can show gradients, hotspots and relationships with regeneration sites or land uses.","Which GIS layer could help explain high traffic scores?","Major roads and junctions","National infant mortality rates","Rock types in the Scottish Highlands","River depth measurements upstream","A"],
  ["GCSE-URF-31","A regenerated site has high EQS but low resident satisfaction. What should students conclude?","One dataset must have been recorded incorrectly","Physical improvement may not meet social needs","The resident sample should be ignored","High EQS proves quality of life is high","B","Quality of life is multidimensional, so clean design may coexist with affordability, displacement or service concerns.","What extra evidence would best investigate the disagreement?","A second river velocity method","A national climate graph","Open responses about resident concerns","A map of distant coastal geology","C"],
  ["GCSE-URF-32","Why can fieldwork rarely prove regeneration caused every observed change?","Urban places do not change over time","Field data cannot contain measurements","Regeneration never affects quality of life","Other factors and baseline data matter","D","A before-after comparison, control area and wider evidence are needed to separate regeneration effects from broader trends.","What would strengthen a causal claim about regeneration?","Compare before-and-after data with a control area","Visit one regenerated site once","Assume all change began on opening day","Use only opinions from project managers","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-BIOSPHERE", "GCSE-BIO", [
  ["GCSE-BIO-28","Why does temperate deciduous forest have a seasonal growing cycle?","Rainfall is absent throughout summer","Temperature and daylight vary through the year","Trees cannot photosynthesise in any season","The soil remains permanently frozen","B","Warm summers allow growth, while colder, darker winters lead many trees to lose their leaves.","Why do many temperate trees shed leaves before winter?","Leaves become unnecessary in high rainfall","Roots stop absorbing minerals forever","It reduces water loss and frost damage","It increases exposure to winter storms","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-29","Why are extensive grasslands found where rainfall is moderate but seasonal?","Rainfall always supports dense tall forest","Grass cannot survive dry periods","Trees grow faster than grass in every climate","Water limits continuous tree cover","D","Seasonal moisture can support grasses while drought and fire restrict dense forest development.","What change could convert some grassland toward scrub or woodland?","More reliable moisture and less burning","A permanent reduction in precipitation","Greater grazing pressure every year","Longer periods of severe drought","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-30","Which adaptation helps a desert plant conserve water?","Broad thin leaves with many open stomata","A waxy surface and reduced leaves","Shallow roots confined to one small point","Soft tissue exposed to midday heat","B","Waxy surfaces, spines and specialised roots reduce water loss or improve access to scarce rainfall.","Why can widely spreading shallow roots benefit a desert plant?","They reach permanently frozen groundwater","They prevent any competition for light","They absorb brief surface rainfall quickly","They increase transpiration during drought","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-31","How does permafrost influence tundra vegetation?","It creates deep freely drained soil","It lengthens the growing season greatly","It allows large tree roots to penetrate deeply","It restricts drainage and root depth","D","Frozen subsoil limits rooting and can cause waterlogging during the short summer thaw.","Why are tundra plants commonly low-growing?","Low growth reduces exposure to cold winds","They compete beneath a dense forest canopy","Permafrost supplies abundant deep nutrients","Low plants receive no summer sunlight","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-32","How can underlying rock alter vegetation within one climate zone?","Rock changes the latitude of a place","It influences soil depth, pH and nutrients","Rock type determines global wind direction","All rocks weather into identical soil","B","Parent material affects soil chemistry, texture and drainage, favouring different plant communities locally.","Which vegetation difference could result from acidic and alkaline soils?","Both soils must support identical species","Soil pH affects only animal migration","Different plant species tolerate each soil","Alkaline soil prevents all decomposition","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-33","How does the biosphere help regulate atmospheric composition?","Plants release only carbon dioxide","Animals prevent gases mixing globally","Soils remove all oxygen from the air","Photosynthesis stores carbon and releases oxygen","D","Vegetation absorbs carbon dioxide and releases oxygen, while ecosystems store carbon in biomass and soils.","What would reduce a forest's carbon-regulating service?","Large-scale removal of living biomass","Natural regeneration of native trees","Increasing soil organic-matter storage","Protection from repeated burning","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-34","How do decomposers help maintain soil health?","They remove every mineral from the soil","They release nutrients from dead matter","They prevent litter reaching the ground","They stop plants taking up nutrients","B","Decomposers break down organic material, returning nutrients to soil for reuse by producers.","What is a likely effect if cold conditions slow decomposition?","Litter disappears more rapidly","Nutrients cycle instantly into plants","Nutrients remain stored in litter longer","Soil respiration stops on every warm day","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-35","Which is commercial exploitation of a water resource?","A household collects rainwater for daily use","A community uses a spring for drinking","Wildlife drinks from a forest stream","A company bottles groundwater for sale","D","Commercial exploitation extracts ecosystem goods at scale for markets rather than primarily for local subsistence.","What question best evaluates commercial water extraction?","Whether withdrawal exceeds natural recharge","Whether the company logo uses blue colours","Whether all groundwater tastes identical","Whether rain falls on another continent","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-36","How can rising affluence increase pressure on food resources?","Higher incomes always reduce total consumption","Demand for meat and processed food may rise","Affluence prevents food waste and imports","Wealth removes the need for agricultural land","B","Richer diets often require more land, water and energy, especially where livestock and processed foods increase.","Why can meat-rich diets have large resource footprints?","Livestock need no feed or water","Meat production uses only urban land","Feed conversion requires land and energy","All livestock permanently improve biodiversity","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-37","Why can rapid urbanisation increase water demand?","Cities eliminate industrial and domestic use","Urban residents collect no water at all","Piped systems always reduce total demand","Households and industry concentrate demand","D","Growing cities require water for homes, services, sanitation, energy and manufacturing.","Which strategy addresses urban water demand most sustainably?","Reduce leakage and promote efficient use","Extract groundwater faster than recharge","Allow untreated sewage into water sources","Ignore seasonal supply variation","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-38","What is a Malthusian preventative check?","Famine raising the death rate","Later marriage reducing birth rates","Disease following severe food shortage","War increasing mortality rapidly","B","Preventative checks lower birth rates; positive checks such as famine, disease and war raise death rates.","Which is a positive check in Malthus's theory?","Voluntary reduction in family size","Delayed marriage during hard times","Famine increasing mortality","Technology raising crop yields","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-39","What is one environmental cost of Boserupian agricultural intensification?","Innovation always reduces fertiliser use","Higher yields require no water","Intensification prevents soil erosion","Inputs may pollute soil and water","D","Technology can expand food supply but irrigation, fertilisers and pesticides may create environmental trade-offs.","What makes an evaluation of Boserup balanced?","Compare higher yields with environmental costs","Assume innovation has only benefits","Ignore whether poorer farmers can afford inputs","Treat carrying capacity as permanently fixed","A"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-40","Why do tundra and taiga form broad belts across high northern latitudes?","Governments planted them in straight lines","Large-scale climate controls their distribution","Every high-latitude soil has identical geology","Ocean currents have no regional influence","B","Latitude shapes temperature, sunshine and growing-season length, producing broad biome patterns modified locally by other factors.","Why can a mountain show several vegetation zones over a short distance?","Latitude changes rapidly up the slope","Rock type becomes identical with height","Temperature decreases with altitude","Drainage has no effect on vegetation","C"],
  ["GCSE-BIO-41","A climate graph shows temperatures from 4°C to 19°C. What is the annual temperature range?","4°C","15°C","19°C","23°C","B","Temperature range is the highest monthly mean minus the lowest: 19 - 4 = 15°C.","Monthly temperatures range from -6°C to 14°C. What is the annual range?","8°C","14°C","18°C","20°C","D"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-FORESTS", "GCSE-FOR", [
  ["GCSE-FOR-31","Why do many taiga animals migrate seasonally?","Summer food is absent from the biome","They track food and avoid severe winter conditions","Migration makes conifer trees grow faster","All taiga predators hibernate underground","B","Seasonal migration helps animals exploit summer productivity and escape extreme winter cold or food shortage.","Which change could disrupt a migratory taiga species?","Continuous protected habitat along its route","Greater availability of seasonal food","Roads fragmenting movement corridors","Reduced disturbance near breeding grounds","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-32","How do evergreen needles help conifers use the short taiga growing season?","Needles fall before every period of sunlight","Broad leaves develop under winter snow","Trees stop photosynthesis throughout summer","Leaves are ready when conditions improve","D","Retaining needles avoids the time and nutrients needed to regrow a full canopy each short summer.","Why is a waxy needle useful in the taiga?","It reduces water loss when water is frozen","It increases the surface area exposed to wind","It absorbs nutrients directly from snow","It makes branches hold heavier snow","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-33","Why are many taiga podzol soils acidic and nutrient-poor?","Rapid decomposition releases abundant nutrients","Slow decay and leaching remove nutrients","Warm conditions build a thick fertile topsoil","Broad leaves neutralise all soil acids","B","Cold slows decomposition, acidic needles form litter and precipitation leaches minerals downward.","How does slow decomposition affect the taiga nutrient cycle?","It moves all nutrients into biomass rapidly","It prevents a litter layer forming","Nutrients remain locked in litter","It makes the soil identical to rainforest soil","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-34","Why might warmer winters increase pest damage in the taiga?","Pests require permanent snow cover","Cold winters cause every insect to reproduce","Trees become immune to all disease","More pests survive to reproduce","D","Milder winters can reduce pest mortality and allow outbreaks that weaken or kill large areas of forest.","What can make pest outbreaks a climate feedback?","Dead trees store less carbon and may burn","Insects permanently lower atmospheric carbon","Damaged forests absorb more carbon dioxide","Pests stop decomposition of fallen trees","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-35","How can severe forest fires contribute to further climate change?","Burning creates a permanent carbon sink","Stored carbon is released to the atmosphere","Fire prevents any future soil respiration","Ash removes all greenhouse gases","B","Combustion releases biomass carbon and loss of trees reduces future sequestration; warming may also raise fire risk.","Which management reduces wildfire risk without removing the whole forest?","Allow fuel to accumulate beside settlements","Ban all monitoring during dry periods","Use controlled burns and firebreaks","Clear every tree across the biome","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-36","Why can acid precipitation damage forests far from its pollution source?","Acid gases remain inside factory chimneys","Rainfall cannot cross national borders","Conifers neutralise every acidic compound","Winds transport pollutants before deposition","D","Sulphur and nitrogen compounds can travel long distances and acidify soils and water downwind.","Which response best addresses transboundary acid rain?","International agreements to reduce emissions","Move monitoring stations nearer one tree","Increase sulphur content in power-station fuel","Treat each damaged leaf after rainfall","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-37","Why can local fuel-wood demand cause forest degradation?","Gathering always increases tree density","Repeated cutting exceeds regrowth","Fuel wood is obtained only from plantations","Local use cannot affect biodiversity","B","Where households depend on wood or charcoal, repeated extraction can thin forest and reduce habitat if regeneration is slower.","Which action could reduce fuel-wood pressure?","Prevent access to every alternative fuel","Increase charcoal demand in nearby cities","Provide efficient stoves and alternatives","Remove community rights without consultation","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-38","Why can commercial cattle ranching cause more extensive clearance than subsistence farming?","Commercial farms never use machinery","Cattle require dense intact canopy","Subsistence plots always cover continents","Large areas are cleared for market production","D","Commercial ranching serves wider markets and often converts continuous forest into pasture at large scale.","What evidence would distinguish commercial from subsistence clearance?","Large output is sold to distant markets","All crops are consumed by one household","No roads connect the farm to buyers","The cleared plot supplies only local food","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-39","How can ecotourism support rainforest conservation?","It earns income while maintaining forest value","It requires complete removal of wildlife","Tourism revenue automatically reaches everyone","Visitor travel creates no emissions","A","Low-impact tourism can fund protection and alternative livelihoods, but leakage, disturbance and unequal benefits must be managed.","What would make an ecotourism project more locally beneficial?","External firms retain all revenue","Local people gain jobs and decision-making power","Visitor numbers exceed ecological capacity","Traditional access to land is prohibited","B"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-40","How can agroforestry reduce pressure on remaining rainforest?","It separates crops from all trees","It combines productive crops with tree cover","It guarantees no soil nutrients are lost","It depends on permanent clear felling","B","Agroforestry maintains trees alongside crops, providing products, shade, soil protection and habitat.","Which practice best represents agroforestry?","Burn the whole plot after one harvest","Replace forest with a single paved surface","Grow cocoa beneath retained shade trees","Remove roots before every rainy season","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-41","What limits the effectiveness of CITES?","It encourages unrestricted wildlife trade","It protects habitats without enforcement","It applies only to fossil-fuel extraction","Illegal trade may continue without enforcement","D","CITES regulates international trade in threatened species, but monitoring, enforcement and domestic markets affect success.","Which action would strengthen CITES implementation?","Improve customs monitoring and penalties","Remove species-identification training","Permit undocumented wildlife exports","Ignore online trade in protected species","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-42","What is forest-protection leakage?","Trees absorb carbon outside a project","Clearance shifts to another location","Protected forests receive extra rainfall","Payments remain within local communities","B","Protection in one area may displace logging or agriculture elsewhere, reducing the net conservation gain.","How can monitoring detect leakage?","Measure only trees inside one small plot","Ignore land-use change beyond a boundary","Compare wider regional forest change","Record tourism numbers instead of clearance","C"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-43","Why can national-park creation conflict with Indigenous interests?","Indigenous people never use forest resources","Protection always recognises customary tenure","National parks permit all industrial extraction","Rules may restrict traditional land use","D","Conservation imposed without participation can limit livelihoods and rights even when communities have managed forest sustainably.","Which approach is most likely to reduce this conflict?","Co-manage the park with Indigenous communities","Exclude residents from every decision","Replace customary rights with logging licences","Move park boundaries without consultation","A"],
  ["GCSE-FOR-44","Why is taiga logging important to pulp and paper industries?","Conifers provide abundant softwood fibre","Only tropical hardwood can make paper","Pulp production requires no tree harvesting","Taiga wood is used solely for fuel","A","Large conifer forests supply straight, soft fibres suitable for timber, pulp and paper products.","Which practice makes softwood production more sustainable?","Harvest faster than forest regrowth","Replant and maintain mixed-age stands","Clear protected river buffers","Build roads through every intact area","B"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-ENERGY", "GCSE-ENE", [
  ["GCSE-ENE-30","What distinguishes a conventional oil source from an unconventional one?","Conventional oil is always renewable","It can usually be extracted by standard wells","Unconventional oil contains no hydrocarbons","Conventional reserves occur only on land","B","Conventional oil flows through reservoirs accessible by standard drilling; unconventional sources need methods such as mining, heating or fracturing.","Why are tar sands classed as unconventional?","They are refined without using energy","They form only beneath ocean trenches","Bitumen needs intensive extraction and processing","They can be replaced within one year","C"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-31","Why can a country have large oil reserves but modest current production?","Reserves are the same as annual output","All reserves must be extracted immediately","Production depends only on population size","Costs and access may constrain extraction","D","Reserves estimate recoverable stocks, while production depends on investment, technology, infrastructure, policy and market price.","What could make previously known reserves economically viable?","Higher prices or improved technology","A permanent collapse in oil demand","Removal of all transport infrastructure","Lower geological knowledge of the field","A"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-32","Why can an economic boom raise oil prices?","Industrial and transport demand may grow","Booms eliminate manufacturing activity","Oil supply automatically doubles first","Economic growth prevents air travel","A","Higher output, freight and consumer travel increase demand; if supply responds slowly, prices can rise.","When might a boom not cause a large price rise?","Demand rises while supply falls","Supply expands enough to meet demand","Refineries close during higher demand","Conflict blocks major export routes","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-33","How can a diplomatic dispute affect an energy-importing country?","It makes domestic geology change","It guarantees lower world prices","A supplier may restrict exports","It turns imported gas into renewable energy","C","Political relations can affect contracts, pipelines, sanctions and export decisions, exposing import-dependent countries.","Which strategy reduces vulnerability to one supplier?","Close all storage facilities","Depend on a single pipeline","Stop monitoring international markets","Diversify suppliers and energy sources","D"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-34","Why can tar-sands extraction damage water quality?","Processing uses water and can create polluted waste","Bitumen naturally purifies nearby rivers","Extraction requires no chemicals or tailings","Tar sands are collected only by wind power","A","Mining and processing disturb land, use large water volumes and create tailings that can contaminate ecosystems.","Which evidence best assesses downstream water impact?","Company employment totals alone","Water chemistry above and below the site","A photograph of an unrelated wind farm","National population density","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-35","Why can hydraulic fracturing cause small earthquakes?","Gas combustion changes the plate boundary","Shale becomes a moving tectonic plate","Fluid injection can reactivate faults","Fracking removes all pressure underground","C","High-pressure injection and wastewater disposal can alter stresses on existing faults and induce seismic events.","Which monitoring is most relevant near a fracking site?","Daily retail footfall","Coastal longshore drift","Forest canopy height only","Groundwater quality and seismic activity","D"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-36","What is one social cost of a large HEP reservoir?","Communities may be displaced by flooding","Dams require no land beyond the river","Reservoirs preserve every cultural site","Electricity cannot reach urban areas","A","Reservoir creation may displace settlements and livelihoods, alongside habitat and sediment impacts.","What is one benefit that can offset some HEP costs?","Permanent elimination of river ecosystems","Reliable low-carbon electricity generation","Guaranteed equal compensation for residents","No construction emissions or expense","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-37","Why can expanding biofuel crops threaten food security?","Biofuels use no agricultural inputs","Fuel crops always restore natural forest","Land and water may shift from food production","Biofuels can grow only on city rooftops","C","Competition for land, water and crops can raise food prices or encourage deforestation.","When is a biofuel most likely to have a lower land-use impact?","When rainforest is cleared for plantations","When food crops are diverted during shortage","When irrigation depletes local drinking water","When made from genuine waste material","D"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-38","What is the main grid challenge created by wind power?","Output varies with wind conditions","Turbines produce only fossil-fuel electricity","Wind farms cannot connect to transmission lines","Wind speed is constant across all seasons","A","Variable output requires storage, interconnection, flexible demand or backup generation to balance supply.","How can geographic spread make wind generation more reliable?","Every region becomes calm simultaneously","Different wind conditions smooth total output","Longer cables create extra wind energy","Turbines no longer need maintenance","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-39","Why can a very large solar farm create land-use conflict?","Panels prevent any electricity generation","Solar power requires underground coal seams","It competes with farming or habitats","Sunshine is unavailable outside cities","C","Utility-scale solar needs land and grid connections, creating trade-offs with food production, landscape and biodiversity.","Which design could reduce land-use conflict?","Clear protected habitat for new panels","Build only on the most fertile farmland","Remove panels from industrial roofs","Use rooftops and previously developed land","D"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-40","Which transport change is mainly an energy-conservation measure?","Replace a car journey with walking","Fit a more efficient engine to the same car","Use lighter materials in vehicle design","Recover braking energy in an electric car","A","Conservation reduces the amount of energy service demanded; efficiency delivers the service with less energy.","Which action is mainly transport energy efficiency?","Cancel an unnecessary journey","Use a vehicle requiring less energy per kilometre","Walk instead of taking a short car trip","Share one journey instead of driving twice","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-41","Why is hydrogen difficult to store and transport?","It is a solid at room temperature","It cannot release energy in a fuel cell","It has low density and needs compression","It occurs in unlimited pure underground lakes","C","Hydrogen has low volumetric energy density, requiring high-pressure tanks, liquefaction or chemical carriers.","What additional factor determines whether hydrogen is low carbon?","The colour of the storage tank","The distance from the Equator","The age of the delivery vehicle","The energy source used to produce it","D"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-42","How does a diverse energy mix improve energy security?","Disruption to one source has less impact","It guarantees every source has zero cost","Diversity removes the need for a power grid","All fuels become domestically available","A","Multiple fuels, suppliers and technologies reduce dependence on any single vulnerable source.","What is one cost of maintaining a diverse energy mix?","It always increases dependence on one supplier","Different systems need investment and coordination","It prevents renewable generation expanding","It makes storage technology unnecessary","B"],
  ["GCSE-ENE-43","A person's footprint falls from 12 to 9 tonnes CO2e. What is the absolute reduction?","1 tonne CO2e","2 tonnes CO2e","3 tonnes CO2e","4 tonnes CO2e","C","Absolute change is the original value minus the new value: 12 - 9 = 3 tonnes CO2e.","Emissions fall from 20 to 15 tonnes CO2e. What is the percentage decrease?","5%","15%","20%","25%","D"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-DECISIONS", "GCSE-DEC", [
  ["GCSE-DEC-23","What does AO4 mainly reward in a resource-booklet decision task?","Recall of every case-study statistic","Selecting and using source evidence","Writing the longest possible introduction","Choosing the examiner's preferred option","B","AO4 assesses skills in selecting, interpreting and applying information from the provided resources.","Which sentence demonstrates AO4 most clearly?","Deforestation is an important issue","I remember learning about forests","Figure 4 shows clearance doubled near the road","Option A sounds like the best choice","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-24","How should students use their own knowledge in the final decision?","Replace all resource evidence with memorised facts","Add any case study even if unrelated","Avoid subject terminology from taught topics","Apply relevant concepts to strengthen evidence","D","Own knowledge should explain or evaluate source evidence using concepts such as carbon sinks, scale or sustainability.","Which use of own knowledge is most effective?","Link habitat fragmentation to source evidence about a road","Describe an unrelated earthquake case study","List definitions without applying them","Ignore every figure in the booklet","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-25","Why should criteria be weighted before selecting an option?","Every criterion must be equally important","Priorities make the judgement transparent","The cheapest option always receives priority","Weighting removes all value judgements","B","Explicit priorities such as cost, fairness, sustainability or urgency explain why some evidence carries more weight.","Which conclusion uses weighted criteria?","Option C has several advantages","All options are equally good","Long-term water security matters most, so C is preferred","I chose C before reading the sources","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-26","Why should impacts on local stakeholders be separated from national impacts?","Local people never experience national policy","National growth automatically benefits everyone","Scale has no role in geographical decisions","Benefits and costs may be unevenly distributed","D","A scheme can raise national income while displacing or polluting a particular community.","Which is a distributional judgement?","National gains do not compensate the affected village without support","The project has one cost and one benefit","Every stakeholder will value the scheme equally","Local and national scales are identical","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-27","Why consider both short- and long-term effects?","Only immediate impacts can be evidenced","Benefits may change or create later costs","Long-term impacts are always more important","Time scale removes uncertainty from evidence","B","Jobs may arise quickly while ecosystem loss or debt persists, so a decision should compare duration and timing.","Which option shows a time trade-off?","A scheme has the same impact every year","A source contains no date","High initial cost leads to lasting savings","A map uses two different scales","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-28","Why does reversibility matter when assessing an impact?","Reversible impacts are always harmless","Irreversible change can be ignored if cheap","Every environmental effect is reversible","Permanent losses reduce future choices","D","An impact such as species extinction or ancient habitat loss may deserve greater weight because it cannot readily be undone.","Which impact is least reversible?","Extinction of an endemic species","Temporary construction noise","A short closure of one footpath","Seasonal traffic congestion","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-29","How should uncertainty in a source affect a decision?","Treat the estimate as exact fact","Acknowledge the range and manage risk","Discard all projections automatically","Select only the most favourable value","B","Uncertain evidence can still inform decisions when assumptions, ranges and consequences of error are considered.","A flood projection ranges from 20 to 45 hectares. What is the best response?","State exactly 45 hectares will flood","Ignore the source because it has a range","Test whether the option works across the range","Use 20 hectares without explaining why","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-30","How can qualitative evidence contribute to a decision?","It provides only exact numerical totals","It is automatically less useful than data","It proves every stakeholder agrees","It reveals experiences and priorities","D","Interviews and testimony provide insight into impacts that may not be captured by numerical measures.","How should qualitative and quantitative sources be combined?","Use statistics for scale and testimony for experience","Convert every interview into national GDP","Ignore views when numerical data exist","Assume one quotation represents everyone","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-31","What should a student do when two credible sources conflict?","Choose the source with the brighter graphic","Compare methods, interests and evidence","Assume both claims must be false","Avoid mentioning either in the answer","B","Conflicting evidence should be evaluated through provenance, methodology, scale and relevance rather than hidden.","Which response best handles conflicting job estimates?","Use only the larger number","Average them without checking methods","Explain why assumptions produce different totals","State that precise figures cannot be useful","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-32","Why does the date of a source matter?","Old evidence is always incorrect","The date changes the command word","All geographical conditions remain fixed","Conditions or technology may have changed","D","Data may become less representative after economic, environmental or policy change.","When might an older map remain useful?","When showing how land use has changed over time","When current boundaries are required precisely","When recent population totals are needed","When new infrastructure is being located","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-33","How can Paper 1 climate knowledge strengthen a Paper 3 decision?","It replaces the need to discuss people","It explains future hazard or resource pressures","It proves every renewable project is sustainable","It applies only to tectonic boundaries","B","Synoptic thinking can connect climate change to forests, energy demand, water or hazards within the source issue.","Which is a valid synoptic link?","Plate margins determine household recycling","CBD land value controls solar output","Deforestation weakens a carbon sink","River velocity measures political influence","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-34","Why must the final Paper 3 decision combine physical and human geography?","The issue has no environmental dimension","Economic evidence is always sufficient","Physical processes determine every viewpoint","People and environments interact","D","Resource decisions involve physical opportunities or hazards alongside social, economic and political impacts.","Which pairing shows a people-environment interaction?","Dam electricity benefits versus flooded habitat","Two figures using different font sizes","A population total and an unrelated date","A compass direction and a page number","A"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-35","What must a student do in the final choice-of-three question?","Recommend all options equally","Select one option and justify it","Invent a fourth option instead","Describe the booklet without deciding","B","Students choose one proposal and defend it using sustained evidence, own knowledge and comparison with alternatives.","Can a student gain high marks with any of the three options?","Only the first option can reach the top level","The shortest option is always preferred","Yes, if the justification is well evidenced","No, the mark scheme selects one correct policy","C"],
  ["GCSE-DEC-36","Why should a conclusion avoid introducing a completely new unsupported argument?","New claims automatically gain evaluation marks","Conclusions should contain no judgement","Evidence is needed only in the introduction","It should synthesise the case already made","D","The conclusion should draw together weighted evidence and criteria, not surprise the reader with an undeveloped claim.","What is the best role of a final paragraph?","Restate the decision and decisive evidence","List every source without interpretation","Introduce an unrelated case study","Repeat the question word for word","A"]
]);

addGeo640("GCSE-GEO-SKILLS", "GCSE-SKILL", [
  ["GCSE-SKILL-21","On an OS map, what does the number 184 beside a small dot usually show?","The grid-square population","The exact elevation at that point","The bearing to the nearest summit","The annual rainfall in millimetres","B","A spot height marks the surveyed elevation of a precise point, normally in metres above mean sea level.","What does a triangulation pillar symbol identify?","The centre of a settlement","A public telephone beside a road","A surveyed control point","The start of a national trail","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-22","When contour lines cross a river valley, which way does their V-shape point?","Toward the river mouth","Across the steepest valley side","Toward the lowest adjacent contour","Upstream toward higher land","D","Contours bend upstream where they cross a valley because elevation rises toward the river's source.","How can a valley be recognised from contours alone?","Contour bends point toward higher ground","Every contour forms a perfect circle","The lines remain equally straight throughout","Contour values fall toward the source","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-23","A path rises 120 m over 3 km. What is the average gradient in metres per kilometre?","20 m per km","40 m per km","60 m per km","360 m per km","B","Divide vertical change by horizontal distance: 120 / 3 = 40 metres per kilometre.","A road climbs 150 m in 5 km. What is its average gradient?","15 m per km","25 m per km","30 m per km","75 m per km","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-24","Why can a printed map's stated scale become wrong after photocopy enlargement?","Ground distances expand with the sheet","North changes direction on the enlarged copy","Contour heights double automatically","Map distances change but ground distances do not","D","Enlarging a map changes distances measured on paper, so the representative fraction or scale bar must be adjusted.","What is the safest way to measure distance on a map that may have been resized?","Use a scale bar printed on the resized map","Use the original ratio without checking","Assume every photocopy is exactly full size","Count grid squares as one kilometre each","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-25","What should a student do when an unfamiliar OS map symbol appears?","Infer its meaning from its colour alone","Consult the map legend","Assume it marks a tourist attraction","Replace it with a contour value","B","OS symbols are standardised, but the key is the authoritative guide; colour alone may represent several feature types.","Why is the legend essential when interpreting a blue map symbol?","Blue always means a navigable river","Every blue symbol marks the coastline","It distinguishes different water-related features","Map symbols change meaning with altitude","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-26","How is a topographic cross-section constructed?","Plot land use against population density","Join every settlement with a straight line","Copy contour lines without using height","Plot heights along a transect","D","Where a transect crosses contours, transfer distance and elevation to axes and join the points smoothly.","What does a cross-section show especially well?","Changes in relief along a line","Exact annual rainfall at each point","The age of every building","Migration flows between countries","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-27","A percentage rises from 40% to 50%. What is the increase in percentage points?","25 percentage points","10 percentage points","50 percentage points","90 percentage points","B","Percentage-point change subtracts the two percentages: 50 - 40 = 10 points. This differs from the relative percentage increase.","A share rises from 20% to 25%. What is the percentage-point increase?","2 percentage points","4 percentage points","5 percentage points","25 percentage points","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-28","A district has 24,000 people in 12 km². What is its population density?","288,000 people per km²","12,000 people per km²","24,000 people per km²","2,000 people per km²","D","Population density equals population divided by area: 24,000 / 12 = 2,000 people per square kilometre.","A town has 45,000 people across 15 km². What is its density?","3,000 people per km²","30,000 people per km²","45,000 people per km²","675,000 people per km²","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-29","A map area ranges from 120 m to 360 m elevation. What is its relief?","120 m","240 m","360 m","480 m","B","Relief is the difference between the highest and lowest elevations: 360 - 120 = 240 m.","The highest point is 510 m and the lowest is 170 m. What is the relief?","170 m","280 m","340 m","680 m","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-30","An index uses 100 as its base. What does a value of 125 mean?","The value fell by 125%","The value increased by 125%","The value is 25% of the base","The value is 25% above the base","D","An index of 100 represents the base; 125 is 25 index points, or 25%, higher.","A chart sets 2020 equal to 100 and shows 2025 at 80. How has the value changed?","20% below the base","20% above the base","80% below the base","The base increased by 80%","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-31","What is the mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7 and 8?","2","3","5","8","B","The mode is the value occurring most frequently, which is 3.","A land-use survey records R, C, R, I, R, C. What is the modal category?","Commercial","Industrial","Residential","There is no mode","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-32","What does a downward trend from left to right on a scatter graph indicate?","A positive correlation","No variables were measured","A perfect causal relationship","A negative correlation","D","A negative correlation means one variable tends to decrease as the other increases.","Which pattern shows no clear correlation?","Points scattered without a trend","Points rising tightly left to right","Points falling tightly left to right","Every point on one upward line","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-33","What is a divided bar chart especially useful for showing?","The exact route between two places","The composition of a whole","Relief along a cross-section","Direction measured as a bearing","B","A divided bar partitions a fixed total into proportional categories, making composition easy to compare.","What should the segments of a percentage divided bar total?","The largest category only","The number of survey sites","100% of the whole","The map's contour interval","C"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-34","Why might a radar graph be used to compare two places?","It shows migration direction with arrows","It maps exact locations using coordinates","It calculates distance from a ratio scale","It compares several indicators together","D","Each spoke represents an indicator, so profiles for places or times can be compared across multiple dimensions.","What is essential for a fair radar-graph comparison?","Use consistent scales on corresponding spokes","Give each place different indicator meanings","Remove labels from every axis","Join categories in a random order","A"],
  ["GCSE-SKILL-35","What does a proportional circle map communicate?","Direction of flow using arrowheads","Magnitude through symbol area","Relief using contour spacing","Change over time using joined points","B","Circle sizes represent values at locations, allowing spatial differences in magnitude to be compared.","What must a proportional-symbol map include for interpretation?","A climate projection to 2100","A written conclusion inside each circle","A size key linked to values","Identical symbols for every magnitude","C"]
]);

