  "media representation|The way newspapers, television, film and online platforms portray crime|Representations select stories and can shape perceived risk",
  "news values|Criteria such as drama, novelty, violence and celebrity that make events more newsworthy|They can make rare serious crimes appear typical",
  "crime amplification|A process where publicity and reaction increase fear, attention or offending|Media and official responses can sometimes intensify the problem they describe",
  "moral panic|Exaggerated public concern about a group or behaviour presented as a threat to social values|It often involves stereotyping, hostile reaction and demands for control",
  "folk devil|A person or group portrayed as responsible for a perceived social threat|Labelling a group this way can legitimise stronger control",
  "copycat behaviour|Offending allegedly influenced by exposure to similar acts in media or public discussion|The claim is difficult to prove because media use and other causes overlap",
  "fear of crime|Anxiety about becoming a victim or about crime in the wider community|Fear may be greater than objective risk and uneven between groups",
  "public perception|The beliefs held by people about crime, its causes and its frequency|Perception can be shaped by experience, media and political messaging",
  "crime myths|Popular beliefs about crime that are inaccurate or oversimplified|Comparing beliefs with surveys and records tests their validity",
  "campaign for change|Organised action seeking changes to law, policy, awareness or support|Campaigns use evidence, media, lobbying and public pressure",
  "Sarah's Law campaign|Campaigning that contributed to public access to information about people posing a sexual risk to children|It shows how high-profile cases can influence policy while raising privacy questions",
  "Hillsborough campaign|A long campaign seeking accountability and justice for victims and families after the disaster|It demonstrates how persistence can challenge official narratives",
  "Lawrence campaign|Campaigning after the murder of Stephen Lawrence that contributed to changes in policing and race policy|It shows how a case can expose institutional discrimination",
  "Macpherson report|The inquiry report that described the Metropolitan Police as institutionally racist|Its findings influenced policing, training and public debate",
  "media reliability|The extent to which media accounts are accurate, balanced and supported by evidence|Reliability varies with source, editorial pressure and access to information",
  "agenda setting|The media's influence over which issues receive attention|It can make some crimes politically urgent while leaving others marginal",
  "news framing|The selection and emphasis used to present an event or issue|Frames influence who is blamed, sympathised with or feared",
