  "psychological theory|An explanation focusing on cognition, personality, development or learning|It can explain individual differences but may neglect wider social forces",
  "Eysenck's personality theory|The claim that inherited personality dimensions can make conditioning against crime more difficult|It links temperament to social learning but has measurement limitations",
  "cognitive theory|An explanation focusing on how offenders interpret situations, rules and consequences|It can inform interventions that challenge distorted thinking",
  "differential association|Sutherland's theory that criminal behaviour is learned through interaction with others|It explains peer influence but cannot fully explain isolated or corporate offending",
  "social learning theory|The view that behaviour is learned through observing, imitating and being rewarded|It connects offending to role models and reinforcement",
  "behavioural reinforcement|A reward or consequence that increases the likelihood of repeated behaviour|It helps explain how offending can become habitual",
  "social control theory|The view that strong bonds to family, school and society restrain offending|It asks why most people conform rather than only why some offend",
  "attachment bond|A social connection that creates emotional commitment and discourages harmful behaviour|Weak or disrupted bonds may reduce informal control",
  "strain theory|The claim that blocked goals or unequal opportunities create pressure towards crime|It links offending to social structure but not all people respond to strain alike",
  "Merton's anomie|The tension produced when cultural success goals are not matched by legitimate means|It helps explain some acquisitive crime in unequal societies",
  "institutional anomie|The idea that economic values dominate other institutions and weaken social controls|It links crime to the organisation of society rather than individual pathology",
  "subcultural theory|The view that groups develop norms that support behaviour rejected by wider society|It explains group patterns but can stereotype working-class communities",
  "status frustration|The experience of failure to gain status through mainstream routes, leading some young people to alternative values|It was used to explain delinquent subcultures",
  "conflict subculture|A subculture where violence provides status or protection in areas with limited legitimate opportunities|It may explain some violence but risks treating communities as uniform",
  "Marxist theory|The view that crime and law reflect class inequality and capitalist interests|It highlights power and selective enforcement but may underplay victimisation and agency",
  "criminogenic capitalism|The claim that competitive profit-seeking and inequality generate pressures and opportunities for crime|It links white-collar and street crime to economic structures",
  "selective law enforcement|The unequal application of criminal law to different groups or offences|It supports arguments that official crime patterns reflect power as well as behaviour",
  "labelling theory|The view that social reactions and labels can shape identity and future behaviour|It explains secondary deviance but does not explain the original act well",
  "deviant career|A pattern in which repeated labelling and exclusion reinforce continued offending|It highlights the consequences of criminal justice intervention",
  "moral entrepreneur|A person or group that campaigns to define behaviour as deviant or criminal|They can influence law, policing and public concern",
  "interactionism|An approach studying meanings, interactions and how deviance is constructed in everyday life|It shifts attention from causes to social processes and reactions",
  "left realism|A perspective arguing that crime is real and disproportionately harms disadvantaged communities|It supports practical strategies addressing deprivation, marginalisation and subculture",
  "relative deprivation|The perception of unfair disadvantage compared with others|It can create resentment even where absolute living standards improve",
  "right realism|A perspective emphasising individual responsibility, opportunity, weak control and disorder|It supports situational prevention and firm social control",
  "broken windows theory|The claim that visible disorder can signal weak control and encourage more serious offending|It influenced policing but is criticised for criminalising poverty and minor behaviour",
  "situational crime prevention|Reducing opportunities for crime through design, security, surveillance and target hardening|It can reduce specific offences without addressing wider causes",
  "feminist criminology|An approach examining gendered power, victimisation, offending and criminal justice|It corrected the male focus of traditional criminology",
  "patriarchal control|The use of social, economic or physical power to regulate women and other marginalised groups|It helps analyse both victimisation and the operation of law",
  "intersectionality|The idea that gender, class, ethnicity, sexuality and disability combine to shape experience|It avoids treating any category as internally identical",
  "critical criminology|An approach questioning whose interests law and criminal justice serve|It focuses on power, inequality and the definition of harm",
  "evaluation of a theory|The assessment of a theory's evidence, strengths, weaknesses and usefulness|A high-level response compares explanatory power rather than naming a favourite",
  "reductionism|Explaining a complex behaviour through one narrow factor|It can make a theory simple but incomplete",
  "determinism debate|The disagreement over whether behaviour is caused or chosen|It affects ideas of responsibility, punishment and rehabilitation",
